export default {"format":"cadcraft-help","version":1,"app":"civilcraft","name":"CivilCraft","common":"basics","sections":{"start":"Getting started","tasks":"Walkthroughs","switching":"Coming from other software","reference":"Reference","trouble":"Troubleshooting and limits"},"variants":{"os":{"mac":"Mac","windows":"Windows","linux":"Linux"},"browser":{"chromium":"Chrome or Edge","firefox":"Firefox","safari":"Safari"},"shell":{"web":"In the browser","desktop":"Desktop app"},"net":{"online":"Online","offline":"Offline"},"input":{"mouse":"Mouse","touch":"Touch"}},"topics":[{"slug":"whats-new","scope":"civilcraft","title":"What's new in 0.6","summary":"CivilCraft 0.6 adds arcs in feature lines, corridor edges that follow other lines, a structure builder for pipe networks, vehicle swept paths, sanitary design flows, cut and fill sent to TakeoffCraft, and sums wherever a length is typed.","section":"start","order":5,"keywords":["whats new","what's new","release notes","changelog","changes","0.6","version","new features","arc","curved feature line","corridor target","widening","gutter grade","structure builder","structure type","rectangular structure","manhole","swept path","turning path","design vehicle","trailer","reversing","sanitary","sewer","peak flow","peaking factor","cut and fill","earthwork","takeoff","calculation","arithmetic","toolbar","interface size","pen","touch","indonesian"],"headings":[{"id":"new","text":"New","level":2},{"id":"feature-lines","text":"Feature lines","level":3},{"id":"corridors","text":"Corridors","level":3},{"id":"pipe-networks","text":"Pipe networks","level":3},{"id":"swept-paths","text":"Swept paths","level":3},{"id":"earthwork","text":"Earthwork","level":3},{"id":"typing-lengths","text":"Typing lengths","level":3},{"id":"fixed","text":"Fixed","level":2},{"id":"in-every-app","text":"In every app","level":2},{"id":"not-in-this-version-yet","text":"Not in this version yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThis is what changed in CivilCraft 0.6, compared with 0.5. Most of it was asked for on public ideas boards and in the CadCraft Discord.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"new\"\u003ENew\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"feature-lines\"\u003EFeature lines\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EArcs in feature lines.\u003C/strong\u003E A segment can be an arc whose ends sit at different elevations, with its grade running evenly along the curve. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"C\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while drawing (a point on the curve, then its end), or type a radius and \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E in the vertex&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003EArc\u003C/strong\u003E box in Properties. Properties shows the plan length and the 3D length. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/feature-lines#arcs\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/feature-lines\" data-hash=\"arcs\"\u003EArcs\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EArcs everywhere the line goes.\u003C/strong\u003E Stepped offsets, the profile view&#039;s crossings, the surfaces and the 3D view follow the arc. LandXML writes and reads feature lines with their arcs; DXF and DWG get 3D polylines in short pieces that CivilCraft reads back as the same feature lines, arcs and all. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/feature-lines#arcs-in-other-files\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/feature-lines\" data-hash=\"arcs-in-other-files\"\u003EArcs in other files\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"corridors\"\u003ECorridors\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECorridor targets.\u003C/strong\u003E A corridor&#039;s edge of pavement or shoulder edge, on one side, can follow a line in width or in elevation: a feature line, to widen a lane or hold a gutter grade, or another corridor&#039;s line, which keeps following when that corridor changes. Each target says at how many sections it reached its line. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/corridor#targets\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/corridor\" data-hash=\"targets\"\u003ETargets\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"pipe-networks\"\u003EPipe networks\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA structure builder.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew structure type\u003C/span\u003E builds a structure from its parameters: round or rectangular, inside size, wall, floor slab, sump and frame and cover, drawn in plan and section as you type. Rectangular structures show as rectangles on the plan, in 3D, DXF, DWG and PDF, and go to LandXML as rectangular structures. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/pipe-network#structure-types\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/pipe-network\" data-hash=\"structure-types\"\u003EStructure types\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EShare a type through the library.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as library item\u003C/span\u003E saves a structure type as a library item, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen a library item\u003C/span\u003E reads one back. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/pipe-network#share-a-type-through-the-library\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/pipe-network\" data-hash=\"share-a-type-through-the-library\"\u003EShare a type through the library\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESanitary design flows.\u003C/strong\u003E Set a pipe network&#039;s type to Sanitary and enter the people and area that enter at each manhole. CivilCraft works out each pipe&#039;s peak flow with a peaking factor and infiltration, how full it runs and how fast, lists pipes over the limit, too slow or too fast, and sizes the pipes. It was checked against a published design sheet. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/sanitary-design\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/sanitary-design\"\u003EWork out sanitary design flows and check the sewer\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"swept-paths\"\u003ESwept paths\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EVehicle swept paths.\u003C/strong\u003E Drive a vehicle with up to three trailers along a feature line or an alignment, forward or reversing: say where the trailer goes, and CivilCraft works out the steering the cab needs. The swept outline and the outer wheel paths show on the plan and go to the PDF, DXF and DWG. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/swept-paths\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/swept-paths\"\u003EVehicle swept paths\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERead the result.\u003C/strong\u003E Properties gives the largest steering and articulation against their limits, where it jackknifes and the swept area, and a slider shows the vehicle anywhere along the line. The vehicles to start from are placeholders: set your own design vehicle. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/swept-paths#read-the-result\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/swept-paths\" data-hash=\"read-the-result\"\u003ERead the result\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"earthwork\"\u003EEarthwork\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECut and fill to TakeoffCraft.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESend to TakeoffCraft\u003C/span\u003E at the foot of the Earthwork block saves the project&#039;s cut and fill, in cubic yards or cubic meters, as a quantities file. TakeoffCraft opens it as two items to price. Send again after a design change: the same items take the new numbers and keep their rates. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/volumes#send-them-to-takeoffcraft\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/volumes\" data-hash=\"send-them-to-takeoffcraft\"\u003ESend them to TakeoffCraft\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"typing-lengths\"\u003ETyping lengths\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESums wherever a length is typed.\u003C/strong\u003E Every box that takes a length or an elevation reads \u003Ccode\u003E4+7/16\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;6&quot; + 3/4&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E(24&#039;-0&quot;)/3\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E2*450mm\u003C/code\u003E, and plain lengths such as \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E450 mm\u003C/code\u003E, in the project&#039;s units. Station boxes keep reading \u003Ccode\u003E10+00\u003C/code\u003E as a station. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/units-and-stations#typing-lengths-and-sums\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/units-and-stations\" data-hash=\"typing-lengths-and-sums\"\u003ETyping lengths and sums\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"fixed\"\u003EFixed\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClosing a project where only feature lines had changed didn&#039;t ask about unsaved work. Feature lines count now.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"in-every-app\"\u003EIn every app\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe toolbar can&#039;t get lost.\u003C/strong\u003E While full screen hides the bars, a small \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow bars\u003C/span\u003E tab stays at the top of the window. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the layout\u003C/span\u003E puts the toolbar, panels, screen mode and menu style back to how the app starts (your interface size and panel text size stay). In CadCraft Studio, its own \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E has \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the layout in every app\u003C/span\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/lost-toolbar\" data-topic=\"basics/lost-toolbar\"\u003EWhere did my toolbar go?\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA menu on the toolbar.\u003C/strong\u003E Right-click the toolbar, or press and hold it, to arrange it, lock it so nothing moves by accident, or reset it. Ribbon menu styles collapse to their tab names: a tab opens its tools for one command; double-click a tab or use the pin to keep the ribbon open. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/lost-toolbar#the-toolbars-own-menu\" data-topic=\"basics/lost-toolbar\" data-hash=\"the-toolbars-own-menu\"\u003EThe toolbar&#039;s own menu\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EInterface size.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInterface size\u003C/span\u003E makes the bars, toolbars, panels and dialogs 80 % to 140 % of normal, for this app only, so two apps side by side on one monitor both fit. The drawing keeps its own zoom. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/lost-toolbar#everything-is-too-big-or-too-small\" data-topic=\"basics/lost-toolbar\" data-hash=\"everything-is-too-big-or-too-small\"\u003EEverything is too big or too small\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPen and touch.\u003C/strong\u003E On a tablet or a touch-screen laptop with a pen, your palm is ignored while you draw, fingers can be left to moving the view, a two-finger tap undoes and a three-finger tap redoes, and the pen&#039;s side button and eraser end can undo or redo. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPen and touch\u003C/span\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/pen-and-touch\" data-topic=\"basics/pen-and-touch\"\u003EDraw with a pen on a tablet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELive sessions can grow past 20 people.\u003C/strong\u003E In CadCraft Studio, when more people knock than the usual 20, Studio tells you what to expect and you choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAllow anyway\u003C/span\u003E for this session or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeep it at 20\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERecent edits\u003C/span\u003E in the Live panel shows who changed what. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/work-together-live#when-many-people-join\" data-topic=\"basics/work-together-live\" data-hash=\"when-many-people-join\"\u003EWork together live\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBahasa Indonesia\u003C/strong\u003E is new in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELanguage\u003C/span\u003E: a machine-made draft of every word, not yet reviewed by a native speaker. \u003Cstrong\u003ESpanish (Mexico)\u003C/strong\u003E is still a draft too. Help topics are in English.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EInstallers are still not signed\u003C/strong\u003E (signing comes next, in 0.7), and CadCraft Studio is not in an app store yet. Check the SHA-256 beside each file on getcadcraft.com/download. On Windows, if SmartScreen stops the installer, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore info\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERun anyway\u003C/span\u003E. On a Mac, the first time, right-click CadCraft Studio and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E. There is no iPad or iPhone build yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFixed:\u003C/strong\u003E with the bars hidden, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArrange the toolbar…\u003C/span\u003E in Settings was grayed out, and from the keyboard it lost its place after the first move.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-in-this-version-yet\"\u003ENot in this version yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EArcs in drawn breaklines.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECorridor targets move only the edge of pavement and the shoulder edge, in width or elevation, one side each. Daylight targets, regions and tapers aren&#039;t built yet. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/corridor#what-is-not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/corridor\" data-hash=\"what-is-not-built-yet\"\u003EWhat is not built yet\u003C/a\u003E for corridors.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStandard design-vehicle tables: the five vehicles that ship are labeled placeholders.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStructure types saved to the library are filed under &quot;other&quot;, because the library has no drainage category yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWater mains, flows that change over time, and links between water and storm networks. The sanitary design is steady peak flow only.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFeature lines shared between projects wait for team projects, and site massing isn&#039;t built yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEverything else is in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EWhat is not built yet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"This is what changed in CivilCraft 0.6, compared with 0.5. Most of it was asked for on public ideas boards and in the CadCraft Discord. New Feature lines Arcs in feature lines. A segment can be an arc whose ends sit at different elevations, with its grade running evenly along the curve. Press C while drawing (a point on the curve, then its end), or type a radius and L or R in the vertex's Arc box in Properties. Properties shows the plan length and the 3D length. See Arcs. Arcs everywhere the line goes. Stepped offsets, the profile view's crossings, the surfaces and the 3D view follow the arc. LandXML writes and reads feature lines with their arcs; DXF and DWG get 3D polylines in short pieces that CivilCraft reads back as the same feature lines, arcs and all. See Arcs in other files. Corridors Corridor targets. A corridor's edge of pavement or shoulder edge, on one side, can follow a line in width or in elevation: a feature line, to widen a lane or hold a gutter grade, or another corridor's line, which keeps following when that corridor changes. Each target says at how many sections it reached its line. See Targets. Pipe networks A structure builder. New structure type builds a structure from its parameters: round or rectangular, inside size, wall, floor slab, sump and frame and cover, drawn in plan and section as you type. Rectangular structures show as rectangles on the plan, in 3D, DXF, DWG and PDF, and go to LandXML as rectangular structures. See Structure types. Share a type through the library. Save as library item saves a structure type as a library item, and Open a library item reads one back. See Share a type through the library. Sanitary design flows. Set a pipe network's type to Sanitary and enter the people and area that enter at each manhole. CivilCraft works out each pipe's peak flow with a peaking factor and infiltration, how full it runs and how fast, lists pipes over the limit, too slow or too fast, and sizes the pipes. It was checked against a published design sheet. See Work out sanitary design flows and check the sewer. Swept paths Vehicle swept paths. Drive a vehicle with up to three trailers along a feature line or an alignment, forward or reversing: say where the trailer goes, and CivilCraft works out the steering the cab needs. The swept outline and the outer wheel paths show on the plan and go to the PDF, DXF and DWG. See Vehicle swept paths. Read the result. Properties gives the largest steering and articulation against their limits, where it jackknifes and the swept area, and a slider shows the vehicle anywhere along the line. The vehicles to start from are placeholders: set your own design vehicle. See Read the result. Earthwork Cut and fill to TakeoffCraft. Send to TakeoffCraft at the foot of the Earthwork block saves the project's cut and fill, in cubic yards or cubic meters, as a quantities file. TakeoffCraft opens it as two items to price. Send again after a design change: the same items take the new numbers and keep their rates. See Send them to TakeoffCraft. Typing lengths Sums wherever a length is typed. Every box that takes a length or an elevation reads 4+7/16, 12'6\" + 3/4\", (24'-0\")/3 or 2*450mm, and plain lengths such as 12'6\" or 450 mm, in the project's units. Station boxes keep reading 10+00 as a station. See Typing lengths and sums. Fixed Closing a project where only feature lines had changed didn't ask about unsaved work. Feature lines count now. In every app The toolbar can't get lost. While full screen hides the bars, a small Show bars tab stays at the top of the window. Settings › General › Reset the layout puts the toolbar, panels, screen mode and menu style back to how the app starts (your interface size and panel text size stay). In CadCraft Studio, its own Settings has Reset the layout in every app. See Where did my toolbar go?. A menu on the toolbar. Right-click the toolbar, or press and hold it, to arrange it, lock it so nothing moves by accident, or reset it. Ribbon menu styles collapse to their tab names: a tab opens its tools for one command; double-click a tab or use the pin to keep the ribbon open. See The toolbar's own menu. Interface size. Settings › General › Interface size makes the bars, toolbars, panels and dialogs 80 % to 140 % of normal, for this app only, so two apps side by side on one monitor both fit. The drawing keeps its own zoom. See Everything is too big or too small. Pen and touch. On a tablet or a touch-screen laptop with a pen, your palm is ignored while you draw, fingers can be left to moving the view, a two-finger tap undoes and a three-finger tap redoes, and the pen's side button and eraser end can undo or redo. Settings › General › Pen and touch. See Draw with a pen on a tablet. Live sessions can grow past 20 people. In CadCraft Studio, when more people knock than the usual 20, Studio tells you what to expect and you choose Allow anyway for this session or Keep it at 20. Recent edits in the Live panel shows who changed what. See Work together live. Bahasa Indonesia is new in Settings › General › Language: a machine-made draft of every word, not yet reviewed by a native speaker. Spanish (Mexico) is still a draft too. Help topics are in English. Installers are still not signed (signing comes next, in 0.7), and CadCraft Studio is not in an app store yet. Check the SHA-256 beside each file on getcadcraft.com/download. On Windows, if SmartScreen stops the installer, choose More info, then Run anyway. On a Mac, the first time, right-click CadCraft Studio and choose Open. There is no iPad or iPhone build yet. Fixed: with the bars hidden, Arrange the toolbar… in Settings was grayed out, and from the keyboard it lost its place after the first move. Not in this version yet Arcs in drawn breaklines. Corridor targets move only the edge of pavement and the shoulder edge, in width or elevation, one side each. Daylight targets, regions and tapers aren't built yet. See What is not built yet for corridors. Standard design-vehicle tables: the five vehicles that ship are labeled placeholders. Structure types saved to the library are filed under \"other\", because the library has no drainage category yet. Water mains, flows that change over time, and links between water and storm networks. The sanitary design is steady peak flow only. Feature lines shared between projects wait for team projects, and site massing isn't built yet. Everything else is in What is not built yet."},{"slug":"getting-started","scope":"civilcraft","title":"Getting started with CivilCraft","summary":"What CivilCraft does, how to open it, and a first ten minutes with the sample site from points to contours, a road and a saved project.","section":"start","order":10,"keywords":["start","first run","sample site","introduction","quick start","tutorial","begin","new project"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-need","text":"What you need","level":2},{"id":"open-civilcraft","text":"Open CivilCraft","level":2},{"id":"your-first-ten-minutes-the-sample-site","text":"Your first ten minutes: the sample site","level":2},{"id":"lay-out-a-short-road","text":"Lay out a short road","level":2},{"id":"save-your-work","text":"Save your work","level":2},{"id":"where-to-go-next","text":"Where to go next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ECivilCraft is the civil and site design app of the CadCraft family. It takes survey points and turns them into a surface (a TIN) with contours, then lets you lay out alignments with design profiles, sweep a simple road corridor, grade building pads to daylight, check a gravity pipe network against your rules and see cut and fill volumes. It reads and writes LandXML, exports the plan as DXF and plots it to PDF.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf you have used Civil 3D, the ideas will be familiar: points, point groups, breaklines, alignments by PI, profiles by PVI, corridors, grading, pipe networks. The way you work is different, and much smaller: there is no command line, no styles and no drawing file. Read \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/coming-from\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/coming-from\"\u003EComing from other civil software\u003C/a\u003E once before your first real job.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-need\"\u003EWhat you need\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA recent desktop browser: Chrome, Edge, Firefox or Safari. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/browsers\" data-topic=\"basics/browsers\"\u003EBrowsers\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA mouse or trackpad and a keyboard, or a touch screen: drawing has on-screen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFinish\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo point\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECancel\u003C/span\u003E buttons there (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/keyboard#mouse-trackpad-and-touch\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/keyboard\" data-hash=\"mouse-trackpad-and-touch\"\u003EMouse, trackpad and touch\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe plan draws in 2D with the browser&#039;s canvas. The \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/site-3d\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/site-3d\"\u003E3D view\u003C/a\u003E uses the browser&#039;s WebGL graphics; if it cannot start, the status bar says why and the plan still works.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENo account and no upload. Files open on your computer and save as downloads. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/your-files\" data-topic=\"basics/your-files\"\u003EYour files\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECivilCraft runs today as an early browser build that is still being made. Desktop builds are not available yet, and cloud saving is not available yet. Everything you make stays on your computer until you save or export it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-civilcraft\"\u003EOpen CivilCraft\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen CivilCraft in your browser.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWait for the screen to fill: a toolbar across the top, a panel on the left, the plan in the middle and a \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E panel on the right.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn an empty plan you see a box titled \u003Cstrong\u003EStart with survey points\u003C/strong\u003E. It lists the first steps and has a link, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen the sample site\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo use CivilCraft with no connection later, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E in the app bar and wait for \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E. It includes this help, the sample site, and the 3D and GIS libraries. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003EOffline\u003C/a\u003E. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHelp\u003C/span\u003E at any time to open this help beside the app.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-first-ten-minutes-the-sample-site\"\u003EYour first ten minutes: the sample site\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe sample site is a small survey in US feet: 276 points on state plane coordinates, a ridge, a swale with a flowline and two tops of bank. It comes with the app, so you can follow along without a file of your own.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen the sample site\u003C/span\u003E in the box on the plan. The \u003Cstrong\u003EImport points\u003C/strong\u003E dialog opens. It says &quot;276 points read as PNEZD (from the header row), separated by commas.&quot; and shows the first rows.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport\u003C/span\u003E. The plan fills with points, contours and the surface edge. The status bar at the bottom says the points were imported and how many triangles the surface has (546).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELook at the left panel under \u003Cstrong\u003EPoints\u003C/strong\u003E. The points are grouped by the first word of their description: CP, FL, GRD, RIDGE, TBN, TBS and TREE.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the \u003Cstrong\u003ESurface\u003C/strong\u003E column of that table, set \u003Cstrong\u003EFL\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ERIDGE\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ETBN\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003ETBS\u003C/strong\u003E to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBreakline\u003C/span\u003E. The surface rebuilds at once. The \u003Cstrong\u003ESurface\u003C/strong\u003E facts now say &quot;Breaklines: 4&quot;, and the contours bend sharply along the flowline and the ridge instead of cutting across them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the pointer over the plan. The status bar shows the easting and northing under the pointer and the ground elevation there.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cstrong\u003EDisplay\u003C/strong\u003E, set \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EContours every\u003C/span\u003E to 0.5 ft and turn on \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElevation bands\u003C/span\u003E. A color ramp appears with a legend under the checkboxes. Turn the bands off again when you are done.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou now have an existing ground surface. Next, a road.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"lay-out-a-short-road\"\u003ELay out a short road\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"A\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAlignment\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cstrong\u003EDesign\u003C/strong\u003E group).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick three points across the site, one after another. A red line follows the pointer and shows the length and bearing of the next tangent.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to finish. The alignment appears with stationing. The right panel shows \u003Cstrong\u003EAlignment\u003C/strong\u003E: its length, the PI table and the curve at the middle PI (100 ft radius, or smaller if 100 ft does not fit).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the right panel, under \u003Cstrong\u003EDesign profile\u003C/strong\u003E, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStart from the ground\u003C/span\u003E. The profile view opens under the plan with the existing ground and a straight design grade from end to end.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"I\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to turn on \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd PVIs\u003C/span\u003E, then click once in the profile view near the middle. A PVI appears.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the PVI table on the right, type a curve length (for example 100) in the \u003Cstrong\u003ECurve L\u003C/strong\u003E box of the middle PVI and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The profile now has a vertical curve, with its K value labeled.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cstrong\u003ECorridor\u003C/strong\u003E, tick \u003Cstrong\u003ESweep an assembly along the design profile\u003C/strong\u003E. The road is graded into the ground: lanes, shoulders and side slopes to daylight. The \u003Cstrong\u003EEarthwork\u003C/strong\u003E block shows cut and fill.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDo not worry about exact values. Every number can be changed in the right panel, and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes the last change.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save-your-work\"\u003ESave your work\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType a name in \u003Cstrong\u003EName\u003C/strong\u003E at the top of the left panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E). The browser downloads a file named after the site, ending in \u003Ccode\u003E.civilcraft.json\u003C/code\u003E. It holds the points, the surface settings, alignments, profiles, corridors, pads and pipes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo continue later, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E (or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and choose that file, or drop it on the plan.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThere is no automatic saving. If you close the tab with unsaved changes, the browser asks whether to leave; if you leave, the changes are gone. Save often.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-to-go-next\"\u003EWhere to go next\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/import-points\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/import-points\"\u003EImport survey points\u003C/a\u003E with your own files.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/build-surface\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/build-surface\"\u003EBuild and refine the surface\u003C/a\u003E with breaklines and a boundary.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/workspace\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/workspace\"\u003EThe screen, part by part\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/keyboard\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/keyboard\"\u003EKeys and mouse\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"CivilCraft is the civil and site design app of the CadCraft family. It takes survey points and turns them into a surface (a TIN) with contours, then lets you lay out alignments with design profiles, sweep a simple road corridor, grade building pads to daylight, check a gravity pipe network against your rules and see cut and fill volumes. It reads and writes LandXML, exports the plan as DXF and plots it to PDF. If you have used Civil 3D, the ideas will be familiar: points, point groups, breaklines, alignments by PI, profiles by PVI, corridors, grading, pipe networks. The way you work is different, and much smaller: there is no command line, no styles and no drawing file. Read Coming from other civil software once before your first real job. What you need A recent desktop browser: Chrome, Edge, Firefox or Safari. See Browsers. A mouse or trackpad and a keyboard, or a touch screen: drawing has on-screen Finish, Undo point and Cancel buttons there (see Mouse, trackpad and touch). The plan draws in 2D with the browser's canvas. The 3D view uses the browser's WebGL graphics; if it cannot start, the status bar says why and the plan still works. No account and no upload. Files open on your computer and save as downloads. See Your files. CivilCraft runs today as an early browser build that is still being made. Desktop builds are not available yet, and cloud saving is not available yet. Everything you make stays on your computer until you save or export it. Open CivilCraft Open CivilCraft in your browser. Wait for the screen to fill: a toolbar across the top, a panel on the left, the plan in the middle and a Properties panel on the right. On an empty plan you see a box titled Start with survey points. It lists the first steps and has a link, Open the sample site. To use CivilCraft with no connection later, click Save for offline in the app bar and wait for Offline ready. It includes this help, the sample site, and the 3D and GIS libraries. See Offline. Press F1 or click Help at any time to open this help beside the app. Your first ten minutes: the sample site The sample site is a small survey in US feet: 276 points on state plane coordinates, a ridge, a swale with a flowline and two tops of bank. It comes with the app, so you can follow along without a file of your own. Click Open the sample site in the box on the plan. The Import points dialog opens. It says \"276 points read as PNEZD (from the header row), separated by commas.\" and shows the first rows. Click Import. The plan fills with points, contours and the surface edge. The status bar at the bottom says the points were imported and how many triangles the surface has (546). Look at the left panel under Points. The points are grouped by the first word of their description: CP, FL, GRD, RIDGE, TBN, TBS and TREE. In the Surface column of that table, set FL, RIDGE, TBN and TBS to Breakline. The surface rebuilds at once. The Surface facts now say \"Breaklines: 4\", and the contours bend sharply along the flowline and the ridge instead of cutting across them. Move the pointer over the plan. The status bar shows the easting and northing under the pointer and the ground elevation there. Under Display, set Contours every to 0.5 ft and turn on Elevation bands. A color ramp appears with a legend under the checkboxes. Turn the bands off again when you are done. You now have an existing ground surface. Next, a road. Lay out a short road Press A (or click Alignment in the Design group). Click three points across the site, one after another. A red line follows the pointer and shows the length and bearing of the next tangent. Press Enter to finish. The alignment appears with stationing. The right panel shows Alignment: its length, the PI table and the curve at the middle PI (100 ft radius, or smaller if 100 ft does not fit). In the right panel, under Design profile, click Start from the ground. The profile view opens under the plan with the existing ground and a straight design grade from end to end. Press I to turn on Add PVIs, then click once in the profile view near the middle. A PVI appears. In the PVI table on the right, type a curve length (for example 100) in the Curve L box of the middle PVI and press Enter. The profile now has a vertical curve, with its K value labeled. Under Corridor, tick Sweep an assembly along the design profile. The road is graded into the ground: lanes, shoulders and side slopes to daylight. The Earthwork block shows cut and fill. Do not worry about exact values. Every number can be changed in the right panel, and Ctrl+Z undoes the last change. Save your work Type a name in Name at the top of the left panel. Press Ctrl+S (or click Save). The browser downloads a file named after the site, ending in .civilcraft.json. It holds the points, the surface settings, alignments, profiles, corridors, pads and pipes. To continue later, click Open (or press Ctrl+O) and choose that file, or drop it on the plan. There is no automatic saving. If you close the tab with unsaved changes, the browser asks whether to leave; if you leave, the changes are gone. Save often. Where to go next Import survey points with your own files. Build and refine the surface with breaklines and a boundary. The screen, part by part. Keys and mouse."},{"slug":"workspace","scope":"civilcraft","title":"The screen, part by part","summary":"A tour of every bar and panel in CivilCraft by its on-screen name, from the toolbar to the status bar.","section":"start","order":20,"keywords":["interface","screen","toolbar","panels","properties","status bar","profile view","layout","tour","ui"],"headings":[{"id":"app-bar","text":"App bar","level":2},{"id":"toolbar","text":"Toolbar","level":2},{"id":"left-panel","text":"Left panel","level":2},{"id":"site","text":"Site","level":3},{"id":"points","text":"Points","level":3},{"id":"surface","text":"Surface","level":3},{"id":"display","text":"Display","level":3},{"id":"plan","text":"Plan","level":2},{"id":"profile-view","text":"Profile view","level":2},{"id":"right-panel-properties","text":"Right panel: Properties","level":2},{"id":"status-bar","text":"Status bar","level":2},{"id":"dialogs","text":"Dialogs","level":2},{"id":"small-screens","text":"Small screens","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ECivilCraft has one screen. There are no tabs and no layouts. The side panels can move, float or go to a window of their own: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/arrange-panels\" data-topic=\"basics/arrange-panels\"\u003EArrange the panels and use a second monitor\u003C/a\u003E. From top to bottom and left to right:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe \u003Cstrong\u003Eapp bar\u003C/strong\u003E (CadCraft mark, app name, version, About, screen mode, Help, Save for offline, Paper and Model)\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe \u003Cstrong\u003Etoolbar\u003C/strong\u003E with seven groups of buttons\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe \u003Cstrong\u003Eleft panel\u003C/strong\u003E: Site, Points, Surface, Display and, once you import some, GIS layers\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe \u003Cstrong\u003Eplan\u003C/strong\u003E in the middle, with the \u003Cstrong\u003Eprofile view\u003C/strong\u003E under it when it is open\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe \u003Cstrong\u003Eright panel\u003C/strong\u003E: Properties of what is selected\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe \u003Cstrong\u003Estatus bar\u003C/strong\u003E along the bottom\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"app-bar\"\u003EApp bar\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe thin bar at the very top belongs to the CadCraft site, not to CivilCraft itself.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe version menu shows which build you are running.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAbout CivilCraft\u003C/span\u003E opens the app&#039;s page on the site.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFull screen\u003C/span\u003E cycles the screen modes: full screen, then full screen with the bars hidden, then back to normal. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E does the same on every platform, including a Mac (it is the Control key there, not Command). With the bars hidden, move the pointer to the top edge to bring them back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHelp\u003C/span\u003E (or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) opens this help in a side panel; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E closes it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E keeps CivilCraft in this browser so it opens with no connection; it then shows \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/files#online-and-offline\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/files\" data-hash=\"online-and-offline\"\u003EFiles you can open, save and export\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaper\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E switch between a light and a dark theme. The plan and profile follow the theme; PDFs always print on white.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"toolbar\"\u003EToolbar\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach group has a small label above its buttons. Hover over a button to see its tooltip and key.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EGroup\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EButtons\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat they do\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFile\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStart over, open a point file, LandXML or project, save the project as a download\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExport\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELandXML\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDXF\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDWG\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPDF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWrite LandXML 1.2, an R12 DXF or a DWG of the plan, or plot the plan to PDF\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESurface\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBreakline\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBoundary\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraw a breakline through survey points; draw the outer boundary\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDesign\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAlignment\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPad\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPipes\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProfile\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraw an alignment, a pad, a pipe run; show or hide the profile view\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUndo, redo, copy and paste survey points; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E holds Cut, Paste in place, Duplicate, Delete, Select all points and Select none\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGIS\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECoordinates\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMap\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESet the coordinate system, import and export GIS files, show map layers under the plan\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EView\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPick and move things; zoom to show everything; show the site in 3D (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/site-3d\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/site-3d\"\u003EView the site in 3D\u003C/a\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA drawing tool stays pressed while it is active. Clicking it again, pressing \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or pressing \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"V\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E returns to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E and the items in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E are grayed out when they cannot act; the tooltip says why (for example &quot;Select survey points first&quot;). Every button is listed in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/toolbar\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/toolbar\"\u003EToolbar reference\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a narrow window the toolbar scrolls sideways.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"left-panel\"\u003ELeft panel\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"site\"\u003ESite\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EName\u003C/strong\u003E: the project name. It names every file you save or export and is printed in the PDF title strip.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUnits\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUS feet\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMetres\u003C/span\u003E. This says what the numbers mean. Changing it does \u003Cstrong\u003Enot\u003C/strong\u003E convert coordinates; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/units-and-stations\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/units-and-stations\"\u003EUnits, stations and number formats\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"points\"\u003EPoints\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEmpty, it tells you how to open a point file. With points it shows the file name, the number of points and how many have an elevation, then a table of \u003Cstrong\u003Epoint groups\u003C/strong\u003E: one row per first word of the description (GRD, FL, TBS …), with a count and a \u003Cstrong\u003ESurface\u003C/strong\u003E choice:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPoints\u003C/span\u003E: the points go into the surface (the default for every group).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBreakline\u003C/span\u003E: the group&#039;s points are joined, in point-number order, into one breakline.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELeft out\u003C/span\u003E: the group does not touch the surface.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"surface\"\u003ESurface\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFacts about the existing ground: points, triangles, lowest and highest elevation, mean elevation, 2D and 3D area, breaklines and whether there is a boundary. Under the facts, a box lists anything the surface builder had to fix or could not do. Then:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELongest outside edge\u003C/strong\u003E: 0 keeps every outside triangle; a number trims long thin triangles off the outside.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELinks that appear when they apply: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove the boundary\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove drawn breaklines\u003C/span\u003E and, for a surface read from LandXML, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERebuild the surface from the points\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"display\"\u003EDisplay\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESurface\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExisting ground\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProposed (graded)\u003C/span\u003E. The second is available once a pad or corridor grades without problems.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EContours every\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EMajor every\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EBands\u003C/strong\u003E (the number of color bands).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECheckboxes: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EContours\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EContour labels\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETriangles\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElevation bands\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESlope arrows\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPoints\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPoint labels\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBreaklines\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESurface edge\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPipes\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA legend of band elevations appears when bands are on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDisplay settings are saved with the project but are not undo steps: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E never turns a checkbox back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"plan\"\u003EPlan\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe plan shows the points, the surface and everything you design, in plan view with north up. Wheel to zoom at the pointer, drag empty space to pan. The full list is in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/keyboard\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/keyboard\"\u003EKeys and mouse\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn an empty project a box titled \u003Cstrong\u003EStart with survey points\u003C/strong\u003E lists the first steps and offers \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen the sample site\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhile a drawing tool is active, a hint at the top of the plan says what to click and how to finish.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDragging a file over the plan shows &quot;Drop a point file, LandXML or project&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelected things are highlighted. A selected alignment or pad shows square grips at its PIs or corners; a selected structure shows one grip. Drag a grip to move it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelected survey points have a ring. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and a click adds or removes a point; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and a drag draws a box (or, dragged round them, a lasso) that adds the points inside it. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/copy-paste-points\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/copy-paste-points\"\u003ESelect, copy and paste survey points\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"profile-view\"\u003EProfile view\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProfile\u003C/span\u003E to open it under the plan. Its bar has:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ean alignment list (enabled when there are two or more alignments)\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd PVIs\u003C/span\u003E: while it is pressed, a click in the profile adds a PVI (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"I\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E toggles it)\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ea readout of station, ground, design elevation, grade and cut or fill under the pointer\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ea close button\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe profile shows the existing ground, the design profile, grades on each tangent, each PVI&#039;s station and elevation, each vertical curve&#039;s L and K with its BVC and EVC, high and low points, and the vertical exaggeration. It always fits the whole alignment: it does not zoom or pan. Moving the pointer along it puts a dot on the alignment in the plan.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"right-panel-properties\"\u003ERight panel: Properties\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIts heading says what it shows.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EProject\u003C/strong\u003E (nothing selected): the alignments and pads as links, the \u003Cstrong\u003EEarthwork\u003C/strong\u003E block, and the \u003Cstrong\u003EPipe network\u003C/strong\u003E block with its rules and problems.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAlignment\u003C/strong\u003E: name, start station, length, the PI table, the curves, the design profile&#039;s PVI table and facts, and the \u003Cstrong\u003ECorridor\u003C/strong\u003E settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPad\u003C/strong\u003E: name, elevation, cut and fill slopes, area, daylight facts and earthwork.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EStructure\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003EPipe\u003C/strong\u003E: sizes, rims, inverts, slope, cover and any problems.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPoint\u003C/strong\u003E and its number: the point&#039;s coordinates, elevation, description and group (read only).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFields take effect when you press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or leave the field. A value that is not accepted is marked as invalid and nothing changes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"status-bar\"\u003EStatus bar\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EE\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EN\u003C/strong\u003E: easting and northing under the pointer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EGround\u003C/strong\u003E elevation there (and \u003Cstrong\u003EGraded\u003C/strong\u003E where a pad or corridor changed it), or &quot;Off the surface&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe nearest alignment (or the selected one) with the \u003Cstrong\u003ESta\u003C/strong\u003E and offset (\u003Cstrong\u003EL\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003ER\u003C/strong\u003E) under the pointer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn the right, messages: what just happened, or why something was refused. A message clears itself after a few seconds.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"dialogs\"\u003EDialogs\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EImport points\u003C/strong\u003E: opens with every point file. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/import-points\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/import-points\"\u003EImport survey points\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPlot the plan to PDF\u003C/strong\u003E: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/plot-pdf\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/plot-pdf\"\u003EPlot the plan to PDF\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUnsaved changes\u003C/strong\u003E: asks before \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew\u003C/span\u003E or opening a project or LandXML file throws away unsaved work. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave a copy\u003C/span\u003E downloads the project first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"small-screens\"\u003ESmall screens\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUp to 760 pixels wide, such as on a phone, the plan keeps the screen: the toolbar is one row that scrolls sideways, and the panels open as sheets from a bar at the bottom. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETools\u003C/span\u003E hides the toolbar and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E puts it at the bottom.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"CivilCraft has one screen. There are no tabs and no layouts. The side panels can move, float or go to a window of their own: see Arrange the panels and use a second monitor. From top to bottom and left to right: the app bar (CadCraft mark, app name, version, About, screen mode, Help, Save for offline, Paper and Model) the toolbar with seven groups of buttons the left panel: Site, Points, Surface, Display and, once you import some, GIS layers the plan in the middle, with the profile view under it when it is open the right panel: Properties of what is selected the status bar along the bottom App bar The thin bar at the very top belongs to the CadCraft site, not to CivilCraft itself. The version menu shows which build you are running. About CivilCraft opens the app's page on the site. Full screen cycles the screen modes: full screen, then full screen with the bars hidden, then back to normal. Ctrl+Shift+F does the same on every platform, including a Mac (it is the Control key there, not Command). With the bars hidden, move the pointer to the top edge to bring them back. Help (or F1) opens this help in a side panel; F1 or Esc closes it. Save for offline keeps CivilCraft in this browser so it opens with no connection; it then shows Offline ready. See Files you can open, save and export. Paper and Model switch between a light and a dark theme. The plan and profile follow the theme; PDFs always print on white. Toolbar Each group has a small label above its buttons. Hover over a button to see its tooltip and key. Group Buttons What they do File New, Open, Save Start over, open a point file, LandXML or project, save the project as a download Export LandXML, DXF, DWG, PDF Write LandXML 1.2, an R12 DXF or a DWG of the plan, or plot the plan to PDF Surface Breakline, Boundary Draw a breakline through survey points; draw the outer boundary Design Alignment, Pad, Pipes, Profile Draw an alignment, a pad, a pipe run; show or hide the profile view Edit Undo, Redo, Copy, Paste, More Undo, redo, copy and paste survey points; More holds Cut, Paste in place, Duplicate, Delete, Select all points and Select none GIS Coordinates, Import, Export, Map Set the coordinate system, import and export GIS files, show map layers under the plan View Select, Fit, 3D Pick and move things; zoom to show everything; show the site in 3D (View the site in 3D) A drawing tool stays pressed while it is active. Clicking it again, pressing Esc or pressing V returns to Select. Undo, Redo, Copy and the items in More are grayed out when they cannot act; the tooltip says why (for example \"Select survey points first\"). Every button is listed in Toolbar reference. On a narrow window the toolbar scrolls sideways. Left panel Site Name: the project name. It names every file you save or export and is printed in the PDF title strip. Units: US feet or Metres. This says what the numbers mean. Changing it does not convert coordinates; see Units, stations and number formats. Points Empty, it tells you how to open a point file. With points it shows the file name, the number of points and how many have an elevation, then a table of point groups: one row per first word of the description (GRD, FL, TBS …), with a count and a Surface choice: Points: the points go into the surface (the default for every group). Breakline: the group's points are joined, in point-number order, into one breakline. Left out: the group does not touch the surface. Surface Facts about the existing ground: points, triangles, lowest and highest elevation, mean elevation, 2D and 3D area, breaklines and whether there is a boundary. Under the facts, a box lists anything the surface builder had to fix or could not do. Then: Longest outside edge: 0 keeps every outside triangle; a number trims long thin triangles off the outside. Links that appear when they apply: Remove the boundary, Remove drawn breaklines and, for a surface read from LandXML, Rebuild the surface from the points. Display Surface: Existing ground or Proposed (graded). The second is available once a pad or corridor grades without problems. Contours every, Major every and Bands (the number of color bands). Checkboxes: Contours, Contour labels, Triangles, Elevation bands, Slope arrows, Points, Point labels, Breaklines, Surface edge, Pipes. A legend of band elevations appears when bands are on. Display settings are saved with the project but are not undo steps: Ctrl+Z never turns a checkbox back. Plan The plan shows the points, the surface and everything you design, in plan view with north up. Wheel to zoom at the pointer, drag empty space to pan. The full list is in Keys and mouse. On an empty project a box titled Start with survey points lists the first steps and offers Open the sample site. While a drawing tool is active, a hint at the top of the plan says what to click and how to finish. Dragging a file over the plan shows \"Drop a point file, LandXML or project\". Selected things are highlighted. A selected alignment or pad shows square grips at its PIs or corners; a selected structure shows one grip. Drag a grip to move it. Selected survey points have a ring. Shift and a click adds or removes a point; Shift and a drag draws a box (or, dragged round them, a lasso) that adds the points inside it. See Select, copy and paste survey points. Profile view Press R or click Profile to open it under the plan. Its bar has: an alignment list (enabled when there are two or more alignments) Add PVIs: while it is pressed, a click in the profile adds a PVI (I toggles it) a readout of station, ground, design elevation, grade and cut or fill under the pointer a close button The profile shows the existing ground, the design profile, grades on each tangent, each PVI's station and elevation, each vertical curve's L and K with its BVC and EVC, high and low points, and the vertical exaggeration. It always fits the whole alignment: it does not zoom or pan. Moving the pointer along it puts a dot on the alignment in the plan. Right panel: Properties Its heading says what it shows. Project (nothing selected): the alignments and pads as links, the Earthwork block, and the Pipe network block with its rules and problems. Alignment: name, start station, length, the PI table, the curves, the design profile's PVI table and facts, and the Corridor settings. Pad: name, elevation, cut and fill slopes, area, daylight facts and earthwork. Structure or Pipe: sizes, rims, inverts, slope, cover and any problems. Point and its number: the point's coordinates, elevation, description and group (read only). Fields take effect when you press Enter or leave the field. A value that is not accepted is marked as invalid and nothing changes. Status bar E and N: easting and northing under the pointer. Ground elevation there (and Graded where a pad or corridor changed it), or \"Off the surface\". The nearest alignment (or the selected one) with the Sta and offset (L or R) under the pointer. On the right, messages: what just happened, or why something was refused. A message clears itself after a few seconds. Dialogs Import points: opens with every point file. See Import survey points. Plot the plan to PDF: see Plot the plan to PDF. Unsaved changes: asks before New or opening a project or LandXML file throws away unsaved work. Save a copy downloads the project first. Small screens Up to 760 pixels wide, such as on a phone, the plan keeps the screen: the toolbar is one row that scrolls sideways, and the panels open as sheets from a bar at the bottom. Tools hides the toolbar and Move puts it at the bottom."},{"slug":"files","scope":"civilcraft","title":"Files you can open, save and export","summary":"Point files, LandXML and CivilCraft projects in; projects, LandXML, DXF and PDF out; what each keeps, where the files go, and what changes offline.","section":"start","order":30,"keywords":["open","save","export","import","file formats","csv","txt","landxml","xml","dxf","pdf","json","download","project file"],"headings":[{"id":"what-it-opens","text":"What it opens","level":2},{"id":"what-it-saves","text":"What it saves","level":2},{"id":"what-it-exports","text":"What it exports","level":2},{"id":"where-the-files-go","text":"Where the files go","level":2},{"id":"online-and-offline","text":"Online and offline","level":2},{"id":"closing-with-unsaved-changes","text":"Closing with unsaved changes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ECivilCraft opens files from your computer and saves by downloading files back to it. Nothing is uploaded and nothing is kept on a server. See also \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/your-files\" data-topic=\"basics/your-files\"\u003EYour files\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-opens\"\u003EWhat it opens\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E (or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and choose a file, or drag the file from \u003Cspan class=\"hp\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:mac\" title=\"Mac\"\u003EFinder\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:windows\" title=\"Windows\"\u003EFile Explorer\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:linux\" title=\"Linux\"\u003Eyour file manager\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/span\u003E onto the plan. One file at a time; files over 200 MB are refused.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKind\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFile names\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat happens\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPoint file\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.csv\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.txt\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.pnt\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.xyz\u003C/code\u003E (any text file)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe \u003Cstrong\u003EImport points\u003C/strong\u003E dialog opens. The points replace the project&#039;s points; alignments, pads and pipes stay.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELandXML\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.xml\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.landxml\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Enew project\u003C/strong\u003E is made from the file (you are asked first if the current one has unsaved changes).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECivilCraft project\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.civilcraft.json\u003C/code\u003E (any \u003Ccode\u003E.json\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe project opens as it was saved (you are asked first if the current one has unsaved changes).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECivilCraft decides by the file&#039;s ending, then by its first characters: text starting with \u003Ccode\u003E{\u003C/code\u003E is read as a project, text starting with \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;\u003C/code\u003E as LandXML, anything else as a point file. A file the open box grays out can still be dropped on the plan.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EImporting a point file into a project that already has points replaces them without asking, and it clears the undo history (opening any file is a fresh start for undo). If the surface came from LandXML, importing points switches it back to a surface built from the points. Save first if you might want the old points back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDetails: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/point-file-formats\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/point-file-formats\"\u003EPoint file formats\u003C/a\u003E, \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/landxml-reference\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/landxml-reference\"\u003ELandXML: what is read and written\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-saves\"\u003EWhat it saves\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) downloads the whole project as \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt;.civilcraft.json\u003C/code\u003E, named after \u003Cstrong\u003EName\u003C/strong\u003E in the left panel. It keeps:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eevery point (number, coordinates, elevation, description) and the name of the point file they came from\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe surface settings: group choices, drawn breaklines, the boundary, the longest outside edge, and a TIN read from LandXML\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe display settings\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ealignments with their PIs, radii and start station, their design profiles (PVIs and curve lengths) and corridor settings\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Epads and the pipe network with its rules\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt does not keep the undo history. Surfaces, contours, daylight lines and volumes are not stored; they are rebuilt from the saved inputs when the project opens. A project file from a newer CivilCraft is refused with a message that names its version.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-exports\"\u003EWhat it exports\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EButton\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFile\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EContents\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELandXML\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt;.xml\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELandXML 1.2: points, the existing ground (and the graded surface when there is one) as TIN surfaces, alignments, ground and design profiles, the pipe network\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDXF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt;.dxf\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ER12 DXF of the plan linework as it is displayed, on named layers\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDWG\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt;.dwg\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same linework as a DWG drawing, in the format you pick (2000 to 2018)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPDF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt;.pdf\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA vector plot of the plan on a sheet, at a scale you pick\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGLB\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOBJ\u003C/span\u003E (on the 3D view&#039;s bar)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt;-3d.glb\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt;-3d.obj\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhat the 3D view shows as a 3D model: meters, Y up, true scale, around a recorded local origin\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat each one leaves out: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/landxml\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/landxml\"\u003EExchange LandXML\u003C/a\u003E, \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/dxf-export\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/dxf-export\"\u003EExport the plan to DXF\u003C/a\u003E (DWG too), \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/plot-pdf\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/plot-pdf\"\u003EPlot the plan to PDF\u003C/a\u003E, \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/export-3d\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/export-3d\"\u003EExport the site in 3D\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-the-files-go\"\u003EWhere the files go\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESaved and exported files go wherever your browser puts downloads, usually the Downloads folder. To choose a folder each time:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:chromium\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EChrome or Edge\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen the browser&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003ESettings\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003EDownloads\u003C/strong\u003E, and turn on \u003Cstrong\u003EAsk where to save each file before downloading\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:firefox\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EFirefox\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cstrong\u003ESettings\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003EGeneral\u003C/strong\u003E, and under \u003Cstrong\u003EFiles and Applications\u003C/strong\u003E choose \u003Cstrong\u003EAlways ask you where to save files\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ESafari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cstrong\u003ESafari &gt; Settings &gt; General\u003C/strong\u003E and set \u003Cstrong\u003EFile download location\u003C/strong\u003E to \u003Cstrong\u003EAsk for each download\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf you save twice with the same name, the browser may add a number to the second file, for example \u003Ccode\u003ESite (1).civilcraft.json\u003C/code\u003E. Open the newest one next time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"online-and-offline\"\u003EOnline and offline\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECivilCraft works only on your computer either way: opening reads the file in the browser, saving downloads it. To open CivilCraft with no connection at all, save it for offline use first:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhile online, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E in the app bar at the top.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe button counts up (&quot;Saving 12 of 30&quot;) and then shows \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E. CivilCraft is small (about 0.6 MB), so this takes a moment. The saved copy includes this help and the sample site.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith no connection, open the same address (or a bookmark of it). Everything works, including \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen the sample site\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe button&#039;s other states:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECould not save\u003C/span\u003E: some files did not download. Check the connection and click it to try again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline not available\u003C/span\u003E: this browser window cannot keep an offline copy (private windows and some managed computers block it). Use a normal window.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E to see when the copy was saved, with two choices: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave again (get the newest version)\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove the offline copy\u003C/span\u003E. Save again before a site visit so the copy matches the newest build. More in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003EOffline\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"net:offline\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EOffline\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou are offline now. If CivilCraft was saved for offline use, everything works, including the sample site. If not, keep working in this tab, but do not reload it until you are online again; without a saved copy, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen the sample site\u003C/span\u003E fails with &quot;The sample could not be loaded&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor field use, save CivilCraft for offline use and bring your point files on the laptop itself. Projects and exports save as downloads with no connection, so you can import a day&#039;s shots, check the surface and save the project on site.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECloud saving is not available yet, and desktop builds are not out yet. Today, the files you save are the only copy: keep them somewhere that is backed up.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"closing-with-unsaved-changes\"\u003EClosing with unsaved changes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen the project has unsaved changes and you close the tab, reload, or leave the page, the browser asks whether you want to leave. Its wording and look belong to the browser and cannot be changed by CivilCraft. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew\u003C/span\u003E, and opening a project or LandXML file, ask in CivilCraft&#039;s own \u003Cstrong\u003EUnsaved changes\u003C/strong\u003E box instead, with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECancel\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDon’t save\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave a copy\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"CivilCraft opens files from your computer and saves by downloading files back to it. Nothing is uploaded and nothing is kept on a server. See also Your files. What it opens Click Open (or press Ctrl+O) and choose a file, or drag the file from Finder / File Explorer / your file manager onto the plan. One file at a time; files over 200 MB are refused. Kind File names What happens Point file .csv, .txt, .pnt, .xyz (any text file) The Import points dialog opens. The points replace the project's points; alignments, pads and pipes stay. LandXML .xml, .landxml A new project is made from the file (you are asked first if the current one has unsaved changes). CivilCraft project .civilcraft.json (any .json) The project opens as it was saved (you are asked first if the current one has unsaved changes). CivilCraft decides by the file's ending, then by its first characters: text starting with { is read as a project, text starting with \u003C as LandXML, anything else as a point file. A file the open box grays out can still be dropped on the plan. Importing a point file into a project that already has points replaces them without asking, and it clears the undo history (opening any file is a fresh start for undo). If the surface came from LandXML, importing points switches it back to a surface built from the points. Save first if you might want the old points back. Details: Point file formats, LandXML: what is read and written. What it saves Save (Ctrl+S) downloads the whole project as \u003Cname\u003E.civilcraft.json, named after Name in the left panel. It keeps: every point (number, coordinates, elevation, description) and the name of the point file they came from the surface settings: group choices, drawn breaklines, the boundary, the longest outside edge, and a TIN read from LandXML the display settings alignments with their PIs, radii and start station, their design profiles (PVIs and curve lengths) and corridor settings pads and the pipe network with its rules It does not keep the undo history. Surfaces, contours, daylight lines and volumes are not stored; they are rebuilt from the saved inputs when the project opens. A project file from a newer CivilCraft is refused with a message that names its version. What it exports Button File Contents LandXML \u003Cname\u003E.xml LandXML 1.2: points, the existing ground (and the graded surface when there is one) as TIN surfaces, alignments, ground and design profiles, the pipe network DXF \u003Cname\u003E.dxf R12 DXF of the plan linework as it is displayed, on named layers DWG \u003Cname\u003E.dwg The same linework as a DWG drawing, in the format you pick (2000 to 2018) PDF \u003Cname\u003E.pdf A vector plot of the plan on a sheet, at a scale you pick GLB or OBJ (on the 3D view's bar) \u003Cname\u003E-3d.glb, \u003Cname\u003E-3d.obj What the 3D view shows as a 3D model: meters, Y up, true scale, around a recorded local origin What each one leaves out: Exchange LandXML, Export the plan to DXF (DWG too), Plot the plan to PDF, Export the site in 3D. Where the files go Saved and exported files go wherever your browser puts downloads, usually the Downloads folder. To choose a folder each time: Open the browser's Settings, then Downloads, and turn on Ask where to save each file before downloading. Open Settings, then General, and under Files and Applications choose Always ask you where to save files. Open Safari \u003E Settings \u003E General and set File download location to Ask for each download. If you save twice with the same name, the browser may add a number to the second file, for example Site (1).civilcraft.json. Open the newest one next time. Online and offline CivilCraft works only on your computer either way: opening reads the file in the browser, saving downloads it. To open CivilCraft with no connection at all, save it for offline use first: While online, click Save for offline in the app bar at the top. The button counts up (\"Saving 12 of 30\") and then shows Offline ready. CivilCraft is small (about 0.6 MB), so this takes a moment. The saved copy includes this help and the sample site. With no connection, open the same address (or a bookmark of it). Everything works, including Open the sample site. The button's other states: Could not save: some files did not download. Check the connection and click it to try again. Offline not available: this browser window cannot keep an offline copy (private windows and some managed computers block it). Use a normal window. Click Offline ready to see when the copy was saved, with two choices: Save again (get the newest version) and Remove the offline copy. Save again before a site visit so the copy matches the newest build. More in Offline. You are offline now. If CivilCraft was saved for offline use, everything works, including the sample site. If not, keep working in this tab, but do not reload it until you are online again; without a saved copy, Open the sample site fails with \"The sample could not be loaded\". For field use, save CivilCraft for offline use and bring your point files on the laptop itself. Projects and exports save as downloads with no connection, so you can import a day's shots, check the surface and save the project on site. Cloud saving is not available yet, and desktop builds are not out yet. Today, the files you save are the only copy: keep them somewhere that is backed up. Closing with unsaved changes When the project has unsaved changes and you close the tab, reload, or leave the page, the browser asks whether you want to leave. Its wording and look belong to the browser and cannot be changed by CivilCraft. New, and opening a project or LandXML file, ask in CivilCraft's own Unsaved changes box instead, with Cancel, Don’t save and Save a copy."},{"slug":"import-points","scope":"civilcraft","title":"Import survey points","summary":"Open a CSV or text point file, check the column order in the Import points dialog, fix rows that cannot be read, and sort the points into groups.","section":"tasks","order":10,"keywords":["import points","csv","txt","pnezd","penzd","point file","survey","cogo points","point groups","columns","northing easting"],"headings":[{"id":"before-you-start","text":"Before you start","level":2},{"id":"import-the-file","text":"Import the file","level":2},{"id":"check-the-groups","text":"Check the groups","level":2},{"id":"rows-that-are-not-read","text":"Rows that are not read","level":2},{"id":"replace-or-add-points","text":"Replace or add points","level":2},{"id":"related","text":"Related","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA point file is a text file with one point per line: point number, northing, easting, elevation and description in some order, separated by commas, tabs, semicolons or spaces. CivilCraft reads it in the browser and builds a surface from it at once.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"before-you-start\"\u003EBefore you start\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EKnow the file&#039;s column order if you can (PNEZD and PENZD look the same to a computer).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EKnow its units. CivilCraft does not convert: set \u003Cstrong\u003EUnits\u003C/strong\u003E in the left panel to match the file (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/units-and-stations\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/units-and-stations\"\u003EUnits, stations and number formats\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECoordinates can be large (state plane northings and eastings in the millions are fine).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"import-the-file\"\u003EImport the file\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, and choose the file. You can also drag it onto the plan. The \u003Cstrong\u003EImport points\u003C/strong\u003E dialog opens with the file name, a \u003Cstrong\u003EColumns\u003C/strong\u003E list set to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDetect\u003C/span\u003E, a summary line and the first six points.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERead the summary line. It says how many points were read, in which column order, how that order was found, and what separates the columns. The three ways the order is found:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;from the header row&quot;: the first line names its columns (Point, Northing, Easting, Elevation, Description, or X, Y, Z …). This is the safest.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;as chosen&quot;: you picked an order in \u003Cstrong\u003EColumns\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;guessed from the column count and your point row setting (PNEZD) — check that northing and easting are not swapped&quot;: there was no usable header. If your files are usually easting first, set \u003Cstrong\u003EPoint rows\u003C/strong\u003E to P, E, N, Z, D in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/settings\"\u003ECivilCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECheck the preview table. Its columns are always \u003Cstrong\u003EPoint\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ENorthing\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EEasting\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EElevation\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EDescription\u003C/strong\u003E, whatever order the file uses. Northings and eastings of a real site are usually clearly different sizes: if they look swapped, the guess was wrong.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf the order is wrong, choose the right one in \u003Cstrong\u003EColumns\u003C/strong\u003E (for example \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPENZD — point, easting, northing, elevation, description\u003C/span\u003E). The summary and preview update at once.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELook under the preview for \u003Cstrong\u003ERows not read\u003C/strong\u003E. Each line names the file line number, the reason and the start of the row. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/import-points#rows-that-are-not-read\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/import-points\" data-hash=\"rows-that-are-not-read\"\u003ERows that are not read\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport\u003C/span\u003E. The points appear on the plan, the view zooms to them and the surface is built. The status bar says, for example, &quot;Imported 276 points from site.csv; surface: 546 triangles.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf the project had no name yet, it takes the file&#039;s name.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"check-the-groups\"\u003ECheck the groups\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery point belongs to a group named by the \u003Cstrong\u003Efirst word of its description\u003C/strong\u003E, in capitals: &quot;GRD&quot;, &quot;FL 12&quot; and &quot;fl&quot; are groups GRD, FL and FL. Points with no description go in &quot;(no description)&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELook at the table under \u003Cstrong\u003EPoints\u003C/strong\u003E in the left panel: group, number of points and a \u003Cstrong\u003ESurface\u003C/strong\u003E choice.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEvery group starts as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPoints\u003C/span\u003E. Change flowlines, edges of pavement, tops and toes of slope, ridges and similar coded lines to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBreakline\u003C/span\u003E. Set groups that should not shape the ground (control points, tree tops, utility poles shot at the top) to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELeft out\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a point on the plan (with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E) to see its number, coordinates, elevation, description and group in the right panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA breakline group joins \u003Cstrong\u003Eall\u003C/strong\u003E its points into \u003Cstrong\u003Eone\u003C/strong\u003E line, in point-number order. That works when each coded line was shot in order with its own code. Two separate flowlines both coded &quot;FL&quot; become one line zig-zagging between them. If your codes are shared, use the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBreakline\u003C/span\u003E tool to click the points of each line instead (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/build-surface\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/build-surface\"\u003EBuild and refine the surface\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"rows-that-are-not-read\"\u003ERows that are not read\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EReason shown\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it means\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat to do\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;northing or easting is not a number&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA coordinate column holds text\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECheck the column order, or a stray text row in the middle of the file\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;elevation is not a number&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe elevation column holds text\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWrong column order, or a description in the elevation column\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;no point number&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe point column is empty\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENumber the point, or choose an order without P (NEZD, NEZ …): points are then numbered 1, 2, 3 …\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;point 12 appears twice (the first one is kept)&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo rows share a number\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERenumber one of them in the file if both are real shots\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;3 columns, expected 5 (PNEZD)&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe row is short\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA broken line, or the wrong column order\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA point with an empty elevation is kept, but it is not used in the surface. The \u003Cstrong\u003EPoints\u003C/strong\u003E facts show how many points have an elevation.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELines starting with \u003Ccode\u003E#\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E;\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E//\u003C/code\u003E are comments and are skipped. Blank lines are skipped.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"replace-or-add-points\"\u003EReplace or add points\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EImporting again \u003Cstrong\u003Ereplaces\u003C/strong\u003E all the points; it does not add to them. Your alignments, pads, pipes, drawn breaklines and boundary stay. The undo history is cleared, so save the project first if you may want the old points back. To combine two files, join them in a text editor or spreadsheet before importing (watch for repeated point numbers).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPoints cannot be moved, edited or deleted inside CivilCraft yet. Fix them in the file and import again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"related\"\u003ERelated\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/point-file-formats\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/point-file-formats\"\u003EPoint file formats\u003C/a\u003E: every column order and how headers are recognized.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/build-surface\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/build-surface\"\u003EBuild and refine the surface\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"A point file is a text file with one point per line: point number, northing, easting, elevation and description in some order, separated by commas, tabs, semicolons or spaces. CivilCraft reads it in the browser and builds a surface from it at once. Before you start Know the file's column order if you can (PNEZD and PENZD look the same to a computer). Know its units. CivilCraft does not convert: set Units in the left panel to match the file (see Units, stations and number formats). Coordinates can be large (state plane northings and eastings in the millions are fine). Import the file Click Open in the toolbar, or press Ctrl+O, and choose the file. You can also drag it onto the plan. The Import points dialog opens with the file name, a Columns list set to Detect, a summary line and the first six points. Read the summary line. It says how many points were read, in which column order, how that order was found, and what separates the columns. The three ways the order is found: \"from the header row\": the first line names its columns (Point, Northing, Easting, Elevation, Description, or X, Y, Z …). This is the safest. \"as chosen\": you picked an order in Columns. \"guessed from the column count and your point row setting (PNEZD) — check that northing and easting are not swapped\": there was no usable header. If your files are usually easting first, set Point rows to P, E, N, Z, D in CivilCraft settings. Check the preview table. Its columns are always Point, Northing, Easting, Elevation, Description, whatever order the file uses. Northings and eastings of a real site are usually clearly different sizes: if they look swapped, the guess was wrong. If the order is wrong, choose the right one in Columns (for example PENZD — point, easting, northing, elevation, description). The summary and preview update at once. Look under the preview for Rows not read. Each line names the file line number, the reason and the start of the row. See Rows that are not read. Click Import. The points appear on the plan, the view zooms to them and the surface is built. The status bar says, for example, \"Imported 276 points from site.csv; surface: 546 triangles.\" If the project had no name yet, it takes the file's name. Check the groups Every point belongs to a group named by the first word of its description, in capitals: \"GRD\", \"FL 12\" and \"fl\" are groups GRD, FL and FL. Points with no description go in \"(no description)\". Look at the table under Points in the left panel: group, number of points and a Surface choice. Every group starts as Points. Change flowlines, edges of pavement, tops and toes of slope, ridges and similar coded lines to Breakline. Set groups that should not shape the ground (control points, tree tops, utility poles shot at the top) to Left out. Click a point on the plan (with Select) to see its number, coordinates, elevation, description and group in the right panel. A breakline group joins all its points into one line, in point-number order. That works when each coded line was shot in order with its own code. Two separate flowlines both coded \"FL\" become one line zig-zagging between them. If your codes are shared, use the Breakline tool to click the points of each line instead (see Build and refine the surface). Rows that are not read Reason shown What it means What to do \"northing or easting is not a number\" A coordinate column holds text Check the column order, or a stray text row in the middle of the file \"elevation is not a number\" The elevation column holds text Wrong column order, or a description in the elevation column \"no point number\" The point column is empty Number the point, or choose an order without P (NEZD, NEZ …): points are then numbered 1, 2, 3 … \"point 12 appears twice (the first one is kept)\" Two rows share a number Renumber one of them in the file if both are real shots \"3 columns, expected 5 (PNEZD)\" The row is short A broken line, or the wrong column order A point with an empty elevation is kept, but it is not used in the surface. The Points facts show how many points have an elevation. Lines starting with #, ; or // are comments and are skipped. Blank lines are skipped. Replace or add points Importing again replaces all the points; it does not add to them. Your alignments, pads, pipes, drawn breaklines and boundary stay. The undo history is cleared, so save the project first if you may want the old points back. To combine two files, join them in a text editor or spreadsheet before importing (watch for repeated point numbers). Points cannot be moved, edited or deleted inside CivilCraft yet. Fix them in the file and import again. Related Point file formats: every column order and how headers are recognized. Build and refine the surface."},{"slug":"edit-points","scope":"civilcraft","title":"Edit a survey point","summary":"Change one point's number, coordinates, elevation or description, or drag it to a new place on the plan.","section":"tasks","order":14,"keywords":["edit point","move point","change elevation","renumber","point number","description","drag","northing","easting","fix a point","bust"],"headings":[{"id":"change-a-point-s-values","text":"Change a point's values","level":2},{"id":"move-a-point-on-the-plan","text":"Move a point on the plan","level":2},{"id":"breaklines-follow-the-point","text":"Breaklines follow the point","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA busted shot, a wrong rod height, a description typed wrong: fix the one point, and the surface follows.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-a-point-s-values\"\u003EChange a point&#039;s values\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E, click the point on the plan. \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E shows its number, easting, northing, elevation and description in boxes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the new value and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (or click away). Leave \u003Cstrong\u003EElevation\u003C/strong\u003E empty for a point with no elevation.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe surface, contours and everything built on them update at once.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach change is one step: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes it and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E redoes it. A value CivilCraft cannot use (a number another point already has, letters in a coordinate) is refused: the box is outlined and the status bar says why.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA new description code makes a new point group, as an import does (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/build-surface\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/build-surface\"\u003EBuild a surface\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"move-a-point-on-the-plan\"\u003EMove a point on the plan\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the point (it gets a ring).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag the ring. The status bar shows the easting and northing where it will go.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELet go to move it; its elevation stays. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E before letting go to put it back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"breaklines-follow-the-point\"\u003EBreaklines follow the point\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA breakline drawn with the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBreakline\u003C/span\u003E tool copies the points it goes through. When you move a point or change its elevation, the breakline corners on it move with it, in the same step. Breakline groups made from descriptions are always rebuilt from the points.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA surface read from a LandXML file keeps its own triangles: point edits change it only after \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERebuild the surface from the points\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"A busted shot, a wrong rod height, a description typed wrong: fix the one point, and the surface follows. Change a point's values With Select, click the point on the plan. Properties shows its number, easting, northing, elevation and description in boxes. Type the new value and press Enter (or click away). Leave Elevation empty for a point with no elevation. The surface, contours and everything built on them update at once. Each change is one step: Ctrl+Z undoes it and Ctrl+Y redoes it. A value CivilCraft cannot use (a number another point already has, letters in a coordinate) is refused: the box is outlined and the status bar says why. A new description code makes a new point group, as an import does (see Build a surface). Move a point on the plan Select the point (it gets a ring). Drag the ring. The status bar shows the easting and northing where it will go. Let go to move it; its elevation stays. Press Esc before letting go to put it back. Breaklines follow the point A breakline drawn with the Breakline tool copies the points it goes through. When you move a point or change its elevation, the breakline corners on it move with it, in the same step. Breakline groups made from descriptions are always rebuilt from the points. A surface read from a LandXML file keeps its own triangles: point edits change it only after Rebuild the surface from the points."},{"slug":"elevation-grids-and-lidar","scope":"civilcraft","title":"Elevation grids and LiDAR","summary":"Build the existing ground from a DEM (GeoTIFF or ASCII grid) or LiDAR points (LAS, LAZ) — pick the area, thin the data, choose classes and check the elevation units — and hand a surface back as a GeoTIFF elevation grid.","section":"tasks","order":14,"keywords":["dem","dtm","elevation grid","geotiff","tif","ascii grid","asc","lidar","las","laz","point cloud","classes","ground","thinning","nodata","raster","export geotiff","gis"],"headings":[{"id":"open-the-file","text":"Open the file","level":2},{"id":"choose-what-to-bring-in","text":"Choose what to bring in","level":2},{"id":"what-you-get","text":"What you get","level":2},{"id":"coordinate-systems","text":"Coordinate systems","level":2},{"id":"export-a-surface-as-a-geotiff","text":"Export a surface as a GeoTIFF","level":2},{"id":"what-was-checked","text":"What was checked","level":2},{"id":"not-built-yet","text":"Not built yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EPublic elevation data comes two ways: \u003Cstrong\u003Eelevation grids\u003C/strong\u003E (a DEM: one elevation per square cell, usually a GeoTIFF \u003Ccode\u003E.tif\u003C/code\u003E or an ASCII grid \u003Ccode\u003E.asc\u003C/code\u003E) and \u003Cstrong\u003ELiDAR points\u003C/strong\u003E (millions of laser returns in a \u003Ccode\u003E.las\u003C/code\u003E file, or \u003Ccode\u003E.laz\u003C/code\u003E when compressed). CivilCraft turns either into survey points or a surface, inside the browser: nothing is uploaded.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-the-file\"\u003EOpen the file\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport\u003C/span\u003E in the GIS group) and choose the file, or drag it onto the plan. For an ASCII grid, choose its \u003Ccode\u003E.prj\u003C/code\u003E file too, so CivilCraft knows its coordinate system.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe import dialog shows what the file says about itself: its size, cells or points, its coordinate system, where it is in latitude and longitude, and its elevation range. For LiDAR it first reads every point once to count the \u003Cstrong\u003Eclasses\u003C/strong\u003E (a progress bar shows how far it is; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECancel\u003C/span\u003E stops it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA GeoTIFF with three or four bands (red, green, blue) is a picture, not elevations: it goes to the map layers instead.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"choose-what-to-bring-in\"\u003EChoose what to bring in\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EArea\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThe project’s extents and a margin\u003C/span\u003E (type the margin), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhat the plan shows now\u003C/span\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThe whole file\u003C/span\u003E. Only that part of the file is read.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThin to\u003C/strong\u003E: the spacing between the points you get, in the project&#039;s units. The line under the settings shows how many points that gives as you type. For a grid, CivilCraft keeps every second, third … cell, so the elevations stay exactly the file&#039;s; it never averages cells together. For LiDAR, the area is cut into squares of that size and one point is kept per square: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThe point nearest each cell’s center\u003C/span\u003E (the default), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThe lowest point in each cell\u003C/span\u003E (good when the file has no ground class: the ground is under the trees) or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThe average of each cell’s points\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EClasses\u003C/strong\u003E (LiDAR): ground (class 2) is ticked when the file has it; buildings, trees and noise are left out. When there is no ground class every class is ticked, and the dialog says so.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EElevations in the file\u003C/strong\u003E: meters, feet or US survey feet. When the file says, its unit is already chosen. When it does not, you must choose: the dialog shows the elevation range both ways so you can tell which makes sense for the site.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EImport as\u003C/strong\u003E (grids only): \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESurvey points (description DEM)\u003C/span\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThe surface itself (a grid of triangles)\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOne import takes at most 500,000 points. When a choice would give more, the dialog says by how much and what spacing would fit, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport\u003C/span\u003E stays off.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport\u003C/span\u003E. A progress bar shows the reading; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECancel\u003C/span\u003E stops it and nothing changes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EVertical units are a common trap. Many state plane DEMs have eastings and northings in feet but elevations in meters. Check the range the dialog shows against what you know of the site.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-get\"\u003EWhat you get\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESurvey points\u003C/strong\u003E: numbered after your highest point number, with the description \u003Ccode\u003EDEM\u003C/code\u003E (grids) or \u003Ccode\u003ELIDAR\u003C/code\u003E, so they form one point group under \u003Cstrong\u003EPoints\u003C/strong\u003E and build the surface like any other points. Importing them is one step: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes them out again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe surface itself\u003C/strong\u003E: a grid TIN, two triangles per cell, split on the south-west to north-east diagonal. A triangle with a corner that has no data is left out, so holes in the data stay holes. The surface equals the grid at every grid point and is a flat plane inside each triangle between them. It replaces the project&#039;s surface (the dialog asks first); \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E brings the previous surface back. The left panel&#039;s heading says \u003Cstrong\u003ESurface (from an elevation grid)\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"coordinate-systems\"\u003ECoordinate systems\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf the project has a coordinate system and the file names another, every point is converted to the project&#039;s system (and to its ground or local coordinates when it has them).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf the project has none and the file&#039;s system is in the project&#039;s units (feet or meters), the dialog offers to make it the project&#039;s coordinate system, in the same undo step as the points.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf the file is in latitude and longitude, or in meters for a feet project, the dialog asks you to pick the project&#039;s coordinate system first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf the file does not say, its numbers are taken as the project&#039;s coordinates. You can pick the file&#039;s system instead.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"export-a-surface-as-a-geotiff\"\u003EExport a surface as a GeoTIFF\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E in the GIS group.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the GeoTIFF part choose the surface (existing ground or the graded surface) and the cell size. The default comes from the surface&#039;s average triangle edge. The dialog shows the size of the grid it will write.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload GeoTIFF\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe file is one band of 32-bit floating point elevations in the project&#039;s units, over the surface&#039;s extent; cells outside the surface are &quot;no data&quot; (−3.4028235e38). Each cell&#039;s value is the surface&#039;s elevation at the cell&#039;s center. Coordinates are \u003Cstrong\u003Egrid\u003C/strong\u003E coordinates: a project in ground or local coordinates is converted back to the grid. The file names the project&#039;s coordinate system by its EPSG code; a system without an EPSG code is not written, and the dialog warns you to tell the GIS department which system it is.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-was-checked\"\u003EWhat was checked\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe GeoTIFF and ASCII grid readers were checked against GDAL (through rasterio), the library most GIS programs use to read these files: 32-bit float and 16-bit integer grids, tiled and stripped, DEFLATE and LZW, nodata, scale and offset, pixel-is-point, a UTM grid, a state plane grid in US survey feet and a latitude/longitude grid — every value equal.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe LAS and LAZ readers were checked against laspy: LAS 1.2 and 1.4, point formats 0, 1, 3, 6, 7 and 8, with and without a coordinate system, and LAZ written by its LASzip-compatible backend — every point equal.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EConverted coordinates were checked against PROJ to 1 mm.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EExported GeoTIFFs were read back by GDAL: coordinate system, cell positions, nodata, units, and values equal to the surface to 0.0001.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENothing here has been opened in other GIS or CAD programs.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EVertical datum changes (geoid models): elevations are used as the file has them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWaveform data, LiDAR returns other than by class, colors and intensities.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESeveral bands, overviews, or files over 200 MB.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWriting LAS or LAZ.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Public elevation data comes two ways: elevation grids (a DEM: one elevation per square cell, usually a GeoTIFF .tif or an ASCII grid .asc) and LiDAR points (millions of laser returns in a .las file, or .laz when compressed). CivilCraft turns either into survey points or a surface, inside the browser: nothing is uploaded. Open the file Click Open (or Import in the GIS group) and choose the file, or drag it onto the plan. For an ASCII grid, choose its .prj file too, so CivilCraft knows its coordinate system. The import dialog shows what the file says about itself: its size, cells or points, its coordinate system, where it is in latitude and longitude, and its elevation range. For LiDAR it first reads every point once to count the classes (a progress bar shows how far it is; Cancel stops it). A GeoTIFF with three or four bands (red, green, blue) is a picture, not elevations: it goes to the map layers instead. Choose what to bring in Area: The project’s extents and a margin (type the margin), What the plan shows now, or The whole file. Only that part of the file is read. Thin to: the spacing between the points you get, in the project's units. The line under the settings shows how many points that gives as you type. For a grid, CivilCraft keeps every second, third … cell, so the elevations stay exactly the file's; it never averages cells together. For LiDAR, the area is cut into squares of that size and one point is kept per square: The point nearest each cell’s center (the default), The lowest point in each cell (good when the file has no ground class: the ground is under the trees) or The average of each cell’s points. Classes (LiDAR): ground (class 2) is ticked when the file has it; buildings, trees and noise are left out. When there is no ground class every class is ticked, and the dialog says so. Elevations in the file: meters, feet or US survey feet. When the file says, its unit is already chosen. When it does not, you must choose: the dialog shows the elevation range both ways so you can tell which makes sense for the site. Import as (grids only): Survey points (description DEM), or The surface itself (a grid of triangles). One import takes at most 500,000 points. When a choice would give more, the dialog says by how much and what spacing would fit, and Import stays off. Click Import. A progress bar shows the reading; Cancel stops it and nothing changes. Vertical units are a common trap. Many state plane DEMs have eastings and northings in feet but elevations in meters. Check the range the dialog shows against what you know of the site. What you get Survey points: numbered after your highest point number, with the description DEM (grids) or LIDAR, so they form one point group under Points and build the surface like any other points. Importing them is one step: Ctrl+Z takes them out again. The surface itself: a grid TIN, two triangles per cell, split on the south-west to north-east diagonal. A triangle with a corner that has no data is left out, so holes in the data stay holes. The surface equals the grid at every grid point and is a flat plane inside each triangle between them. It replaces the project's surface (the dialog asks first); Ctrl+Z brings the previous surface back. The left panel's heading says Surface (from an elevation grid). Coordinate systems If the project has a coordinate system and the file names another, every point is converted to the project's system (and to its ground or local coordinates when it has them). If the project has none and the file's system is in the project's units (feet or meters), the dialog offers to make it the project's coordinate system, in the same undo step as the points. If the file is in latitude and longitude, or in meters for a feet project, the dialog asks you to pick the project's coordinate system first. If the file does not say, its numbers are taken as the project's coordinates. You can pick the file's system instead. Export a surface as a GeoTIFF Click Export in the GIS group. In the GeoTIFF part choose the surface (existing ground or the graded surface) and the cell size. The default comes from the surface's average triangle edge. The dialog shows the size of the grid it will write. Click Download GeoTIFF. The file is one band of 32-bit floating point elevations in the project's units, over the surface's extent; cells outside the surface are \"no data\" (−3.4028235e38). Each cell's value is the surface's elevation at the cell's center. Coordinates are grid coordinates: a project in ground or local coordinates is converted back to the grid. The file names the project's coordinate system by its EPSG code; a system without an EPSG code is not written, and the dialog warns you to tell the GIS department which system it is. What was checked The GeoTIFF and ASCII grid readers were checked against GDAL (through rasterio), the library most GIS programs use to read these files: 32-bit float and 16-bit integer grids, tiled and stripped, DEFLATE and LZW, nodata, scale and offset, pixel-is-point, a UTM grid, a state plane grid in US survey feet and a latitude/longitude grid — every value equal. The LAS and LAZ readers were checked against laspy: LAS 1.2 and 1.4, point formats 0, 1, 3, 6, 7 and 8, with and without a coordinate system, and LAZ written by its LASzip-compatible backend — every point equal. Converted coordinates were checked against PROJ to 1 mm. Exported GeoTIFFs were read back by GDAL: coordinate system, cell positions, nodata, units, and values equal to the surface to 0.0001. Nothing here has been opened in other GIS or CAD programs. Not built yet Vertical datum changes (geoid models): elevations are used as the file has them. Waveform data, LiDAR returns other than by class, colors and intensities. Several bands, overviews, or files over 200 MB. Writing LAS or LAZ."},{"slug":"copy-paste-points","scope":"civilcraft","title":"Select, copy and paste survey points","summary":"Select survey points by click, Shift and a box, or all at once; copy, cut, paste, paste in place, duplicate and delete them with new point numbers that never clash; paste point rows to and from a spreadsheet.","section":"tasks","order":15,"keywords":["copy","paste","cut","duplicate","delete points","select points","select all","box select","window","clipboard","spreadsheet","csv","renumber","point numbers","paste in place","clone","stakeout"],"headings":[{"id":"select-points","text":"Select points","level":2},{"id":"copy-cut-and-delete","text":"Copy, cut and delete","level":2},{"id":"paste","text":"Paste","level":2},{"id":"point-numbers-never-clash","text":"Point numbers never clash","level":2},{"id":"duplicate","text":"Duplicate","level":2},{"id":"from-a-spreadsheet-or-a-text-file","text":"From a spreadsheet or a text file","level":2},{"id":"between-projects-and-tabs","text":"Between projects and tabs","level":2},{"id":"what-is-not-copied","text":"What is not copied","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ESurvey points can be copied and pasted within a project, between projects in two browser tabs, and to and from a spreadsheet. Every change is one undo step, and the surface rebuilds from the new points at once.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"select-points\"\u003ESelect points\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"V\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E):\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EClick\u003C/strong\u003E a point to select it (and only it). Its numbers show under \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003Eclick\u003C/strong\u003E adds a point to the selection, or takes it out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003Edrag\u003C/strong\u003E draws a box: the points inside it are added to the selection. Drag round the points instead and it becomes a lasso; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/lasso-selection\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/lasso-selection\"\u003ELasso selection of survey points\u003C/a\u003E. A drag without \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E pans, as always.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect all points\u003C/span\u003E) selects every point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect none\u003C/span\u003E) clears the selection.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelected points have a ring. With more than one selected, \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E shows how many, their numbers, their elevation range and their groups.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"copy-cut-and-delete\"\u003ECopy, cut and delete\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E copies the selected points. CivilCraft keeps the copy, and also puts it on the system clipboard as point rows, so you can paste it straight into a spreadsheet or a text file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command X\"\u003E⌘X\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+X\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E copies them and deletes them, in one step.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E deletes them. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E brings them back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe rows are point, northing, easting, elevation, description (P,N,E,Z,D), separated by commas, with three decimals. To get easting before northing, set \u003Cstrong\u003EPoint rows\u003C/strong\u003E in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/settings\"\u003ECivilCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E. A description a spreadsheet would treat as a formula (starting with =, +, - or @) gets an apostrophe in front, so it stays text.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"paste\"\u003EPaste\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E. The points follow the pointer as small crosses, with a dotted line back to where they came from.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick where they go. The middle of the copied points lands on the click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOr press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to put them at their own coordinates, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to cancel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste in place\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command V\"\u003E⇧⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E pastes at the points&#039; own coordinates straight away. The pasted points are selected afterwards, so you can copy, move or delete them again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClicking \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar reads the system clipboard. The first time, the browser may ask whether CivilCraft may see the clipboard. If you refuse, the toolbar button pastes CivilCraft&#039;s own copy only; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E still pastes rows from a spreadsheet without asking.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"point-numbers-never-clash\"\u003EPoint numbers never clash\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETwo points never share a number. When you paste:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA pasted point keeps its number when no point in the project has it. So after \u003Cstrong\u003ECut\u003C/strong\u003E, the points paste back with their own numbers, and points copied from another project keep theirs where they are free.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA pasted point whose number is taken gets a new whole number: one more than the highest number in use, counting up.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe status bar says what happened, for example &quot;1 kept its number; 9 got new numbers: 10–18 became 277–285&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/settings\"\u003ECivilCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E you can choose \u003Cstrong\u003EAlways new numbers\u003C/strong\u003E, and a number for new numbers to start at, for example 5000 for design points.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPoint groups that build the surface as a \u003Cstrong\u003Ebreakline\u003C/strong\u003E join their points in number order. Pasted copies of breakline points (for example FL shots) get new, higher numbers and join the end of that breakline, possibly with a long jump. Check the plan after pasting such points, or set their group to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPoints\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"duplicate\"\u003EDuplicate\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuplicate\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E makes a copy of the selected points that follows the pointer: click where it goes. The copies always get new numbers, and CivilCraft&#039;s clipboard is left as it was.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"from-a-spreadsheet-or-a-text-file\"\u003EFrom a spreadsheet or a text file\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect rows of points in a spreadsheet (point, northing, easting, elevation, description) and copy them. In CivilCraft press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and click where they go, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command V\"\u003E⇧⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to put them at their own coordinates, which is usually what you want for real survey data.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe rows are read exactly as a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/point-file-formats\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/point-file-formats\"\u003Epoint file\u003C/a\u003E: commas, tabs or spaces, a header row if there is one. Without a header, the column order comes from \u003Cstrong\u003EPoint rows\u003C/strong\u003E in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/settings\"\u003ECivilCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen both are there, CivilCraft decides by what is newer: rows on the system clipboard that differ from CivilCraft&#039;s last copy were copied later, so they are pasted. Text that holds no points is ignored, and CivilCraft&#039;s own copy is pasted instead.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"between-projects-and-tabs\"\u003EBetween projects and tabs\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe copy is kept in this browser, so a second tab of CivilCraft can paste it, even after the first tab closes. Points copied in a metre project and pasted into a feet project keep their numbers as they are: nothing is converted, and the status bar says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-not-copied\"\u003EWhat is not copied\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOnly survey points are copied for now. Alignments, pads, pipe networks, breaklines and boundaries cannot be copied or pasted yet. Pasting cannot move existing points: use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut\u003C/span\u003E and paste, or move one point at a time (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/edit-points\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/edit-points\"\u003EEdit a survey point\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Survey points can be copied and pasted within a project, between projects in two browser tabs, and to and from a spreadsheet. Every change is one undo step, and the surface rebuilds from the new points at once. Select points With Select (V): Click a point to select it (and only it). Its numbers show under Properties. Shift and click adds a point to the selection, or takes it out. Shift and drag draws a box: the points inside it are added to the selection. Drag round the points instead and it becomes a lasso; see Lasso selection of survey points. A drag without Shift pans, as always. Ctrl+A (or More › Select all points) selects every point. Esc (or More › Select none) clears the selection. Selected points have a ring. With more than one selected, Properties shows how many, their numbers, their elevation range and their groups. Copy, cut and delete Copy or Ctrl+C copies the selected points. CivilCraft keeps the copy, and also puts it on the system clipboard as point rows, so you can paste it straight into a spreadsheet or a text file. More › Cut or Ctrl+X copies them and deletes them, in one step. More › Delete or Delete deletes them. Ctrl+Z brings them back. The rows are point, northing, easting, elevation, description (P,N,E,Z,D), separated by commas, with three decimals. To get easting before northing, set Point rows in CivilCraft settings. A description a spreadsheet would treat as a formula (starting with =, +, - or @) gets an apostrophe in front, so it stays text. Paste Press Ctrl+V or click Paste. The points follow the pointer as small crosses, with a dotted line back to where they came from. Click where they go. The middle of the copied points lands on the click. Or press Enter to put them at their own coordinates, or Esc to cancel. More › Paste in place or Ctrl+Shift+V pastes at the points' own coordinates straight away. The pasted points are selected afterwards, so you can copy, move or delete them again. Clicking Paste in the toolbar reads the system clipboard. The first time, the browser may ask whether CivilCraft may see the clipboard. If you refuse, the toolbar button pastes CivilCraft's own copy only; Ctrl+V still pastes rows from a spreadsheet without asking. Point numbers never clash Two points never share a number. When you paste: A pasted point keeps its number when no point in the project has it. So after Cut, the points paste back with their own numbers, and points copied from another project keep theirs where they are free. A pasted point whose number is taken gets a new whole number: one more than the highest number in use, counting up. The status bar says what happened, for example \"1 kept its number; 9 got new numbers: 10–18 became 277–285\". In CivilCraft settings you can choose Always new numbers, and a number for new numbers to start at, for example 5000 for design points. Point groups that build the surface as a breakline join their points in number order. Pasted copies of breakline points (for example FL shots) get new, higher numbers and join the end of that breakline, possibly with a long jump. Check the plan after pasting such points, or set their group to Points. Duplicate More › Duplicate or Ctrl+D makes a copy of the selected points that follows the pointer: click where it goes. The copies always get new numbers, and CivilCraft's clipboard is left as it was. From a spreadsheet or a text file Select rows of points in a spreadsheet (point, northing, easting, elevation, description) and copy them. In CivilCraft press Ctrl+V and click where they go, or press Ctrl+Shift+V to put them at their own coordinates, which is usually what you want for real survey data. The rows are read exactly as a point file: commas, tabs or spaces, a header row if there is one. Without a header, the column order comes from Point rows in CivilCraft settings. When both are there, CivilCraft decides by what is newer: rows on the system clipboard that differ from CivilCraft's last copy were copied later, so they are pasted. Text that holds no points is ignored, and CivilCraft's own copy is pasted instead. Between projects and tabs The copy is kept in this browser, so a second tab of CivilCraft can paste it, even after the first tab closes. Points copied in a metre project and pasted into a feet project keep their numbers as they are: nothing is converted, and the status bar says so. What is not copied Only survey points are copied for now. Alignments, pads, pipe networks, breaklines and boundaries cannot be copied or pasted yet. Pasting cannot move existing points: use Cut and paste, or move one point at a time (Edit a survey point)."},{"slug":"description-keys","scope":"civilcraft","title":"Description keys and line codes","summary":"Turn field codes into point groups with descriptions, and join coded points into lines (begin, end, close) that become breaklines.","section":"tasks","order":16,"keywords":["description keys","field codes","line codes","figures","linework","begin","end","close","point groups","codes","survey codes","data collector","breaklines","curb","edge of pavement"],"headings":[{"id":"line-codes","text":"Line codes","level":2},{"id":"description-keys","text":"Description keys","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ESurvey crews code every shot in the field: \u003Ccode\u003EEP\u003C/code\u003E for edge of pavement, \u003Ccode\u003ETC\u003C/code\u003E for top of curb, \u003Ccode\u003EBLDG\u003C/code\u003E for a building corner. The code is the first word of the point&#039;s description. CivilCraft groups points by it, and turns line codes after it into lines.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"line-codes\"\u003ELine codes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA line code is the word right after the field code:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDescription\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeaning\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EEP B\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBegin a new edge-of-pavement line at this point\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EEP\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EContinue the current EP line\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EEP E\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEnd the current EP line at this point\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EBLDG C\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClose the building: join this point back to the line&#039;s first point\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPoints with the same code are joined in point-number order. Different codes are different lines even when they share a group, so \u003Ccode\u003EEP1\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EEP2\u003C/code\u003E give two edges at once. A code that never uses a line code is joined through all its points, as in earlier versions.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELines in a group used as \u003Cstrong\u003EBreakline\u003C/strong\u003E become breaklines, one per line, so a curb with a gap is not stretched across the gap. Lines in other groups are drawn on the plan (layer V-NODE-LINE) and go out in DXF, DWG and PDF.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe words for begin, end and close are \u003Ccode\u003EB\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EBEG\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESTART\u003C/code\u003E; \u003Ccode\u003EE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EEND\u003C/code\u003E; and \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ECLO\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ECLOSE\u003C/code\u003E. Change them, or turn line codes off, in the dialog below.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"description-keys\"\u003EDescription keys\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA description key turns a code into a point group, a use and a long description.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cstrong\u003EPoints\u003C/strong\u003E, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDescription keys and line codes\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a key\u003C/span\u003E and fill in the row:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECode\u003C/strong\u003E: the field code to match. \u003Ccode\u003E*\u003C/code\u003E stands for any characters and \u003Ccode\u003E?\u003C/code\u003E for one, so \u003Ccode\u003EEP*\u003C/code\u003E catches EP, EP1 and EP12.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EGroup\u003C/strong\u003E: the point group these points join (empty = the code itself). \u003Ccode\u003EEP*\u003C/code\u003E with group \u003Ccode\u003EEP\u003C/code\u003E puts EP1 and EP2 in one group while they stay separate lines.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUse\u003C/strong\u003E: Points, Breakline or Left out, for when the group first appears (or leave it to the \u003Cstrong\u003EPoints\u003C/strong\u003E table).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDescription\u003C/strong\u003E: the long name, shown in a point&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe \u003Cstrong\u003EPoints\u003C/strong\u003E column counts the points each key catches as you type. The first key that matches a code wins.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApply\u003C/span\u003E. The groups, the surface and the plan follow in one step: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA group keeps the use you gave it in the \u003Cstrong\u003EPoints\u003C/strong\u003E table; a key&#039;s use only sets it for a group that is new. The keys and line codes are saved in the project file.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Survey crews code every shot in the field: EP for edge of pavement, TC for top of curb, BLDG for a building corner. The code is the first word of the point's description. CivilCraft groups points by it, and turns line codes after it into lines. Line codes A line code is the word right after the field code: Description Meaning EP B Begin a new edge-of-pavement line at this point EP Continue the current EP line EP E End the current EP line at this point BLDG C Close the building: join this point back to the line's first point Points with the same code are joined in point-number order. Different codes are different lines even when they share a group, so EP1 and EP2 give two edges at once. A code that never uses a line code is joined through all its points, as in earlier versions. Lines in a group used as Breakline become breaklines, one per line, so a curb with a gap is not stretched across the gap. Lines in other groups are drawn on the plan (layer V-NODE-LINE) and go out in DXF, DWG and PDF. The words for begin, end and close are B, BEG, START; E, END; and C, CLO, CLOSE. Change them, or turn line codes off, in the dialog below. Description keys A description key turns a code into a point group, a use and a long description. Under Points, click Description keys and line codes. Click Add a key and fill in the row: Code: the field code to match. * stands for any characters and ? for one, so EP* catches EP, EP1 and EP12. Group: the point group these points join (empty = the code itself). EP* with group EP puts EP1 and EP2 in one group while they stay separate lines. Use: Points, Breakline or Left out, for when the group first appears (or leave it to the Points table). Description: the long name, shown in a point's Properties. The Points column counts the points each key catches as you type. The first key that matches a code wins. Click Apply. The groups, the surface and the plan follow in one step: Ctrl+Z undoes it. A group keeps the use you gave it in the Points table; a key's use only sets it for a group that is new. The keys and line codes are saved in the project file."},{"slug":"lasso-selection","scope":"civilcraft","title":"Lasso selection of survey points","summary":"Shift and drag a freehand outline round survey points to select them, as a window, a crossing or a fence; switch with Space while you drag. A straight drag still draws a box.","section":"tasks","order":16,"keywords":["lasso","freehand","select points","selection","window","crossing","fence","space","polygon","box select","shift drag"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"draw-a-lasso","text":"Draw a lasso","level":2},{"id":"window-crossing-and-fence","text":"Window, crossing and fence","level":2},{"id":"see-also","text":"See also","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to select survey points by drawing round them, when a box would take points you do not want.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-a-lasso\"\u003EDraw a lasso\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E (or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"V\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, press on the plan and drag \u003Cstrong\u003Eround\u003C/strong\u003E the points you want, moving \u003Cstrong\u003Eright\u003C/strong\u003E first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELet go.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe points inside the outline are added to the selection. A drag that stays close to a straight line draws a box, as before; as soon as it bends away from that line (more than about 12 pixels) it becomes a lasso.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"window-crossing-and-fence\"\u003EWindow, crossing and fence\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYou drag\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ELasso\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESelects\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERight first\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWindow (solid outline)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe points inside it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELeft first\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECrossing (dashed, green)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe points inside it, and the points the outline touches\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while dragging\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWindow, then crossing, then fence\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFence (a dashed orange path, not closed): the points the path passes over\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe status line names the lasso while you drag. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while you drag cancels it and selects nothing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA fence is the quick way to pick the shots along a curb or a ditch line: drag along them and let go.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"see-also\"\u003ESee also\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/copy-paste-points\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/copy-paste-points\"\u003ESelect, copy and paste survey points\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/keyboard\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/keyboard\"\u003EKeys and mouse\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to select survey points by drawing round them, when a box would take points you do not want. Draw a lasso Click Select (or press V). Hold Shift, press on the plan and drag round the points you want, moving right first. Let go. The points inside the outline are added to the selection. A drag that stays close to a straight line draws a box, as before; as soon as it bends away from that line (more than about 12 pixels) it becomes a lasso. Window, crossing and fence You drag Lasso Selects Right first Window (solid outline) The points inside it Left first Crossing (dashed, green) The points inside it, and the points the outline touches Press Space while dragging Window, then crossing, then fence Fence (a dashed orange path, not closed): the points the path passes over The status line names the lasso while you drag. Esc while you drag cancels it and selects nothing. A fence is the quick way to pick the shots along a curb or a ditch line: drag along them and let go. See also Select, copy and paste survey points Keys and mouse"},{"slug":"build-surface","scope":"civilcraft","title":"Build and refine the surface","summary":"How CivilCraft triangulates the existing ground, and how to shape it with breakline groups, drawn breaklines, an outer boundary and the longest outside edge.","section":"tasks","order":20,"keywords":["tin","surface","triangulation","breakline","boundary","existing ground","eg","create surface","delaunay","triangles","max edge","outer boundary"],"headings":[{"id":"what-goes-into-the-surface","text":"What goes into the surface","level":2},{"id":"make-coded-lines-into-breaklines","text":"Make coded lines into breaklines","level":2},{"id":"draw-a-breakline-through-points","text":"Draw a breakline through points","level":2},{"id":"draw-the-outer-boundary","text":"Draw the outer boundary","level":2},{"id":"trim-long-outside-triangles","text":"Trim long outside triangles","level":2},{"id":"read-the-surface-report","text":"Read the surface report","level":2},{"id":"a-surface-read-from-landxml","text":"A surface read from LandXML","level":2},{"id":"related","text":"Related","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ECivilCraft has one existing ground surface per project. It is a TIN (triangulated irregular network) built from your points, and it rebuilds automatically every time an input changes. There is no &quot;rebuild&quot; command and no out-of-date surface.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-goes-into-the-surface\"\u003EWhat goes into the surface\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEvery point with an elevation in a group set to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPoints\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEvery group set to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBreakline\u003C/span\u003E, joined in point-number order.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEvery breakline you draw with the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBreakline\u003C/span\u003E tool.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe outer boundary, if you draw one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe \u003Cstrong\u003ELongest outside edge\u003C/strong\u003E setting.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETriangles never cross a breakline: the surface is forced to follow it, then made as even as possible around it (Delaunay everywhere else).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-coded-lines-into-breaklines\"\u003EMake coded lines into breaklines\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the left panel under \u003Cstrong\u003EPoints\u003C/strong\u003E, find the group (for example FL).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet its \u003Cstrong\u003ESurface\u003C/strong\u003E choice to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBreakline\u003C/span\u003E. The surface rebuilds, the line is drawn in orange (with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBreaklines\u003C/span\u003E on under \u003Cstrong\u003EDisplay\u003C/strong\u003E), and \u003Cstrong\u003EBreaklines\u003C/strong\u003E in the surface facts goes up by one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECheck the line on the plan. It must run point to point in the order the line was shot. If it zig-zags, the group holds more than one line, or the points are not numbered in order: set the group back to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPoints\u003C/span\u003E and draw the lines by hand (next section).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-a-breakline-through-points\"\u003EDraw a breakline through points\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"B\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBreakline\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cstrong\u003ESurface\u003C/strong\u003E group. The hint says &quot;Breakline: click survey points in order (it takes their elevations). Enter finishes.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the pointer near a point. A small orange square marks the point it will take.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the points of the line one after another. Each click names the point and its elevation in the status bar.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to finish. The status bar says &quot;Breakline 1 added; the surface was rebuilt.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClicks must land on a survey point that has an elevation; a click elsewhere is refused with &quot;Click a survey point with an elevation: a breakline takes the points’ elevations.&quot; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E drops the last point; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E cancels the whole line.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFinish with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Double-clicking also finishes, but in this build it drops the point you double-clicked. Click the last point once, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo remove every drawn breakline at once, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove drawn breaklines\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cstrong\u003ESurface\u003C/strong\u003E section. To remove only the last one, use \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Drawn breaklines cannot be selected or edited one by one yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-the-outer-boundary\"\u003EDraw the outer boundary\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA boundary trims the surface to the area you trust, for example the survey limits, so that long triangles across unsurveyed corners go away.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"O\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBoundary\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the corners in order around the area. A dashed line shows the shape.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The status bar says &quot;Boundary set; triangles outside it are gone.&quot; \u003Cstrong\u003EBoundary\u003C/strong\u003E in the surface facts says &quot;Yes&quot; and the number of corners.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThings to know:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThere is one boundary. Drawing another replaces it. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove the boundary\u003C/span\u003E takes it away.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIt can only take away: a boundary that reaches past the points is cut back to their outline, with the note &quot;The boundary reaches past the points; it was cut back to their outline.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIts corners take their elevations from the surface, so the edge follows the ground exactly.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHide (inner) boundaries are not available yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"trim-long-outside-triangles\"\u003ETrim long outside triangles\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWithout a boundary, the surface fills the whole outline of the points, including long thin triangles across concave edges.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cstrong\u003ESurface\u003C/strong\u003E, type a length in \u003Cstrong\u003ELongest outside edge\u003C/strong\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Start with about two or three times your typical shot spacing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOutside triangles whose outer edge is longer than that are removed, working inward until every outer edge is short enough. An edge that is a breakline is never peeled.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType 0 to keep every triangle again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"read-the-surface-report\"\u003ERead the surface report\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder the surface facts, a box lists what the builder did. With an ochre bar on its left it is information; with a red bar it holds an error and there is no surface.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMessage\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeaning\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;3 points were at the same place as another and joined it.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPoints closer than 0.0001 in plan became one vertex (the first one kept).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;2 places have two different elevations …&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPoints on top of each other, or breaklines crossing, disagree in height. The breakline&#039;s elevation wins, then the first one.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;1 breakline piece could not be forced into the surface.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA very short or nearly overlapping breakline piece did not fit; check that spot.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;A surface needs at least three points with elevations.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EToo few points: check the column order and the groups.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;The points are all on one line: no triangles.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe points have no width, for example a single traverse line.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;The boundary does not overlap the points.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe boundary is somewhere else, often a units or coordinate mix-up.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;No triangles are left inside the boundary.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe boundary is too small or misplaced.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-surface-read-from-landxml\"\u003EA surface read from LandXML\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen you open LandXML, its first TIN surface is used as it is: the \u003Cstrong\u003ESurface\u003C/strong\u003E heading says &quot;Surface (from LandXML)&quot; and \u003Cstrong\u003ELongest outside edge\u003C/strong\u003E is grayed out. While that surface is in use, breakline groups, drawn breaklines and a new boundary do not change it. To build the surface from the file&#039;s points instead, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERebuild the surface from the points\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E brings the LandXML surface back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"related\"\u003ERelated\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/contours\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/contours\"\u003ERead contours, elevations and slopes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/import-points\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/import-points\"\u003EImport survey points\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"CivilCraft has one existing ground surface per project. It is a TIN (triangulated irregular network) built from your points, and it rebuilds automatically every time an input changes. There is no \"rebuild\" command and no out-of-date surface. What goes into the surface Every point with an elevation in a group set to Points. Every group set to Breakline, joined in point-number order. Every breakline you draw with the Breakline tool. The outer boundary, if you draw one. The Longest outside edge setting. Triangles never cross a breakline: the surface is forced to follow it, then made as even as possible around it (Delaunay everywhere else). Make coded lines into breaklines In the left panel under Points, find the group (for example FL). Set its Surface choice to Breakline. The surface rebuilds, the line is drawn in orange (with Breaklines on under Display), and Breaklines in the surface facts goes up by one. Check the line on the plan. It must run point to point in the order the line was shot. If it zig-zags, the group holds more than one line, or the points are not numbered in order: set the group back to Points and draw the lines by hand (next section). Draw a breakline through points Press B, or click Breakline in the Surface group. The hint says \"Breakline: click survey points in order (it takes their elevations). Enter finishes.\" Move the pointer near a point. A small orange square marks the point it will take. Click the points of the line one after another. Each click names the point and its elevation in the status bar. Press Enter to finish. The status bar says \"Breakline 1 added; the surface was rebuilt.\" Clicks must land on a survey point that has an elevation; a click elsewhere is refused with \"Click a survey point with an elevation: a breakline takes the points’ elevations.\" Backspace drops the last point; Esc cancels the whole line. Finish with Enter. Double-clicking also finishes, but in this build it drops the point you double-clicked. Click the last point once, then press Enter. To remove every drawn breakline at once, click Remove drawn breaklines in the Surface section. To remove only the last one, use Ctrl+Z. Drawn breaklines cannot be selected or edited one by one yet. Draw the outer boundary A boundary trims the surface to the area you trust, for example the survey limits, so that long triangles across unsurveyed corners go away. Press O, or click Boundary. Click the corners in order around the area. A dashed line shows the shape. Press Enter. The status bar says \"Boundary set; triangles outside it are gone.\" Boundary in the surface facts says \"Yes\" and the number of corners. Things to know: There is one boundary. Drawing another replaces it. Remove the boundary takes it away. It can only take away: a boundary that reaches past the points is cut back to their outline, with the note \"The boundary reaches past the points; it was cut back to their outline.\" Its corners take their elevations from the surface, so the edge follows the ground exactly. Hide (inner) boundaries are not available yet. Trim long outside triangles Without a boundary, the surface fills the whole outline of the points, including long thin triangles across concave edges. In Surface, type a length in Longest outside edge and press Enter. Start with about two or three times your typical shot spacing. Outside triangles whose outer edge is longer than that are removed, working inward until every outer edge is short enough. An edge that is a breakline is never peeled. Type 0 to keep every triangle again. Read the surface report Under the surface facts, a box lists what the builder did. With an ochre bar on its left it is information; with a red bar it holds an error and there is no surface. Message Meaning \"3 points were at the same place as another and joined it.\" Points closer than 0.0001 in plan became one vertex (the first one kept). \"2 places have two different elevations …\" Points on top of each other, or breaklines crossing, disagree in height. The breakline's elevation wins, then the first one. \"1 breakline piece could not be forced into the surface.\" A very short or nearly overlapping breakline piece did not fit; check that spot. \"A surface needs at least three points with elevations.\" Too few points: check the column order and the groups. \"The points are all on one line: no triangles.\" The points have no width, for example a single traverse line. \"The boundary does not overlap the points.\" The boundary is somewhere else, often a units or coordinate mix-up. \"No triangles are left inside the boundary.\" The boundary is too small or misplaced. A surface read from LandXML When you open LandXML, its first TIN surface is used as it is: the Surface heading says \"Surface (from LandXML)\" and Longest outside edge is grayed out. While that surface is in use, breakline groups, drawn breaklines and a new boundary do not change it. To build the surface from the file's points instead, click Rebuild the surface from the points. Ctrl+Z brings the LandXML surface back. Related Read contours, elevations and slopes. Import survey points."},{"slug":"open-dwg-dxf","scope":"civilcraft","title":"Open a DWG or DXF as linework","summary":"Open a drawing's linework on the plan, layer by layer, use its contours, 3D polylines and closed outlines for the surface, and turn its point blocks into survey points.","section":"tasks","order":22,"keywords":["dwg","dxf","cad","drawing","linework","contours","breaklines","boundary","polyline","3d polyline","layers","base map","topo","survey drawing","blocks","block references","survey points from blocks","attributes","point blocks","insertion point"],"headings":[{"id":"open-the-drawing","text":"Open the drawing","level":2},{"id":"what-each-kind-of-layer-can-give","text":"What each kind of layer can give","level":2},{"id":"survey-points-from-blocks","text":"Survey points from blocks","level":2},{"id":"how-the-drawing-is-read","text":"How the drawing is read","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA topographic survey often arrives as a drawing: contours as polylines at their elevation, breaklines as 3D polylines, the site outline as a closed polyline. CivilCraft opens the drawing&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003Emodel space\u003C/strong\u003E as layers on the plan, and can turn those lines straight into the surface.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-the-drawing\"\u003EOpen the drawing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinework\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cstrong\u003ESurface\u003C/strong\u003E group (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E, or drop the file on the plan) and choose a \u003Ccode\u003E.dwg\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E.dxf\u003C/code\u003E file. The drawing is read on this computer; nothing is uploaded. DWG from 2000 to 2018 and ASCII DXF are read.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe \u003Cstrong\u003ELinework\u003C/strong\u003E dialog lists the drawing&#039;s layers that hold lines, closed shapes or points, and whether they have \u003Cstrong\u003Eelevations\u003C/strong\u003E. Layers that are off in the drawing start unticked. Text, hatches, dimensions and other objects that are not linework are counted and left out (the dialog says which).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECheck \u003Cstrong\u003EDrawing units\u003C/strong\u003E. When the drawing says its units, CivilCraft converts them into the project&#039;s (millimeters into meters, meters into feet, inches into feet). A drawing in feet is read as US survey feet without scaling: the two differ by 2 parts per million, and scaling state plane coordinates by that would move them by feet. When the drawing does not say its units, its numbers are read in the project&#039;s units; pick the right units in the list if they are not.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cstrong\u003EUse for the surface\u003C/strong\u003E, choose what each layer gives the surface (or leave \u003Cstrong\u003EOnly show it\u003C/strong\u003E). Layers named like contours (TOPO, CONT, CNTR) that have elevations start as breaklines.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd\u003C/span\u003E. The layers appear under \u003Cstrong\u003EGIS layers\u003C/strong\u003E in the left panel; each use is put into the surface. Every part is a step of its own: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes them back one at a time.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-each-kind-of-layer-can-give\"\u003EWhat each kind of layer can give\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EThe layer holds\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EUse for the surface\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EContours (polylines at an elevation)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBreaklines at the lines&#039; own elevation, or at the ELEV field\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E3D polylines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBreaklines at their own elevations\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA closed outline\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe surface boundary (the largest closed shape, or the one you selected)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPoints with an elevation\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESurvey points, described with the layer name\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELines at elevation 0 (plan linework)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENothing: it stays on the plan as a base drawing\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA drawing CivilCraft wrote holds its feature lines as 3D polylines that remember their arcs. The dialog then shows \u003Cstrong\u003EFeature lines\u003C/strong\u003E: leave it ticked to bring them back as feature lines with their arcs and uses (one undo step), or untick it to read them as linework like any other layer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELater, use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUse for the surface\u003C/span\u003E in the layer&#039;s menu under \u003Cstrong\u003EGIS layers\u003C/strong\u003E to do the same for a layer you only showed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"survey-points-from-blocks\"\u003ESurvey points from blocks\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESurvey drawings often carry their points as blocks: a small symbol at each shot, with attributes for the point number, the description and the elevation. When the drawing has block references, the dialog has a \u003Cstrong\u003ESurvey points from blocks\u003C/strong\u003E section:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETick the blocks to take (the table says how many references each has, on which layers, and which attributes), or tick layers to take every block reference on them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose where each point&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003Enumber\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003Edescription\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003Eelevation\u003C/strong\u003E come from: an attribute, the block&#039;s or the layer&#039;s name, the next free numbers, or the insertion point&#039;s Z. CivilCraft starts on the likely attributes (PT, DESC, ELEV and similar), and takes the elevation from an attribute only when the blocks sit at zero.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUntick the linework layers if you only want the points, then click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach reference becomes one survey point at its insertion point. Only references placed in model space count; blocks nested inside other blocks do not. Numbers already in the project, or used twice in the drawing, get new numbers after the highest, and an elevation attribute that is not a number leaves that point without an elevation; the status line counts both. The drawing is never changed, and each point remembers its block: its Properties show \u003Cstrong\u003EFrom block\u003C/strong\u003E with the block, layer and handle. Adding the points is one step, so \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes them all away.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-the-drawing-is-read\"\u003EHow the drawing is read\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EArcs, circles, ellipses, bulged polyline segments and splines become short straight pieces (every 5° at most).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBlock references are drawn where they are placed, nested blocks too; what a block holds on layer 0 takes the reference&#039;s layer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EObjects drawn in another plane (a different extrusion direction) are placed in world coordinates.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach object gets three fields: LAYER, TYPE (the object type) and ELEV (its elevation when all its points share one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECoordinates are taken as they are: the drawing must use the same coordinates as the project (for example the same state plane zone). Linked drawings (xrefs) are not opened: open them on their own.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA contour drawing gives the best surface with a few spot elevations on hilltops and in hollows, where contours alone leave flat triangles. Add them as survey points, or from a points layer in the drawing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"A topographic survey often arrives as a drawing: contours as polylines at their elevation, breaklines as 3D polylines, the site outline as a closed polyline. CivilCraft opens the drawing's model space as layers on the plan, and can turn those lines straight into the surface. Open the drawing Click Linework in the Surface group (or Open, or drop the file on the plan) and choose a .dwg or .dxf file. The drawing is read on this computer; nothing is uploaded. DWG from 2000 to 2018 and ASCII DXF are read. The Linework dialog lists the drawing's layers that hold lines, closed shapes or points, and whether they have elevations. Layers that are off in the drawing start unticked. Text, hatches, dimensions and other objects that are not linework are counted and left out (the dialog says which). Check Drawing units. When the drawing says its units, CivilCraft converts them into the project's (millimeters into meters, meters into feet, inches into feet). A drawing in feet is read as US survey feet without scaling: the two differ by 2 parts per million, and scaling state plane coordinates by that would move them by feet. When the drawing does not say its units, its numbers are read in the project's units; pick the right units in the list if they are not. In Use for the surface, choose what each layer gives the surface (or leave Only show it). Layers named like contours (TOPO, CONT, CNTR) that have elevations start as breaklines. Click Add. The layers appear under GIS layers in the left panel; each use is put into the surface. Every part is a step of its own: Ctrl+Z takes them back one at a time. What each kind of layer can give The layer holds Use for the surface Contours (polylines at an elevation) Breaklines at the lines' own elevation, or at the ELEV field 3D polylines Breaklines at their own elevations A closed outline The surface boundary (the largest closed shape, or the one you selected) Points with an elevation Survey points, described with the layer name Lines at elevation 0 (plan linework) Nothing: it stays on the plan as a base drawing A drawing CivilCraft wrote holds its feature lines as 3D polylines that remember their arcs. The dialog then shows Feature lines: leave it ticked to bring them back as feature lines with their arcs and uses (one undo step), or untick it to read them as linework like any other layer. Later, use Use for the surface in the layer's menu under GIS layers to do the same for a layer you only showed. Survey points from blocks Survey drawings often carry their points as blocks: a small symbol at each shot, with attributes for the point number, the description and the elevation. When the drawing has block references, the dialog has a Survey points from blocks section: Tick the blocks to take (the table says how many references each has, on which layers, and which attributes), or tick layers to take every block reference on them. Choose where each point's number, description and elevation come from: an attribute, the block's or the layer's name, the next free numbers, or the insertion point's Z. CivilCraft starts on the likely attributes (PT, DESC, ELEV and similar), and takes the elevation from an attribute only when the blocks sit at zero. Untick the linework layers if you only want the points, then click Add. Each reference becomes one survey point at its insertion point. Only references placed in model space count; blocks nested inside other blocks do not. Numbers already in the project, or used twice in the drawing, get new numbers after the highest, and an elevation attribute that is not a number leaves that point without an elevation; the status line counts both. The drawing is never changed, and each point remembers its block: its Properties show From block with the block, layer and handle. Adding the points is one step, so Ctrl+Z takes them all away. How the drawing is read Arcs, circles, ellipses, bulged polyline segments and splines become short straight pieces (every 5° at most). Block references are drawn where they are placed, nested blocks too; what a block holds on layer 0 takes the reference's layer. Objects drawn in another plane (a different extrusion direction) are placed in world coordinates. Each object gets three fields: LAYER, TYPE (the object type) and ELEV (its elevation when all its points share one). Coordinates are taken as they are: the drawing must use the same coordinates as the project (for example the same state plane zone). Linked drawings (xrefs) are not opened: open them on their own. A contour drawing gives the best surface with a few spot elevations on hilltops and in hollows, where contours alone leave flat triangles. Add them as survey points, or from a points layer in the drawing."},{"slug":"contours","scope":"civilcraft","title":"Read contours, elevations and slopes","summary":"Set the contour interval and major contours, show elevation bands, slope arrows and triangles, and read ground elevations under the pointer.","section":"tasks","order":30,"keywords":["contours","contour interval","major contours","labels","elevation bands","slope arrows","triangles","spot elevation","surface analysis","elevation analysis","slope analysis"],"headings":[{"id":"set-the-contour-interval","text":"Set the contour interval","level":2},{"id":"read-an-elevation-anywhere","text":"Read an elevation anywhere","level":2},{"id":"show-elevation-bands","text":"Show elevation bands","level":2},{"id":"show-slope-arrows","text":"Show slope arrows","level":2},{"id":"show-triangles-points-and-edges","text":"Show triangles, points and edges","level":2},{"id":"existing-or-proposed","text":"Existing or proposed","level":2},{"id":"what-gets-exported","text":"What gets exported","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe surface is shown with contours as soon as it exists. Everything in this topic is set under \u003Cstrong\u003EDisplay\u003C/strong\u003E in the left panel. Display settings change only what you see (and what you export); they are not undo steps.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"set-the-contour-interval\"\u003ESet the contour interval\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cstrong\u003EDisplay\u003C/strong\u003E, choose a value in \u003Cstrong\u003EContours every\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn feet: 0.25, 0.5, 1, 2, 5, 10 or 20 ft.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn metres: 0.1, 0.25, 0.5, 1, 2, 5 or 10 m.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cstrong\u003EMajor every\u003C/strong\u003E: the 2nd, 4th, 5th or 10th contour. Major contours are drawn heavier and labeled with their elevation. They fall on whole multiples of the major spacing (with 1 ft contours and every 5th major: 245, 250, 255 …).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETurn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EContour labels\u003C/span\u003E off if the labels crowd a small plot.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA new project starts at 1 unit with every 5th contour major. Contours bend at breaklines; if they cut straight across a flowline or ridge, that line is not a breakline yet (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/build-surface\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/build-surface\"\u003EBuild and refine the surface\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EContours are drawn only when there are no more than 2,000 levels between the lowest and highest point. On a site with a lot of relief and a very small interval, the plan shows no contours at all: choose a larger interval.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"read-an-elevation-anywhere\"\u003ERead an elevation anywhere\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMove the pointer over the plan. The status bar shows:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EE\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EN\u003C/strong\u003E under the pointer\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EGround\u003C/strong\u003E and the existing ground elevation there, interpolated on the triangle under the pointer\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EGraded\u003C/strong\u003E and the proposed elevation, where a pad or corridor has changed the ground\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;Off the surface&quot; outside the surface\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo read a survey point exactly, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E and click the point: the right panel shows its number, easting, northing, elevation, description and group.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"show-elevation-bands\"\u003EShow elevation bands\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElevation bands\u003C/span\u003E. The surface is filled with color from low (blue-green) to high (ochre-brown). A legend under the checkboxes lists each band&#039;s elevation range.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose how many bands in \u003Cstrong\u003EBands\u003C/strong\u003E: 5, 8, 10 or 12. The bands split the range from the lowest to the highest elevation into equal steps.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBands are for looking only. They are not exported to DXF or plotted to PDF.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"show-slope-arrows\"\u003EShow slope arrows\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESlope arrows\u003C/span\u003E. A short arrow on each triangle large enough to hold one points downhill. Flat triangles get no arrow. Arrows are a quick check for drainage direction; the slope value itself is not labeled yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"show-triangles-points-and-edges\"\u003EShow triangles, points and edges\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETriangles\u003C/span\u003E draws the TIN&#039;s edges. Turn it on to check how the surface connects the points, especially along breaklines and at the outside.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPoints\u003C/span\u003E draws each survey point; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPoint labels\u003C/span\u003E adds its number, elevation and description (or less: see \u003Cstrong\u003EPoint labels\u003C/strong\u003E in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/settings\"\u003ECivilCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBreaklines\u003C/span\u003E draws breaklines (and the boundary) in orange.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESurface edge\u003C/span\u003E draws the outline of the surface.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"existing-or-proposed\"\u003EExisting or proposed\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOnce a pad or corridor grades without problems, \u003Cstrong\u003ESurface\u003C/strong\u003E under \u003Cstrong\u003EDisplay\u003C/strong\u003E offers \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProposed (graded)\u003C/span\u003E. The contours, bands and arrows then show the graded surface, so you can see the new ground and the daylight around it. Switch back to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExisting ground\u003C/span\u003E to see the survey.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-gets-exported\"\u003EWhat gets exported\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDXF and PDF output follow these settings: what you have turned off is not written, and the contours are those of the surface shown. Elevation bands are never exported.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The surface is shown with contours as soon as it exists. Everything in this topic is set under Display in the left panel. Display settings change only what you see (and what you export); they are not undo steps. Set the contour interval Under Display, choose a value in Contours every. In feet: 0.25, 0.5, 1, 2, 5, 10 or 20 ft. In metres: 0.1, 0.25, 0.5, 1, 2, 5 or 10 m. Choose Major every: the 2nd, 4th, 5th or 10th contour. Major contours are drawn heavier and labeled with their elevation. They fall on whole multiples of the major spacing (with 1 ft contours and every 5th major: 245, 250, 255 …). Turn Contour labels off if the labels crowd a small plot. A new project starts at 1 unit with every 5th contour major. Contours bend at breaklines; if they cut straight across a flowline or ridge, that line is not a breakline yet (see Build and refine the surface). Contours are drawn only when there are no more than 2,000 levels between the lowest and highest point. On a site with a lot of relief and a very small interval, the plan shows no contours at all: choose a larger interval. Read an elevation anywhere Move the pointer over the plan. The status bar shows: E and N under the pointer Ground and the existing ground elevation there, interpolated on the triangle under the pointer Graded and the proposed elevation, where a pad or corridor has changed the ground \"Off the surface\" outside the surface To read a survey point exactly, choose Select and click the point: the right panel shows its number, easting, northing, elevation, description and group. Show elevation bands Tick Elevation bands. The surface is filled with color from low (blue-green) to high (ochre-brown). A legend under the checkboxes lists each band's elevation range. Choose how many bands in Bands: 5, 8, 10 or 12. The bands split the range from the lowest to the highest elevation into equal steps. Bands are for looking only. They are not exported to DXF or plotted to PDF. Show slope arrows Tick Slope arrows. A short arrow on each triangle large enough to hold one points downhill. Flat triangles get no arrow. Arrows are a quick check for drainage direction; the slope value itself is not labeled yet. Show triangles, points and edges Triangles draws the TIN's edges. Turn it on to check how the surface connects the points, especially along breaklines and at the outside. Points draws each survey point; Point labels adds its number, elevation and description (or less: see Point labels in CivilCraft settings). Breaklines draws breaklines (and the boundary) in orange. Surface edge draws the outline of the surface. Existing or proposed Once a pad or corridor grades without problems, Surface under Display offers Proposed (graded). The contours, bands and arrows then show the graded surface, so you can see the new ground and the daylight around it. Switch back to Existing ground to see the survey. What gets exported DXF and PDF output follow these settings: what you have turned off is not written, and the contours are those of the surface shown. Elevation bands are never exported."},{"slug":"alignment","scope":"civilcraft","title":"Lay out a horizontal alignment","summary":"Draw an alignment by its PIs, set curve radii and the start station, move PIs, and read the curve data and station and offset.","section":"tasks","order":40,"keywords":["alignment","no solution","curve does not fit","largest radius","smallest radius","minimum radius","design speed","overlap","centerline","pi","horizontal curve","radius","stationing","tangent","create alignment","alignment layout","pc","pt","station offset"],"headings":[{"id":"draw-the-alignment","text":"Draw the alignment","level":2},{"id":"set-radii-and-coordinates","text":"Set radii and coordinates","level":2},{"id":"when-a-curve-does-not-fit","text":"When a curve does not fit","level":2},{"id":"a-design-minimum-radius","text":"A design minimum radius","level":3},{"id":"move-pis-on-the-plan","text":"Move PIs on the plan","level":2},{"id":"read-station-and-offset","text":"Read station and offset","level":2},{"id":"what-is-drawn","text":"What is drawn","level":2},{"id":"delete-an-alignment","text":"Delete an alignment","level":2},{"id":"next","text":"Next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EAn alignment in CivilCraft is a chain of tangents through points of intersection (PIs), with a circular curve at any interior PI that has a radius. It is stationed from a start station you choose. A project can hold many alignments.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-the-alignment\"\u003EDraw the alignment\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"A\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAlignment\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cstrong\u003EDesign\u003C/strong\u003E group. The hint says &quot;Alignment: click the PIs in order. Enter or double-click finishes, Backspace drops the last, Esc cancels.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the first PI (the start of the alignment).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick each following PI in order. A red line follows the pointer and shows the length and bearing of the tangent you are drawing, for example &quot;212.40 ft  N 63°12&#039;05&quot; E&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the last PI (the end of the alignment).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The alignment is drawn with stationing and is selected. The status bar says, for example, &quot;Alignment 1: 386.36 ft, curves of 100 ft radius where they fit. Edit radii in Properties; Profile (R) to design its grade.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile drawing: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E removes the last PI, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E cancels, and dragging on empty space pans without adding a PI.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFinish with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Double-clicking also finishes, but in this build it drops the PI you double-clicked. Click the last PI once, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery interior PI gets a curve of 100 ft (30 m in a metre project), or the largest whole radius that fits in half of each tangent if 100 ft does not fit. Very sharp or straight-through PIs get no curve.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"set-radii-and-coordinates\"\u003ESet radii and coordinates\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith the alignment selected, the right panel shows \u003Cstrong\u003EAlignment\u003C/strong\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EName\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EStart station\u003C/strong\u003E. Type a station as \u003Ccode\u003E10+00\u003C/code\u003E (feet) or \u003Ccode\u003E0+500\u003C/code\u003E (metres), or as a plain number. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/units-and-stations\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/units-and-stations\"\u003EUnits, stations and number formats\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELength\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EEnd station\u003C/strong\u003E are worked out for you.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe \u003Cstrong\u003EPIs\u003C/strong\u003E table lists each PI&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003EEasting\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003ENorthing\u003C/strong\u003E, and a \u003Cstrong\u003ERadius\u003C/strong\u003E for each interior PI. Type a new value and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA radius of 0 makes an angle point with no curve.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe first and last PI never have a radius.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe small x at the end of a row removes that PI (an alignment keeps at least two).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERead the \u003Cstrong\u003ECurves\u003C/strong\u003E list: for each curve, its PC and PT stations, radius R, deflection Δ and direction (left or right), length L, tangent T, external E and chord.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe line under the curves gives the bearing of the first tangent.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-a-curve-does-not-fit\"\u003EWhen a curve does not fit\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf curves need more tangent than there is between their PIs, the alignment has no solution: it is not drawn correctly until you fix it, and it is left out of LandXML exports. A red box says why, in numbers, and offers what would fit:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;The curves at PI 2 and PI 3 need 240.00 ft of tangent between them (130.00 + 110.00), but the PIs are 180.00 ft apart.&quot; — or, when only one of them has a curve, &quot;The curve at PI 2 needs 240.00 ft of tangent towards PI 1 …&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA button for each PI sets \u003Cstrong\u003Ethe largest radius that fits\u003C/strong\u003E there with the other curve kept as it is (it also has to fit the PI&#039;s other tangent). Where both PIs have curves, \u003Cstrong\u003EBoth\u003C/strong\u003E shrinks the two radii in proportion so they just meet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhen one curve alone is longer than the tangent, the box says no curve at the other PI can fit while it is that big.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA PI that turns straight back cannot take a curve; the box offers \u003Cstrong\u003ENo curve at PI n\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach button is one step: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E puts the radii back. Radii offered are rounded down to 0.01 so they always fit.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"a-design-minimum-radius\"\u003EA design minimum radius\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType a \u003Cstrong\u003EDesign minimum radius\u003C/strong\u003E (for example from your design speed) to have every curve checked against it; leave it empty for none. A curve under it is listed with a button for \u003Cstrong\u003Ethe smallest radius that meets the minimum\u003C/strong\u003E, when that radius fits. When it does not fit, the box says so and gives the largest radius the tangents allow, so you know to move a PI or lower the minimum.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"move-pis-on-the-plan\"\u003EMove PIs on the plan\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E active, click the alignment to select it. Square grips appear at its PIs.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag a grip. The alignment, its stationing, profile and corridor follow.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERelease. The move is one undo step.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAdding a PI between two existing ones is not available yet: remove the alignment and draw it again, or type new coordinates for the PIs you have.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"read-station-and-offset\"\u003ERead station and offset\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMove the pointer over the plan. The status bar shows the nearest alignment (or the selected one) with the station and the offset, \u003Cstrong\u003EL\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003ER\u003C/strong\u003E looking up-station, for example &quot;Alignment 1: Sta 2+15.30, offset 18.42 R&quot;. Beyond either end it adds &quot;(beyond the end)&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-drawn\"\u003EWhat is drawn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn the plan, in DXF and in PDF: the centerline (tangents as lines, curves as true arcs), a tick every 50 ft (20 m) with a longer tick and a station label every 100 units, and PC and PT ticks labeled with their stations.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"delete-an-alignment\"\u003EDelete an alignment\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect it and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar or at the bottom of the right panel. Its profile and corridor go with it. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E brings it back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a Mac keyboard, the key labeled \u003Cstrong\u003Edelete\u003C/strong\u003E is \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to the browser and does not delete a selection. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (Fn and delete), or use the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E button.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"next\"\u003ENext\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/profile\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/profile\"\u003EDesign a vertical profile\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESpirals are not available yet; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"An alignment in CivilCraft is a chain of tangents through points of intersection (PIs), with a circular curve at any interior PI that has a radius. It is stationed from a start station you choose. A project can hold many alignments. Draw the alignment Press A, or click Alignment in the Design group. The hint says \"Alignment: click the PIs in order. Enter or double-click finishes, Backspace drops the last, Esc cancels.\" Click the first PI (the start of the alignment). Click each following PI in order. A red line follows the pointer and shows the length and bearing of the tangent you are drawing, for example \"212.40 ft N 63°12'05\" E\". Click the last PI (the end of the alignment). Press Enter. The alignment is drawn with stationing and is selected. The status bar says, for example, \"Alignment 1: 386.36 ft, curves of 100 ft radius where they fit. Edit radii in Properties; Profile (R) to design its grade.\" While drawing: Backspace removes the last PI, Esc cancels, and dragging on empty space pans without adding a PI. Finish with Enter. Double-clicking also finishes, but in this build it drops the PI you double-clicked. Click the last PI once, then press Enter. Every interior PI gets a curve of 100 ft (30 m in a metre project), or the largest whole radius that fits in half of each tangent if 100 ft does not fit. Very sharp or straight-through PIs get no curve. Set radii and coordinates With the alignment selected, the right panel shows Alignment: Name and Start station. Type a station as 10+00 (feet) or 0+500 (metres), or as a plain number. See Units, stations and number formats. Length and End station are worked out for you. The PIs table lists each PI's Easting and Northing, and a Radius for each interior PI. Type a new value and press Enter. A radius of 0 makes an angle point with no curve. The first and last PI never have a radius. The small x at the end of a row removes that PI (an alignment keeps at least two). Read the Curves list: for each curve, its PC and PT stations, radius R, deflection Δ and direction (left or right), length L, tangent T, external E and chord. The line under the curves gives the bearing of the first tangent. When a curve does not fit If curves need more tangent than there is between their PIs, the alignment has no solution: it is not drawn correctly until you fix it, and it is left out of LandXML exports. A red box says why, in numbers, and offers what would fit: \"The curves at PI 2 and PI 3 need 240.00 ft of tangent between them (130.00 + 110.00), but the PIs are 180.00 ft apart.\" — or, when only one of them has a curve, \"The curve at PI 2 needs 240.00 ft of tangent towards PI 1 …\". A button for each PI sets the largest radius that fits there with the other curve kept as it is (it also has to fit the PI's other tangent). Where both PIs have curves, Both shrinks the two radii in proportion so they just meet. When one curve alone is longer than the tangent, the box says no curve at the other PI can fit while it is that big. A PI that turns straight back cannot take a curve; the box offers No curve at PI n. Each button is one step: Ctrl+Z puts the radii back. Radii offered are rounded down to 0.01 so they always fit. A design minimum radius Type a Design minimum radius (for example from your design speed) to have every curve checked against it; leave it empty for none. A curve under it is listed with a button for the smallest radius that meets the minimum, when that radius fits. When it does not fit, the box says so and gives the largest radius the tangents allow, so you know to move a PI or lower the minimum. Move PIs on the plan With Select active, click the alignment to select it. Square grips appear at its PIs. Drag a grip. The alignment, its stationing, profile and corridor follow. Release. The move is one undo step. Adding a PI between two existing ones is not available yet: remove the alignment and draw it again, or type new coordinates for the PIs you have. Read station and offset Move the pointer over the plan. The status bar shows the nearest alignment (or the selected one) with the station and the offset, L or R looking up-station, for example \"Alignment 1: Sta 2+15.30, offset 18.42 R\". Beyond either end it adds \"(beyond the end)\". What is drawn On the plan, in DXF and in PDF: the centerline (tangents as lines, curves as true arcs), a tick every 50 ft (20 m) with a longer tick and a station label every 100 units, and PC and PT ticks labeled with their stations. Delete an alignment Select it and press Delete, or click Delete in the toolbar or at the bottom of the right panel. Its profile and corridor go with it. Ctrl+Z brings it back. On a Mac keyboard, the key labeled delete is Backspace to the browser and does not delete a selection. Press Delete (Fn and delete), or use the Delete button. Next Design a vertical profile. Spirals are not available yet; see Not built yet."},{"slug":"profile","scope":"civilcraft","title":"Design a vertical profile","summary":"Open the profile view, start a design profile from the ground, add and drag PVIs, set vertical curve lengths, and read grades, K values and high and low points.","section":"tasks","order":50,"keywords":["profile","vertical alignment","pvi","vertical curve","grade","k value","crest","sag","bvc","evc","profile view","finished grade","fg","existing ground profile","feature lines in profile","crossings","projected","curb","ditch","labels"],"headings":[{"id":"open-the-profile-view","text":"Open the profile view","level":2},{"id":"start-the-design-profile","text":"Start the design profile","level":2},{"id":"add-pvis","text":"Add PVIs","level":2},{"id":"adjust-pvis","text":"Adjust PVIs","level":2},{"id":"vertical-curves","text":"Vertical curves","level":2},{"id":"read-the-facts","text":"Read the facts","level":2},{"id":"feature-lines-in-the-profile","text":"Feature lines in the profile","level":2},{"id":"next","text":"Next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EEach alignment has one design profile: a list of PVIs (station and elevation) joined by straight grades, with a symmetric parabolic vertical curve at any interior PVI that has a length. The existing ground profile is sampled from the surface for you.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-the-profile-view\"\u003EOpen the profile view\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the alignment (click it with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProfile\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProfile view\u003C/span\u003E at the bottom of the right panel. The profile view opens under the plan with the existing ground along the alignment. With two or more alignments, pick one in the list at the left of the profile bar.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe view always shows the whole alignment and stretches the elevations to fill the panel; the exaggeration is printed in the lower-left corner, for example &quot;Vertical exaggeration 10×&quot;. It does not zoom or pan.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMove the pointer along the profile: the bar shows the station, ground and design elevations, the grade and the cut or fill there, and a dot follows on the alignment in the plan.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"start-the-design-profile\"\u003EStart the design profile\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the right panel under \u003Cstrong\u003EDesign profile\u003C/strong\u003E, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStart from the ground\u003C/span\u003E. Two PVIs are placed on the ground at the two ends of the alignment, joined by one straight grade. The status bar says &quot;Design profile started at the ground at both ends. Turn on Add PVIs and click to add grade breaks; give them a curve length in the table.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf the alignment does not cross the surface, you get &quot;This alignment has no ground under it to start from.&quot; Then add PVIs by clicking instead (next section).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-pvis\"\u003EAdd PVIs\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"I\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd PVIs\u003C/span\u003E in the profile bar. It stays pressed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick in the profile where you want a grade break. A PVI is added at that station and elevation, rounded to 0.01, and selected.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd as many as you need, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"I\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again to stop adding.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA click where there is already a PVI is refused (&quot;There is already a PVI at …&quot;). PVIs always stay in station order.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"adjust-pvis\"\u003EAdjust PVIs\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDrag\u003C/strong\u003E a PVI in the profile to move it. It cannot pass its neighbors. The move is one undo step.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EType\u003C/strong\u003E exact values in the PVI table in the right panel: \u003Cstrong\u003EStation\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EElevation\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003ECurve L\u003C/strong\u003E (vertical curve length). Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E after each.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERemove\u003C/strong\u003E a PVI with the x at the end of its row, or select it (click it) and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. When the profile view has the focus, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E also removes it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe first and last PVI never take a curve.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"vertical-curves\"\u003EVertical curves\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType a length in \u003Cstrong\u003ECurve L\u003C/strong\u003E for an interior PVI, for example 200.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe curve is drawn centered on the PVI. The profile and the right panel show its kind (crest or sag), L and K, the BVC and EVC stations and elevations, and its high or low point when it has one inside the curve.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EK is worked out from L (K = L divided by the algebraic difference in grades, in percent). You type L; typing K directly is not available yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf a vertical curve does not fit, a red box says why: it runs back into the PVI or curve before it, or past the next PVI, and how much room there is on that side (a curve needs half its length on each side of its PVI). Its button sets \u003Cstrong\u003Ethe longest curve that fits\u003C/strong\u003E, with its K, as one step (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes it). Or move the PVIs apart.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"read-the-facts\"\u003ERead the facts\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder the PVI table, a list gives each grade in percent (&quot;Grade 1: +1.25%&quot;) and each curve, for example &quot;Crest at 2+00.00: L 200.00, K 85.3, BVC 1+00.00 (253.10), EVC 3+00.00 (252.40)&quot;, then &quot;High point 1+62.40, 254.12&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf the PVIs do not reach both ends of the alignment, a note says so. A corridor is built only where the profile and alignment overlap.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"feature-lines-in-the-profile\"\u003EFeature lines in the profile\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFeature lines show in the profile view too, so you can see a curb, a ditch or a wall top against the road&#039;s profile.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhere a feature line \u003Cstrong\u003Ecrosses\u003C/strong\u003E the alignment, the profile shows a small diamond at that station and elevation, labelled with the line&#039;s name and its elevation there (the line&#039;s own elevation, worked out along the segment that crosses).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType a distance in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAlongside within\u003C/span\u003E to also draw the feature lines that \u003Cstrong\u003Erun beside\u003C/strong\u003E the alignment within that distance, as dashed lines at their own elevations. Empty or 0 shows only the crossings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe marks and labels follow every change: move a vertex, type a grade or raise the line, and the profile shows the new elevations at once.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a crossing&#039;s diamond to select that feature line; the selected line is drawn in the highlight color.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFeature lines\u003C/span\u003E in the profile bar hides or shows them all. Both settings are saved with the project.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/feature-lines\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/feature-lines\"\u003EFeature lines\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"next\"\u003ENext\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/corridor\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/corridor\"\u003ESweep a road corridor\u003C/a\u003E along the alignment and this profile.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe profile is written to LandXML with the alignment; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/landxml\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/landxml\"\u003EExchange LandXML\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Each alignment has one design profile: a list of PVIs (station and elevation) joined by straight grades, with a symmetric parabolic vertical curve at any interior PVI that has a length. The existing ground profile is sampled from the surface for you. Open the profile view Select the alignment (click it with Select). Press R, or click Profile in the toolbar, or Profile view at the bottom of the right panel. The profile view opens under the plan with the existing ground along the alignment. With two or more alignments, pick one in the list at the left of the profile bar. The view always shows the whole alignment and stretches the elevations to fill the panel; the exaggeration is printed in the lower-left corner, for example \"Vertical exaggeration 10×\". It does not zoom or pan. Move the pointer along the profile: the bar shows the station, ground and design elevations, the grade and the cut or fill there, and a dot follows on the alignment in the plan. Start the design profile In the right panel under Design profile, click Start from the ground. Two PVIs are placed on the ground at the two ends of the alignment, joined by one straight grade. The status bar says \"Design profile started at the ground at both ends. Turn on Add PVIs and click to add grade breaks; give them a curve length in the table.\" If the alignment does not cross the surface, you get \"This alignment has no ground under it to start from.\" Then add PVIs by clicking instead (next section). Add PVIs Press I, or click Add PVIs in the profile bar. It stays pressed. Click in the profile where you want a grade break. A PVI is added at that station and elevation, rounded to 0.01, and selected. Add as many as you need, then press I again to stop adding. A click where there is already a PVI is refused (\"There is already a PVI at …\"). PVIs always stay in station order. Adjust PVIs Drag a PVI in the profile to move it. It cannot pass its neighbors. The move is one undo step. Type exact values in the PVI table in the right panel: Station, Elevation and Curve L (vertical curve length). Press Enter after each. Remove a PVI with the x at the end of its row, or select it (click it) and press Delete. When the profile view has the focus, Backspace also removes it. The first and last PVI never take a curve. Vertical curves Type a length in Curve L for an interior PVI, for example 200. The curve is drawn centered on the PVI. The profile and the right panel show its kind (crest or sag), L and K, the BVC and EVC stations and elevations, and its high or low point when it has one inside the curve. K is worked out from L (K = L divided by the algebraic difference in grades, in percent). You type L; typing K directly is not available yet. If a vertical curve does not fit, a red box says why: it runs back into the PVI or curve before it, or past the next PVI, and how much room there is on that side (a curve needs half its length on each side of its PVI). Its button sets the longest curve that fits, with its K, as one step (Ctrl+Z undoes it). Or move the PVIs apart. Read the facts Under the PVI table, a list gives each grade in percent (\"Grade 1: +1.25%\") and each curve, for example \"Crest at 2+00.00: L 200.00, K 85.3, BVC 1+00.00 (253.10), EVC 3+00.00 (252.40)\", then \"High point 1+62.40, 254.12\". If the PVIs do not reach both ends of the alignment, a note says so. A corridor is built only where the profile and alignment overlap. Feature lines in the profile Feature lines show in the profile view too, so you can see a curb, a ditch or a wall top against the road's profile. Where a feature line crosses the alignment, the profile shows a small diamond at that station and elevation, labelled with the line's name and its elevation there (the line's own elevation, worked out along the segment that crosses). Type a distance in Alongside within to also draw the feature lines that run beside the alignment within that distance, as dashed lines at their own elevations. Empty or 0 shows only the crossings. The marks and labels follow every change: move a vertex, type a grade or raise the line, and the profile shows the new elevations at once. Click a crossing's diamond to select that feature line; the selected line is drawn in the highlight color. Feature lines in the profile bar hides or shows them all. Both settings are saved with the project. See Feature lines. Next Sweep a road corridor along the alignment and this profile. The profile is written to LandXML with the alignment; see Exchange LandXML."},{"slug":"sections","scope":"civilcraft","title":"Sample lines and cross sections","summary":"Cut sections square to an alignment, see the ground, the design and the cut and fill at each station, and get volumes by average end areas.","section":"tasks","order":55,"keywords":["mass haul","mass diagram","balance point","shrink","swell","haul distance","borrow","waste","section sheets","cross section sheets","sections pdf","sections","cross sections","sample lines","section view","cut","fill","areas","average end area","end area volumes","earthwork","station","offset","csv"],"headings":[{"id":"add-sample-lines","text":"Add sample lines","level":2},{"id":"the-section-view","text":"The section view","level":2},{"id":"section-sheets","text":"Section sheets","level":2},{"id":"volumes-by-average-end-areas","text":"Volumes by average end areas","level":2},{"id":"mass-haul","text":"Mass haul","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA cross section shows the road the way it will be built: across the alignment at one station, the existing ground, the design (the corridor or pad graded in) and the cut and fill between them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-sample-lines\"\u003EAdd sample lines\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect an alignment. In \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E, under \u003Cstrong\u003ESample lines\u003C/strong\u003E, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd sample lines\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet \u003Cstrong\u003EEvery\u003C/strong\u003E (the spacing: 50 ft or 20 m to start), \u003Cstrong\u003ELeft width\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003ERight width\u003C/strong\u003E (how far each section reaches from the centerline).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo cut a section at a particular station, type it in \u003Cstrong\u003EAdd a station\u003C/strong\u003E (for example \u003Ccode\u003E2+35\u003C/code\u003E) and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESections are cut at every whole multiple of the spacing, at both ends of the alignment, at the start and end of every curve (PC and PT), and at the stations you add. The sample lines show on the plan as gray lines across the alignment; they also go out in DXF, DWG and PDF on layer C-ROAD-SCTN. Every change is one step: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-section-view\"\u003EThe section view\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen it with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESection view\u003C/span\u003E in the alignment&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESections\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cstrong\u003ESurface\u003C/strong\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"X\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. It takes the place of the profile view under the plan.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStep through the sections with the arrows in its bar, the station list, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"←\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"→\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E with the section view focused (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ←\"\u003Efn ←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EHome\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn →\"\u003Efn →\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnd\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the first and last).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe gray line is the existing ground, the red line the design. Cut is shaded orange, fill green. Corridor points are marked: CL (centerline), EOP (edge of pavement), SH (shoulder) and DL (daylight).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe header gives the station, this section&#039;s cut and fill areas and the vertical exaggeration. \u003Cstrong\u003EFit\u003C/strong\u003E fills the panel; choose 1× to see the section true to scale.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe thick line on the plan shows which section is on screen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"section-sheets\"\u003ESection sheets\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESection sheets (PDF)\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cstrong\u003ESample lines\u003C/strong\u003E puts every section on PDF pages, in rows from left to right and top to bottom in station order. Choose the \u003Cstrong\u003EPaper\u003C/strong\u003E, the \u003Cstrong\u003EPlan scale\u003C/strong\u003E (the horizontal scale of the sections) and the \u003Cstrong\u003EProfile exaggeration\u003C/strong\u003E (1×, 2×, 5× or 10×); the dialog says how many sections fit on a page and how many pages that makes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach section shows a grid with offsets and elevations, the ground (dashed), the design, cut and fill shaded (orange and green), the corridor points, and under it its station and its cut and fill areas. Every section on the sheets is drawn the same size, tall enough for the biggest one; when that does not fit the page at your exaggeration, CivilCraft uses the largest one that does and says so. The title strip names the alignment, the stations on that page, the scales and &quot;Sheet 1 of 3&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"volumes-by-average-end-areas\"\u003EVolumes by average end areas\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBetween two neighboring sections, the volume is the average of their two areas times the distance between their stations. The bar shows the totals for the alignment, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECSV\u003C/span\u003E downloads every section&#039;s areas and volumes with running totals (cubic yards for feet projects, cubic meters for metric ones).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E shows the same totals next to the grid-method volumes from \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/volumes\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/volumes\"\u003EEarthwork volumes\u003C/a\u003E. The two are different approximations: closer sections bring them closer, and on a straight road on flat ground they are the same.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECut and fill are counted only inside the graded areas (a corridor&#039;s or a pad&#039;s daylight lines), as the grid method does. Without a corridor or pad the design is the ground, so a section shows no cut or fill.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mass-haul\"\u003EMass haul\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMass haul\u003C/strong\u003E under \u003Cstrong\u003ESample lines\u003C/strong\u003E draws the mass-haul diagram from the same end-area volumes: along the stations, the running total of cut less fill.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EShrink (−) or swell (+) of the cut\u003C/strong\u003E says what a cubic yard of cut makes once it is placed as fill: −10 % (the starting value, a placeholder) means it shrinks to 0.9; rock that swells might be +5 %. Use your geotechnical report&#039;s figure.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhere the line rises the road is in cut; where it falls, in fill. Above zero (shaded orange) earth moves up station; below zero (green), down station.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBalance points\u003C/strong\u003E are where the line crosses zero: between two of them the cut and fill balance. The table lists each balanced stretch, the volume moved (its highest or lowest point) and the average haul distance (the shaded area divided by that volume).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhere the line ends off zero, what is left over is \u003Cstrong\u003Ewaste\u003C/strong\u003E (above) or \u003Cstrong\u003Eborrow\u003C/strong\u003E (below). In that last stretch only the earth beyond the level it ends on counts as moved.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECSV\u003C/strong\u003E saves the mass ordinate at every section, the stretches and the factor.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"A cross section shows the road the way it will be built: across the alignment at one station, the existing ground, the design (the corridor or pad graded in) and the cut and fill between them. Add sample lines Select an alignment. In Properties, under Sample lines, click Add sample lines. Set Every (the spacing: 50 ft or 20 m to start), Left width and Right width (how far each section reaches from the centerline). To cut a section at a particular station, type it in Add a station (for example 2+35) and press Enter. Sections are cut at every whole multiple of the spacing, at both ends of the alignment, at the start and end of every curve (PC and PT), and at the stations you add. The sample lines show on the plan as gray lines across the alignment; they also go out in DXF, DWG and PDF on layer C-ROAD-SCTN. Every change is one step: Ctrl+Z undoes it. The section view Open it with Section view in the alignment's Properties, Sections under Surface › More, or X. It takes the place of the profile view under the plan. Step through the sections with the arrows in its bar, the station list, or ← and → with the section view focused (Home and End for the first and last). The gray line is the existing ground, the red line the design. Cut is shaded orange, fill green. Corridor points are marked: CL (centerline), EOP (edge of pavement), SH (shoulder) and DL (daylight). The header gives the station, this section's cut and fill areas and the vertical exaggeration. Fit fills the panel; choose 1× to see the section true to scale. The thick line on the plan shows which section is on screen. Section sheets Section sheets (PDF) under Sample lines puts every section on PDF pages, in rows from left to right and top to bottom in station order. Choose the Paper, the Plan scale (the horizontal scale of the sections) and the Profile exaggeration (1×, 2×, 5× or 10×); the dialog says how many sections fit on a page and how many pages that makes. Each section shows a grid with offsets and elevations, the ground (dashed), the design, cut and fill shaded (orange and green), the corridor points, and under it its station and its cut and fill areas. Every section on the sheets is drawn the same size, tall enough for the biggest one; when that does not fit the page at your exaggeration, CivilCraft uses the largest one that does and says so. The title strip names the alignment, the stations on that page, the scales and \"Sheet 1 of 3\". Volumes by average end areas Between two neighboring sections, the volume is the average of their two areas times the distance between their stations. The bar shows the totals for the alignment, and CSV downloads every section's areas and volumes with running totals (cubic yards for feet projects, cubic meters for metric ones). Properties shows the same totals next to the grid-method volumes from Earthwork volumes. The two are different approximations: closer sections bring them closer, and on a straight road on flat ground they are the same. Cut and fill are counted only inside the graded areas (a corridor's or a pad's daylight lines), as the grid method does. Without a corridor or pad the design is the ground, so a section shows no cut or fill. Mass haul Mass haul under Sample lines draws the mass-haul diagram from the same end-area volumes: along the stations, the running total of cut less fill. Shrink (−) or swell (+) of the cut says what a cubic yard of cut makes once it is placed as fill: −10 % (the starting value, a placeholder) means it shrinks to 0.9; rock that swells might be +5 %. Use your geotechnical report's figure. Where the line rises the road is in cut; where it falls, in fill. Above zero (shaded orange) earth moves up station; below zero (green), down station. Balance points are where the line crosses zero: between two of them the cut and fill balance. The table lists each balanced stretch, the volume moved (its highest or lowest point) and the average haul distance (the shaded area divided by that volume). Where the line ends off zero, what is left over is waste (above) or borrow (below). In that last stretch only the earth beyond the level it ends on counts as moved. CSV saves the mass ordinate at every section, the stretches and the factor."},{"slug":"plan-profile-sheets","scope":"civilcraft","title":"Plan and profile sheets","summary":"Plot an alignment as a set of plan and profile sheets in one PDF, with match lines between them.","section":"tasks","order":58,"keywords":["plan and profile","sheets","plot","pdf","match line","plan production","road plans","profile plot","vertical exaggeration","sheet set"],"headings":[{"id":"make-the-sheets","text":"Make the sheets","level":2},{"id":"what-is-on-a-sheet","text":"What is on a sheet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EPlan and profile sheets are how road plans are usually drawn: each sheet shows one stretch of the road twice, the plan above and the profile below, at the same horizontal scale, so a station sits at the same place in both.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-the-sheets\"\u003EMake the sheets\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the alignment. In \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlan and profile sheets\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the \u003Cstrong\u003EPaper\u003C/strong\u003E, the \u003Cstrong\u003EPlan scale\u003C/strong\u003E and the \u003Cstrong\u003EProfile exaggeration\u003C/strong\u003E (how many times taller the profile is drawn than it is long; 10× to start). The dialog says how many sheets that makes and how much of the alignment each one shows.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo plot part of the alignment, change \u003Cstrong\u003EFrom station\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003ETo station\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload PDF\u003C/span\u003E. The PDF has one page per sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-on-a-sheet\"\u003EWhat is on a sheet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EPart\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it shows\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEverything on the plan (contours, survey points, the alignment and its stationing, corridors, pads, feature lines, pipes, sample lines) turned so this stretch runs across the sheet, with a north arrow turned to match\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMatch lines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA dash-dot line square to the alignment at each end that continues on another sheet, labeled &quot;Match line sta 5+50.00, see sheet 2&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EProfile\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe existing ground (dashed), the design profile (red), the grade of each tangent and every vertical curve&#039;s PVI, elevation, L and K; stations along the bottom, elevations on both sides\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETitle strip\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe project name, the alignment and its station range, &quot;Sheet 1 of 3&quot;, the horizontal and vertical scales and the exaggeration\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe stretch per sheet is as long as the plan window allows at the scale, rounded down to a whole 50 ft (20 m) on long stretches or a whole 10 ft (5 m) on short ones.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen the profile&#039;s elevations would not fit at the exaggeration you chose, CivilCraft draws every sheet at the largest of 10×, 5×, 4×, 2× and 1× that fits, and the status bar says so. A curved alignment is turned by the direction from the start to the end of each stretch, so the road may run slightly up or down on the sheet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"Plan and profile sheets are how road plans are usually drawn: each sheet shows one stretch of the road twice, the plan above and the profile below, at the same horizontal scale, so a station sits at the same place in both. Make the sheets Select the alignment. In Properties, click Plan and profile sheets. Choose the Paper, the Plan scale and the Profile exaggeration (how many times taller the profile is drawn than it is long; 10× to start). The dialog says how many sheets that makes and how much of the alignment each one shows. To plot part of the alignment, change From station and To station. Click Download PDF. The PDF has one page per sheet. What is on a sheet Part What it shows Plan Everything on the plan (contours, survey points, the alignment and its stationing, corridors, pads, feature lines, pipes, sample lines) turned so this stretch runs across the sheet, with a north arrow turned to match Match lines A dash-dot line square to the alignment at each end that continues on another sheet, labeled \"Match line sta 5+50.00, see sheet 2\" Profile The existing ground (dashed), the design profile (red), the grade of each tangent and every vertical curve's PVI, elevation, L and K; stations along the bottom, elevations on both sides Title strip The project name, the alignment and its station range, \"Sheet 1 of 3\", the horizontal and vertical scales and the exaggeration The stretch per sheet is as long as the plan window allows at the scale, rounded down to a whole 50 ft (20 m) on long stretches or a whole 10 ft (5 m) on short ones. When the profile's elevations would not fit at the exaggeration you chose, CivilCraft draws every sheet at the largest of 10×, 5×, 4×, 2× and 1× that fits, and the status bar says so. A curved alignment is turned by the direction from the start to the end of each stretch, so the road may run slightly up or down on the sheet."},{"slug":"assistants","scope":"civilcraft","title":"Let an AI assistant work with your site","summary":"Turn on assistants in Settings so an assistant on your computer can read the open site (points, surfaces, stations and offsets) and select survey points, through CadCraft Studio. Off until you turn it on.","section":"tasks","order":60,"keywords":["ai","assistant","mcp","script","automation","studio","points","elevation","station","offset","alignment","permissions","disconnect","activity"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"what-an-assistant-can-do-in-civilcraft","text":"What an assistant can do in CivilCraft","level":2},{"id":"turn-it-on","text":"Turn it on","level":2},{"id":"while-an-assistant-is-connected","text":"While an assistant is connected","level":2},{"id":"turn-it-off","text":"Turn it off","level":2},{"id":"see-also","text":"See also","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to let an AI assistant (any assistant that speaks MCP) look into the site you have open, what it can and cannot do, and how to stop it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-an-assistant-can-do-in-civilcraft\"\u003EWhat an assistant can do in CivilCraft\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIt asks for\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it gets\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENeeds\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe site\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EName, units, coordinate system, point groups, alignments, surfaces, pads, parcels and pipes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESurvey points\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENumber, easting, northing, elevation and description, by group, by description or inside a box\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESurface elevations\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe existing ground (or the graded surface) at any points it names\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStations and offsets\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe station and offset of points along an alignment, or the coordinates and ground elevation of stations\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA selection\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe points it names are selected on the plan, as if you had picked them\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect survey points\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn assistant never changes the site: it cannot move, add, delete or save anything in CivilCraft, and it cannot open files. You open the site; it works with the one that is open.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"turn-it-on\"\u003ETurn it on\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAssistants reach CivilCraft through CadCraft Studio on your computer; the plain browser has no way in.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E and choose the \u003Cstrong\u003ECivilCraft\u003C/strong\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cstrong\u003EAssistants\u003C/strong\u003E, tick \u003Cstrong\u003ELet AI assistants and scripts use CivilCraft\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERead\u003C/strong\u003E is ticked for you. Tick \u003Cstrong\u003ESelect survey points on the plan\u003C/strong\u003E too if the assistant may select points for you.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EConnect the assistant in Studio (Studio shows the address and the settings to paste into it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"while-an-assistant-is-connected\"\u003EWhile an assistant is connected\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA note at the top of the right panel says which assistant is connected and what it can do. Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDisconnect\u003C/span\u003E to end its session at once; it has to connect again to continue.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EActivity\u003C/strong\u003E, under \u003Cstrong\u003EAssistants\u003C/strong\u003E in Settings, lists every call the assistant made, newest first, with whether it worked.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAsk in plain words, for example &quot;what is the ground elevation every 50 feet along Main Street?&quot; or &quot;select the tree shots east of the creek&quot;. The assistant reads the site and answers, or selects the points for you to copy or delete.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"turn-it-off\"\u003ETurn it off\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUntick \u003Cstrong\u003ELet AI assistants and scripts use CivilCraft\u003C/strong\u003E. Every assistant is disconnected straight away, and the setting is kept for next time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"see-also\"\u003ESee also\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/alignment\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/alignment\"\u003ELay out a horizontal alignment\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/build-surface\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/build-surface\"\u003EBuild and refine the surface\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/copy-paste-points\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/copy-paste-points\"\u003ESelect, copy and paste survey points\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/settings\"\u003ECivilCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to let an AI assistant (any assistant that speaks MCP) look into the site you have open, what it can and cannot do, and how to stop it. What an assistant can do in CivilCraft It asks for What it gets Needs The site Name, units, coordinate system, point groups, alignments, surfaces, pads, parcels and pipes Read Survey points Number, easting, northing, elevation and description, by group, by description or inside a box Read Surface elevations The existing ground (or the graded surface) at any points it names Read Stations and offsets The station and offset of points along an alignment, or the coordinates and ground elevation of stations Read A selection The points it names are selected on the plan, as if you had picked them Select survey points An assistant never changes the site: it cannot move, add, delete or save anything in CivilCraft, and it cannot open files. You open the site; it works with the one that is open. Turn it on Assistants reach CivilCraft through CadCraft Studio on your computer; the plain browser has no way in. Open Settings and choose the CivilCraft tab. Under Assistants, tick Let AI assistants and scripts use CivilCraft. Read is ticked for you. Tick Select survey points on the plan too if the assistant may select points for you. Connect the assistant in Studio (Studio shows the address and the settings to paste into it). While an assistant is connected A note at the top of the right panel says which assistant is connected and what it can do. Click Disconnect to end its session at once; it has to connect again to continue. Activity, under Assistants in Settings, lists every call the assistant made, newest first, with whether it worked. Ask in plain words, for example \"what is the ground elevation every 50 feet along Main Street?\" or \"select the tree shots east of the creek\". The assistant reads the site and answers, or selects the points for you to copy or delete. Turn it off Untick Let AI assistants and scripts use CivilCraft. Every assistant is disconnected straight away, and the setting is kept for next time. See also Lay out a horizontal alignment Build and refine the surface Select, copy and paste survey points CivilCraft settings"},{"slug":"corridor","scope":"civilcraft","title":"Sweep a road corridor","summary":"Turn on a corridor for an alignment, set lane, shoulder and side slope values, and read the sections, daylight lines and earthwork it produces.","section":"tasks","order":60,"keywords":["superelevation","super","banking","runoff","runout","rate table","cross slope","rotation","corridor","assembly","subassembly","typical section","road","lane","shoulder","cross slope","side slope","daylight","cut","fill","sections","target","targets","width target","elevation target","offset target","widening","edge of pavement","follow another corridor"],"headings":[{"id":"the-assembly","text":"The assembly","level":2},{"id":"build-the-corridor","text":"Build the corridor","level":2},{"id":"when-it-does-not-build","text":"When it does not build","level":2},{"id":"corridors-and-pads-together","text":"Corridors and pads together","level":2},{"id":"superelevation","text":"Superelevation","level":2},{"id":"targets","text":"Targets","level":2},{"id":"what-is-not-built-yet","text":"What is not built yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA corridor sweeps one simple typical section (an assembly) along an alignment and its design profile, grades it into the existing ground and adds the result to the proposed surface. In this build each alignment can carry one corridor, and the assembly is the same on both sides.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-assembly\"\u003EThe assembly\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFrom the centerline outward, on each side:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ea \u003Cstrong\u003Elane\u003C/strong\u003E of a given width, falling away from the crown at the \u003Cstrong\u003Elane slope\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ea \u003Cstrong\u003Eshoulder\u003C/strong\u003E of a given width at the \u003Cstrong\u003Eshoulder slope\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ea \u003Cstrong\u003Eside slope\u003C/strong\u003E from the shoulder edge to where it meets the ground: the \u003Cstrong\u003Ecut slope\u003C/strong\u003E where the ground is above, the \u003Cstrong\u003Efill slope\u003C/strong\u003E where it is below\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe starting values are placeholders, not a design standard:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EValue\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EUS feet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMetres\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELane width\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E12 ft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E3.6 m\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELane slope\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2%\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2%\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShoulder width\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E4 ft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E1.2 m\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShoulder slope\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E4%\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E4%\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECut slope\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2 : 1\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2 : 1\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFill slope\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E3 : 1\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E3 : 1\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESection every\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E10 ft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E5 m\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESlopes for side slopes are horizontal to vertical (H:V): 3 : 1 means 3 across for 1 down.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"build-the-corridor\"\u003EBuild the corridor\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou need an alignment without errors and a design profile with at least two PVIs and no errors.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the alignment.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the right panel, under \u003Cstrong\u003ECorridor\u003C/strong\u003E, tick \u003Cstrong\u003ESweep an assembly along the design profile\u003C/strong\u003E. The fields appear and the corridor is built at once. The plan shows the edges of lane and shoulder and the daylight lines; with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProposed (graded)\u003C/span\u003E chosen under \u003Cstrong\u003EDisplay\u003C/strong\u003E, the contours show the new ground.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChange any value: \u003Cstrong\u003ELane width\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ELane slope\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EShoulder width\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EShoulder slope\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ECut slope\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EFill slope\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ESection every\u003C/strong\u003E. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E after each. Cut, fill and section spacing must be more than 0.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERead the line under the fields, for example &quot;39 sections from 0+00.00 to 3+86.36; cut 240.2 cu yd, fill 556.0 cu yd.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESections are cut at every \u003Cstrong\u003ESection every\u003C/strong\u003E step, at the ends, and at the start and end of every horizontal and vertical curve. Each section and the lines through them (centerline, edges of lane and shoulder, daylight) become breaklines of the proposed surface.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo turn the corridor off, untick the box. Its values are kept for next time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-it-does-not-build\"\u003EWhen it does not build\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA red box under the fields says why:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMessage\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat to do\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;The corridor needs a design profile without errors.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdd PVIs, or fix overlapping vertical curves (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/profile\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/profile\"\u003EDesign a vertical profile\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;The design profile does not overlap the alignment.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMove the PVIs onto the alignment&#039;s station range.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;The corridor needs at least two sections on the surface.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMost of the corridor is off the existing ground.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;4 side slopes run off the surface before meeting the ground.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENear the edge of the surface the slope cannot reach the ground. Keep the road away from the edge, or lower the fill or cut height there.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;The corridor’s daylight lines cross (sections overlap, for example on the inside of a tight curve). Use a larger radius or narrower assembly.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESections on the inside of a sharp curve overlap. Increase the radius or narrow the assembly.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA corridor with a problem is not graded in and does not count in the earthwork.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"corridors-and-pads-together\"\u003ECorridors and pads together\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe proposed surface is built in order: every corridor first, then every pad. Where a pad&#039;s grading overlaps a corridor, the pad wins. Volumes are worked out inside all the daylight lines together; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/volumes\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/volumes\"\u003EEarthwork volumes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"superelevation\"\u003ESuperelevation\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThrough a curve the road can tilt toward the inside, so traffic is pushed less to the outside. Tick \u003Cstrong\u003ESuperelevate the curves\u003C/strong\u003E under \u003Cstrong\u003ESuperelevation\u003C/strong\u003E in the alignment&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach circular curve takes a rate \u003Cstrong\u003Ee\u003C/strong\u003E from the rate table: the first row whose radius the curve reaches (a row with an empty rate keeps normal crown). The table and the \u003Cstrong\u003EMax relative gradient\u003C/strong\u003E (0.5 % to start) are placeholders, not a standard: set them from your own design criteria. Add or remove rows as you need.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe lanes rotate about the centerline. Before the curve the outside lane flattens over the \u003Cstrong\u003Erunout\u003C/strong\u003E (normal crown ÷ e × runoff), then over the \u003Cstrong\u003Erunoff\u003C/strong\u003E (lane width × e ÷ the gradient) the outside lane keeps turning until the whole road slopes at e toward the inside; two thirds of the runoff lies before the curve and one third on it. After the curve the same happens in reverse.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA shoulder on the low side is never flatter than its lane; on the high side it keeps its own slope (it rolls over).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe table under it lists each curve&#039;s radius, rate, runoff and runout and where full superelevation starts and ends. The \u003Cstrong\u003Esuperelevation diagram\u003C/strong\u003E draws both lanes&#039; cross slopes along the stations (the left lane solid, the right one dashed).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA curve too short to reach full superelevation, or two curves whose transitions overlap, is said in a box above the table.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESections, volumes, the 3D view and the sheets all show the tilted road. Each change is one step: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"targets\"\u003ETargets\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA target moves one point of one side of the road to a line: the \u003Cstrong\u003EEdge of pavement\u003C/strong\u003E or the \u003Cstrong\u003EShoulder edge\u003C/strong\u003E, on the \u003Cstrong\u003ELeft\u003C/strong\u003E or the \u003Cstrong\u003ERight\u003C/strong\u003E. Each section meets the line square to the centerline, going out on that side only, so a target on the right never moves the left side.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESets\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat happens where a section meets the line\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWidth\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe point sits on the line: the lane (or the shoulder) widens or narrows to it, keeping its cross slope.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EElevation\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe point takes the line&#039;s elevation there, at its own offset: the lane&#039;s (or shoulder&#039;s) cross slope follows.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe line can be a feature line (whatever its \u003Cstrong\u003EUse\u003C/strong\u003E) or a line of another corridor: its centerline, edges of pavement, shoulder edges or daylight lines. A corridor&#039;s line follows that corridor: change the other road and this one follows. CivilCraft builds the other corridor first; two corridors that would target each other in a loop are refused on one side, and the table says which.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the alignment and turn its corridor on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cstrong\u003ETargets\u003C/strong\u003E, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a target\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the \u003Cstrong\u003ESide\u003C/strong\u003E, the \u003Cstrong\u003EPoint\u003C/strong\u003E, what it \u003Cstrong\u003ESets\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003ETo the line\u003C/strong\u003E. Each change is one undo step.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EResult\u003C/strong\u003E says at how many sections the line was reached. Sections that do not reach it (past its ends, or where it is behind the centerline) keep the assembly&#039;s values. A line that is deleted leaves the corridor as it was before the target, and the table says so. One side can have one width and one elevation target for each point.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-not-built-yet\"\u003EWhat is not built yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECurb and gutter, sidewalks, different assemblies on each side or along the road, regions, and targets for the daylight slope are not available yet. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"A corridor sweeps one simple typical section (an assembly) along an alignment and its design profile, grades it into the existing ground and adds the result to the proposed surface. In this build each alignment can carry one corridor, and the assembly is the same on both sides. The assembly From the centerline outward, on each side: a lane of a given width, falling away from the crown at the lane slope a shoulder of a given width at the shoulder slope a side slope from the shoulder edge to where it meets the ground: the cut slope where the ground is above, the fill slope where it is below The starting values are placeholders, not a design standard: Value US feet Metres Lane width 12 ft 3.6 m Lane slope 2% 2% Shoulder width 4 ft 1.2 m Shoulder slope 4% 4% Cut slope 2 : 1 2 : 1 Fill slope 3 : 1 3 : 1 Section every 10 ft 5 m Slopes for side slopes are horizontal to vertical (H:V): 3 : 1 means 3 across for 1 down. Build the corridor You need an alignment without errors and a design profile with at least two PVIs and no errors. Select the alignment. In the right panel, under Corridor, tick Sweep an assembly along the design profile. The fields appear and the corridor is built at once. The plan shows the edges of lane and shoulder and the daylight lines; with Proposed (graded) chosen under Display, the contours show the new ground. Change any value: Lane width, Lane slope, Shoulder width, Shoulder slope, Cut slope, Fill slope, Section every. Press Enter after each. Cut, fill and section spacing must be more than 0. Read the line under the fields, for example \"39 sections from 0+00.00 to 3+86.36; cut 240.2 cu yd, fill 556.0 cu yd.\" Sections are cut at every Section every step, at the ends, and at the start and end of every horizontal and vertical curve. Each section and the lines through them (centerline, edges of lane and shoulder, daylight) become breaklines of the proposed surface. To turn the corridor off, untick the box. Its values are kept for next time. When it does not build A red box under the fields says why: Message What to do \"The corridor needs a design profile without errors.\" Add PVIs, or fix overlapping vertical curves (see Design a vertical profile). \"The design profile does not overlap the alignment.\" Move the PVIs onto the alignment's station range. \"The corridor needs at least two sections on the surface.\" Most of the corridor is off the existing ground. \"4 side slopes run off the surface before meeting the ground.\" Near the edge of the surface the slope cannot reach the ground. Keep the road away from the edge, or lower the fill or cut height there. \"The corridor’s daylight lines cross (sections overlap, for example on the inside of a tight curve). Use a larger radius or narrower assembly.\" Sections on the inside of a sharp curve overlap. Increase the radius or narrow the assembly. A corridor with a problem is not graded in and does not count in the earthwork. Corridors and pads together The proposed surface is built in order: every corridor first, then every pad. Where a pad's grading overlaps a corridor, the pad wins. Volumes are worked out inside all the daylight lines together; see Earthwork volumes. Superelevation Through a curve the road can tilt toward the inside, so traffic is pushed less to the outside. Tick Superelevate the curves under Superelevation in the alignment's Properties: Each circular curve takes a rate e from the rate table: the first row whose radius the curve reaches (a row with an empty rate keeps normal crown). The table and the Max relative gradient (0.5 % to start) are placeholders, not a standard: set them from your own design criteria. Add or remove rows as you need. The lanes rotate about the centerline. Before the curve the outside lane flattens over the runout (normal crown ÷ e × runoff), then over the runoff (lane width × e ÷ the gradient) the outside lane keeps turning until the whole road slopes at e toward the inside; two thirds of the runoff lies before the curve and one third on it. After the curve the same happens in reverse. A shoulder on the low side is never flatter than its lane; on the high side it keeps its own slope (it rolls over). The table under it lists each curve's radius, rate, runoff and runout and where full superelevation starts and ends. The superelevation diagram draws both lanes' cross slopes along the stations (the left lane solid, the right one dashed). A curve too short to reach full superelevation, or two curves whose transitions overlap, is said in a box above the table. Sections, volumes, the 3D view and the sheets all show the tilted road. Each change is one step: Ctrl+Z undoes it. Targets A target moves one point of one side of the road to a line: the Edge of pavement or the Shoulder edge, on the Left or the Right. Each section meets the line square to the centerline, going out on that side only, so a target on the right never moves the left side. Sets What happens where a section meets the line Width The point sits on the line: the lane (or the shoulder) widens or narrows to it, keeping its cross slope. Elevation The point takes the line's elevation there, at its own offset: the lane's (or shoulder's) cross slope follows. The line can be a feature line (whatever its Use) or a line of another corridor: its centerline, edges of pavement, shoulder edges or daylight lines. A corridor's line follows that corridor: change the other road and this one follows. CivilCraft builds the other corridor first; two corridors that would target each other in a loop are refused on one side, and the table says which. Select the alignment and turn its corridor on. Under Targets, click Add a target. Choose the Side, the Point, what it Sets and To the line. Each change is one undo step. Result says at how many sections the line was reached. Sections that do not reach it (past its ends, or where it is behind the centerline) keep the assembly's values. A line that is deleted leaves the corridor as it was before the target, and the table says so. One side can have one width and one elevation target for each point. What is not built yet Curb and gutter, sidewalks, different assemblies on each side or along the road, regions, and targets for the daylight slope are not available yet. See Not built yet."},{"slug":"swept-paths","scope":"civilcraft","title":"Vehicle swept paths","summary":"Drive a vehicle with up to three trailers along a feature line or an alignment, forward or reversing, and see the area it sweeps, its wheel paths, its steering and where it jackknifes.","section":"tasks","order":63,"keywords":["swept path","turning path","vehicle tracking","turning template","design vehicle","truck","bus","trailer","semitrailer","reversing","backing","jackknife","articulation","steering lock","offtracking","wheel path","loading dock","turning radius"],"headings":[{"id":"drive-a-vehicle-along-a-line","text":"Drive a vehicle along a line","level":2},{"id":"forward-and-reversing","text":"Forward and reversing","level":2},{"id":"read-the-result","text":"Read the result","level":2},{"id":"how-it-is-worked-out","text":"How it is worked out","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA swept path drives a vehicle along a line and shows the ground its body passes over. Use it to check that a truck can make a corner, reach a loading dock or back a trailer into place without leaving the paving.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"drive-a-vehicle-along-a-line\"\u003EDrive a vehicle along a line\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw the line the vehicle follows, in the direction of travel: a feature line (arcs are fine: press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"C\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while drawing it, see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/feature-lines\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/feature-lines\"\u003EFeature lines\u003C/a\u003E) or an alignment.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect it and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESwept path…\u003C/span\u003E in its \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cstrong\u003EStart from\u003C/strong\u003E, pick a vehicle, then set its sizes from your own design vehicle: width, front overhang, wheelbase, rear overhang, track and \u003Cstrong\u003ESteering lock\u003C/strong\u003E. Add up to three trailers, each with where it is hitched, its length from the hitch to its axle, its overhangs and its \u003Cstrong\u003ELargest articulation\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cstrong\u003EDriving\u003C/strong\u003E: forward or reversing. Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrive it\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe vehicles to start from are placeholders, not a standard. Set the sizes from the design vehicle your project uses.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"forward-and-reversing\"\u003EForward and reversing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDriving\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat follows the line\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat CivilCraft works out\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EForward\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe front axle&#039;s centre\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhere the rear axle and every trailer go, the steering angle and the articulation\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EReversing\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe last axle&#039;s centre (the back of the trailer)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhere each unit ahead goes, and the steering the cab needs to put the trailer there\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EReversing is how a backing move is checked: say where the trailer should go, and CivilCraft says whether the cab can make it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"read-the-result\"\u003ERead the result\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe swept path is drawn on the plan in orange: its outline and, dashed, the paths of the outermost wheels. Select it (click inside it) to see in \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELength driven\u003C/strong\u003E, the largest \u003Cstrong\u003ESteering\u003C/strong\u003E angle and \u003Cstrong\u003EArticulation\u003C/strong\u003E against their limits, and the \u003Cstrong\u003ESwept area\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAny problem along the line, with its distance: the steering past the lock, or a trailer turning past its articulation limit (it jackknifes).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe vehicle along the line\u003C/strong\u003E: move the slider to see the vehicle at any point, with its steering and articulation there.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChange the line and the swept path follows. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit the vehicle…\u003C/span\u003E changes the sizes; \u003Cstrong\u003EDriving\u003C/strong\u003E switches between forward and reversing. Each change is one step: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes it. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E deletes the selected swept path (the line stays).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe outline and the wheel paths go to the PDF, DXF and DWG on layer C-ROAD-SWPT.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-it-is-worked-out\"\u003EHow it is worked out\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach unit turns about its rear axle&#039;s line and is dragged by the point ahead of it (the front axle, or the hitch), the low-speed, no-slip model swept path checks use. The swept area is every position&#039;s body outline put together. It does not model speed, tyre slip, the time the steering takes to turn, steering trailers or the clearance under the body.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"A swept path drives a vehicle along a line and shows the ground its body passes over. Use it to check that a truck can make a corner, reach a loading dock or back a trailer into place without leaving the paving. Drive a vehicle along a line Draw the line the vehicle follows, in the direction of travel: a feature line (arcs are fine: press C while drawing it, see Feature lines) or an alignment. Select it and click Swept path… in its Properties. Under Start from, pick a vehicle, then set its sizes from your own design vehicle: width, front overhang, wheelbase, rear overhang, track and Steering lock. Add up to three trailers, each with where it is hitched, its length from the hitch to its axle, its overhangs and its Largest articulation. Choose Driving: forward or reversing. Click Drive it. The vehicles to start from are placeholders, not a standard. Set the sizes from the design vehicle your project uses. Forward and reversing Driving What follows the line What CivilCraft works out Forward The front axle's centre Where the rear axle and every trailer go, the steering angle and the articulation Reversing The last axle's centre (the back of the trailer) Where each unit ahead goes, and the steering the cab needs to put the trailer there Reversing is how a backing move is checked: say where the trailer should go, and CivilCraft says whether the cab can make it. Read the result The swept path is drawn on the plan in orange: its outline and, dashed, the paths of the outermost wheels. Select it (click inside it) to see in Properties: Length driven, the largest Steering angle and Articulation against their limits, and the Swept area. Any problem along the line, with its distance: the steering past the lock, or a trailer turning past its articulation limit (it jackknifes). The vehicle along the line: move the slider to see the vehicle at any point, with its steering and articulation there. Change the line and the swept path follows. Edit the vehicle… changes the sizes; Driving switches between forward and reversing. Each change is one step: Ctrl+Z undoes it. Delete deletes the selected swept path (the line stays). The outline and the wheel paths go to the PDF, DXF and DWG on layer C-ROAD-SWPT. How it is worked out Each unit turns about its rear axle's line and is dragged by the point ahead of it (the front axle, or the hitch), the low-speed, no-slip model swept path checks use. The swept area is every position's body outline put together. It does not model speed, tyre slip, the time the steering takes to turn, steering trailers or the clearance under the body."},{"slug":"feature-lines","scope":"civilcraft","title":"Feature lines","summary":"Draw 3D lines you grade vertex by vertex, make stepped offsets of them, and use them as breaklines of the ground or as sloped pads in the design.","section":"tasks","order":65,"keywords":["arc","curve","radius","3d arc","helix","curved curb","grade overlay","elevation overlay","grade arrows","feature line","grade","slope","elevation","stepped offset","offset","breakline","curb","ditch","berm","sloped pad","daylight","grading","3d polyline"],"headings":[{"id":"draw-one","text":"Draw one","level":2},{"id":"arcs","text":"Arcs","level":2},{"id":"set-elevations-and-grades","text":"Set elevations and grades","level":2},{"id":"stepped-offsets","text":"Stepped offsets","level":2},{"id":"grades-and-elevations-on-the-screen","text":"Grades and elevations on the screen","level":2},{"id":"in-the-profile-view","text":"In the profile view","level":2},{"id":"what-the-surfaces-do-with-it","text":"What the surfaces do with it","level":2},{"id":"arcs-in-other-files","text":"Arcs in other files","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA feature line is a line with an elevation at every vertex: a curb, a ditch flow line, the top of a berm, the edge of a sloped pad. You set its elevations by number or by grade, copy it sideways with a step up or down, and choose what the surfaces do with it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-one\"\u003EDraw one\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cstrong\u003ESurface\u003C/strong\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFeature line\u003C/span\u003E, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"L\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick its vertices on the plan. A click on a survey point takes the point&#039;s place and elevation; anywhere else the vertex takes the existing ground&#039;s elevation there.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to finish an open line, or click the first vertex again to close it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe new line is selected and starts as a breakline of the existing ground, lying on the ground.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"arcs\"\u003EArcs\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA segment can be an arc in plan whose ends sit at different elevations: a curb return, a curved ditch, a ramp that turns. Its grade runs evenly along the arc, and its length is measured along the curve.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWhile drawing:\u003C/strong\u003E press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"C\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (on a touch screen, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArc\u003C/span\u003E in the bar over the plan) to turn arcs on. After a vertex, click a point on the curve, then click where the arc ends. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"C\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again for straight segments. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E drops the point on the curve first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAfterwards:\u003C/strong\u003E in \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E, type a radius and the way it turns in the vertex&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003EArc\u003C/strong\u003E box, for example \u003Ccode\u003E50 L\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E50 R\u003C/code\u003E (left or right as you walk the line), and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Empty the box to make the segment straight again. A radius smaller than half the segment is refused.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe selected line shows each arc&#039;s radius next to its grade. \u003Cstrong\u003ELength\u003C/strong\u003E is along the arcs in plan; \u003Cstrong\u003E3D length\u003C/strong\u003E also counts the rise and fall. Stepped offsets keep the arcs: an arc on the outside of a bend grows, one on the inside shrinks, and an offset past an arc&#039;s centre is refused.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe surfaces, the 3D view and DXF and DWG take each arc as short straight pieces that stay within 0.01 of the curve. LandXML keeps them as arcs (see \u003Cstrong\u003EArcs in other files\u003C/strong\u003E below).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"set-elevations-and-grades\"\u003ESet elevations and grades\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E lists the vertices with their distance along the line, their \u003Cstrong\u003EElevation\u003C/strong\u003E and the \u003Cstrong\u003EGrade to next\u003C/strong\u003E (in percent). Type either and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAn elevation moves only that vertex.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA grade sets the elevation at the far end of its segment; the vertices after it stay where they are, so the next segment&#039;s grade changes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAlso in \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EAction\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERaise or lower all by\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdds the height you type to every vertex.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElevations from the ground\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPuts every vertex back on the existing ground (vertices off the surface keep theirs, and are listed).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOne grade, first to last\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EKeeps the first and last elevations and puts the vertices between on one even grade.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe selected line shows its elevations at the vertices and its grades along the segments on the plan. Drag a vertex with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E to move it; its elevation stays.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"stepped-offsets\"\u003EStepped offsets\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder \u003Cstrong\u003EStepped offset\u003C/strong\u003E, type a \u003Cstrong\u003EDistance\u003C/strong\u003E (positive to the right of the line&#039;s direction, negative to the left), then a height (up or down by) or a slope in percent, and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake the offset line\u003C/span\u003E. The copy is a new feature line: for example the back of a curb 0.5 ft to the side and 0.5 ft higher, or a ditch edge 6 ft out at 2 %. A distance that would fold the copy over itself at a tight bend is refused.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"grades-and-elevations-on-the-screen\"\u003EGrades and elevations on the screen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"E\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or tick \u003Cstrong\u003EGrades and elevations on feature lines\u003C/strong\u003E under Display, to see every feature line&#039;s grade along each segment (with a small arrow pointing downhill) and its elevation at the vertices. They show on the screen only: nothing is added to the drawing, and the PDF, DXF and DWG stay as they are. Short segments and crowded vertices are left unlabelled until you zoom in. The selected line always shows its own grades and elevations.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"in-the-profile-view\"\u003EIn the profile view\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA feature line that crosses an alignment shows in that alignment&#039;s profile view, with its name and elevation at the crossing; lines running beside it can be shown as well. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/profile\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/profile\"\u003EDesign a vertical profile\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-the-surfaces-do-with-it\"\u003EWhat the surfaces do with it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cstrong\u003EUse\u003C/strong\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EUse\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat happens\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBreakline of the existing ground\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe existing ground runs through the line at its elevations, like a drawn breakline.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGraded into the design\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Eclosed\u003C/strong\u003E line becomes a sloped pad: the proposed surface inside it is graded to its edge. Turn on \u003Cstrong\u003EDaylight to the ground\u003C/strong\u003E to run cut and fill slopes (horizontal to 1 vertical) from its edge to the ground, as a pad does; each slope starts at the edge&#039;s own elevation there.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOnly drawn\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIt stays on the plan and goes out in DXF, DWG and PDF (layer C-GRAD-FEAT, 3D polylines), but the surfaces ignore it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EVolumes, sections and the 3D view include feature lines graded into the design, like pads. Each change is one step: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"arcs-in-other-files\"\u003EArcs in other files\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFile\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat happens to a feature line with arcs\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECivilCraft project\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EKept exactly.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELandXML\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWritten as a plan feature of lines and arcs with their elevations, and read back with its arcs. A feature line from another program comes in as \u003Cstrong\u003EOnly drawn\u003C/strong\u003E: choose its \u003Cstrong\u003EUse\u003C/strong\u003E to put it in a surface.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDWG and DXF\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA 3D polyline can&#039;t hold an arc, so the line is written as short pieces within 0.01 of each arc. CivilCraft also notes in the drawing which pieces were arcs: when you open the drawing as linework, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFeature lines\u003C/span\u003E brings the lines back as feature lines with their arcs. Other programs see an ordinary 3D polyline.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E","text":"A feature line is a line with an elevation at every vertex: a curb, a ditch flow line, the top of a berm, the edge of a sloped pad. You set its elevations by number or by grade, copy it sideways with a step up or down, and choose what the surfaces do with it. Draw one Choose Surface › More › Feature line, or press L. Click its vertices on the plan. A click on a survey point takes the point's place and elevation; anywhere else the vertex takes the existing ground's elevation there. Press Enter to finish an open line, or click the first vertex again to close it. The new line is selected and starts as a breakline of the existing ground, lying on the ground. Arcs A segment can be an arc in plan whose ends sit at different elevations: a curb return, a curved ditch, a ramp that turns. Its grade runs evenly along the arc, and its length is measured along the curve. While drawing: press C (on a touch screen, Arc in the bar over the plan) to turn arcs on. After a vertex, click a point on the curve, then click where the arc ends. Press C again for straight segments. Backspace drops the point on the curve first. Afterwards: in Properties, type a radius and the way it turns in the vertex's Arc box, for example 50 L or 50 R (left or right as you walk the line), and press Enter. Empty the box to make the segment straight again. A radius smaller than half the segment is refused. The selected line shows each arc's radius next to its grade. Length is along the arcs in plan; 3D length also counts the rise and fall. Stepped offsets keep the arcs: an arc on the outside of a bend grows, one on the inside shrinks, and an offset past an arc's centre is refused. The surfaces, the 3D view and DXF and DWG take each arc as short straight pieces that stay within 0.01 of the curve. LandXML keeps them as arcs (see Arcs in other files below). Set elevations and grades Properties lists the vertices with their distance along the line, their Elevation and the Grade to next (in percent). Type either and press Enter: An elevation moves only that vertex. A grade sets the elevation at the far end of its segment; the vertices after it stay where they are, so the next segment's grade changes. Also in Properties: Action What it does Raise or lower all by Adds the height you type to every vertex. Elevations from the ground Puts every vertex back on the existing ground (vertices off the surface keep theirs, and are listed). One grade, first to last Keeps the first and last elevations and puts the vertices between on one even grade. The selected line shows its elevations at the vertices and its grades along the segments on the plan. Drag a vertex with Select to move it; its elevation stays. Stepped offsets Under Stepped offset, type a Distance (positive to the right of the line's direction, negative to the left), then a height (up or down by) or a slope in percent, and click Make the offset line. The copy is a new feature line: for example the back of a curb 0.5 ft to the side and 0.5 ft higher, or a ditch edge 6 ft out at 2 %. A distance that would fold the copy over itself at a tight bend is refused. Grades and elevations on the screen Press E, or tick Grades and elevations on feature lines under Display, to see every feature line's grade along each segment (with a small arrow pointing downhill) and its elevation at the vertices. They show on the screen only: nothing is added to the drawing, and the PDF, DXF and DWG stay as they are. Short segments and crowded vertices are left unlabelled until you zoom in. The selected line always shows its own grades and elevations. In the profile view A feature line that crosses an alignment shows in that alignment's profile view, with its name and elevation at the crossing; lines running beside it can be shown as well. See Design a vertical profile. What the surfaces do with it Choose Use: Use What happens Breakline of the existing ground The existing ground runs through the line at its elevations, like a drawn breakline. Graded into the design A closed line becomes a sloped pad: the proposed surface inside it is graded to its edge. Turn on Daylight to the ground to run cut and fill slopes (horizontal to 1 vertical) from its edge to the ground, as a pad does; each slope starts at the edge's own elevation there. Only drawn It stays on the plan and goes out in DXF, DWG and PDF (layer C-GRAD-FEAT, 3D polylines), but the surfaces ignore it. Volumes, sections and the 3D view include feature lines graded into the design, like pads. Each change is one step: Ctrl+Z undoes it. Arcs in other files File What happens to a feature line with arcs CivilCraft project Kept exactly. LandXML Written as a plan feature of lines and arcs with their elevations, and read back with its arcs. A feature line from another program comes in as Only drawn: choose its Use to put it in a surface. DWG and DXF A 3D polyline can't hold an arc, so the line is written as short pieces within 0.01 of each arc. CivilCraft also notes in the drawing which pieces were arcs: when you open the drawing as linework, Feature lines brings the lines back as feature lines with their arcs. Other programs see an ordinary 3D polyline."},{"slug":"grading","scope":"civilcraft","title":"Grade a building pad","summary":"Draw a flat pad, set its elevation and cut and fill slopes, and let CivilCraft daylight it to the existing ground and add it to the proposed surface.","section":"tasks","order":70,"keywords":["grading","pad","building pad","daylight","feature line","grading group","cut slope","fill slope","finished grade","proposed surface","site grading"],"headings":[{"id":"draw-the-pad","text":"Draw the pad","level":2},{"id":"set-elevation-and-slopes","text":"Set elevation and slopes","level":2},{"id":"how-the-daylight-is-found","text":"How the daylight is found","level":2},{"id":"move-corners","text":"Move corners","level":2},{"id":"when-it-does-not-grade","text":"When it does not grade","level":2},{"id":"see-the-result","text":"See the result","level":2},{"id":"delete-a-pad","text":"Delete a pad","level":2},{"id":"not-built-yet","text":"Not built yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA pad is a flat area at one elevation. From its edge, CivilCraft grades outward at your cut slope where the ground is higher than the pad and at your fill slope where it is lower, until the slope meets the existing ground. That meeting line is the daylight line. The pad and its slopes replace the existing ground inside the daylight line to make the proposed surface.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-the-pad\"\u003EDraw the pad\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou need an existing ground surface first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"G\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPad\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cstrong\u003EDesign\u003C/strong\u003E group. The hint says &quot;Pad: click its corners. Enter or double-click finishes, Backspace drops the last, Esc cancels.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the corners of the pad in order, either way round. A blue outline follows the pointer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E after the last corner (at least three). The pad is drawn and selected. It starts at the ground elevation at its middle, rounded to 0.1, with a 2 : 1 cut slope and a 3 : 1 fill slope. The status bar says, for example, &quot;Pad 1 at elevation 254.3 (the ground at its middle), cut 2:1, fill 3:1. Change them in Properties.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFinish with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Double-clicking also finishes, but in this build it drops the corner you double-clicked. Click the last corner once, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe pad outline must not cross itself (&quot;The pad outline crosses itself.&quot;).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"set-elevation-and-slopes\"\u003ESet elevation and slopes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith the pad selected, the right panel shows \u003Cstrong\u003EPad\u003C/strong\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EName\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EElevation\u003C/strong\u003E: the pad&#039;s flat elevation.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECut slope\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EFill slope\u003C/strong\u003E, as H:V (2 means 2 : 1). Both must be more than 0.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPad area\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003ECorners\u003C/strong\u003E are shown for reference.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E after each value. The daylight line, the proposed surface and the volumes update at once.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder the fields, a line reads for example &quot;Daylight line: 48 slope lines (20 in cut, 28 in fill), farthest 31.20 ft out.&quot; The plan shows the daylight line and the slope lines in magenta.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-the-daylight-is-found\"\u003EHow the daylight is found\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESlope lines leave each edge square to it, about every twentieth of the pad&#039;s size.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt outside corners they fan out every 10°, so the slope wraps round the corner.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt inside corners one line runs along the bisector, where the slopes from the two edges meet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach line climbs at the cut slope or falls at the fill slope until it reaches the ground.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"move-corners\"\u003EMove corners\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect the pad, then drag a square grip at a corner. Each drag is one undo step. Adding or removing a corner is not available yet: delete the pad and draw it again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-it-does-not-grade\"\u003EWhen it does not grade\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA red box under \u003Cstrong\u003EEarthwork\u003C/strong\u003E names the pad and the problem:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMessage\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat to do\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;3 slope lines run off the surface before meeting it: the pad is too close to the surface&#039;s edge or too high/low.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMove the pad in from the edge of the surface, or bring its elevation nearer the ground.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;The daylight line crosses itself (slopes from different sides of the pad run into each other). Reshape the pad or change the slopes.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETypical for narrow or L-shaped pads in deep cut or high fill. Widen the pad, flatten the difference, or steepen the slopes.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;The pad outline crosses itself.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERedraw the corners in order around the pad.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA pad with a problem is left out of the proposed surface and the volumes until it is fixed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"see-the-result\"\u003ESee the result\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cstrong\u003EDisplay\u003C/strong\u003E, set \u003Cstrong\u003ESurface\u003C/strong\u003E to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProposed (graded)\u003C/span\u003E. The contours now run flat across the pad and follow the slopes out to the daylight line.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the pointer over the pad: the status bar shows \u003Cstrong\u003EGround\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EGraded\u003C/strong\u003E elevations.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERead cut and fill under \u003Cstrong\u003EEarthwork\u003C/strong\u003E; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/volumes\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/volumes\"\u003EEarthwork volumes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPads are graded after corridors, in the order they were drawn. A later pad replaces anything earlier inside its own daylight line.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"delete-a-pad\"\u003EDelete a pad\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect it and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (on a Mac, Fn and delete), or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPads are flat: sloped pads, feature lines with varying grade, benches, and grading to a distance or elevation target are not available yet. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"A pad is a flat area at one elevation. From its edge, CivilCraft grades outward at your cut slope where the ground is higher than the pad and at your fill slope where it is lower, until the slope meets the existing ground. That meeting line is the daylight line. The pad and its slopes replace the existing ground inside the daylight line to make the proposed surface. Draw the pad You need an existing ground surface first. Press G, or click Pad in the Design group. The hint says \"Pad: click its corners. Enter or double-click finishes, Backspace drops the last, Esc cancels.\" Click the corners of the pad in order, either way round. A blue outline follows the pointer. Press Enter after the last corner (at least three). The pad is drawn and selected. It starts at the ground elevation at its middle, rounded to 0.1, with a 2 : 1 cut slope and a 3 : 1 fill slope. The status bar says, for example, \"Pad 1 at elevation 254.3 (the ground at its middle), cut 2:1, fill 3:1. Change them in Properties.\" Finish with Enter. Double-clicking also finishes, but in this build it drops the corner you double-clicked. Click the last corner once, then press Enter. The pad outline must not cross itself (\"The pad outline crosses itself.\"). Set elevation and slopes With the pad selected, the right panel shows Pad: Name. Elevation: the pad's flat elevation. Cut slope and Fill slope, as H:V (2 means 2 : 1). Both must be more than 0. Pad area and Corners are shown for reference. Press Enter after each value. The daylight line, the proposed surface and the volumes update at once. Under the fields, a line reads for example \"Daylight line: 48 slope lines (20 in cut, 28 in fill), farthest 31.20 ft out.\" The plan shows the daylight line and the slope lines in magenta. How the daylight is found Slope lines leave each edge square to it, about every twentieth of the pad's size. At outside corners they fan out every 10°, so the slope wraps round the corner. At inside corners one line runs along the bisector, where the slopes from the two edges meet. Each line climbs at the cut slope or falls at the fill slope until it reaches the ground. Move corners Select the pad, then drag a square grip at a corner. Each drag is one undo step. Adding or removing a corner is not available yet: delete the pad and draw it again. When it does not grade A red box under Earthwork names the pad and the problem: Message What to do \"3 slope lines run off the surface before meeting it: the pad is too close to the surface's edge or too high/low.\" Move the pad in from the edge of the surface, or bring its elevation nearer the ground. \"The daylight line crosses itself (slopes from different sides of the pad run into each other). Reshape the pad or change the slopes.\" Typical for narrow or L-shaped pads in deep cut or high fill. Widen the pad, flatten the difference, or steepen the slopes. \"The pad outline crosses itself.\" Redraw the corners in order around the pad. A pad with a problem is left out of the proposed surface and the volumes until it is fixed. See the result Under Display, set Surface to Proposed (graded). The contours now run flat across the pad and follow the slopes out to the daylight line. Move the pointer over the pad: the status bar shows Ground and Graded elevations. Read cut and fill under Earthwork; see Earthwork volumes. Pads are graded after corridors, in the order they were drawn. A later pad replaces anything earlier inside its own daylight line. Delete a pad Select it and press Delete (on a Mac, Fn and delete), or click Delete. Not built yet Pads are flat: sloped pads, feature lines with varying grade, benches, and grading to a distance or elevation target are not available yet. See Not built yet."},{"slug":"volumes","scope":"civilcraft","title":"Earthwork volumes","summary":"Read cut, fill and net volumes between the existing ground and the proposed surface, per pad and corridor and in total, and understand the grid method behind them.","section":"tasks","order":80,"keywords":["volumes","earthwork","cut","fill","net","cut and fill","quantities","grid method","surface comparison","volume surface","cubic yards","cubic meters"],"headings":[{"id":"where-to-read-them","text":"Where to read them","level":2},{"id":"send-them-to-takeoffcraft","text":"Send them to TakeoffCraft","level":2},{"id":"how-they-are-worked-out","text":"How they are worked out","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2},{"id":"checking-a-result","text":"Checking a result","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EAs soon as a pad or a corridor grades without problems, CivilCraft compares the proposed surface with the existing ground and reports cut and fill. The numbers update with every change.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-to-read-them\"\u003EWhere to read them\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEarthwork\u003C/strong\u003E in the right panel with nothing selected (the \u003Cstrong\u003EProject\u003C/strong\u003E view), when there is at least one pad or one corridor.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEarthwork\u003C/strong\u003E in the right panel when a pad is selected (the same totals for the whole project).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe line under a corridor&#039;s settings, with that corridor&#039;s own cut and fill.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cstrong\u003EEarthwork\u003C/strong\u003E block shows:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECut\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EFill\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003ENet\u003C/strong\u003E (with &quot;fill&quot; or &quot;cut&quot; after it, whichever is larger)\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ea line per item when there is more than one, for example &quot;Pad 1: cut 120.4 cu yd, fill 310.2 cu yd&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe method line: &quot;Grid method: existing ground and the graded surface sampled every 0.5 ft inside the daylight lines (92,891 cells). No swell or shrink applied.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EVolumes are in cubic yards in a feet project and cubic metres in a metre project.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"send-them-to-takeoffcraft\"\u003ESend them to TakeoffCraft\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo price the earthwork, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESend to TakeoffCraft\u003C/span\u003E at the foot of the \u003Cstrong\u003EEarthwork\u003C/strong\u003E block. CivilCraft saves a quantities file (\u003Ccode\u003E&lt;site&gt;.quantities.json\u003C/code\u003E) with two quantities: cut and fill, in cubic yards (feet project) or cubic metres (metre project), the same numbers as the block, to the hundredth. Open it in TakeoffCraft with \u003Cstrong\u003EOpen takeoff\u003C/strong\u003E, or drop it there: they become two items you can price (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/takeoffcraft/earthwork#cut-and-fill-from-civilcraft\" data-topic=\"takeoffcraft/earthwork\" data-hash=\"cut-and-fill-from-civilcraft\"\u003ECut and fill from CivilCraft\u003C/a\u003E). Send again after a change and open the new file: the same items take the new numbers.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOnly the totals are sent, so nothing is counted twice; the file&#039;s note lists each pad&#039;s and corridor&#039;s own cut and fill.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-they-are-worked-out\"\u003EHow they are worked out\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECivilCraft uses the \u003Cstrong\u003Egrid method\u003C/strong\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA grid of square cells is laid over the area inside every daylight line.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt the center of each cell, both surfaces are sampled.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach cell counts as a prism: the difference in elevation times the cell&#039;s area. Where the proposed surface is lower, it is cut; higher, it is fill.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe cells are added up.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe cell size is chosen for you so that the rectangle around the graded areas holds about 250,000 cells, rounded to a tidy value (1, 2 or 5 times a power of ten). You cannot set it yourself yet. The method line tells you the size and the number of cells. Smaller cells are more accurate along the edges; on whole cells between flat planes the result is exact.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOnly the area inside daylight lines is counted. Ground outside them is unchanged.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA pad or corridor with a problem is not graded in and adds nothing. If the block says &quot;No volumes yet: a pad needs to grade without problems.&quot;, look for a red box above it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhere items overlap, the later one has replaced the earlier inside its daylight line, and the overlap is counted once.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENo swell or shrink factor is applied. Multiply the results yourself if you need bank, loose or compacted volumes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETopsoil stripping, pavement and subgrade depths, and TIN-to-TIN prism volumes are not available yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"checking-a-result\"\u003EChecking a result\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETurn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProposed (graded)\u003C/span\u003E on under \u003Cstrong\u003EDisplay\u003C/strong\u003E and look at the contours inside the daylight lines.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the pointer over the graded area: the status bar shows \u003Cstrong\u003EGround\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EGraded\u003C/strong\u003E. The difference is the cut or fill depth at that spot.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFor a corridor, the profile view&#039;s readout shows cut or fill on the centerline at every station.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"As soon as a pad or a corridor grades without problems, CivilCraft compares the proposed surface with the existing ground and reports cut and fill. The numbers update with every change. Where to read them Earthwork in the right panel with nothing selected (the Project view), when there is at least one pad or one corridor. Earthwork in the right panel when a pad is selected (the same totals for the whole project). The line under a corridor's settings, with that corridor's own cut and fill. The Earthwork block shows: Cut, Fill and Net (with \"fill\" or \"cut\" after it, whichever is larger) a line per item when there is more than one, for example \"Pad 1: cut 120.4 cu yd, fill 310.2 cu yd\" the method line: \"Grid method: existing ground and the graded surface sampled every 0.5 ft inside the daylight lines (92,891 cells). No swell or shrink applied.\" Volumes are in cubic yards in a feet project and cubic metres in a metre project. Send them to TakeoffCraft To price the earthwork, choose Send to TakeoffCraft at the foot of the Earthwork block. CivilCraft saves a quantities file (\u003Csite\u003E.quantities.json) with two quantities: cut and fill, in cubic yards (feet project) or cubic metres (metre project), the same numbers as the block, to the hundredth. Open it in TakeoffCraft with Open takeoff, or drop it there: they become two items you can price (Cut and fill from CivilCraft). Send again after a change and open the new file: the same items take the new numbers. Only the totals are sent, so nothing is counted twice; the file's note lists each pad's and corridor's own cut and fill. How they are worked out CivilCraft uses the grid method: A grid of square cells is laid over the area inside every daylight line. At the center of each cell, both surfaces are sampled. Each cell counts as a prism: the difference in elevation times the cell's area. Where the proposed surface is lower, it is cut; higher, it is fill. The cells are added up. The cell size is chosen for you so that the rectangle around the graded areas holds about 250,000 cells, rounded to a tidy value (1, 2 or 5 times a power of ten). You cannot set it yourself yet. The method line tells you the size and the number of cells. Smaller cells are more accurate along the edges; on whole cells between flat planes the result is exact. Things to know Only the area inside daylight lines is counted. Ground outside them is unchanged. A pad or corridor with a problem is not graded in and adds nothing. If the block says \"No volumes yet: a pad needs to grade without problems.\", look for a red box above it. Where items overlap, the later one has replaced the earlier inside its daylight line, and the overlap is counted once. No swell or shrink factor is applied. Multiply the results yourself if you need bank, loose or compacted volumes. Topsoil stripping, pavement and subgrade depths, and TIN-to-TIN prism volumes are not available yet. Checking a result Turn Proposed (graded) on under Display and look at the contours inside the daylight lines. Move the pointer over the graded area: the status bar shows Ground and Graded. The difference is the cut or fill depth at that spot. For a corridor, the profile view's readout shows cut or fill on the centerline at every station."},{"slug":"pipe-network","scope":"civilcraft","title":"Lay out a gravity pipe network","summary":"Draw storm or sanitary pipe runs structure by structure, set your slope, cover and drop rules, let CivilCraft set inverts, and fix the problems it lists.","section":"tasks","order":90,"keywords":["structure builder","structure type","rectangular structure","catch basin","frame","cover","wall thickness","library item","pipe network","storm","sanitary","sewer","structures","manholes","inlets","inverts","cover","slope","drop","rim","sump","gravity pipes"],"headings":[{"id":"draw-a-run","text":"Draw a run","level":2},{"id":"set-your-rules","text":"Set your rules","level":2},{"id":"let-civilcraft-set-inverts","text":"Let CivilCraft set inverts","level":2},{"id":"edit-structures-and-pipes","text":"Edit structures and pipes","level":2},{"id":"read-and-fix-problems","text":"Read and fix problems","level":2},{"id":"structure-types","text":"Structure types","level":2},{"id":"share-a-type-through-the-library","text":"Share a type through the library","level":3},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete","level":2},{"id":"not-built-yet","text":"Not built yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA project holds one gravity pipe network, storm or sanitary. It is made of structures (manholes, inlets) and straight pipes between them. Flow runs in the direction you draw. CivilCraft checks every pipe and structure against three rules you set, and can set every invert from those rules.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-a-run\"\u003EDraw a run\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou can draw pipes without a surface, but rims, cover and automatic inverts need one.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPipes\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cstrong\u003EDesign\u003C/strong\u003E group. The hint says &quot;Pipes: click structures from the top of the run down (click an existing one to join it). Enter finishes.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick where the upstream structure goes. A structure is placed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick each next structure downstream. To connect to a structure that already exists, click on it: the status bar says &quot;Joined S3.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The pipes are drawn, their inverts are set from the rules, and the status bar says, for example, &quot;3 pipes drawn, 12&quot;, inverts set from the rules; every check passes.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENew structures are named S1, S2 … with a 48 in (1200 mm) diameter. New pipes are named P1, P2 … with a 12 in (300 mm) diameter. Diameters are in inches in a feet project and millimetres in a metre project.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFinish with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Double-clicking also works, but when you double-click on an existing structure to end the run there, the join is dropped: click it once, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"set-your-rules\"\u003ESet your rules\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith nothing selected, the right panel (\u003Cstrong\u003EProject\u003C/strong\u003E) has a \u003Cstrong\u003EPipe network\u003C/strong\u003E block:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESetting\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EStarting value\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeaning\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStorm\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStorm\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESanitary\u003C/span\u003E; written to LandXML\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELeast slope\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0.50%\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEvery pipe must fall at least this much\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELeast cover\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2 ft (0.6 m)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGround to pipe crown, all along the pipe\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrop in structures\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0.10 ft (0.03 m)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe outlet invert must be at least this far below every inlet invert\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThese starting values are placeholders, not a standard.\u003C/strong\u003E Set the values your agency or project requires. Cover is measured from the ground to the crown (invert plus inside diameter); wall thickness is not counted. Where pads or corridors are graded, the graded surface is the ground.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"let-civilcraft-set-inverts\"\u003ELet CivilCraft set inverts\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESet every invert from the rules\u003C/span\u003E (in the \u003Cstrong\u003EPipe network\u003C/strong\u003E block) sets all pipes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESet its inverts from the rules\u003C/span\u003E (with one pipe selected) sets that pipe only.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWorking downstream, each run starts at least cover at its top structure, falls at least the least slope, drops at least the drop through each structure, and goes deeper wherever the ground along the pipe would leave less than the least cover. Inverts are rounded down to 0.01, never up. The status bar says &quot;Inverts set: least cover at the top of each run, at least the least slope, at least the drop in each structure.&quot; or names what it could not do.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"edit-structures-and-pipes\"\u003EEdit structures and pipes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick a structure or pipe with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E to see it in the right panel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EStructure\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cstrong\u003EName\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ERim\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ESump depth\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EDiameter\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003ERim used\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ESump\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EEasting\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003ENorthing\u003C/strong\u003E, and the pipes in and out with their inverts.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELeave \u003Cstrong\u003ERim\u003C/strong\u003E empty to take it from the surface; the box then shows the surface value in gray. Type a value to fix the rim.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag the structure&#039;s grip on the plan to move it. Its pipes follow; their inverts stay as they were, so check the problems afterwards.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EType\u003C/strong\u003E: choose one of the network&#039;s structure types (below), or none for a round structure of its own diameter.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPipe\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cstrong\u003EName\u003C/strong\u003E, the structures it runs between, \u003Cstrong\u003EDiameter\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EUpper invert\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ELower invert\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003ELength\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ESlope\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003ELeast cover\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E after each value.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"read-and-fix-problems\"\u003ERead and fix problems\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cstrong\u003EPipe network\u003C/strong\u003E block lists every problem (up to 40); click one to select the pipe or structure. A selected pipe or structure lists its own. With none, it says &quot;Every pipe and structure passes the rules.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMessage (examples)\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat to do\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;P2 runs uphill (-0.40%).&quot; or &quot;… runs flat …&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESwap the inverts, or set them from the rules.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;P2 at 0.30% is flatter than the minimum 0.50%.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELower the downstream invert or raise the upstream one.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;P3 has 1.40 of cover 35 along it, less than the minimum 2.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELower the pipe; set its inverts from the rules.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;In S3, P4 leaves at 245.20, less than 0.1 below P3 coming in at 245.25.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELower the outlet pipe&#039;s upper invert.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;P1&#039;s crown is above S1&#039;s rim.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe pipe is too high for the structure.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;S2 has 2 pipes leaving it; a gravity network should have one way out of each structure.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERemove one outgoing pipe, or redraw it in the right direction.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;The pipes form a loop; flow has to end at an outlet.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA run was drawn back into itself; delete a pipe.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;S5 is off the surface and has no rim elevation.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType a rim, or move the structure onto the surface.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;P6 runs partly off the surface; cover there is unknown.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExtend the surface, or accept that cover is not checked there.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;P7 has no inverts yet.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType them, or set them from the rules.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EProblem pipes and structures are also marked in red on the plan.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"structure-types\"\u003EStructure types\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA structure type is a structure built from its parameters: a manhole, inlet, junction box or outlet, \u003Cstrong\u003Eround\u003C/strong\u003E (inside diameter) or \u003Cstrong\u003Erectangular\u003C/strong\u003E (inside width and length), with its \u003Cstrong\u003Ewall thickness\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003Efloor slab\u003C/strong\u003E, a \u003Cstrong\u003Esump\u003C/strong\u003E below the lowest pipe and a \u003Cstrong\u003Eframe and cover\u003C/strong\u003E (round or rectangular opening, frame height). Types belong to the network, under \u003Cstrong\u003EStructure types\u003C/strong\u003E in the project&#039;s Properties.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew structure type\u003C/span\u003E. The dialog draws the structure in plan and in section as you type.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd the type\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect a structure and choose the type under \u003Cstrong\u003EType\u003C/strong\u003E. It takes the type&#039;s size and sump; its own sump can still be changed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEdit a type and every structure of that type follows its sizes. Remove a type and its structures stay as round structures of the same width. Each change is one step: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA rectangular structure is drawn on the plan, in DXF, DWG and PDF, and in 3D as a rectangle with its length along its outlet pipe; pipes start at its wall. LandXML writes it as a rectangular structure (\u003Ccode\u003ERectStruct\u003C/code\u003E) with its wall thickness, and reads such structures back as types.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"share-a-type-through-the-library\"\u003EShare a type through the library\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as library item\u003C/span\u003E writes the type as a CadCraft library item (a \u003Ccode\u003E.json\u003C/code\u003E file): its plan symbol, a 3D body to a typical depth, and every size. Upload it to the library to share it. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen a library item\u003C/span\u003E reads a structure saved this way back into the network, converted to the project&#039;s units. Library items that are not structures made in CivilCraft are refused, with the reason.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect a pipe and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to remove it. Deleting a structure removes the pipes connected to it as well. On a Mac, press Fn and delete, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFlows, pipe sizes and the hydraulic grade line are in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/storm-drainage\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/storm-drainage\"\u003EStorm drainage\u003C/a\u003E. Pipe catalogs, non-round pipes, pipes in the profile view, curved pipes and more than one network per project are not available yet. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"A project holds one gravity pipe network, storm or sanitary. It is made of structures (manholes, inlets) and straight pipes between them. Flow runs in the direction you draw. CivilCraft checks every pipe and structure against three rules you set, and can set every invert from those rules. Draw a run You can draw pipes without a surface, but rims, cover and automatic inverts need one. Press P, or click Pipes in the Design group. The hint says \"Pipes: click structures from the top of the run down (click an existing one to join it). Enter finishes.\" Click where the upstream structure goes. A structure is placed. Click each next structure downstream. To connect to a structure that already exists, click on it: the status bar says \"Joined S3.\" Press Enter. The pipes are drawn, their inverts are set from the rules, and the status bar says, for example, \"3 pipes drawn, 12\", inverts set from the rules; every check passes.\" New structures are named S1, S2 … with a 48 in (1200 mm) diameter. New pipes are named P1, P2 … with a 12 in (300 mm) diameter. Diameters are in inches in a feet project and millimetres in a metre project. Finish with Enter. Double-clicking also works, but when you double-click on an existing structure to end the run there, the join is dropped: click it once, then press Enter. Set your rules With nothing selected, the right panel (Project) has a Pipe network block: Setting Starting value Meaning Type Storm Storm or Sanitary; written to LandXML Least slope 0.50% Every pipe must fall at least this much Least cover 2 ft (0.6 m) Ground to pipe crown, all along the pipe Drop in structures 0.10 ft (0.03 m) The outlet invert must be at least this far below every inlet invert These starting values are placeholders, not a standard. Set the values your agency or project requires. Cover is measured from the ground to the crown (invert plus inside diameter); wall thickness is not counted. Where pads or corridors are graded, the graded surface is the ground. Let CivilCraft set inverts Set every invert from the rules (in the Pipe network block) sets all pipes. Set its inverts from the rules (with one pipe selected) sets that pipe only. Working downstream, each run starts at least cover at its top structure, falls at least the least slope, drops at least the drop through each structure, and goes deeper wherever the ground along the pipe would leave less than the least cover. Inverts are rounded down to 0.01, never up. The status bar says \"Inverts set: least cover at the top of each run, at least the least slope, at least the drop in each structure.\" or names what it could not do. Edit structures and pipes Click a structure or pipe with Select to see it in the right panel. Structure: Name, Rim, Sump depth, Diameter, then Rim used, Sump, Easting and Northing, and the pipes in and out with their inverts. Leave Rim empty to take it from the surface; the box then shows the surface value in gray. Type a value to fix the rim. Drag the structure's grip on the plan to move it. Its pipes follow; their inverts stay as they were, so check the problems afterwards. Type: choose one of the network's structure types (below), or none for a round structure of its own diameter. Pipe: Name, the structures it runs between, Diameter, Upper invert, Lower invert, then Length, Slope and Least cover. Press Enter after each value. Read and fix problems The Pipe network block lists every problem (up to 40); click one to select the pipe or structure. A selected pipe or structure lists its own. With none, it says \"Every pipe and structure passes the rules.\" Message (examples) What to do \"P2 runs uphill (-0.40%).\" or \"… runs flat …\" Swap the inverts, or set them from the rules. \"P2 at 0.30% is flatter than the minimum 0.50%.\" Lower the downstream invert or raise the upstream one. \"P3 has 1.40 of cover 35 along it, less than the minimum 2.\" Lower the pipe; set its inverts from the rules. \"In S3, P4 leaves at 245.20, less than 0.1 below P3 coming in at 245.25.\" Lower the outlet pipe's upper invert. \"P1's crown is above S1's rim.\" The pipe is too high for the structure. \"S2 has 2 pipes leaving it; a gravity network should have one way out of each structure.\" Remove one outgoing pipe, or redraw it in the right direction. \"The pipes form a loop; flow has to end at an outlet.\" A run was drawn back into itself; delete a pipe. \"S5 is off the surface and has no rim elevation.\" Type a rim, or move the structure onto the surface. \"P6 runs partly off the surface; cover there is unknown.\" Extend the surface, or accept that cover is not checked there. \"P7 has no inverts yet.\" Type them, or set them from the rules. Problem pipes and structures are also marked in red on the plan. Structure types A structure type is a structure built from its parameters: a manhole, inlet, junction box or outlet, round (inside diameter) or rectangular (inside width and length), with its wall thickness, floor slab, a sump below the lowest pipe and a frame and cover (round or rectangular opening, frame height). Types belong to the network, under Structure types in the project's Properties. Click New structure type. The dialog draws the structure in plan and in section as you type. Click Add the type. Select a structure and choose the type under Type. It takes the type's size and sump; its own sump can still be changed. Edit a type and every structure of that type follows its sizes. Remove a type and its structures stay as round structures of the same width. Each change is one step: Ctrl+Z undoes it. A rectangular structure is drawn on the plan, in DXF, DWG and PDF, and in 3D as a rectangle with its length along its outlet pipe; pipes start at its wall. LandXML writes it as a rectangular structure (RectStruct) with its wall thickness, and reads such structures back as types. Share a type through the library Save as library item writes the type as a CadCraft library item (a .json file): its plan symbol, a 3D body to a typical depth, and every size. Upload it to the library to share it. Open a library item reads a structure saved this way back into the network, converted to the project's units. Library items that are not structures made in CivilCraft are refused, with the reason. Delete Select a pipe and press Delete to remove it. Deleting a structure removes the pipes connected to it as well. On a Mac, press Fn and delete, or click Delete. Not built yet Flows, pipe sizes and the hydraulic grade line are in Storm drainage. Pipe catalogs, non-round pipes, pipes in the profile view, curved pipes and more than one network per project are not available yet. See Not built yet."},{"slug":"storm-drainage","scope":"civilcraft","title":"Work out storm flows, pipe sizes and the hydraulic grade line","summary":"Draw the catchments that drain to each structure, add up the flows down the network with the rational method, size the pipes by Manning's equation, and see where the hydraulic grade line runs full or reaches the surface.","section":"tasks","order":92,"keywords":["storm drainage","catchment","drainage area","rational method","runoff coefficient","time of concentration","rainfall intensity","idf","manning","pipe sizing","hydraulic grade line","hgl","energy grade line","egl","surcharge","flooding","tailwater"],"headings":[{"id":"draw-a-catchment","text":"Draw a catchment","level":2},{"id":"set-the-design-storm","text":"Set the design storm","level":2},{"id":"how-the-flows-add-up","text":"How the flows add up","level":2},{"id":"size-the-pipes","text":"Size the pipes","level":2},{"id":"read-the-grade-lines","text":"Read the grade lines","level":2},{"id":"saved-with-the-project","text":"Saved with the project","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EStorm drainage works on the \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/pipe-network\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/pipe-network\"\u003Epipe network\u003C/a\u003E: draw the network first, then the areas that drain into it. Everything appears under \u003Cstrong\u003EStorm drainage\u003C/strong\u003E in the right panel when nothing is selected.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-a-catchment\"\u003EDraw a catchment\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cstrong\u003EDraw a catchment\u003C/strong\u003E under \u003Cstrong\u003EStorm drainage\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the corners of the area that drains to one structure. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or a double-click finishes; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes back the last corner, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E stops.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe catchment drains to a structure inside it, else the nearest one. Its \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E open: set its name, the \u003Cstrong\u003ERunoff coefficient C\u003C/strong\u003E (the share of the rain that runs off, 0 to 1), the \u003Cstrong\u003ETime of concentration\u003C/strong\u003E in minutes, and which structure it \u003Cstrong\u003EDrains to\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDraw another catchment\u003C/strong\u003E in its Properties starts the next one. Click inside a catchment on the plan to select it; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E removes it. Each change is one step: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"set-the-design-storm\"\u003ESet the design storm\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe rainfall intensity is i = a ÷ (tc + b) to the power c, in inches per hour for a feet project and millimeters per hour for a meter project. Fit \u003Cstrong\u003EIntensity a\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003Eb\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003Ec\u003C/strong\u003E to your own intensity-duration-frequency curve for the return period you design for.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe starting intensity curve, Manning n (0.013) and junction loss (0.5) are placeholders, not a standard. Set them from your own rainfall data and design criteria.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EShortest time of concentration\u003C/strong\u003E: no pipe is worked out at a shorter time (10 minutes to start).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EManning n\u003C/strong\u003E: the roughness of the pipes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EJunction loss\u003C/strong\u003E: the head lost in each structure, as a share of the velocity head V²/2g of the pipe leaving it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWater level at the outlet\u003C/strong\u003E: the tailwater, where the last pipe empties. Leave it empty when the outlet runs free.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-the-flows-add-up\"\u003EHow the flows add up\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach pipe carries every catchment above it. Q = C × i × A, where C × A is summed down the network and i is the intensity at the longest time of concentration of the water arriving: a catchment&#039;s own time, or the time from an upstream catchment plus the travel time through the pipes (length ÷ the velocity flowing full). Q is in cubic feet per second (A in acres) or cubic meters per second (A in hectares).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"size-the-pipes\"\u003ESize the pipes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe table under \u003Cstrong\u003EFlows and grade lines\u003C/strong\u003E shows, for each pipe, the flow, what it carries flowing full at its slope, the velocity, the hydraulic grade at its top and foot, and how deep it flows.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESize the pipes for these flows\u003C/strong\u003E gives each pipe the smallest standard size that carries its flow at its own slope (12 to 72 in, or 300 to 1800 mm). It is one step. The inverts stay where they are: if sizes change, use \u003Cstrong\u003ESet every invert from the rules\u003C/strong\u003E to keep the cover, then check the table again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"read-the-grade-lines\"\u003ERead the grade lines\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe hydraulic grade line (HGL) is worked out from the outlet upstream:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA pipe flowing part full has its HGL at the water surface: the invert plus the normal depth.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhen the water below is higher than its crown, or it carries more than it can flowing full, it flows full (surcharged) and its HGL rises by the friction slope over its length.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt each structure the HGL is the leaving pipe&#039;s top HGL plus the junction loss.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe energy grade line (EGL) is the HGL plus the velocity head.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA pipe that flows full is listed, and so is any structure where the HGL is above the rim: water reaches the surface there, and the structure is ringed in red on the plan. Select a pipe or a structure to see its flow, depth, velocity and both grade lines in its Properties.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"saved-with-the-project\"\u003ESaved with the project\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECatchments and the storm settings are saved in the project file. They are not yet in the PDF plot, the DWG or DXF, or LandXML: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Storm drainage works on the pipe network: draw the network first, then the areas that drain into it. Everything appears under Storm drainage in the right panel when nothing is selected. Draw a catchment Click Draw a catchment under Storm drainage. Click the corners of the area that drains to one structure. Enter or a double-click finishes; Backspace takes back the last corner, Esc stops. The catchment drains to a structure inside it, else the nearest one. Its Properties open: set its name, the Runoff coefficient C (the share of the rain that runs off, 0 to 1), the Time of concentration in minutes, and which structure it Drains to. Draw another catchment in its Properties starts the next one. Click inside a catchment on the plan to select it; Delete removes it. Each change is one step: Ctrl+Z undoes it. Set the design storm The rainfall intensity is i = a ÷ (tc + b) to the power c, in inches per hour for a feet project and millimeters per hour for a meter project. Fit Intensity a, b and c to your own intensity-duration-frequency curve for the return period you design for. The starting intensity curve, Manning n (0.013) and junction loss (0.5) are placeholders, not a standard. Set them from your own rainfall data and design criteria. Shortest time of concentration: no pipe is worked out at a shorter time (10 minutes to start). Manning n: the roughness of the pipes. Junction loss: the head lost in each structure, as a share of the velocity head V²/2g of the pipe leaving it. Water level at the outlet: the tailwater, where the last pipe empties. Leave it empty when the outlet runs free. How the flows add up Each pipe carries every catchment above it. Q = C × i × A, where C × A is summed down the network and i is the intensity at the longest time of concentration of the water arriving: a catchment's own time, or the time from an upstream catchment plus the travel time through the pipes (length ÷ the velocity flowing full). Q is in cubic feet per second (A in acres) or cubic meters per second (A in hectares). Size the pipes The table under Flows and grade lines shows, for each pipe, the flow, what it carries flowing full at its slope, the velocity, the hydraulic grade at its top and foot, and how deep it flows. Size the pipes for these flows gives each pipe the smallest standard size that carries its flow at its own slope (12 to 72 in, or 300 to 1800 mm). It is one step. The inverts stay where they are: if sizes change, use Set every invert from the rules to keep the cover, then check the table again. Read the grade lines The hydraulic grade line (HGL) is worked out from the outlet upstream: A pipe flowing part full has its HGL at the water surface: the invert plus the normal depth. When the water below is higher than its crown, or it carries more than it can flowing full, it flows full (surcharged) and its HGL rises by the friction slope over its length. At each structure the HGL is the leaving pipe's top HGL plus the junction loss. The energy grade line (EGL) is the HGL plus the velocity head. A pipe that flows full is listed, and so is any structure where the HGL is above the rim: water reaches the surface there, and the structure is ringed in red on the plan. Select a pipe or a structure to see its flow, depth, velocity and both grade lines in its Properties. Saved with the project Catchments and the storm settings are saved in the project file. They are not yet in the PDF plot, the DWG or DXF, or LandXML: see Not built yet."},{"slug":"sanitary-design","scope":"civilcraft","title":"Work out sanitary design flows and check the sewer","summary":"Enter the people and area that enter at each manhole of a sanitary network; CivilCraft adds them up down the network, works out the peak flow with a peaking factor and infiltration, and checks each pipe's capacity and velocity.","section":"tasks","order":93,"keywords":["sanitary","sewer","sanitary sewer","wastewater","design flow","peak flow","peaking factor","harmon","babbitt","infiltration","population","per capita","percent full","self-cleansing","velocity","pipe sizing","design sheet"],"headings":[{"id":"enter-what-comes-in","text":"Enter what comes in","level":2},{"id":"the-settings","text":"The settings","level":2},{"id":"what-it-works-out","text":"What it works out","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ESanitary design flows work on the \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/pipe-network\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/pipe-network\"\u003Epipe network\u003C/a\u003E when its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EType\u003C/span\u003E is \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESanitary\u003C/span\u003E. With nothing selected, the right panel then shows \u003Cstrong\u003ESanitary design flows\u003C/strong\u003E where a storm network shows storm drainage.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"enter-what-comes-in\"\u003EEnter what comes in\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect a manhole. Under \u003Cstrong\u003ESanitary design flows\u003C/strong\u003E, type the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPeople entering here\u003C/span\u003E and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea entering here\u003C/span\u003E (hectares in a metre project, acres in a feet project).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFor shops, offices or industry, type equivalent people: the area times the people per hectare (or acre) your agency uses. The area counts for infiltration either way.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERepeat for each manhole where a street or a lot connects. A manhole where nothing connects keeps both at 0.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach pipe carries everything that enters at its upper manhole and above it. Select a manhole to see what passes through it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-settings\"\u003EThe settings\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESetting\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it means\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFlow per person\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethe average flow of one person a day: litres (metre project) or US gallons (feet project)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInfiltration\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Egroundwater that leaks in: litres per second per hectare, or gallons a day per acre\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPeaking factor\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHarmon, 1 + 14 / (4 + √P), or Babbitt, 5 / P^0.2, with P the population in thousands\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPeaking factor at least\u003C/span\u003E / \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPeaking factor at most\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethe factor is held between these two\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EManning n\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Efor capacity and velocity\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELeast velocity\u003C/span\u003E / \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMost velocity\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eat the peak flow&#039;s own depth in the pipe\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMost of full capacity\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ehow full a pipe may run at the peak, as a percent of what it carries flowing full\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe starting values are placeholders, not a standard. Use the values your agency publishes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-works-out\"\u003EWhat it works out\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor each pipe, in flow order:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Epeak flow = people × flow per person × peaking factor + area × infiltration\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ecapacity flowing full (Manning&#039;s equation at the pipe&#039;s slope), and how full the peak makes it\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe normal depth of the peak flow and the velocity there\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cstrong\u003ESanitary results\u003C/strong\u003E table lists each pipe&#039;s people, peaking factor, peak flow (L/s, or gpm and cubic feet per second), percent full and velocity. Problems are listed under it: a pipe fuller than the limit, slower than the least velocity (it may not clean itself), faster than the most, or with no fall. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESize the pipes for these flows\u003C/span\u003E picks for each pipe the smallest standard size (200 mm or 8 inches and up) that carries its peak within the limit; one \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis is the steady peak flow a design sheet works out, checked against a published design sheet to its printed rounding. It is not a model of the sewer over time: no hydrographs, storage, pumps or overflows. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EVelocities use Manning&#039;s equation with the same n at every depth. Some agencies&#039; part-full charts let n change with depth, which gives a slightly lower velocity in a pipe running 60 to 70 percent full (about 3 percent less).\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Sanitary design flows work on the pipe network when its Type is Sanitary. With nothing selected, the right panel then shows Sanitary design flows where a storm network shows storm drainage. Enter what comes in Select a manhole. Under Sanitary design flows, type the People entering here and the Area entering here (hectares in a metre project, acres in a feet project). For shops, offices or industry, type equivalent people: the area times the people per hectare (or acre) your agency uses. The area counts for infiltration either way. Repeat for each manhole where a street or a lot connects. A manhole where nothing connects keeps both at 0. Each pipe carries everything that enters at its upper manhole and above it. Select a manhole to see what passes through it. The settings Setting What it means Flow per person the average flow of one person a day: litres (metre project) or US gallons (feet project) Infiltration groundwater that leaks in: litres per second per hectare, or gallons a day per acre Peaking factor Harmon, 1 + 14 / (4 + √P), or Babbitt, 5 / P^0.2, with P the population in thousands Peaking factor at least / Peaking factor at most the factor is held between these two Manning n for capacity and velocity Least velocity / Most velocity at the peak flow's own depth in the pipe Most of full capacity how full a pipe may run at the peak, as a percent of what it carries flowing full The starting values are placeholders, not a standard. Use the values your agency publishes. What it works out For each pipe, in flow order: peak flow = people × flow per person × peaking factor + area × infiltration capacity flowing full (Manning's equation at the pipe's slope), and how full the peak makes it the normal depth of the peak flow and the velocity there The Sanitary results table lists each pipe's people, peaking factor, peak flow (L/s, or gpm and cubic feet per second), percent full and velocity. Problems are listed under it: a pipe fuller than the limit, slower than the least velocity (it may not clean itself), faster than the most, or with no fall. Size the pipes for these flows picks for each pipe the smallest standard size (200 mm or 8 inches and up) that carries its peak within the limit; one Ctrl+Z undoes it. This is the steady peak flow a design sheet works out, checked against a published design sheet to its printed rounding. It is not a model of the sewer over time: no hydrographs, storage, pumps or overflows. See Not built yet. Velocities use Manning's equation with the same n at every depth. Some agencies' part-full charts let n change with depth, which gives a slightly lower velocity in a pipe running 60 to 70 percent full (about 3 percent less)."},{"slug":"parcels","scope":"civilcraft","title":"Draw parcels and write their legal descriptions","summary":"Draw a parcel by its corners or enter the calls from a deed or plat, check how well the calls close, label bearings and distances on the plan, make any course a curve, and copy a draft legal description.","section":"tasks","order":95,"keywords":["parcels","lots","property lines","boundary","deed","plat","metes and bounds","bearing","distance","closure","misclosure","precision","legal description","point of beginning","curve","radius","area","acres"],"headings":[{"id":"draw-a-parcel","text":"Draw a parcel","level":2},{"id":"enter-the-calls-from-a-deed","text":"Enter the calls from a deed","level":2},{"id":"curves","text":"Curves","level":2},{"id":"the-legal-description","text":"The legal description","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EParcels sit on the plan with their bearings and distances along each course and their name and area in the middle. They go into the PDF plot and the DWG and DXF exports, on the layers \u003Ccode\u003EV-PROP\u003C/code\u003E (lines) and \u003Ccode\u003EV-PROP-TEXT\u003C/code\u003E (labels). Everything is under \u003Cstrong\u003EParcels\u003C/strong\u003E in the right panel when nothing is selected.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-a-parcel\"\u003EDraw a parcel\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cstrong\u003EDraw a parcel\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the corners, starting at the point of beginning. A click near a survey point takes that point&#039;s exact place.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the first corner again, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, to finish. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes back the last corner; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E stops.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"enter-the-calls-from-a-deed\"\u003EEnter the calls from a deed\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cstrong\u003EEnter deed calls\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESay where the calls start: a survey point&#039;s number, or a northing and easting.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType or paste the calls, one a line:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ea line: a bearing and a distance, such as \u003Ccode\u003EN 45°30&#039;15&quot; E 125.50\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003EN45-30-15E\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EN 45 30 15 E\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EN 45.5 E\u003C/code\u003E and azimuths (\u003Ccode\u003EAZ 45°30&#039;15&quot;\u003C/code\u003E) work too, and &quot;thence&quot;, commas and &quot;feet&quot; are ignored.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ea curve: \u003Ccode\u003EC R 300 L 52.36 right\u003C/code\u003E (radius and arc length), or \u003Ccode\u003EC R 300 D 10°00&#039;00&quot; left\u003C/code\u003E (radius and central angle). A curve follows on from the course before it; when it does not, add its chord bearing: \u003Ccode\u003EC R 300 L 52.36 right CB N 50 00 00 E\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E#\u003C/code\u003E starts a comment line.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAs you type, the dialog shows the length of the calls, the \u003Cstrong\u003Emisclosure\u003C/strong\u003E (how far from the start they end, and in which direction), the \u003Cstrong\u003Eprecision\u003C/strong\u003E (the length divided by the misclosure, 1 : 10,000 and so on) and the area. Lines it cannot read are listed with their line numbers.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAdd the parcel\u003C/strong\u003E. When the calls do not close, the parcel closes with a straight course across the gap: check the calls against the deed. To spread a field traverse&#039;s error over its courses, adjust it first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"curves\"\u003ECurves\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn a parcel&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E, each course has a \u003Cstrong\u003ERadius\u003C/strong\u003E. Type one to make that course a curve through the same two corners, and choose which way it curves: a curve to the right has its center to the right of the direction of travel, as in a deed. Clear the radius to make it straight again. Each change is one step: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-legal-description\"\u003EThe legal description\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA parcel&#039;s Properties end with its legal description, written from the drawing:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;Beginning at&quot; the \u003Cstrong\u003EPoint of beginning\u003C/strong\u003E you type (for example &quot;the northeast corner of Lot 4&quot;), else the first corner&#039;s northing and easting, or the survey point the calls started from.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;thence&quot; each course: a bearing and a distance, or a curve with its radius, arc length, central angle, and chord bearing and distance.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;Containing&quot; its area in square feet and acres (square meters and hectares in a meter project).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECopy\u003C/strong\u003E puts it on the clipboard; \u003Cstrong\u003EDownload as text\u003C/strong\u003E saves it as a \u003Ccode\u003E.txt\u003C/code\u003E file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe legal description is a draft from the drawing, not a survey. A licensed surveyor has to check it against the record and sign it before anyone relies on it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick inside a parcel on the plan to select it; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E removes it. Parcels are saved in the project file.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Parcels sit on the plan with their bearings and distances along each course and their name and area in the middle. They go into the PDF plot and the DWG and DXF exports, on the layers V-PROP (lines) and V-PROP-TEXT (labels). Everything is under Parcels in the right panel when nothing is selected. Draw a parcel Click Draw a parcel. Click the corners, starting at the point of beginning. A click near a survey point takes that point's exact place. Click the first corner again, or press Enter, to finish. Backspace takes back the last corner; Esc stops. Enter the calls from a deed Click Enter deed calls. Say where the calls start: a survey point's number, or a northing and easting. Type or paste the calls, one a line: a line: a bearing and a distance, such as N 45°30'15\" E 125.50. N45-30-15E, N 45 30 15 E, N 45.5 E and azimuths (AZ 45°30'15\") work too, and \"thence\", commas and \"feet\" are ignored. a curve: C R 300 L 52.36 right (radius and arc length), or C R 300 D 10°00'00\" left (radius and central angle). A curve follows on from the course before it; when it does not, add its chord bearing: C R 300 L 52.36 right CB N 50 00 00 E. # starts a comment line. As you type, the dialog shows the length of the calls, the misclosure (how far from the start they end, and in which direction), the precision (the length divided by the misclosure, 1 : 10,000 and so on) and the area. Lines it cannot read are listed with their line numbers. Add the parcel. When the calls do not close, the parcel closes with a straight course across the gap: check the calls against the deed. To spread a field traverse's error over its courses, adjust it first. Curves In a parcel's Properties, each course has a Radius. Type one to make that course a curve through the same two corners, and choose which way it curves: a curve to the right has its center to the right of the direction of travel, as in a deed. Clear the radius to make it straight again. Each change is one step: Ctrl+Z undoes it. The legal description A parcel's Properties end with its legal description, written from the drawing: \"Beginning at\" the Point of beginning you type (for example \"the northeast corner of Lot 4\"), else the first corner's northing and easting, or the survey point the calls started from. \"thence\" each course: a bearing and a distance, or a curve with its radius, arc length, central angle, and chord bearing and distance. \"Containing\" its area in square feet and acres (square meters and hectares in a meter project). Copy puts it on the clipboard; Download as text saves it as a .txt file. The legal description is a draft from the drawing, not a survey. A licensed surveyor has to check it against the record and sign it before anyone relies on it. Click inside a parcel on the plan to select it; Delete removes it. Parcels are saved in the project file."},{"slug":"traverse","scope":"civilcraft","title":"Enter a traverse and adjust it by the compass rule","summary":"Type field angles or bearings and distances, check the angular and linear closure and the precision, adjust by the compass rule, and add the stations as survey points or the loop as a parcel.","section":"tasks","order":96,"keywords":["traverse","closed traverse","loop","angles right","backsight","bearings","distances","closure","misclosure","precision","compass rule","bowditch","adjustment","survey"],"headings":[{"id":"where-it-starts-and-ends","text":"Where it starts and ends","level":2},{"id":"type-the-legs","text":"Type the legs","level":2},{"id":"read-the-closure","text":"Read the closure","level":2},{"id":"the-adjustment","text":"The adjustment","level":2},{"id":"use-the-result","text":"Use the result","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA traverse is a run of measured legs: at each station an angle (or a bearing) and a distance to the next. Click \u003Cstrong\u003EEnter a traverse\u003C/strong\u003E under \u003Cstrong\u003ETraverse\u003C/strong\u003E in the right panel when nothing is selected.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-it-starts-and-ends\"\u003EWhere it starts and ends\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EStart at survey point\u003C/strong\u003E: its number. Or type a northing and easting.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELegs as\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cstrong\u003EAngles right and distances\u003C/strong\u003E, or \u003Cstrong\u003EBearings and distances\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith angles, say where they start from: a \u003Cstrong\u003EBacksight survey point\u003C/strong\u003E, or \u003Cstrong\u003Eits azimuth\u003C/strong\u003E (from the first station toward the backsight, clockwise from north).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEnds\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cstrong\u003Eback at the start (a loop)\u003C/strong\u003E, or \u003Cstrong\u003Eon survey point\u003C/strong\u003E and its number (an open traverse between known points).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"type-the-legs\"\u003EType the legs\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOne leg a line, then the distance, then a name for the station it reaches if you like:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAngles right, turned clockwise at the station the leg leaves, from the station behind to the next: \u003Ccode\u003E270°00&#039;10&quot; 125.50 B\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003E270-00-10\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E270 00 10\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E270.0028\u003C/code\u003E work too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBearings: \u003Ccode\u003EN 45°30&#039;15&quot; E 125.50 B\u003C/code\u003E, or an azimuth as one word: \u003Ccode\u003EAZ 45-30-15 125.50 B\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn a loop with angles, end with \u003Ccode\u003Eclose\u003C/code\u003E and the angle at the start again, turned from the last station to the second: \u003Ccode\u003Eclose 270°00&#039;05&quot;\u003C/code\u003E. That is what checks the angles.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELines that cannot be read are listed with their line numbers. &quot;feet&quot;, commas and \u003Ccode\u003E#\u003C/code\u003E comment lines are fine.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"read-the-closure\"\u003ERead the closure\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAs you type, the dialog shows:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAngular misclosure\u003C/strong\u003E: how far the angles are from closing, and the share each angle takes. Without a closing angle the angles are not checked, and any error in them shows up in the linear misclosure.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELinear misclosure\u003C/strong\u003E: how far from where it should end the traverse ends, and in which direction.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPrecision\u003C/strong\u003E: the length of the traverse divided by the linear misclosure: 1 : 10,000 means 1 ft in 10,000 ft.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe stations after the adjustment, and how far each one moved.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-adjustment\"\u003EThe adjustment\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFirst the angular misclosure is spread equally over the angles. Then the linear misclosure is spread over the legs by the compass rule (Bowditch): each station moves against the misclosure in proportion to the length travelled to reach it, so the traverse ends exactly where it should.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe compass rule is a standard adjustment, not a least-squares one, and it does not weigh angles against distances. Whether the precision is good enough is for your own standards and your jurisdiction&#039;s to say.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"use-the-result\"\u003EUse the result\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAdd as survey points\u003C/strong\u003E: the adjusted stations become survey points with the names you gave (else the next free numbers), description \u003Ccode\u003ETRAV\u003C/code\u003E, no elevation. A loop&#039;s last station is its start, so it is not added twice. One step: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAdd as a parcel\u003C/strong\u003E (loops): the adjusted stations become a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/parcels\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/parcels\"\u003Eparcel\u003C/a\u003E, with its labels and legal description.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EReport (CSV)\u003C/strong\u003E: the closure lines, then each station&#039;s direction, distance, unadjusted and adjusted coordinates and corrections.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"A traverse is a run of measured legs: at each station an angle (or a bearing) and a distance to the next. Click Enter a traverse under Traverse in the right panel when nothing is selected. Where it starts and ends Start at survey point: its number. Or type a northing and easting. Legs as: Angles right and distances, or Bearings and distances. With angles, say where they start from: a Backsight survey point, or its azimuth (from the first station toward the backsight, clockwise from north). Ends: back at the start (a loop), or on survey point and its number (an open traverse between known points). Type the legs One leg a line, then the distance, then a name for the station it reaches if you like: Angles right, turned clockwise at the station the leg leaves, from the station behind to the next: 270°00'10\" 125.50 B. 270-00-10, 270 00 10 and 270.0028 work too. Bearings: N 45°30'15\" E 125.50 B, or an azimuth as one word: AZ 45-30-15 125.50 B. On a loop with angles, end with close and the angle at the start again, turned from the last station to the second: close 270°00'05\". That is what checks the angles. Lines that cannot be read are listed with their line numbers. \"feet\", commas and # comment lines are fine. Read the closure As you type, the dialog shows: Angular misclosure: how far the angles are from closing, and the share each angle takes. Without a closing angle the angles are not checked, and any error in them shows up in the linear misclosure. Linear misclosure: how far from where it should end the traverse ends, and in which direction. Precision: the length of the traverse divided by the linear misclosure: 1 : 10,000 means 1 ft in 10,000 ft. The stations after the adjustment, and how far each one moved. The adjustment First the angular misclosure is spread equally over the angles. Then the linear misclosure is spread over the legs by the compass rule (Bowditch): each station moves against the misclosure in proportion to the length travelled to reach it, so the traverse ends exactly where it should. The compass rule is a standard adjustment, not a least-squares one, and it does not weigh angles against distances. Whether the precision is good enough is for your own standards and your jurisdiction's to say. Use the result Add as survey points: the adjusted stations become survey points with the names you gave (else the next free numbers), description TRAV, no elevation. A loop's last station is its start, so it is not added twice. One step: Ctrl+Z undoes it. Add as a parcel (loops): the adjusted stations become a parcel, with its labels and legal description. Report (CSV): the closure lines, then each station's direction, distance, unadjusted and adjusted coordinates and corrections."},{"slug":"staking","scope":"civilcraft","title":"Make a staking report","summary":"List stakes along an alignment at stations and offsets, or the survey points near it, with station, offset, northing, easting, design and ground elevations and cut or fill, and save the report as CSV or PDF.","section":"tasks","order":97,"keywords":["staking","stakeout","stake report","cut sheet","grade sheet","station","offset","cut","fill","layout","construction staking","as-built"],"headings":[{"id":"stakes-at-stations-and-offsets","text":"Stakes at stations and offsets","level":2},{"id":"survey-points-near-the-alignment","text":"Survey points near the alignment","level":2},{"id":"what-the-report-says","text":"What the report says","level":2},{"id":"save-it","text":"Save it","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ESelect an alignment and click \u003Cstrong\u003EStaking report\u003C/strong\u003E in its \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"stakes-at-stations-and-offsets\"\u003EStakes at stations and offsets\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFrom\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003Eto\u003C/strong\u003E: the stations to stake (1+50 or 150).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eevery\u003C/strong\u003E: the interval. Stakes go at every multiple of it, at both ends, and (with the box ticked) where the alignment&#039;s curves begin and end.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOffsets\u003C/strong\u003E: left and right of the alignment, looking up station: \u003Ccode\u003E12 L, 0, 12 R\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EL12 CL R12\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E-12, 0, 12\u003C/code\u003E (left is minus). An alignment with a corridor starts with its edges of pavement and shoulders.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENumber from\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003Ewith prefix\u003C/strong\u003E name the stakes: 1, 2, 3, or S101, S102.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"survey-points-near-the-alignment\"\u003ESurvey points near the alignment\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cstrong\u003Esurvey points near the alignment\u003C/strong\u003E and how near. Each point within that distance gets its station and offset, the design there, and its own elevation as the ground: a check of points staked or shot as built.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-the-report-says\"\u003EWhat the report says\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EColumn\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it is\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPoint\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe stake&#039;s name, or the survey point&#039;s number\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStation, Offset\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhere it is along the alignment: L left, R right\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENorthing, Easting\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIts coordinates\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDesign elev.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe graded corridor&#039;s surface there (from daylight line to daylight line), else the design profile on the centerline\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGround elev.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe existing surface there, or the survey point&#039;s own elevation\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECut/fill\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EC when the ground is above the design (cut it down), F when below (fill it up), to the hundredth\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA stake with no design elevation (off the corridor, or on an alignment without one away from the centerline) has no cut or fill; the dialog says how many.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save-it\"\u003ESave it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECSV\u003C/strong\u003E: a title line, then the columns above, plus the cut or fill as a signed number (ground minus design) for spreadsheets and data collectors.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPDF\u003C/strong\u003E: table pages on the paper you pick, a stake a row, cuts in orange and fills in green, with the title strip of the other sheets.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EStakes are worked out from the design in the project. Check them against the approved plans before anything is built.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"Select an alignment and click Staking report in its Properties. Stakes at stations and offsets From and to: the stations to stake (1+50 or 150). every: the interval. Stakes go at every multiple of it, at both ends, and (with the box ticked) where the alignment's curves begin and end. Offsets: left and right of the alignment, looking up station: 12 L, 0, 12 R, L12 CL R12 or -12, 0, 12 (left is minus). An alignment with a corridor starts with its edges of pavement and shoulders. Number from and with prefix name the stakes: 1, 2, 3, or S101, S102. Survey points near the alignment Choose survey points near the alignment and how near. Each point within that distance gets its station and offset, the design there, and its own elevation as the ground: a check of points staked or shot as built. What the report says Column What it is Point The stake's name, or the survey point's number Station, Offset Where it is along the alignment: L left, R right Northing, Easting Its coordinates Design elev. The graded corridor's surface there (from daylight line to daylight line), else the design profile on the centerline Ground elev. The existing surface there, or the survey point's own elevation Cut/fill C when the ground is above the design (cut it down), F when below (fill it up), to the hundredth A stake with no design elevation (off the corridor, or on an alignment without one away from the centerline) has no cut or fill; the dialog says how many. Save it CSV: a title line, then the columns above, plus the cut or fill as a signed number (ground minus design) for spreadsheets and data collectors. PDF: table pages on the paper you pick, a stake a row, cuts in orange and fills in green, with the title strip of the other sheets. Stakes are worked out from the design in the project. Check them against the approved plans before anything is built."},{"slug":"landxml","scope":"civilcraft","title":"Exchange LandXML","summary":"Open LandXML from other civil software as a new project, export points, surfaces, alignments, profiles and the pipe network as LandXML 1.2, and know what does not make the trip.","section":"tasks","order":100,"keywords":["landxml","xml","import","export","exchange","tin","surface","alignment","profile","pipe network","interoperability","data exchange"],"headings":[{"id":"open-a-landxml-file","text":"Open a LandXML file","level":2},{"id":"export-landxml","text":"Export LandXML","level":2},{"id":"what-does-not-make-the-trip","text":"What does not make the trip","level":2},{"id":"round-trips","text":"Round trips","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ELandXML is the open exchange format most civil programs read and write. CivilCraft reads and writes LandXML 1.2. If you have used Civil 3D, its LandXML import and export dialogs are the closest match; here there are no choices to make, and the notes tell you what was left out.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-a-landxml-file\"\u003EOpen a LandXML file\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E (or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and choose the \u003Ccode\u003E.xml\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E.landxml\u003C/code\u003E file, or drop it on the plan.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf the current project has unsaved changes, CivilCraft asks first. Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave a copy\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDon’t save\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECancel\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Enew project\u003C/strong\u003E is made. The status bar says what was read, for example &quot;Opened site.xml: 412 points, 2 surfaces, 3 alignments.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELook at the box under the \u003Cstrong\u003ESurface\u003C/strong\u003E facts. It lists everything that was skipped or changed, for example:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;Only the first surface (&quot;EG&quot;) was opened; the file has 1 more.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;Alignment &quot;Ramp A&quot; has spirals or other elements CivilCraft cannot draw yet; it was skipped.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;Pipe network rules were not in the file; the placeholders are in use — set yours under Project.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat becomes what:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIn the file\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIn CivilCraft\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUnits (Imperial or Metric)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe project&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003EUnits\u003C/strong\u003E (feet or metres)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EProject name\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe project \u003Cstrong\u003EName\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECgPoints\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe project&#039;s points; the description (or code) gives the group\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe first TIN surface\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe existing ground, used as it is (&quot;Surface (from LandXML)&quot;)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEvery alignment of lines and arcs\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAn alignment with its PIs and radii rebuilt from the curves\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe first design profile of each alignment\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIts design profile, with parabolic vertical curves\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe first pipe network\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe pipe network, with its rims, sumps and inverts\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlan features of lines and arcs\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFeature lines, arcs kept; from another program they start as \u003Cstrong\u003EOnly drawn\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe full list of elements and attributes is in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/landxml-reference\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/landxml-reference\"\u003ELandXML: what is read and written\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA surface read from LandXML is not rebuilt when you change point groups or draw breaklines. Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERebuild the surface from the points\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cstrong\u003ESurface\u003C/strong\u003E section to build it from the points instead (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E brings the LandXML surface back).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"export-landxml\"\u003EExport LandXML\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELandXML\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cstrong\u003EExport\u003C/strong\u003E group.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe browser downloads \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt;.xml\u003C/code\u003E. The status bar says what went in, for example &quot;Exported LandXML 1.2: 276 points, 2 surfaces, 1 alignment, the pipe network. Pads are not part of LandXML here; the graded surface is.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe file holds:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUnits\u003C/strong\u003E: US survey feet or metres.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECgPoints\u003C/strong\u003E: every point with its number as the name, its description and its group as the code.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESurfaces\u003C/strong\u003E: the existing ground as a TIN, with its breaklines and outer boundary as source data; and, when pads or corridors grade, a second TIN surface named &quot;Proposed&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAlignments\u003C/strong\u003E: lines and arcs with their stations, each with a profile holding the existing ground sampled along it and the design profile (PVIs and parabolic curves) when it has at least two PVIs.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPipeNetworks\u003C/strong\u003E: structures with their rims, sumps and invert per pipe, and the pipes with diameter, length and slope.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPlanFeatures\u003C/strong\u003E: every feature line as lines and arcs with the elevation at each end, and what it is used for.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-does-not-make-the-trip\"\u003EWhat does not make the trip\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPads and corridors as objects. Their result is in the &quot;Proposed&quot; surface.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAlignments with errors (for example overlapping curves): left out, and counted in the status message.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPipes without both inverts, and networks with fewer than two structures.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA rim taken from the surface is written as a fixed rim. Opened again, the structure keeps that number even if the surface changes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENetwork rules (least slope, cover, drop): LandXML has no place for them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDisplay settings and point groups&#039; surface choices: save a CivilCraft project for those.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen opening, CivilCraft does not read spirals, grid surfaces, points that only refer to other points, parcels, spirals in plan features, roadways or survey data. Each is named in the notes, never guessed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"round-trips\"\u003ERound trips\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo hand work to someone using other civil software and get it back, export LandXML, let them edit, and open their LandXML as a new project. Keep your own \u003Ccode\u003E.civilcraft.json\u003C/code\u003E as well: it is the only file that keeps everything CivilCraft knows.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"LandXML is the open exchange format most civil programs read and write. CivilCraft reads and writes LandXML 1.2. If you have used Civil 3D, its LandXML import and export dialogs are the closest match; here there are no choices to make, and the notes tell you what was left out. Open a LandXML file Click Open (or press Ctrl+O) and choose the .xml or .landxml file, or drop it on the plan. If the current project has unsaved changes, CivilCraft asks first. Choose Save a copy, Don’t save or Cancel. A new project is made. The status bar says what was read, for example \"Opened site.xml: 412 points, 2 surfaces, 3 alignments.\" Look at the box under the Surface facts. It lists everything that was skipped or changed, for example: \"Only the first surface (\"EG\") was opened; the file has 1 more.\" \"Alignment \"Ramp A\" has spirals or other elements CivilCraft cannot draw yet; it was skipped.\" \"Pipe network rules were not in the file; the placeholders are in use — set yours under Project.\" What becomes what: In the file In CivilCraft Units (Imperial or Metric) The project's Units (feet or metres) Project name The project Name CgPoints The project's points; the description (or code) gives the group The first TIN surface The existing ground, used as it is (\"Surface (from LandXML)\") Every alignment of lines and arcs An alignment with its PIs and radii rebuilt from the curves The first design profile of each alignment Its design profile, with parabolic vertical curves The first pipe network The pipe network, with its rims, sumps and inverts Plan features of lines and arcs Feature lines, arcs kept; from another program they start as Only drawn The full list of elements and attributes is in LandXML: what is read and written. A surface read from LandXML is not rebuilt when you change point groups or draw breaklines. Click Rebuild the surface from the points in the Surface section to build it from the points instead (Ctrl+Z brings the LandXML surface back). Export LandXML Click LandXML in the Export group. The browser downloads \u003Cname\u003E.xml. The status bar says what went in, for example \"Exported LandXML 1.2: 276 points, 2 surfaces, 1 alignment, the pipe network. Pads are not part of LandXML here; the graded surface is.\" The file holds: Units: US survey feet or metres. CgPoints: every point with its number as the name, its description and its group as the code. Surfaces: the existing ground as a TIN, with its breaklines and outer boundary as source data; and, when pads or corridors grade, a second TIN surface named \"Proposed\". Alignments: lines and arcs with their stations, each with a profile holding the existing ground sampled along it and the design profile (PVIs and parabolic curves) when it has at least two PVIs. PipeNetworks: structures with their rims, sumps and invert per pipe, and the pipes with diameter, length and slope. PlanFeatures: every feature line as lines and arcs with the elevation at each end, and what it is used for. What does not make the trip Pads and corridors as objects. Their result is in the \"Proposed\" surface. Alignments with errors (for example overlapping curves): left out, and counted in the status message. Pipes without both inverts, and networks with fewer than two structures. A rim taken from the surface is written as a fixed rim. Opened again, the structure keeps that number even if the surface changes. Network rules (least slope, cover, drop): LandXML has no place for them. Display settings and point groups' surface choices: save a CivilCraft project for those. When opening, CivilCraft does not read spirals, grid surfaces, points that only refer to other points, parcels, spirals in plan features, roadways or survey data. Each is named in the notes, never guessed. Round trips To hand work to someone using other civil software and get it back, export LandXML, let them edit, and open their LandXML as a new project. Keep your own .civilcraft.json as well: it is the only file that keeps everything CivilCraft knows."},{"slug":"dxf-export","scope":"civilcraft","title":"Export the plan to DXF","summary":"Write the plan linework as an R12 DXF on named layers, with contours at their elevations and breaklines in 3D, for any CAD program.","section":"tasks","order":110,"keywords":["dxf","dwg","cad","export","linework","r12","2000","2004","2007","2010","2013","2018","layers","contours","drawing exchange","base map","background"],"headings":[{"id":"export","text":"Export","level":2},{"id":"what-is-in-the-file","text":"What is in the file","level":2},{"id":"label-size","text":"Label size","level":2},{"id":"what-is-not-exported","text":"What is not exported","level":2},{"id":"dwg","text":"DWG","level":2},{"id":"map-layers-in-the-dwg","text":"Map layers in the DWG","level":3}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDXF\u003C/span\u003E writes what the plan shows as a DXF file in the R12 format, the version every CAD program opens. If you have used AutoCAD or Civil 3D, open or insert it as you would any DXF.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"export\"\u003EExport\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet up the plan the way you want the drawing: under \u003Cstrong\u003EDisplay\u003C/strong\u003E, choose the surface (existing or proposed), the contour interval, and which things are on (contours, labels, points, point labels, triangles, breaklines, surface edge, pipes, slope arrows).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDXF\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cstrong\u003EExport\u003C/strong\u003E group.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe browser downloads \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt;.dxf\u003C/code\u003E. The status bar says, for example, &quot;Exported R12 DXF: 2,140 objects; labels sized for 1&quot; = 40&#039;. Bands are not exported.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECoordinates are written in your project units, at their real values (state plane coordinates stay as they are). Nothing is scaled or moved.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-in-the-file\"\u003EWhat is in the file\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EThing\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDXF entity\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ELayer\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMinor and major contours\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2D polylines at their elevation\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EC-TOPO-MINR, C-TOPO-MAJR\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EContour labels\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EText\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EC-TOPO-TEXT\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETriangles\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPolylines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EC-TOPO-TINN\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBreaklines and boundary\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E3D polylines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EC-TOPO-BRKL\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESurface edge\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPolylines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EC-TOPO-BNDY\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESlope arrows\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPolylines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EC-TOPO-SLOP\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESurvey points and their labels\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPoints (with elevation), text\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EV-NODE, V-NODE-TEXT\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELines from line codes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E3D polylines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EV-NODE-LINE\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAlignment centerlines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELines and arcs\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EC-ROAD-CNTR\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStationing ticks and labels\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPolylines, text\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EC-ROAD-STAN\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECorridor edges\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPolylines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EC-ROAD-CORR\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESample lines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPolylines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EC-ROAD-SCTN\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPads, daylight lines, slope lines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPolylines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EC-GRAD-PADS, C-GRAD-DAYL, C-GRAD-SLOP\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFeature lines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E3D polylines (arcs as pieces within 0.01; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/feature-lines\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/feature-lines\"\u003EFeature lines\u003C/a\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EC-GRAD-FEAT\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESwept paths\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPolylines, text\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EC-ROAD-SWPT\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPipes, structures, labels\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPolylines, text\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EC-STRM-PIPE, C-STRM-STRC, C-STRM-TEXT\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPipes and structures with problems\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPolylines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EC-STRM-PROB\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach layer has a color. The full list is in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/plan-layers\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/plan-layers\"\u003EPlan layers\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"label-size\"\u003ELabel size\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EText has to have a size in drawing units. CivilCraft sizes labels for the scale at which the whole plan fits on a Tabloid sheet (feet) or an A3 sheet (metres), and says which scale in the status message. If you plot at another scale in your CAD program, scale the text there.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-not-exported\"\u003EWhat is not exported\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EElevation bands (screen only).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe profile view. Profiles go out as LandXML, not as a drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe surface itself as a 3D object. Use LandXML for the TIN, or turn on \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETriangles\u003C/span\u003E to get its edges as linework.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAnything you turned off under \u003Cstrong\u003EDisplay\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"dwg\"\u003EDWG\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDWG\u003C/span\u003E writes the same linework as a DWG drawing. A small dialog asks for the format: 2000, 2004, 2007, 2010, 2013 or 2018. 2000 opens in almost every CAD program, old and new; pick a newer one when whoever gets the drawing asks for it. Your choice is remembered in this browser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDWG\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cstrong\u003EExport\u003C/strong\u003E group.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick the \u003Cstrong\u003EFormat\u003C/strong\u003E and click \u003Cstrong\u003EDownload DWG\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe browser downloads \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt;.dwg\u003C/code\u003E. The status bar says what went in, for example &quot;Exported DWG (2018 format): 12 polylines, 40 labels on 9 layers&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe DWG has the same layers, colors, contours (as polylines at their elevation), 3D breaklines, labels and points as the DXF, in the same coordinates. Layer names a DWG cannot hold are changed, and the status message says so. CivilCraft writes DWG with its own code, so nothing leaves your computer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"map-layers-in-the-dwg\"\u003EMap layers in the DWG\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA DWG drawing cannot hold a picture inside it: CAD programs show a picture that sits beside the drawing. When map layers are showing, the dialog offers \u003Cstrong\u003EMap layers as a picture beside the drawing\u003C/strong\u003E. With it ticked, the download is a \u003Ccode\u003E.zip\u003C/code\u003E holding \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt;.dwg\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt;-map.png\u003C/code\u003E (the map layers over the whole drawing, up to 4,096 pixels across) and \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt;-map.pgw\u003C/code\u003E (its world file, for GIS programs). The drawing shows the picture as an image on layer G-MAP-IMAG, in place. Unzip the files into one folder and keep them together.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"DXF writes what the plan shows as a DXF file in the R12 format, the version every CAD program opens. If you have used AutoCAD or Civil 3D, open or insert it as you would any DXF. Export Set up the plan the way you want the drawing: under Display, choose the surface (existing or proposed), the contour interval, and which things are on (contours, labels, points, point labels, triangles, breaklines, surface edge, pipes, slope arrows). Click DXF in the Export group. The browser downloads \u003Cname\u003E.dxf. The status bar says, for example, \"Exported R12 DXF: 2,140 objects; labels sized for 1\" = 40'. Bands are not exported.\" Coordinates are written in your project units, at their real values (state plane coordinates stay as they are). Nothing is scaled or moved. What is in the file Thing DXF entity Layer Minor and major contours 2D polylines at their elevation C-TOPO-MINR, C-TOPO-MAJR Contour labels Text C-TOPO-TEXT Triangles Polylines C-TOPO-TINN Breaklines and boundary 3D polylines C-TOPO-BRKL Surface edge Polylines C-TOPO-BNDY Slope arrows Polylines C-TOPO-SLOP Survey points and their labels Points (with elevation), text V-NODE, V-NODE-TEXT Lines from line codes 3D polylines V-NODE-LINE Alignment centerlines Lines and arcs C-ROAD-CNTR Stationing ticks and labels Polylines, text C-ROAD-STAN Corridor edges Polylines C-ROAD-CORR Sample lines Polylines C-ROAD-SCTN Pads, daylight lines, slope lines Polylines C-GRAD-PADS, C-GRAD-DAYL, C-GRAD-SLOP Feature lines 3D polylines (arcs as pieces within 0.01; see Feature lines) C-GRAD-FEAT Swept paths Polylines, text C-ROAD-SWPT Pipes, structures, labels Polylines, text C-STRM-PIPE, C-STRM-STRC, C-STRM-TEXT Pipes and structures with problems Polylines C-STRM-PROB Each layer has a color. The full list is in Plan layers. Label size Text has to have a size in drawing units. CivilCraft sizes labels for the scale at which the whole plan fits on a Tabloid sheet (feet) or an A3 sheet (metres), and says which scale in the status message. If you plot at another scale in your CAD program, scale the text there. What is not exported Elevation bands (screen only). The profile view. Profiles go out as LandXML, not as a drawing. The surface itself as a 3D object. Use LandXML for the TIN, or turn on Triangles to get its edges as linework. Anything you turned off under Display. DWG DWG writes the same linework as a DWG drawing. A small dialog asks for the format: 2000, 2004, 2007, 2010, 2013 or 2018. 2000 opens in almost every CAD program, old and new; pick a newer one when whoever gets the drawing asks for it. Your choice is remembered in this browser. Click DWG in the Export group. Pick the Format and click Download DWG. The browser downloads \u003Cname\u003E.dwg. The status bar says what went in, for example \"Exported DWG (2018 format): 12 polylines, 40 labels on 9 layers\". The DWG has the same layers, colors, contours (as polylines at their elevation), 3D breaklines, labels and points as the DXF, in the same coordinates. Layer names a DWG cannot hold are changed, and the status message says so. CivilCraft writes DWG with its own code, so nothing leaves your computer. Map layers in the DWG A DWG drawing cannot hold a picture inside it: CAD programs show a picture that sits beside the drawing. When map layers are showing, the dialog offers Map layers as a picture beside the drawing. With it ticked, the download is a .zip holding \u003Cname\u003E.dwg, \u003Cname\u003E-map.png (the map layers over the whole drawing, up to 4,096 pixels across) and \u003Cname\u003E-map.pgw (its world file, for GIS programs). The drawing shows the picture as an image on layer G-MAP-IMAG, in place. Unzip the files into one folder and keep them together."},{"slug":"plot-pdf","scope":"civilcraft","title":"Plot the plan to PDF","summary":"Plot everything or the current view to a vector PDF on a standard sheet at an engineering or metric scale, in layer colors or black.","section":"tasks","order":120,"keywords":["plot","print","pdf","sheet","scale","paper size","plan sheet","publish","title block","north arrow","scale bar"],"headings":[{"id":"plot","text":"Plot","level":2},{"id":"what-is-on-the-sheet","text":"What is on the sheet","level":2},{"id":"what-is-not-plotted","text":"What is not plotted","level":2},{"id":"printing-on-paper","text":"Printing on paper","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPDF\u003C/span\u003E plots the plan to a vector PDF on one sheet, with a title strip, a north arrow and a scale bar. The PDF is made in the browser and downloaded; it does not go through the browser&#039;s print dialog.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"plot\"\u003EPlot\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet up the plan under \u003Cstrong\u003EDisplay\u003C/strong\u003E: the surface shown, the contour interval and which things are on. The plot follows these settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf you want only part of the site, zoom and pan the plan to it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPDF\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cstrong\u003EExport\u003C/strong\u003E group, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command P\"\u003E⌘P\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+P\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The \u003Cstrong\u003EPlot the plan to PDF\u003C/strong\u003E dialog opens.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the \u003Cstrong\u003EArea\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEverything\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhat is on screen\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the \u003Cstrong\u003EPaper\u003C/strong\u003E: Letter, Tabloid, ANSI D, ARCH D, ISO A4, A3 or A1. A feet project starts on Tabloid, a metre project on A3. Sheets are always landscape.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the \u003Cstrong\u003EScale\u003C/strong\u003E. The list marks the largest scale that fits with &quot;(fits)&quot; and selects it.\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFeet: 1&quot; = 5&#039;, 10&#039;, 20&#039;, 30&#039;, 40&#039;, 50&#039;, 60&#039;, 100&#039;, 200&#039;, 300&#039;, 400&#039;, 500&#039;, 1000&#039;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMetres: 1:50, 1:100, 1:200, 1:250, 1:500, 1:1000, 1:2000, 1:2500, 1:5000, 1:10000.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the \u003Cstrong\u003EInk\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayer colors\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBlack\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERead the line under the choices. It gives the size of the area on paper and the sheet size, for example &quot;The area is 297 × 222 mm at 1&quot; = 40&#039; on a 432 × 279 mm sheet (landscape).&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload PDF\u003C/span\u003E. The browser downloads \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt;.pdf\u003C/code\u003E. The status bar says &quot;Plotted &lt;name&gt;.pdf at 1&quot; = 40&#039;.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf the area is larger than the sheet at the scale you chose, the plot is centered and the edges are cut off. Choose the scale marked &quot;(fits)&quot; or a larger sheet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-on-the-sheet\"\u003EWhat is on the sheet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe plan, centered in the drawing area, as true vectors: contours, labels, alignments with stationing, corridors, pads and daylight lines, pipes, points.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELine weights by layer: alignments, pads and pipes heavier; triangles and minor contours fine.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA title strip along the bottom with the project \u003Cstrong\u003EName\u003C/strong\u003E, the scale, a note of the contour interval and which surface (existing or graded), and the date and build on the right.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA north arrow in the upper-right corner (plan north is up) and a scale bar in the title strip.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe map layers shown under the plan (a picture, map tiles or a map service), when \u003Cstrong\u003EMap layers under the plan\u003C/strong\u003E is ticked: they are drawn as one picture of the whole drawing area at 150 dots per inch, under the linework, and a map&#039;s attribution goes in the title strip. CivilCraft waits up to 8 seconds for map pictures still arriving; if some are still missing the status bar says so.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EText uses the PDF&#039;s built-in Helvetica. Characters outside the Western European set (Windows-1252) print as &quot;?&quot;, and the status message counts them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-not-plotted\"\u003EWhat is not plotted\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EElevation bands.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe profile view. Plotting profiles is not available yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMore than one sheet (for an alignment, use \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/plan-profile-sheets\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/plan-profile-sheets\"\u003Eplan and profile sheets\u003C/a\u003E), viewports, a custom title block or portrait sheets.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe dialog says slope arrows are screen-only. In this build, slope arrows that are turned on under \u003Cstrong\u003EDisplay\u003C/strong\u003E are plotted (and exported to DXF). Turn them off before plotting if you do not want them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"printing-on-paper\"\u003EPrinting on paper\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen the downloaded PDF in any PDF viewer and print it at 100% (actual size) so the scale stays true. &quot;Fit to page&quot; changes the scale.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"PDF plots the plan to a vector PDF on one sheet, with a title strip, a north arrow and a scale bar. The PDF is made in the browser and downloaded; it does not go through the browser's print dialog. Plot Set up the plan under Display: the surface shown, the contour interval and which things are on. The plot follows these settings. If you want only part of the site, zoom and pan the plan to it. Click PDF in the Export group, or press Ctrl+P. The Plot the plan to PDF dialog opens. Choose the Area: Everything or What is on screen. Choose the Paper: Letter, Tabloid, ANSI D, ARCH D, ISO A4, A3 or A1. A feet project starts on Tabloid, a metre project on A3. Sheets are always landscape. Choose the Scale. The list marks the largest scale that fits with \"(fits)\" and selects it. Feet: 1\" = 5', 10', 20', 30', 40', 50', 60', 100', 200', 300', 400', 500', 1000'. Metres: 1:50, 1:100, 1:200, 1:250, 1:500, 1:1000, 1:2000, 1:2500, 1:5000, 1:10000. Choose the Ink: Layer colors or Black. Read the line under the choices. It gives the size of the area on paper and the sheet size, for example \"The area is 297 × 222 mm at 1\" = 40' on a 432 × 279 mm sheet (landscape).\" Click Download PDF. The browser downloads \u003Cname\u003E.pdf. The status bar says \"Plotted \u003Cname\u003E.pdf at 1\" = 40'.\" If the area is larger than the sheet at the scale you chose, the plot is centered and the edges are cut off. Choose the scale marked \"(fits)\" or a larger sheet. What is on the sheet The plan, centered in the drawing area, as true vectors: contours, labels, alignments with stationing, corridors, pads and daylight lines, pipes, points. Line weights by layer: alignments, pads and pipes heavier; triangles and minor contours fine. A title strip along the bottom with the project Name, the scale, a note of the contour interval and which surface (existing or graded), and the date and build on the right. A north arrow in the upper-right corner (plan north is up) and a scale bar in the title strip. The map layers shown under the plan (a picture, map tiles or a map service), when Map layers under the plan is ticked: they are drawn as one picture of the whole drawing area at 150 dots per inch, under the linework, and a map's attribution goes in the title strip. CivilCraft waits up to 8 seconds for map pictures still arriving; if some are still missing the status bar says so. Text uses the PDF's built-in Helvetica. Characters outside the Western European set (Windows-1252) print as \"?\", and the status message counts them. What is not plotted Elevation bands. The profile view. Plotting profiles is not available yet. More than one sheet (for an alignment, use plan and profile sheets), viewports, a custom title block or portrait sheets. The dialog says slope arrows are screen-only. In this build, slope arrows that are turned on under Display are plotted (and exported to DXF). Turn them off before plotting if you do not want them. Printing on paper Open the downloaded PDF in any PDF viewer and print it at 100% (actual size) so the scale stays true. \"Fit to page\" changes the scale."},{"slug":"site-3d","scope":"civilcraft","title":"View the site in 3D","summary":"Open the 3D view, orbit, pan and zoom, set the vertical exaggeration and colouring, choose what shows, and click alignments, pads, feature lines, breaklines, pipes, structures and points to select them.","section":"tasks","order":125,"keywords":["labels","3d labels","billboard","labels facing the camera","station labels","contour labels","point labels","drape","aerial","map layers on the surface","3d","three d","3d view","view cube","compass","orbit","perspective","parallel","isometric","iso","vertical exaggeration","terrain","model","site model","fly around","visualize","render","pipes in 3d","structures","manholes","corridor 3d"],"headings":[{"id":"open-the-3d-view","text":"Open the 3D view","level":2},{"id":"move-around","text":"Move around","level":2},{"id":"the-view-cube","text":"The view cube","level":2},{"id":"exaggerate-the-elevations","text":"Exaggerate the elevations","level":2},{"id":"colour-the-surfaces","text":"Colour the surfaces","level":2},{"id":"choose-what-shows","text":"Choose what shows","level":2},{"id":"pipes-and-structures","text":"Pipes and structures","level":2},{"id":"select-from-the-3d-view","text":"Select from the 3D view","level":2},{"id":"drive-along-an-alignment","text":"Drive along an alignment","level":2},{"id":"light-from-the-sun","text":"Light from the sun","level":2},{"id":"export-the-3d-view","text":"Export the 3D view","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe 3D view shows the site as one model: the existing ground and the graded surface, contours draped on them, breaklines and the surface edge, each alignment on its design profile with station ticks, the corridor feature lines, pads with their daylight lines, and the pipe network with pipes and structures at their real elevations. It reads the same project as the plan, so every edit shows in both.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-the-3d-view\"\u003EOpen the 3D view\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen survey points (or a LandXML or project file) so there is a surface.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"D\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E group of the toolbar. The 3D view opens over the plan, looking at the whole site from the southeast. The first time, it takes a moment to load.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"D\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E again, to go back to the plan. Choosing a drawing tool such as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAlignment\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPad\u003C/span\u003E also goes back to the plan, since drawing happens there.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"move-around\"\u003EMove around\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETo\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDo this\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrbit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with the left or the middle button\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with the right button, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and drag\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurn the wheel; it zooms toward the pointer\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrbit on a trackpad\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESwipe with two fingers (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and swipe pans)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom on a trackpad\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPinch\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETouch\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne finger orbits; two fingers pinch to zoom and move to pan\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EKeys (the view has the focus)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"←\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"→\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↑\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↓\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E orbit, with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E they pan; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"+\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"−\"\u003E−\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E−\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E zoom\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe bar over the view has the rest:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe view list: \u003Cstrong\u003EIso\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ETop\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EBottom\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EFront\u003C/strong\u003E (looking north), \u003Cstrong\u003EBack\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ELeft\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ERight\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPerspective\u003C/span\u003E: pressed is a perspective view; click it for a parallel projection (no foreshortening).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtents\u003C/span\u003E: shows everything again. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E does the same while the 3D view is open.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-view-cube\"\u003EThe view cube\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe view cube in the top right corner turns with the view. Click a side, an edge or a corner to look at the site from there (the camera turns smoothly unless your system asks for reduced motion). Drag the cube to orbit. The compass ring under it shows north (grid north, +Y): drag the ring to turn the site about the vertical, or click N, E, S or W to look from that side. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHome\u003C/span\u003E, the small house at its top left, goes back to the iso view of the whole site. With the keyboard: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to the cube, arrows choose a side, edge or corner, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E looks from there.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn the plan the same cube shows the top. Click any other side, edge or corner (or drag it) to open the 3D view looking from there: this is the quickest way into 3D from a given direction. To swap the cube for a small axis icon, or hide it, see \u003Cstrong\u003ECorner indicator\u003C/strong\u003E in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/settings\"\u003ECivilCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"exaggerate-the-elevations\"\u003EExaggerate the elevations\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESites are usually much wider than they are high, so the elevations are stretched by \u003Cstrong\u003E2×\u003C/strong\u003E to start with. Choose \u003Cstrong\u003E1×\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003E2×\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003E3×\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003E5×\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003E10×\u003C/strong\u003E in the list on the bar, or under \u003Cstrong\u003E3D vertical exaggeration\u003C/strong\u003E in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/settings\"\u003ECivilCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E (the two are one setting). Only the view changes: volumes, profiles and exports stay at true scale.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"colour-the-surfaces\"\u003EColour the surfaces\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EElevation bands\u003C/strong\u003E: the same bands and colours as the plan&#039;s elevation bands (the number of bands is set under \u003Cstrong\u003EDisplay\u003C/strong\u003E in the left panel). Each band edge is cut exactly at its elevation.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOne colour\u003C/strong\u003E: the existing ground and the graded surface each in a plain colour.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMap layers\u003C/strong\u003E: the map layers shown under the plan (a georeferenced picture, map tiles or a map service; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/map-layers\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/map-layers\"\u003EMap layers\u003C/a\u003E) laid over the surfaces like a sheet, so an aerial photo shows on the hills. Pictures still arriving fill in as they come. With no map layer showing, the surfaces keep their elevation bands and the view says so. The picture is for the screen: GLB and OBJ exports do not carry it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen both surfaces are shown, the existing ground is see-through over the graded surface, so you can see the cut (ground above the design) and the fill.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"choose-what-shows\"\u003EChoose what shows\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow\u003C/span\u003E on the bar and tick or clear:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EItem\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it is\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExisting ground\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe surface built from the survey points (or read from LandXML)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGraded surface\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe existing ground with the corridors and pads graded in (only when there are some)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETriangle edges\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEvery TIN edge, as lines\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EContours\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMinor and major contours of each surface shown, at the plan&#039;s interval\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESurface edge\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe outer edge of each surface shown, at its elevations\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBreaklines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEvery breakline, at its elevations\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAlignments and stations\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEach alignment on its design profile (on the ground where there is no profile), with ticks every 50 ft or 20 m\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECorridor feature lines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdge of pavement, shoulder and daylight on both sides\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPads and daylight\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEach pad outline at its elevation and its daylight line\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPipes and structures\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPipes as round pipes between their inverts, structures from rim to sump\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESurvey points\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA dot at each point with an elevation (off at first)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGIS layers\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe GIS layers shown on the plan, in their colors: lines and outlines with their own elevations at those elevations, others laid on the existing ground (they follow it exactly); points as small crosses. Shapes without elevations off the surface are left out, and the view says how many\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELabels\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENames and values that always face you: each alignment&#039;s name at its start and its stations every 100 ft (or 100 m), the elevation of each major contour, pads, feature lines and structures by name, and with \u003Cstrong\u003ESurvey points\u003C/strong\u003E ticked each point&#039;s number and elevation\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELabels stay the same size and upright however you turn or zoom, and a hill in front of a label hides it. Where they would crowd, the most important show and the rest wait until you zoom in: alignments first, then structures, pads, feature lines, stations, contours and points, nearer ones first. They are on the screen only; exports and plots leave them out.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYour choices, the exaggeration and the colouring are remembered in this browser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pipes-and-structures\"\u003EPipes and structures\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA pipe is drawn with its bottom inside at the inverts you set and its top inside (the crown) one inside diameter higher. Diameters are in inches on a feet project and millimeters on a meter project, as in the right panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA structure runs from its rim (typed, or from the surface) down to its sump (the lowest invert minus the sump depth). Without any invert, it is drawn 5 ft (1.5 m) deep.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPipes and structures with a problem (see the network check in the right panel) are drawn in red.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA pipe without inverts is not drawn; a note at the bottom of the view says how many.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"select-from-the-3d-view\"\u003ESelect from the 3D view\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick an alignment, a pad, a feature line, a pipe, a structure or a survey point. It is selected just as if you clicked it in the plan: its properties open in the right panel, where you can edit them, and it is drawn in the selection color on top of everything in 3D. Whatever you select on the plan is highlighted in 3D too. A tip next to the pointer names what you clicked with its easting, northing and elevation. Pipes and structures can be clicked even where they are under the ground; a line hidden behind a hill cannot be clicked through it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick a breakline to see which one it is: it is highlighted, and the status bar names it (breaklines have no properties of their own).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick the ground to read the easting, northing and elevation there in the tip.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"drive-along-an-alignment\"\u003EDrive along an alignment\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick \u003Cstrong\u003EDrive\u003C/strong\u003E on the 3D bar and pick an alignment. The view moves to a driver&#039;s seat: 3.5 ft (1.07 m) above the road, on its design profile (on the existing ground along the centerline when it has none yet), looking ahead along it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPlay\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E drives at the speed you pick (10 to 65 mph, or 15 to 100 km/h); \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again pauses. It stops at the end; \u003Cstrong\u003EPlay\u003C/strong\u003E then starts again from the beginning.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe slider jumps to any station; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"←\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"→\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E step 10 ft (5 m) back and on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDragging in the view pauses and lets you look round.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EStop\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E puts the view back where it was.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe vertical exaggeration applies while driving too: set it to 1× to see the road as it will be.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"light-from-the-sun\"\u003ELight from the sun\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cstrong\u003ESun\u003C/strong\u003E on the 3D bar and tick \u003Cstrong\u003ELight from the sun\u003C/strong\u003E. Give a date, a clock time and its time zone (the offset from UTC: in summer, the daylight saving one), and the site&#039;s latitude and longitude. When the project has a coordinate system, they are filled in from it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe site is then lit from where the sun is, with \u003Cstrong\u003EShadows\u003C/strong\u003E across the ground, the corridor and the pads; the menu says the sun&#039;s azimuth and how high it is, or that it is below the horizon. Positions come from NOAA&#039;s solar equations. Shadows fall the right way at any exaggeration, but they stop at the edge of the site: hills and buildings beyond it cast none.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"export-the-3d-view\"\u003EExport the 3D view\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGLB\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOBJ\u003C/span\u003E on the bar. What the view shows is saved, in true scale (no exaggeration), in meters, with Y up. Details: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/export-3d\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/export-3d\"\u003EExport the site in 3D\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The 3D view shows the site as one model: the existing ground and the graded surface, contours draped on them, breaklines and the surface edge, each alignment on its design profile with station ticks, the corridor feature lines, pads with their daylight lines, and the pipe network with pipes and structures at their real elevations. It reads the same project as the plan, so every edit shows in both. Open the 3D view Open survey points (or a LandXML or project file) so there is a surface. Press D, or click 3D in the View group of the toolbar. The 3D view opens over the plan, looking at the whole site from the southeast. The first time, it takes a moment to load. Press D again, or click 3D again, to go back to the plan. Choosing a drawing tool such as Alignment or Pad also goes back to the plan, since drawing happens there. Move around To Do this Orbit Drag with the left or the middle button Pan Drag with the right button, or Shift and drag Zoom Turn the wheel; it zooms toward the pointer Orbit on a trackpad Swipe with two fingers (Shift and swipe pans) Zoom on a trackpad Pinch Touch One finger orbits; two fingers pinch to zoom and move to pan Keys (the view has the focus) ← → ↑ ↓ orbit, with Shift they pan; + and − zoom The bar over the view has the rest: The view list: Iso, Top, Bottom, Front (looking north), Back, Left, Right. Perspective: pressed is a perspective view; click it for a parallel projection (no foreshortening). Extents: shows everything again. F does the same while the 3D view is open. The view cube The view cube in the top right corner turns with the view. Click a side, an edge or a corner to look at the site from there (the camera turns smoothly unless your system asks for reduced motion). Drag the cube to orbit. The compass ring under it shows north (grid north, +Y): drag the ring to turn the site about the vertical, or click N, E, S or W to look from that side. Home, the small house at its top left, goes back to the iso view of the whole site. With the keyboard: Tab to the cube, arrows choose a side, edge or corner, Enter looks from there. On the plan the same cube shows the top. Click any other side, edge or corner (or drag it) to open the 3D view looking from there: this is the quickest way into 3D from a given direction. To swap the cube for a small axis icon, or hide it, see Corner indicator in CivilCraft settings. Exaggerate the elevations Sites are usually much wider than they are high, so the elevations are stretched by 2× to start with. Choose 1×, 2×, 3×, 5× or 10× in the list on the bar, or under 3D vertical exaggeration in CivilCraft settings (the two are one setting). Only the view changes: volumes, profiles and exports stay at true scale. Colour the surfaces Elevation bands: the same bands and colours as the plan's elevation bands (the number of bands is set under Display in the left panel). Each band edge is cut exactly at its elevation. One colour: the existing ground and the graded surface each in a plain colour. Map layers: the map layers shown under the plan (a georeferenced picture, map tiles or a map service; see Map layers) laid over the surfaces like a sheet, so an aerial photo shows on the hills. Pictures still arriving fill in as they come. With no map layer showing, the surfaces keep their elevation bands and the view says so. The picture is for the screen: GLB and OBJ exports do not carry it. When both surfaces are shown, the existing ground is see-through over the graded surface, so you can see the cut (ground above the design) and the fill. Choose what shows Click Show on the bar and tick or clear: Item What it is Existing ground The surface built from the survey points (or read from LandXML) Graded surface The existing ground with the corridors and pads graded in (only when there are some) Triangle edges Every TIN edge, as lines Contours Minor and major contours of each surface shown, at the plan's interval Surface edge The outer edge of each surface shown, at its elevations Breaklines Every breakline, at its elevations Alignments and stations Each alignment on its design profile (on the ground where there is no profile), with ticks every 50 ft or 20 m Corridor feature lines Edge of pavement, shoulder and daylight on both sides Pads and daylight Each pad outline at its elevation and its daylight line Pipes and structures Pipes as round pipes between their inverts, structures from rim to sump Survey points A dot at each point with an elevation (off at first) GIS layers The GIS layers shown on the plan, in their colors: lines and outlines with their own elevations at those elevations, others laid on the existing ground (they follow it exactly); points as small crosses. Shapes without elevations off the surface are left out, and the view says how many Labels Names and values that always face you: each alignment's name at its start and its stations every 100 ft (or 100 m), the elevation of each major contour, pads, feature lines and structures by name, and with Survey points ticked each point's number and elevation Labels stay the same size and upright however you turn or zoom, and a hill in front of a label hides it. Where they would crowd, the most important show and the rest wait until you zoom in: alignments first, then structures, pads, feature lines, stations, contours and points, nearer ones first. They are on the screen only; exports and plots leave them out. Your choices, the exaggeration and the colouring are remembered in this browser. Pipes and structures A pipe is drawn with its bottom inside at the inverts you set and its top inside (the crown) one inside diameter higher. Diameters are in inches on a feet project and millimeters on a meter project, as in the right panel. A structure runs from its rim (typed, or from the surface) down to its sump (the lowest invert minus the sump depth). Without any invert, it is drawn 5 ft (1.5 m) deep. Pipes and structures with a problem (see the network check in the right panel) are drawn in red. A pipe without inverts is not drawn; a note at the bottom of the view says how many. Select from the 3D view Click an alignment, a pad, a feature line, a pipe, a structure or a survey point. It is selected just as if you clicked it in the plan: its properties open in the right panel, where you can edit them, and it is drawn in the selection color on top of everything in 3D. Whatever you select on the plan is highlighted in 3D too. A tip next to the pointer names what you clicked with its easting, northing and elevation. Pipes and structures can be clicked even where they are under the ground; a line hidden behind a hill cannot be clicked through it. Click a breakline to see which one it is: it is highlighted, and the status bar names it (breaklines have no properties of their own). Click the ground to read the easting, northing and elevation there in the tip. Drive along an alignment Click Drive on the 3D bar and pick an alignment. The view moves to a driver's seat: 3.5 ft (1.07 m) above the road, on its design profile (on the existing ground along the centerline when it has none yet), looking ahead along it. Play or Space drives at the speed you pick (10 to 65 mph, or 15 to 100 km/h); Space again pauses. It stops at the end; Play then starts again from the beginning. The slider jumps to any station; ← and → step 10 ft (5 m) back and on. Dragging in the view pauses and lets you look round. Stop or Esc puts the view back where it was. The vertical exaggeration applies while driving too: set it to 1× to see the road as it will be. Light from the sun Open Sun on the 3D bar and tick Light from the sun. Give a date, a clock time and its time zone (the offset from UTC: in summer, the daylight saving one), and the site's latitude and longitude. When the project has a coordinate system, they are filled in from it. The site is then lit from where the sun is, with Shadows across the ground, the corridor and the pads; the menu says the sun's azimuth and how high it is, or that it is below the horizon. Positions come from NOAA's solar equations. Shadows fall the right way at any exaggeration, but they stop at the edge of the site: hills and buildings beyond it cast none. Export the 3D view Click GLB or OBJ on the bar. What the view shows is saved, in true scale (no exaggeration), in meters, with Y up. Details: Export the site in 3D."},{"slug":"export-3d","scope":"civilcraft","title":"Export the site in 3D","summary":"Save what the 3D view shows as a GLB or OBJ file for other 3D programs, in meters with Y up, around a local origin that the file records.","section":"tasks","order":126,"keywords":["glb","gltf","obj","3d export","export 3d","model export","game engine","visualization","3d viewer","meters","y up","origin","local origin","state plane"],"headings":[{"id":"export","text":"Export","level":2},{"id":"what-the-files-hold","text":"What the files hold","level":2},{"id":"units-axes-and-the-origin","text":"Units, axes and the origin","level":2},{"id":"not-built-yet","text":"Not built yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe 3D view exports what it shows as a 3D model another program can open: a 3D viewer, a modeler, a game engine or a visualization tool.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"export\"\u003EExport\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the 3D view (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"D\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and choose what shows with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow\u003C/span\u003E. What is hidden is not exported.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGLB\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOBJ\u003C/span\u003E on the bar. The file downloads as &quot;\u003Cem\u003Eproject name\u003C/em\u003E-3d.glb&quot; or &quot;-3d.obj&quot;, and the status bar says the origin.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-the-files-hold\"\u003EWhat the files hold\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003E\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EGLB\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EOBJ\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESurfaces\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMeshes with normals and the band or surface colours\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMeshes with normals (no colours)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPipes and structures\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERound meshes, problem ones in red\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERound meshes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EContours, breaklines, alignments, ticks, corridor and pad lines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELines, each set in its colour\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELine records (\u003Ccode\u003El\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESurvey points\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPoints\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPoint records (\u003Ccode\u003Ep\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach set keeps its name (&quot;Existing ground&quot;, &quot;Pipes&quot;, &quot;Major contours&quot; and so on), so you can hide or style it in the other program.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"units-axes-and-the-origin\"\u003EUnits, axes and the origin\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMeters.\u003C/strong\u003E A feet project is converted from US survey feet (1 ft = 1200/3937 m).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EY up\u003C/strong\u003E, the usual convention for these formats: easting becomes +X, elevation +Y, and northing −Z.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETrue scale.\u003C/strong\u003E The vertical exaggeration of the view is not applied.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA local origin.\u003C/strong\u003E State plane coordinates are millions of feet, too large for the single-precision numbers these formats store, so the file is written relative to a nearby origin: the existing ground&#039;s own origin for easting and northing, and its lowest elevation rounded down. The origin, in project units, is recorded in the file: in a GLB as \u003Ccode\u003Eextras\u003C/code\u003E on the scene and its top node, in an OBJ as a comment line near the top (\u003Ccode\u003E# origin easting … northing … elevation …\u003C/code\u003E). To place the model back in project coordinates, add the origin (after converting meters back to feet on a feet project).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EExporting the exaggerated view, or exporting in project units instead of meters.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETextures, a material file for OBJ (.mtl) and per-object properties (names of pipes and structures are only in CivilCraft).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EExporting the corridor as separate solids, or the profile view in 3D.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"The 3D view exports what it shows as a 3D model another program can open: a 3D viewer, a modeler, a game engine or a visualization tool. Export Open the 3D view (D) and choose what shows with Show. What is hidden is not exported. Click GLB or OBJ on the bar. The file downloads as \"project name-3d.glb\" or \"-3d.obj\", and the status bar says the origin. What the files hold GLB OBJ Surfaces Meshes with normals and the band or surface colours Meshes with normals (no colours) Pipes and structures Round meshes, problem ones in red Round meshes Contours, breaklines, alignments, ticks, corridor and pad lines Lines, each set in its colour Line records (l) Survey points Points Point records (p) Each set keeps its name (\"Existing ground\", \"Pipes\", \"Major contours\" and so on), so you can hide or style it in the other program. Units, axes and the origin Meters. A feet project is converted from US survey feet (1 ft = 1200/3937 m). Y up, the usual convention for these formats: easting becomes +X, elevation +Y, and northing −Z. True scale. The vertical exaggeration of the view is not applied. A local origin. State plane coordinates are millions of feet, too large for the single-precision numbers these formats store, so the file is written relative to a nearby origin: the existing ground's own origin for easting and northing, and its lowest elevation rounded down. The origin, in project units, is recorded in the file: in a GLB as extras on the scene and its top node, in an OBJ as a comment line near the top (# origin easting … northing … elevation …). To place the model back in project coordinates, add the origin (after converting meters back to feet on a feet project). Not built yet Exporting the exaggerated view, or exporting in project units instead of meters. Textures, a material file for OBJ (.mtl) and per-object properties (names of pipes and structures are only in CivilCraft). Exporting the corridor as separate solids, or the profile view in 3D."},{"slug":"coordinate-systems","scope":"civilcraft","title":"Set the project's coordinate system","summary":"Say which coordinate system the survey is in, so CivilCraft shows latitude and longitude, places GIS files and map layers, and writes .prj files. Pick by code, by name, by the coordinates themselves or from a .prj file.","section":"tasks","order":130,"keywords":["coordinate system","crs","epsg","srid","state plane","spcs","utm","nad83","wgs 84","projection","prj","wkt","datum","latitude","longitude","lat long","geographic","grid","georeference","georeferencing","assign coordinate system"],"headings":[{"id":"pick-the-system","text":"Pick the system","level":2},{"id":"change-or-remove-the-system","text":"Change or remove the system","level":2},{"id":"latitude-and-longitude-in-the-status-bar","text":"Latitude and longitude in the status bar","level":2},{"id":"how-good-the-datum-shift-is","text":"How good the datum shift is","level":2},{"id":"grid-shift-files","text":"Grid shift files","level":2},{"id":"what-was-checked","text":"What was checked","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA project&#039;s coordinates are plain numbers until you say what they mean. Setting the coordinate system \u003Cstrong\u003Enever moves anything\u003C/strong\u003E: it tells CivilCraft that, for example, E 6,150,000 N 2,080,000 are California zone 6 state plane coordinates in US survey feet. Once it knows:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe status bar shows the latitude and longitude of the pointer,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGIS files in other systems (shapefiles, GeoJSON, GeoPackage, elevation grids, LiDAR) are converted into the project as they open,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Emap layers can be drawn under the plan (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/map-layers\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/map-layers\"\u003EShow map layers under the plan\u003C/a\u003E),\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eexports to GIS formats carry the system (a \u003Ccode\u003E.prj\u003C/code\u003E for shapefiles, the EPSG code for GeoPackage and GeoTIFF).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pick-the-system\"\u003EPick the system\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECoordinates\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGIS\u003C/span\u003E group of the toolbar. The \u003Cstrong\u003ECoordinate system\u003C/strong\u003E dialog opens. At the top it says what the project is in now, or \u003Cstrong\u003ENone — coordinates are just numbers\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFind the system one of these ways:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESearch\u003C/strong\u003E: type the EPSG code (\u003Ccode\u003E2277\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EEPSG:2277\u003C/code\u003E) or words from the name (\u003Ccode\u003Etexas central\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eutm 14n\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Elambert-93\u003C/code\u003E). Codes list from the one you typed; words list systems whose name has every word.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESystems these coordinates fit\u003C/span\u003E (when the project has points or a surface): CivilCraft reads the middle of your project in every system of the project&#039;s unit family and lists the ones whose area of use holds it. State plane coordinates usually fit only a few zones: a project in Texas at E 3,115,000 N 10,070,000 US survey feet fits the four Texas Central variants (NAD83, NAD83(2011), HARN, NSRS2007). Each entry shows where your project&#039;s middle would be.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENear (lat, long)\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESuggest\u003C/span\u003E: type where the site is, for example \u003Ccode\u003E30.27, -97.74\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E30°16′N 97°44′W\u003C/code\u003E, and CivilCraft lists the projected systems whose area holds it, national and state grids first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERead a .prj file…\u003C/span\u003E: open the \u003Ccode\u003E.prj\u003C/code\u003E that came with a shapefile or a drawing. CivilCraft matches it to its list by the projection maths (so a \u003Ccode\u003E.prj\u003C/code\u003E that names a datum differently still finds the right system) and says which one it is; when nothing matches exactly, the file&#039;s own description is used. You can also drop a \u003Ccode\u003E.prj\u003C/code\u003E on the dialog, or open or drop one on the plan by itself.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a system in the list. Under \u003Cstrong\u003EChosen\u003C/strong\u003E it shows the name, EPSG code, unit, how well its datum is tied to WGS 84, its area of use in latitude and longitude, and where your project&#039;s middle falls in it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELeave \u003Cstrong\u003EGrid\u003C/strong\u003E selected (most projects; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/ground-and-local-coordinates\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/ground-and-local-coordinates\"\u003EGround and local coordinates\u003C/a\u003E for the others) and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApply\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe status bar now shows the pointer&#039;s latitude and longitude next to E and N. Setting the system is one undo step: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes it back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe units must match: a project in feet takes systems in US survey feet or international feet, a project in meters takes systems in meters. CivilCraft explains a refusal (&quot;The project is in feet but this system is in meters…&quot;). A feet project with a US survey foot system uses survey feet: the project&#039;s &quot;ft&quot; means the system&#039;s foot. Geographic systems (latitude and longitude, such as WGS 84 itself) cannot be a project&#039;s system: drawings need eastings and northings.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-or-remove-the-system\"\u003EChange or remove the system\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECoordinates\u003C/span\u003E again, pick another system and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApply\u003C/span\u003E. When the project has points or a design, CivilCraft asks \u003Cstrong\u003EAre the coordinates in … already?\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EYes — nothing moves\u003C/span\u003E: the numbers stay as they are and only their meaning changes. Use this when the old system was picked by mistake.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENo — convert them\u003C/span\u003E: every survey point, breakline, boundary, alignment PI, pad corner and structure is converted from the old system into the new one, in one undo step. A surface read from LandXML as triangles cannot be converted; rebuild it from the points first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove the system\u003C/span\u003E takes the system away (nothing moves). GIS layers already in the project stay where they are.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"latitude-and-longitude-in-the-status-bar\"\u003ELatitude and longitude in the status bar\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile the project has a system, the status bar shows the pointer&#039;s latitude and longitude on WGS 84, for example \u003Ccode\u003E30°18′00.00″ N  97°42′00.00″ W\u003C/code\u003E. Choose decimal degrees instead under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECivilCraft\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ELatitude and longitude\u003C/strong\u003E (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/settings\"\u003ECivilCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E). With ground or local coordinates the readout goes through that step first, so it is right for the point under the pointer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-good-the-datum-shift-is\"\u003EHow good the datum shift is\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELatitude, longitude, GeoJSON and map layers are all on WGS 84. Each system&#039;s tie to WGS 84 is shown in the dialog:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat the dialog says\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it means\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWGS 84 itself\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo shift at all.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENAD83 is taken as WGS 84\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe two differ by a meter or two in most of North America, a few meters at most. Good for map layers and site context; not for survey control.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EKnown to about \u003Cem\u003En\u003C/em\u003E m (EPSG&#039;s figure)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA published shift; map layers and GeoJSON can be off by about that much.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot known for this datum\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThere is no published shift without a grid file: map layers and GeoJSON may be off by hundreds of meters.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFrom the grid shift file …\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA grid shift file is in use (below): within about 10 cm of NAD83 inside the grid.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYour project&#039;s own coordinates never go through a datum shift: only what is converted to or from latitude and longitude does. Vertical datums and geoid models are not handled (elevations are never converted). Where the best shift needs a grid file, the list carries a rougher published shift and the dialog shows its accuracy (NAD27 systems, for example, about 20 m), until you add the grid.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"grid-shift-files\"\u003EGrid shift files\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor a NAD27 system, \u003Cstrong\u003EGrid shift file\u003C/strong\u003E in the coordinate system dialog puts latitude and longitude, GIS files and map layers within about 10 cm of NAD83 instead of about 20 m:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDownload\u003C/strong\u003E the grid it suggests for the system&#039;s area (NADCON5 for the conterminous United States, NADCON for Alaska, NTv2 for Canada) from cdn.proj.org, PROJ&#039;s public grid server. Only the grid&#039;s file name is sent.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOr \u003Cstrong\u003EAdd a grid file…\u003C/strong\u003E you already have: NTv2 (\u003Ccode\u003E.gsb\u003C/code\u003E) or a PROJ GeoTIFF grid (\u003Ccode\u003E.tif\u003C/code\u003E), up to 20 MB.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe grid is saved in the project file, so it travels with the project. The section says whether the project&#039;s middle is inside the grid: outside it, no shift is applied at all, so use a grid that covers the site. \u003Cstrong\u003EStop using the grid\u003C/strong\u003E goes back to the rough shift. Each change is one step: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EGIS layers already in the project stay where they are when the grid changes; import them again to place them through the grid. The project&#039;s own coordinates never move.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-was-checked\"\u003EWhat was checked\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEvery conversion was compared with PROJ (pyproj 3.7.1 with PROJ 9.5.1), the coordinate library most GIS programs use, on over 330 systems: state plane in US survey feet and feet, UTM north and south, Lambert-93, British National Grid, GDA2020 MGA, New Zealand Transverse Mercator and web Mercator among them. Eastings and northings agree to 1 cm with the same datum shift (most to well under 1 mm), and the projection maths agree with PROJ&#039;s own definitions to 1 cm.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.prj\u003C/code\u003E files written by PROJ in both common forms (ESRI and OGC) were read back for 105 systems: 208 of the 210 matched their exact EPSG code and the other two a system with the same maths on the same ellipsoid. \u003Ccode\u003E.prj\u003C/code\u003E files CivilCraft writes are read by PROJ as the same system. ESRI \u003Ccode\u003E.prj\u003C/code\u003E files for polar stereographic systems (UPS, Alaska polar) cannot be read; pick those systems by code.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe latitude and longitude in the status bar matched PROJ to 0.01″ in the browser.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"A project's coordinates are plain numbers until you say what they mean. Setting the coordinate system never moves anything: it tells CivilCraft that, for example, E 6,150,000 N 2,080,000 are California zone 6 state plane coordinates in US survey feet. Once it knows: the status bar shows the latitude and longitude of the pointer, GIS files in other systems (shapefiles, GeoJSON, GeoPackage, elevation grids, LiDAR) are converted into the project as they open, map layers can be drawn under the plan (Show map layers under the plan), exports to GIS formats carry the system (a .prj for shapefiles, the EPSG code for GeoPackage and GeoTIFF). Pick the system Choose Coordinates in the GIS group of the toolbar. The Coordinate system dialog opens. At the top it says what the project is in now, or None — coordinates are just numbers. Find the system one of these ways: Search: type the EPSG code (2277, EPSG:2277) or words from the name (texas central, utm 14n, lambert-93). Codes list from the one you typed; words list systems whose name has every word. Systems these coordinates fit (when the project has points or a surface): CivilCraft reads the middle of your project in every system of the project's unit family and lists the ones whose area of use holds it. State plane coordinates usually fit only a few zones: a project in Texas at E 3,115,000 N 10,070,000 US survey feet fits the four Texas Central variants (NAD83, NAD83(2011), HARN, NSRS2007). Each entry shows where your project's middle would be. Near (lat, long) and Suggest: type where the site is, for example 30.27, -97.74 or 30°16′N 97°44′W, and CivilCraft lists the projected systems whose area holds it, national and state grids first. Read a .prj file…: open the .prj that came with a shapefile or a drawing. CivilCraft matches it to its list by the projection maths (so a .prj that names a datum differently still finds the right system) and says which one it is; when nothing matches exactly, the file's own description is used. You can also drop a .prj on the dialog, or open or drop one on the plan by itself. Click a system in the list. Under Chosen it shows the name, EPSG code, unit, how well its datum is tied to WGS 84, its area of use in latitude and longitude, and where your project's middle falls in it. Leave Grid selected (most projects; see Ground and local coordinates for the others) and choose Apply. The status bar now shows the pointer's latitude and longitude next to E and N. Setting the system is one undo step: Ctrl+Z takes it back. The units must match: a project in feet takes systems in US survey feet or international feet, a project in meters takes systems in meters. CivilCraft explains a refusal (\"The project is in feet but this system is in meters…\"). A feet project with a US survey foot system uses survey feet: the project's \"ft\" means the system's foot. Geographic systems (latitude and longitude, such as WGS 84 itself) cannot be a project's system: drawings need eastings and northings. Change or remove the system Open Coordinates again, pick another system and choose Apply. When the project has points or a design, CivilCraft asks Are the coordinates in … already? Yes — nothing moves: the numbers stay as they are and only their meaning changes. Use this when the old system was picked by mistake. No — convert them: every survey point, breakline, boundary, alignment PI, pad corner and structure is converted from the old system into the new one, in one undo step. A surface read from LandXML as triangles cannot be converted; rebuild it from the points first. Remove the system takes the system away (nothing moves). GIS layers already in the project stay where they are. Latitude and longitude in the status bar While the project has a system, the status bar shows the pointer's latitude and longitude on WGS 84, for example 30°18′00.00″ N 97°42′00.00″ W. Choose decimal degrees instead under Settings, CivilCraft, Latitude and longitude (CivilCraft settings). With ground or local coordinates the readout goes through that step first, so it is right for the point under the pointer. How good the datum shift is Latitude, longitude, GeoJSON and map layers are all on WGS 84. Each system's tie to WGS 84 is shown in the dialog: What the dialog says What it means WGS 84 itself No shift at all. NAD83 is taken as WGS 84 The two differ by a meter or two in most of North America, a few meters at most. Good for map layers and site context; not for survey control. Known to about n m (EPSG's figure) A published shift; map layers and GeoJSON can be off by about that much. Not known for this datum There is no published shift without a grid file: map layers and GeoJSON may be off by hundreds of meters. From the grid shift file … A grid shift file is in use (below): within about 10 cm of NAD83 inside the grid. Your project's own coordinates never go through a datum shift: only what is converted to or from latitude and longitude does. Vertical datums and geoid models are not handled (elevations are never converted). Where the best shift needs a grid file, the list carries a rougher published shift and the dialog shows its accuracy (NAD27 systems, for example, about 20 m), until you add the grid. Grid shift files For a NAD27 system, Grid shift file in the coordinate system dialog puts latitude and longitude, GIS files and map layers within about 10 cm of NAD83 instead of about 20 m: Download the grid it suggests for the system's area (NADCON5 for the conterminous United States, NADCON for Alaska, NTv2 for Canada) from cdn.proj.org, PROJ's public grid server. Only the grid's file name is sent. Or Add a grid file… you already have: NTv2 (.gsb) or a PROJ GeoTIFF grid (.tif), up to 20 MB. The grid is saved in the project file, so it travels with the project. The section says whether the project's middle is inside the grid: outside it, no shift is applied at all, so use a grid that covers the site. Stop using the grid goes back to the rough shift. Each change is one step: Ctrl+Z undoes it. GIS layers already in the project stay where they are when the grid changes; import them again to place them through the grid. The project's own coordinates never move. What was checked Every conversion was compared with PROJ (pyproj 3.7.1 with PROJ 9.5.1), the coordinate library most GIS programs use, on over 330 systems: state plane in US survey feet and feet, UTM north and south, Lambert-93, British National Grid, GDA2020 MGA, New Zealand Transverse Mercator and web Mercator among them. Eastings and northings agree to 1 cm with the same datum shift (most to well under 1 mm), and the projection maths agree with PROJ's own definitions to 1 cm. .prj files written by PROJ in both common forms (ESRI and OGC) were read back for 105 systems: 208 of the 210 matched their exact EPSG code and the other two a system with the same maths on the same ellipsoid. .prj files CivilCraft writes are read by PROJ as the same system. ESRI .prj files for polar stereographic systems (UPS, Alaska polar) cannot be read; pick those systems by code. The latitude and longitude in the status bar matched PROJ to 0.01″ in the browser."},{"slug":"ground-and-local-coordinates","scope":"civilcraft","title":"Ground and local coordinates","summary":"Work in ground (surface) coordinates scaled from the grid by a combined scale factor you type or compute at a point, or in an assumed site grid tied to the system by two control points.","section":"tasks","order":131,"keywords":["ground coordinates","surface coordinates","grid to ground","combined scale factor","csf","combined factor","grid factor","elevation factor","sea level factor","convergence","mapping angle","scale factor","assumed coordinates","local coordinates","site grid","5000 5000","control points","localization","calibration","two point","misclosure"],"headings":[{"id":"ground-coordinates","text":"Ground coordinates","level":2},{"id":"local-assumed-coordinates","text":"Local (assumed) coordinates","level":2},{"id":"what-was-checked","text":"What was checked","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EState plane and UTM coordinates are \u003Cstrong\u003Egrid\u003C/strong\u003E coordinates: a map projection stretches or shrinks distances a little (tens of parts per million), and the higher the site, the more a measured ground distance differs from the grid one. Many surveys therefore hand over \u003Cstrong\u003Eground\u003C/strong\u003E coordinates, scaled so plan distances equal distances measured on the ground, or an \u003Cstrong\u003Eassumed\u003C/strong\u003E site grid (5000, 5000 at a control point). CivilCraft keeps your numbers as they are and records how they relate to the grid, so latitude and longitude, GIS files and map layers still land in the right place.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECoordinates\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGIS\u003C/span\u003E group, pick (or keep) the system, then choose under \u003Cstrong\u003EGrid, ground or local coordinates\u003C/strong\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EChoice\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYour coordinates are\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EHow they relate to the grid\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGrid\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe system&#039;s own (the start)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same numbers\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGround\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EScaled to ground distances\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Egrid = base + combined factor × (ground − base)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELocal\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAn assumed site grid\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMoved, turned and scaled onto the grid by two control points\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhatever you choose is saved with the project and is one undo step; nothing moves.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"ground-coordinates\"\u003EGround coordinates\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGround\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the \u003Cstrong\u003ECombined scale factor\u003C/strong\u003E from the survey (for example \u003Ccode\u003E0.99987\u003C/code\u003E), or compute it:\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cstrong\u003ECompute the factor at a point\u003C/strong\u003E, the point starts at the middle of the project. Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPick a point on the plan\u003C/span\u003E and click the spot (a click near a survey point takes that point), or type E and N.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe elevation comes from the surface there; type another one if you need to.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECompute\u003C/span\u003E. CivilCraft shows:\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EGrid scale factor k\u003C/strong\u003E: how much the map projection stretches distances there.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EElevation factor\u003C/strong\u003E: how much shorter distances are at the ellipsoid than at that height.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECombined factor\u003C/strong\u003E = k × elevation factor: grid distance = ground distance × this.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EConvergence\u003C/strong\u003E: the angle from grid north to true north there (geodetic azimuth = grid azimuth + convergence).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUse\u003C/span\u003E button under the numbers to put the combined factor in the box.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet the \u003Cstrong\u003EBase point\u003C/strong\u003E, where ground and grid coordinates are the same. It starts at 0, 0: most jurisdictions that publish ground coordinates scale about the origin, so ground coordinates stay recognizably close to grid ones. Some surveys scale about the project&#039;s control point instead: use what the survey says.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApply\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe elevation is taken as height above the ellipsoid. CivilCraft has no geoid model, so where the geoid is 30 m (100 ft) below or above the ellipsoid the elevation factor is off by about 5 ppm. When the survey states its combined factor, type that one.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"local-assumed-coordinates\"\u003ELocal (assumed) coordinates\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELocal\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFor \u003Cstrong\u003EControl point 1\u003C/strong\u003E, type the survey point&#039;s number (or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPick\u003C/span\u003E and click it on the plan). Its local coordinates show beside it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType its \u003Cstrong\u003Egrid\u003C/strong\u003E E and N, or its latitude and longitude in the third box (for example \u003Ccode\u003E30.2672, -97.7431\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDo the same for \u003Cstrong\u003EControl point 2\u003C/strong\u003E, as far from the first as you can.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECivilCraft shows the \u003Cstrong\u003EScale\u003C/strong\u003E and the \u003Cstrong\u003ERotation\u003C/strong\u003E found, and reminds you: \u003Cstrong\u003ETwo points always fit exactly — check a third.\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGive a \u003Cstrong\u003ECheck point\u003C/strong\u003E the same way. CivilCraft shows how far the fit puts it from the grid coordinates you typed (its misclosure, in project units and parts per million). A large misclosure means a wrong point, a typing error, or coordinates in another unit.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApply\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA scale far from 1 (beyond 0.5 to 2) is refused: check the points and their units.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-was-checked\"\u003EWhat was checked\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe scale factor and convergence match PROJ (pyproj 3.7.1 with PROJ 9.5.1), the coordinate library most GIS programs use, to 1 part in a billion on over 300 systems with conformal projections (state plane, UTM, transverse Mercator, Lambert conformal conic), and in the browser at a point picked on the plan.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGround and local steps convert back and forth to 1 part in a billion; two control points give back the scale and rotation they were made with; a check point 0.05 ft off shows a misclosure of 0.05 ft.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"State plane and UTM coordinates are grid coordinates: a map projection stretches or shrinks distances a little (tens of parts per million), and the higher the site, the more a measured ground distance differs from the grid one. Many surveys therefore hand over ground coordinates, scaled so plan distances equal distances measured on the ground, or an assumed site grid (5000, 5000 at a control point). CivilCraft keeps your numbers as they are and records how they relate to the grid, so latitude and longitude, GIS files and map layers still land in the right place. Open Coordinates in the GIS group, pick (or keep) the system, then choose under Grid, ground or local coordinates: Choice Your coordinates are How they relate to the grid Grid The system's own (the start) The same numbers Ground Scaled to ground distances grid = base + combined factor × (ground − base) Local An assumed site grid Moved, turned and scaled onto the grid by two control points Whatever you choose is saved with the project and is one undo step; nothing moves. Ground coordinates Choose Ground. Type the Combined scale factor from the survey (for example 0.99987), or compute it: Under Compute the factor at a point, the point starts at the middle of the project. Choose Pick a point on the plan and click the spot (a click near a survey point takes that point), or type E and N. The elevation comes from the surface there; type another one if you need to. Choose Compute. CivilCraft shows: Grid scale factor k: how much the map projection stretches distances there. Elevation factor: how much shorter distances are at the ellipsoid than at that height. Combined factor = k × elevation factor: grid distance = ground distance × this. Convergence: the angle from grid north to true north there (geodetic azimuth = grid azimuth + convergence). Choose the Use button under the numbers to put the combined factor in the box. Set the Base point, where ground and grid coordinates are the same. It starts at 0, 0: most jurisdictions that publish ground coordinates scale about the origin, so ground coordinates stay recognizably close to grid ones. Some surveys scale about the project's control point instead: use what the survey says. Choose Apply. The elevation is taken as height above the ellipsoid. CivilCraft has no geoid model, so where the geoid is 30 m (100 ft) below or above the ellipsoid the elevation factor is off by about 5 ppm. When the survey states its combined factor, type that one. Local (assumed) coordinates Choose Local. For Control point 1, type the survey point's number (or choose Pick and click it on the plan). Its local coordinates show beside it. Type its grid E and N, or its latitude and longitude in the third box (for example 30.2672, -97.7431). Do the same for Control point 2, as far from the first as you can. CivilCraft shows the Scale and the Rotation found, and reminds you: Two points always fit exactly — check a third. Give a Check point the same way. CivilCraft shows how far the fit puts it from the grid coordinates you typed (its misclosure, in project units and parts per million). A large misclosure means a wrong point, a typing error, or coordinates in another unit. Choose Apply. A scale far from 1 (beyond 0.5 to 2) is refused: check the points and their units. What was checked The scale factor and convergence match PROJ (pyproj 3.7.1 with PROJ 9.5.1), the coordinate library most GIS programs use, to 1 part in a billion on over 300 systems with conformal projections (state plane, UTM, transverse Mercator, Lambert conformal conic), and in the browser at a point picked on the plan. Ground and local steps convert back and forth to 1 part in a billion; two control points give back the scale and rotation they were made with; a check point 0.05 ft off shows a misclosure of 0.05 ft."},{"slug":"map-layers","scope":"civilcraft","title":"Show map layers under the plan","summary":"Put a georeferenced picture, map tiles, a WMS or WMTS server or a map service under the plan as site context, with the internet setting, opacity, order and attribution.","section":"tasks","order":135,"keywords":["map layers","basemap","base map","background","aerial","orthophoto","imagery","satellite","raster","world file","pgw","jgw","tfw","wld","geotiff","georeferenced image","xyz","tiles","slippy map","wms","wmts","getcapabilities","map service","rest","mapserver","imageserver","usgs","national map","attribution","opacity"],"headings":[{"id":"the-internet-setting","text":"The internet setting","level":2},{"id":"a-georeferenced-picture-works-offline","text":"A georeferenced picture (works offline)","level":2},{"id":"tiles-wms-wmts-and-map-services","text":"Tiles, WMS, WMTS and map services","level":2},{"id":"how-layers-are-drawn","text":"How layers are drawn","level":2},{"id":"change-order-and-remove-layers","text":"Change, order and remove layers","level":2},{"id":"when-a-layer-shows-nothing","text":"When a layer shows nothing","level":2},{"id":"what-was-checked","text":"What was checked","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EMap layers are pictures drawn under your design: an aerial photo with its world file, or maps from servers on the internet. They are site context on screen only: they are \u003Cstrong\u003Enot\u003C/strong\u003E in PDF plots, DXF or DWG exports, or the 3D view.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMap\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGIS\u003C/span\u003E group. The \u003Cstrong\u003EMap layers\u003C/strong\u003E dialog lists the project&#039;s layers and adds new ones. Map layers need the project&#039;s coordinate system (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/coordinate-systems\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/coordinate-systems\"\u003ESet the project&#039;s coordinate system\u003C/a\u003E), except a picture whose world file uses the project&#039;s own coordinates.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-internet-setting\"\u003EThe internet setting\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECivilCraft never asks a map server for anything until you allow it: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECivilCraft\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EMap layers from the internet\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cstrong\u003EOff\u003C/strong\u003E (the start) or \u003Cstrong\u003EOn\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen on, CivilCraft asks the map servers you add for pictures of the area on screen. Your drawing stays on this computer; the server sees the area and your internet address.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile it is off, internet layers are listed in the dialog but grayed out, with the reason, and nothing is drawn or requested. Opening a project that names map servers does not contact them either. A picture from a file works either way. The dialog&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen Settings\u003C/span\u003E button takes you there.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-georeferenced-picture-works-offline\"\u003EA georeferenced picture (works offline)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cstrong\u003EAdd a layer\u003C/strong\u003E, keep \u003Cstrong\u003EGeoreferenced picture\u003C/strong\u003E and choose the files: the picture (PNG or JPEG) \u003Cstrong\u003Etogether with\u003C/strong\u003E its world file (\u003Ccode\u003E.pgw\u003C/code\u003E for PNG, \u003Ccode\u003E.jgw\u003C/code\u003E for JPEG, or \u003Ccode\u003E.wld\u003C/code\u003E) and, if there is one, its \u003Ccode\u003E.prj\u003C/code\u003E. You can also drop them on the plan together.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWithout a \u003Ccode\u003E.prj\u003C/code\u003E, CivilCraft asks which system the world file uses: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThe project’s system\u003C/span\u003E (most common), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPick a system…\u003C/span\u003E, or, in a project without a system, \u003Cstrong\u003EProject coordinates\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe picture appears under the plan and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E includes it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn RGB GeoTIFF (an aerial photo saved as GeoTIFF) carries its own placement and system: open it on its own. The picture is kept \u003Cstrong\u003Einside the project file\u003C/strong\u003E (up to 25 MB); a larger one is refused with its size, so save a smaller copy (fewer pixels, or JPEG).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"tiles-wms-wmts-and-map-services\"\u003ETiles, WMS, WMTS and map services\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETurn \u003Cstrong\u003EMap layers from the internet\u003C/strong\u003E on first. Then, in \u003Cstrong\u003EAdd a layer\u003C/strong\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFrom\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat to paste\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat CivilCraft does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETile URL\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA template with \u003Ccode\u003E{z}\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E{x}\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E{y}\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003E{-y}\u003C/code\u003E), and \u003Ccode\u003E{s}\u003C/code\u003E for a server letter, for example \u003Ccode\u003Ehttps://tile.example.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png\u003C/code\u003E. Give it a name and its attribution.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETiles in web Mercator for the area and zoom on screen.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWMS server\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe server&#039;s address, or its GetCapabilities link. Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGet the layers\u003C/span\u003E, then the layer.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne picture of the area on screen, asked in the project&#039;s EPSG code when the server offers it, else in web Mercator or WGS 84.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWMTS server\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe server&#039;s address, or its \u003Ccode\u003EWMTSCapabilities.xml\u003C/code\u003E. Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGet the layers\u003C/span\u003E, then the layer and tile matrix set.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETiles from the set in the project&#039;s system when there is one, else web Mercator.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArcGIS map service (REST)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA URL ending in \u003Ccode\u003E/MapServer\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E/ImageServer\u003C/code\u003E. Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERead the service\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECached web Mercator services are drawn from their tiles; others are asked for one picture of the area on screen.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENo servers come preset. Public examples, all from the USGS National Map (public domain):\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWMS: \u003Ccode\u003Ehttps://basemap.nationalmap.gov/arcgis/services/USGSTopo/MapServer/WMSServer\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWMTS: \u003Ccode\u003Ehttps://basemap.nationalmap.gov/arcgis/rest/services/USGSTopo/MapServer/WMTS/1.0.0/WMTSCapabilities.xml\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMap service: \u003Ccode\u003Ehttps://basemap.nationalmap.gov/arcgis/rest/services/USGSTopo/MapServer\u003C/code\u003E (also \u003Ccode\u003EUSGSImageryOnly\u003C/code\u003E for aerial photos)\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMany city and county GIS departments publish their own WMS or map services; their GIS pages give the address. A server that asks for a sign-in or a key does not work: CivilCraft sends no user names, passwords or keys.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-layers-are-drawn\"\u003EHow layers are drawn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPictures in another system than the project&#039;s are warped into it: the picture is cut into pieces, each placed so it is within a quarter of a screen pixel of where an exact conversion puts it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETiles are chosen for the area on screen and the zoom: never more than 64 for a view, 6 at a time. When you pan or zoom, requests for the old view are cancelled. About 200 tiles are kept in memory (nothing is stored on disk).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA WMS or map service picture is asked for once the view has stopped moving; until it arrives the last one stays in place.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe attribution of each layer on screen shows small at the bottom right of the plan.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-order-and-remove-layers\"\u003EChange, order and remove layers\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn the dialog each layer has a checkbox to show or hide it, an \u003Cstrong\u003EOpacity\u003C/strong\u003E slider, arrows to draw it earlier (lower) or later (on top), a zoom button (pictures) and a remove button. The first layer in the list is drawn at the bottom. Each change is one undo step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-a-layer-shows-nothing\"\u003EWhen a layer shows nothing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe reason appears once in the status line and stays under the layer&#039;s name in the dialog:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMessage\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat to do\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOff: map layers from the internet are off in Settings.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurn \u003Cstrong\u003EMap layers from the internet\u003C/strong\u003E on.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENeeds the project&#039;s coordinate system\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESet it under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECoordinates\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe server did not allow this page to use its images (CORS), or could not be reached.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe server does not let web pages use its pictures, or it is down. After four refusals CivilCraft stops asking; hide and show the layer to try again.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe server answered 404 … / The server sent text/xml, not an image: …\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe URL, layer or style is wrong, or the server&#039;s message says what it wants.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThis layer is not offered in the project&#039;s system, web Mercator or WGS 84\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe server cannot draw where CivilCraft can place it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-was-checked\"\u003EWhat was checked\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETile numbers were checked against the standard web Mercator tiling; WMS 1.1.1 and 1.3.0, WMTS and map service answers were read from hand-written files and from real answers saved from the USGS National Map and terrestris OpenStreetMap servers; WMS 1.3.0 requests put latitude first for EPSG:4326, as the standard says.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWarping was compared with an exact per-pixel conversion through PROJ&#039;s numbers: within 0.13 px for tiles, WMS pictures and a county-sized picture, at site scale and zoomed out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA picture with its world file and an RGB GeoTIFF written by GDAL (through rasterio) were drawn where their placement says, checked pixel by pixel in the browser.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the browser, against a stand-in server on the test computer: tiles land in the tile PROJ puts each point in, a WMS picture lands within 2 px, at most 6 requests run at a time, and with the setting off no request leaves the page. The USGS National Map map service above was also read and drawn live in the browser once (its tiles arrived and were drawn, with its copyright text); the other public servers were read from saved answers only.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Map layers are pictures drawn under your design: an aerial photo with its world file, or maps from servers on the internet. They are site context on screen only: they are not in PDF plots, DXF or DWG exports, or the 3D view. Choose Map in the GIS group. The Map layers dialog lists the project's layers and adds new ones. Map layers need the project's coordinate system (Set the project's coordinate system), except a picture whose world file uses the project's own coordinates. The internet setting CivilCraft never asks a map server for anything until you allow it: Settings, CivilCraft, Map layers from the internet: Off (the start) or On. When on, CivilCraft asks the map servers you add for pictures of the area on screen. Your drawing stays on this computer; the server sees the area and your internet address. While it is off, internet layers are listed in the dialog but grayed out, with the reason, and nothing is drawn or requested. Opening a project that names map servers does not contact them either. A picture from a file works either way. The dialog's Open Settings button takes you there. A georeferenced picture (works offline) In Add a layer, keep Georeferenced picture and choose the files: the picture (PNG or JPEG) together with its world file (.pgw for PNG, .jgw for JPEG, or .wld) and, if there is one, its .prj. You can also drop them on the plan together. Without a .prj, CivilCraft asks which system the world file uses: The project’s system (most common), Pick a system…, or, in a project without a system, Project coordinates. The picture appears under the plan and Fit includes it. An RGB GeoTIFF (an aerial photo saved as GeoTIFF) carries its own placement and system: open it on its own. The picture is kept inside the project file (up to 25 MB); a larger one is refused with its size, so save a smaller copy (fewer pixels, or JPEG). Tiles, WMS, WMTS and map services Turn Map layers from the internet on first. Then, in Add a layer: From What to paste What CivilCraft does Tile URL A template with {z}, {x} and {y} (or {-y}), and {s} for a server letter, for example https://tile.example.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png. Give it a name and its attribution. Tiles in web Mercator for the area and zoom on screen. WMS server The server's address, or its GetCapabilities link. Choose Get the layers, then the layer. One picture of the area on screen, asked in the project's EPSG code when the server offers it, else in web Mercator or WGS 84. WMTS server The server's address, or its WMTSCapabilities.xml. Choose Get the layers, then the layer and tile matrix set. Tiles from the set in the project's system when there is one, else web Mercator. ArcGIS map service (REST) A URL ending in /MapServer or /ImageServer. Choose Read the service. Cached web Mercator services are drawn from their tiles; others are asked for one picture of the area on screen. No servers come preset. Public examples, all from the USGS National Map (public domain): WMS: https://basemap.nationalmap.gov/arcgis/services/USGSTopo/MapServer/WMSServer WMTS: https://basemap.nationalmap.gov/arcgis/rest/services/USGSTopo/MapServer/WMTS/1.0.0/WMTSCapabilities.xml Map service: https://basemap.nationalmap.gov/arcgis/rest/services/USGSTopo/MapServer (also USGSImageryOnly for aerial photos) Many city and county GIS departments publish their own WMS or map services; their GIS pages give the address. A server that asks for a sign-in or a key does not work: CivilCraft sends no user names, passwords or keys. How layers are drawn Pictures in another system than the project's are warped into it: the picture is cut into pieces, each placed so it is within a quarter of a screen pixel of where an exact conversion puts it. Tiles are chosen for the area on screen and the zoom: never more than 64 for a view, 6 at a time. When you pan or zoom, requests for the old view are cancelled. About 200 tiles are kept in memory (nothing is stored on disk). A WMS or map service picture is asked for once the view has stopped moving; until it arrives the last one stays in place. The attribution of each layer on screen shows small at the bottom right of the plan. Change, order and remove layers In the dialog each layer has a checkbox to show or hide it, an Opacity slider, arrows to draw it earlier (lower) or later (on top), a zoom button (pictures) and a remove button. The first layer in the list is drawn at the bottom. Each change is one undo step. When a layer shows nothing The reason appears once in the status line and stays under the layer's name in the dialog: Message What to do Off: map layers from the internet are off in Settings. Turn Map layers from the internet on. Needs the project's coordinate system Set it under Coordinates. The server did not allow this page to use its images (CORS), or could not be reached. The server does not let web pages use its pictures, or it is down. After four refusals CivilCraft stops asking; hide and show the layer to try again. The server answered 404 … / The server sent text/xml, not an image: … The URL, layer or style is wrong, or the server's message says what it wants. This layer is not offered in the project's system, web Mercator or WGS 84 The server cannot draw where CivilCraft can place it. What was checked Tile numbers were checked against the standard web Mercator tiling; WMS 1.1.1 and 1.3.0, WMTS and map service answers were read from hand-written files and from real answers saved from the USGS National Map and terrestris OpenStreetMap servers; WMS 1.3.0 requests put latitude first for EPSG:4326, as the standard says. Warping was compared with an exact per-pixel conversion through PROJ's numbers: within 0.13 px for tiles, WMS pictures and a county-sized picture, at site scale and zoomed out. A picture with its world file and an RGB GeoTIFF written by GDAL (through rasterio) were drawn where their placement says, checked pixel by pixel in the browser. In the browser, against a stand-in server on the test computer: tiles land in the tile PROJ puts each point in, a WMS picture lands within 2 px, at most 6 requests run at a time, and with the setting off no request leaves the page. The USGS National Map map service above was also read and drawn live in the browser once (its tiles arrived and were drawn, with its copyright text); the other public servers were read from saved answers only."},{"slug":"gis-import","scope":"civilcraft","title":"Import GIS data","summary":"Bring shapefiles, GeoJSON and GeoPackages into a project as GIS layers, place them through the right coordinate system, look at their attributes, and use them for the surface.","section":"tasks","order":140,"keywords":["gis","labels","label features","attributes on the plan","kml","kmz","google earth","shapefile","shp","dbf","prj","zip","geojson","geopackage","gpkg","import","layers","attributes","coordinate system","epsg","latitude","longitude","contours","parcels","breaklines","boundary"],"headings":[{"id":"open-the-files","text":"Open the files","level":2},{"id":"the-coordinate-system","text":"The coordinate system","level":2},{"id":"gis-layers","text":"GIS layers","level":2},{"id":"label-features-from-an-attribute","text":"Label features from an attribute","level":2},{"id":"look-at-a-feature","text":"Look at a feature","level":2},{"id":"use-for-the-surface","text":"Use for the surface","level":2},{"id":"point-files-with-latitude-and-longitude","text":"Point files with latitude and longitude","level":2},{"id":"landxml-files-that-name-a-coordinate-system","text":"LandXML files that name a coordinate system","level":2},{"id":"what-was-checked","text":"What was checked","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ECity and county GIS departments hand out site context as GIS files: parcels, easements, contours, utilities, survey control. CivilCraft opens three kinds of them as \u003Cstrong\u003EGIS layers\u003C/strong\u003E that sit under your design on the plan.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFile\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat to open\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShapefile\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe \u003Ccode\u003E.shp\u003C/code\u003E together with its \u003Ccode\u003E.shx\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.dbf\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.prj\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E.cpg\u003C/code\u003E (select them all), or the \u003Ccode\u003E.zip\u003C/code\u003E they came in. A zip holding several shapefiles gives several layers.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGeoJSON\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe \u003Ccode\u003E.geojson\u003C/code\u003E file (a \u003Ccode\u003E.json\u003C/code\u003E file whose first lines say \u003Ccode\u003EFeatureCollection\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003EFeature\u003C/code\u003E works too).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGeoPackage\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe \u003Ccode\u003E.gpkg\u003C/code\u003E file. Every feature table in it is listed.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EKML or KMZ\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe \u003Ccode\u003E.kml\u003C/code\u003E file (from Google Earth or a web map), or the zipped \u003Ccode\u003E.kmz\u003C/code\u003E. Each folder becomes a layer (split into points, lines and polygons when it holds several kinds); a placemark&#039;s name, description and extended data become its attributes. Heights are in meters and latitude and longitude, as in GeoJSON.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPoints, multipoints, lines, polygons with holes and multi-part shapes are read, with their elevations (z) when the file has them. Measure (M) values are left out. Elevation grids and LiDAR open the same way: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/elevation-grids-and-lidar\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/elevation-grids-and-lidar\"\u003EElevation grids and LiDAR\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-the-files\"\u003EOpen the files\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cstrong\u003EGIS\u003C/strong\u003E group (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E), choose the files, or drop them on the plan. The files are read on this computer; nothing is uploaded.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe \u003Cstrong\u003EImport GIS data\u003C/strong\u003E dialog lists every layer found: its kind, how many features (and how many have no geometry), how many attribute fields, and the coordinate system the file names. Untick the layers you do not want.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERead the \u003Cstrong\u003ECoordinate system\u003C/strong\u003E part (below) and the \u003Cstrong\u003EWhat will happen\u003C/strong\u003E line, then click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe layers appear on the plan and under \u003Cstrong\u003EGIS layers\u003C/strong\u003E in the left panel. The import is one step: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes it back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA large file shows a progress line over the plan with a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECancel\u003C/span\u003E button.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-coordinate-system\"\u003EThe coordinate system\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EGIS files say where they are through a coordinate system (a shapefile&#039;s \u003Ccode\u003E.prj\u003C/code\u003E, a GeoPackage&#039;s spatial reference table; GeoJSON is always latitude and longitude). CivilCraft converts each layer from its own system into the project&#039;s. It never guesses:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe project has a coordinate system\u003C/strong\u003E (set in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECoordinates\u003C/span\u003E): every layer with a system of its own is converted into it, including a ground or local adjustment. A file with no \u003Ccode\u003E.prj\u003C/code\u003E gets two choices: \u003Cstrong\u003ESame as the project\u003C/strong\u003E (its numbers are loaded as they are) or \u003Cstrong\u003EPick the file&#039;s system\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe project has none, and the file is in a projected system in your project&#039;s units\u003C/strong\u003E (a state plane zone in US survey feet for a feet project, for example): the dialog offers \u003Cstrong\u003EUse that system as the project&#039;s coordinate system\u003C/strong\u003E. It is set in the same undo step as the import. Nothing in the project moves; the system only says what the numbers mean. You can pick another system for the project instead, and the layers are converted into it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe project has none, and the file is in latitude and longitude\u003C/strong\u003E (every GeoJSON) \u003Cstrong\u003Eor in other units\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport\u003C/span\u003E stays grayed out until you choose the project&#039;s coordinate system. The picker starts near the data.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENeither the project nor the files name a system\u003C/strong\u003E: the numbers are loaded as they are, and the dialog says so.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EElevations are converted too: GeoJSON heights are metres (the GeoJSON standard says so); other files&#039; heights are taken in the unit of their own system.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPositions in a GeoJSON file outside ±180° longitude or ±90° latitude are not latitude and longitude. Those features are left out and the dialog says how many. Such a file is usually a projected file saved without its system; ask for it again, or for the shapefile with its \u003Ccode\u003E.prj\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENAD83 is treated as the same as WGS 84 (the system GeoJSON uses); the two differ by a few metres in North America, so GeoJSON data can be off by that much. Where the best shift needs a grid file (NADCON for NAD27, for example), CivilCraft uses a rougher published shift and the coordinate system dialog shows how rough (for NAD27, about 20 m), until you add the grid: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/coordinate-systems\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/coordinate-systems\"\u003EGrid shift files\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"gis-layers\"\u003EGIS layers\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach row under \u003Cstrong\u003EGIS layers\u003C/strong\u003E has:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ea checkbox to show or hide the layer (hidden layers are not picked either);\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eits colour, chosen from the list;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ea menu (the three dots) with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EZoom to\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUse for the surface…\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERename…\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EShowing, hiding, recolouring, renaming and removing are undo steps. GIS layers are saved in the project file and come back when you open it. They also go out with the plan&#039;s PDF plot and its DXF and DWG exports, each on a layer named \u003Ccode\u003EGIS-\u003C/code\u003E and its number.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"label-features-from-an-attribute\"\u003ELabel features from an attribute\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn the layer&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu (the three dots in \u003Cstrong\u003EGIS layers\u003C/strong\u003E), choose a field under \u003Cstrong\u003ELabel with\u003C/strong\u003E: every feature shows its value on the plan, in the layer&#039;s color. Points are labeled beside the point, lines along the middle of their longest part (turned to read from left to right), polygons at their center. Numbers show up to three decimals. Labels are 1.8 mm high on paper at any scale, and they go into the PDF plot, DXF and DWG. Choose \u003Cstrong\u003ENo labels\u003C/strong\u003E to take them away; each change is one step (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes it). A layer labels at most 5,000 features.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"look-at-a-feature\"\u003ELook at a feature\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E on, click a feature. \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E shows its layer, which feature it is, its shape (parts, holes, vertices), its elevations, the file it came from and that file&#039;s coordinate system, then every attribute in a table. Attributes are read-only: CivilCraft shows GIS data as it came, it does not edit it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPoints win over lines when both are under the pointer; inside a polygon, the smallest polygon is picked.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"use-for-the-surface\"\u003EUse for the surface\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen a layer&#039;s menu and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUse for the surface…\u003C/span\u003E (or the button in \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E). What is offered depends on the layer:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ELayer\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECan become\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELines with elevations (3D lines)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBreaklines at their own elevations\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELines without elevations but with a number field (contours)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBreaklines at the elevation in the field you pick\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPolygons\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe surface boundary (the selected polygon, else the largest), or breaklines along their edges\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPoints with elevations, or with an elevation field\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESurvey points, described with the layer&#039;s name so they form a point group\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach use is one undo step that copies the geometry into the project; the layer itself stays as it is. Elevations are taken in project units.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"point-files-with-latitude-and-longitude\"\u003EPoint files with latitude and longitude\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA point file whose header names \u003Cstrong\u003Elatitude\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003Elongitude\u003C/strong\u003E columns (\u003Ccode\u003Elat\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Elatitude\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Elon\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Elong\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Elongitude\u003C/code\u003E) is read as degrees, not as northing and easting. The import dialog says so. On \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport\u003C/span\u003E, the points are converted to the project&#039;s coordinate system; a project without one asks you to choose it first. Degrees are never loaded as feet. Rows outside ±90° latitude or ±180° longitude are refused with the reason. The elevation column is taken as it is, in project units.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"landxml-files-that-name-a-coordinate-system\"\u003ELandXML files that name a coordinate system\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen a LandXML file you open has a \u003Ccode\u003ECoordinateSystem\u003C/code\u003E element (an EPSG code, a WKT description or a system name), CivilCraft offers it as the project&#039;s coordinate system. Nothing moves if you accept; the system only says what the numbers mean.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-was-checked\"\u003EWhat was checked\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEvery reader was compared with GDAL, the library most GIS programs use to read these files, on files GDAL wrote: shapefiles of every kind with and without elevations, multi-part lines, polygons with holes, empty geometries, text, number, date and true/false attributes, UTF-8 and Windows-1252 text with and without a \u003Ccode\u003E.cpg\u003C/code\u003E, a zip of several shapefiles, GeoJSON with every geometry type, and a GeoPackage with four tables. Every geometry, elevation and attribute matched.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPositions converted from latitude and longitude, and from UTM, matched PROJ (the projection library behind GDAL) to 3 mm.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDamaged files (cut short, lying about their sizes, bad zips, bad GeoPackages) give one plain message; nothing hangs.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENothing here has been opened in other GIS programs yet, and file geodatabases, KML and KMZ are not read yet (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"City and county GIS departments hand out site context as GIS files: parcels, easements, contours, utilities, survey control. CivilCraft opens three kinds of them as GIS layers that sit under your design on the plan. File What to open Shapefile The .shp together with its .shx, .dbf, .prj and .cpg (select them all), or the .zip they came in. A zip holding several shapefiles gives several layers. GeoJSON The .geojson file (a .json file whose first lines say FeatureCollection or Feature works too). GeoPackage The .gpkg file. Every feature table in it is listed. KML or KMZ The .kml file (from Google Earth or a web map), or the zipped .kmz. Each folder becomes a layer (split into points, lines and polygons when it holds several kinds); a placemark's name, description and extended data become its attributes. Heights are in meters and latitude and longitude, as in GeoJSON. Points, multipoints, lines, polygons with holes and multi-part shapes are read, with their elevations (z) when the file has them. Measure (M) values are left out. Elevation grids and LiDAR open the same way: see Elevation grids and LiDAR. Open the files Click Import in the GIS group (or Open), choose the files, or drop them on the plan. The files are read on this computer; nothing is uploaded. The Import GIS data dialog lists every layer found: its kind, how many features (and how many have no geometry), how many attribute fields, and the coordinate system the file names. Untick the layers you do not want. Read the Coordinate system part (below) and the What will happen line, then click Import. The layers appear on the plan and under GIS layers in the left panel. The import is one step: Ctrl+Z takes it back. A large file shows a progress line over the plan with a Cancel button. The coordinate system GIS files say where they are through a coordinate system (a shapefile's .prj, a GeoPackage's spatial reference table; GeoJSON is always latitude and longitude). CivilCraft converts each layer from its own system into the project's. It never guesses: The project has a coordinate system (set in Coordinates): every layer with a system of its own is converted into it, including a ground or local adjustment. A file with no .prj gets two choices: Same as the project (its numbers are loaded as they are) or Pick the file's system. The project has none, and the file is in a projected system in your project's units (a state plane zone in US survey feet for a feet project, for example): the dialog offers Use that system as the project's coordinate system. It is set in the same undo step as the import. Nothing in the project moves; the system only says what the numbers mean. You can pick another system for the project instead, and the layers are converted into it. The project has none, and the file is in latitude and longitude (every GeoJSON) or in other units: Import stays grayed out until you choose the project's coordinate system. The picker starts near the data. Neither the project nor the files name a system: the numbers are loaded as they are, and the dialog says so. Elevations are converted too: GeoJSON heights are metres (the GeoJSON standard says so); other files' heights are taken in the unit of their own system. Positions in a GeoJSON file outside ±180° longitude or ±90° latitude are not latitude and longitude. Those features are left out and the dialog says how many. Such a file is usually a projected file saved without its system; ask for it again, or for the shapefile with its .prj. NAD83 is treated as the same as WGS 84 (the system GeoJSON uses); the two differ by a few metres in North America, so GeoJSON data can be off by that much. Where the best shift needs a grid file (NADCON for NAD27, for example), CivilCraft uses a rougher published shift and the coordinate system dialog shows how rough (for NAD27, about 20 m), until you add the grid: see Grid shift files. GIS layers Each row under GIS layers has: a checkbox to show or hide the layer (hidden layers are not picked either); its colour, chosen from the list; a menu (the three dots) with Zoom to, Use for the surface…, Rename… and Remove. Showing, hiding, recolouring, renaming and removing are undo steps. GIS layers are saved in the project file and come back when you open it. They also go out with the plan's PDF plot and its DXF and DWG exports, each on a layer named GIS- and its number. Label features from an attribute In the layer's More menu (the three dots in GIS layers), choose a field under Label with: every feature shows its value on the plan, in the layer's color. Points are labeled beside the point, lines along the middle of their longest part (turned to read from left to right), polygons at their center. Numbers show up to three decimals. Labels are 1.8 mm high on paper at any scale, and they go into the PDF plot, DXF and DWG. Choose No labels to take them away; each change is one step (Ctrl+Z undoes it). A layer labels at most 5,000 features. Look at a feature With Select on, click a feature. Properties shows its layer, which feature it is, its shape (parts, holes, vertices), its elevations, the file it came from and that file's coordinate system, then every attribute in a table. Attributes are read-only: CivilCraft shows GIS data as it came, it does not edit it. Points win over lines when both are under the pointer; inside a polygon, the smallest polygon is picked. Use for the surface Open a layer's menu and choose Use for the surface… (or the button in Properties). What is offered depends on the layer: Layer Can become Lines with elevations (3D lines) Breaklines at their own elevations Lines without elevations but with a number field (contours) Breaklines at the elevation in the field you pick Polygons The surface boundary (the selected polygon, else the largest), or breaklines along their edges Points with elevations, or with an elevation field Survey points, described with the layer's name so they form a point group Each use is one undo step that copies the geometry into the project; the layer itself stays as it is. Elevations are taken in project units. Point files with latitude and longitude A point file whose header names latitude and longitude columns (lat, latitude, lon, long, longitude) is read as degrees, not as northing and easting. The import dialog says so. On Import, the points are converted to the project's coordinate system; a project without one asks you to choose it first. Degrees are never loaded as feet. Rows outside ±90° latitude or ±180° longitude are refused with the reason. The elevation column is taken as it is, in project units. LandXML files that name a coordinate system When a LandXML file you open has a CoordinateSystem element (an EPSG code, a WKT description or a system name), CivilCraft offers it as the project's coordinate system. Nothing moves if you accept; the system only says what the numbers mean. What was checked Every reader was compared with GDAL, the library most GIS programs use to read these files, on files GDAL wrote: shapefiles of every kind with and without elevations, multi-part lines, polygons with holes, empty geometries, text, number, date and true/false attributes, UTF-8 and Windows-1252 text with and without a .cpg, a zip of several shapefiles, GeoJSON with every geometry type, and a GeoPackage with four tables. Every geometry, elevation and attribute matched. Positions converted from latitude and longitude, and from UTM, matched PROJ (the projection library behind GDAL) to 3 mm. Damaged files (cut short, lying about their sizes, bad zips, bad GeoPackages) give one plain message; nothing hangs. Nothing here has been opened in other GIS programs yet, and file geodatabases, KML and KMZ are not read yet (see Not built yet)."},{"slug":"gis-export","scope":"civilcraft","title":"Export as-built data for GIS","summary":"Hand survey points, contours, alignments, pads, pipes and structures to a city or county GIS department as shapefiles, GeoPackage, GeoJSON or KML, in grid coordinates of the project's coordinate system.","section":"tasks","order":141,"keywords":["gis","export","kml","kmz","google earth","as-built","asbuilt","shapefile","shp","zip","geopackage","gpkg","geojson","pipes","structures","utilities","city","county","grid","ground","epsg","prj"],"headings":[{"id":"before-you-export","text":"Before you export","level":2},{"id":"export","text":"Export","level":2},{"id":"what-each-kind-holds","text":"What each kind holds","level":2},{"id":"the-formats","text":"The formats","level":2},{"id":"grid-not-ground","text":"Grid, not ground","level":2},{"id":"what-was-checked","text":"What was checked","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EMany cities and counties ask for as-built data in a GIS format when a project closes: where the pipes and structures went, final grades, the road centerline. \u003Cstrong\u003EGIS › Export\u003C/strong\u003E writes what CivilCraft holds as GIS layers, one per kind of object.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"before-you-export\"\u003EBefore you export\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet the project&#039;s coordinate system in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECoordinates\u003C/span\u003E. A GIS department needs to know where the numbers are; without a system the files carry the project&#039;s own numbers and name none.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECheck the surface, alignments, pads and pipe network the way you want them delivered.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAsk the department which format and which coordinate system they want. Many still ask for shapefiles in the local state plane zone; newer systems take GeoPackage.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"export\"\u003EExport\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cstrong\u003EGIS\u003C/strong\u003E group.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cstrong\u003EWhat\u003C/strong\u003E, tick the kinds to send. Each shows how many features it has; kinds with nothing yet are grayed out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cstrong\u003EAs\u003C/strong\u003E, pick the format.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERead the notes about coordinates and units, then click the download button. The status line says what went in, and any field names that were shortened.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe dialog also holds the elevation grid export (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESurface as a GeoTIFF elevation grid\u003C/span\u003E); see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/elevation-grids-and-lidar\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/elevation-grids-and-lidar\"\u003EElevation grids and LiDAR\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-each-kind-holds\"\u003EWhat each kind holds\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKind\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EShape\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFields\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESurvey points\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPoints with elevation\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E point number, \u003Ccode\u003EN\u003C/code\u003E northing, \u003Ccode\u003EE\u003C/code\u003E easting, \u003Ccode\u003EZ\u003C/code\u003E elevation, \u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E description\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EContours of existing ground, of the graded surface\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELines at their elevation\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EELEV\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBreaklines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELines with elevations\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ENAME\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESurface boundary\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPolygon (the drawn boundary, else the surface&#039;s edge)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ENAME\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAlignments\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECenterlines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ENAME\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ELENGTH\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESTA_START\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESTA_END\u003C/code\u003E (stations as plain numbers)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPads\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPolygons at the pad elevation\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ENAME\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EELEV\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EAREA\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDaylight lines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELines with elevations (pads and corridors)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ENAME\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECorridor feature lines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELines with elevations: center, edges of pavement, shoulders\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ENAME\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ECODE\u003C/code\u003E (CL, EOP_L, EOP_R, SH_L, SH_R)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPipes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELines from the upper to the lower invert\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ENAME\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EFROM\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ETO\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDIA\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDIA_UNIT\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EINV_UP\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EINV_DN\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESLOPE\u003C/code\u003E (percent), \u003Ccode\u003ELENGTH\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ENETWORK\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStructures\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPoints at the rim\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ENAME\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ERIM\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EINVERT\u003C/code\u003E (the lowest pipe invert there), \u003Ccode\u003ESUMP\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDIA\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDIA_UNIT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEach GIS layer\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAs imported\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIts own fields\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELengths, stations and elevations are in the project&#039;s units (US survey feet or metres). Pipe and structure diameters are in inches for feet projects and millimetres for metre projects; \u003Ccode\u003EDIA_UNIT\u003C/code\u003E says which.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-formats\"\u003EThe formats\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EShapefile\u003C/strong\u003E: one \u003Ccode\u003E.zip\u003C/code\u003E with a shapefile per kind (\u003Ccode\u003E.shp\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.shx\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.dbf\u003C/code\u003E), a \u003Ccode\u003E.cpg\u003C/code\u003E saying the text is UTF-8, and a \u003Ccode\u003E.prj\u003C/code\u003E naming the coordinate system. Shapes with elevations are written as the shapefile&#039;s Z types. Field names longer than 10 characters are shortened, as the format requires, and the status line lists them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EGeoPackage\u003C/strong\u003E: one \u003Ccode\u003E.gpkg\u003C/code\u003E file with a table per kind, the coordinate system stored with its EPSG code. One file instead of many, long field names kept.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EKML\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003EKMZ\u003C/strong\u003E (KML zipped): one file of latitude and longitude for Google Earth and web maps, a folder per kind, each feature a placemark named from its NAME field with every field as extended data; heights in meters (altitude mode absolute). Like GeoJSON, it needs the project&#039;s coordinate system.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EGeoJSON\u003C/strong\u003E: one file of latitude and longitude (the GeoJSON standard allows nothing else) with every feature carrying a \u003Ccode\u003Ekind\u003C/code\u003E property (\u003Ccode\u003Epipes\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Estructures\u003C/code\u003E, …) so the receiver can split them. Heights are converted to metres, as the standard asks; they stay on your project&#039;s vertical datum (CivilCraft has no geoid model). A project without a coordinate system cannot export GeoJSON, and the dialog says why.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"grid-not-ground\"\u003EGrid, not ground\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EGIS departments keep their data in \u003Cstrong\u003Egrid\u003C/strong\u003E coordinates of a coordinate system. If your project uses ground or local coordinates (set in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECoordinates\u003C/span\u003E), every coordinate is converted back to grid on the way out: the numbers in the files differ from the plan&#039;s by your scale factor (and rotation, for a local system). The point fields \u003Ccode\u003EN\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EE\u003C/code\u003E hold the grid values too. The dialog reminds you.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESend a short note with the files: the coordinate system and its EPSG code, the vertical datum of your elevations, the units, and what each file is.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-was-checked\"\u003EWhat was checked\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEvery file written was read back by GDAL, the library most GIS programs use to read these files: each shape, elevation and attribute, the feature counts, and the coordinate system as its EPSG code (\u003Ccode\u003E.prj\u003C/code\u003E and GeoPackage). Our own readers read them back the same.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGeoJSON files follow RFC 7946: longitude before latitude, no \u003Ccode\u003Ecrs\u003C/code\u003E member, outer rings counter-clockwise and holes clockwise; positions matched PROJ to 2 mm.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA ground-coordinate project was exported and every point checked against its grid coordinates.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENothing here has been opened in other GIS programs yet. Check the first delivery with the department before relying on it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Many cities and counties ask for as-built data in a GIS format when a project closes: where the pipes and structures went, final grades, the road centerline. GIS › Export writes what CivilCraft holds as GIS layers, one per kind of object. Before you export Set the project's coordinate system in Coordinates. A GIS department needs to know where the numbers are; without a system the files carry the project's own numbers and name none. Check the surface, alignments, pads and pipe network the way you want them delivered. Ask the department which format and which coordinate system they want. Many still ask for shapefiles in the local state plane zone; newer systems take GeoPackage. Export Click Export in the GIS group. Under What, tick the kinds to send. Each shows how many features it has; kinds with nothing yet are grayed out. Under As, pick the format. Read the notes about coordinates and units, then click the download button. The status line says what went in, and any field names that were shortened. The dialog also holds the elevation grid export (Surface as a GeoTIFF elevation grid); see Elevation grids and LiDAR. What each kind holds Kind Shape Fields Survey points Points with elevation P point number, N northing, E easting, Z elevation, D description Contours of existing ground, of the graded surface Lines at their elevation ELEV Breaklines Lines with elevations NAME Surface boundary Polygon (the drawn boundary, else the surface's edge) NAME Alignments Centerlines NAME, LENGTH, STA_START, STA_END (stations as plain numbers) Pads Polygons at the pad elevation NAME, ELEV, AREA Daylight lines Lines with elevations (pads and corridors) NAME Corridor feature lines Lines with elevations: center, edges of pavement, shoulders NAME, CODE (CL, EOP_L, EOP_R, SH_L, SH_R) Pipes Lines from the upper to the lower invert NAME, FROM, TO, DIA, DIA_UNIT, INV_UP, INV_DN, SLOPE (percent), LENGTH, NETWORK Structures Points at the rim NAME, RIM, INVERT (the lowest pipe invert there), SUMP, DIA, DIA_UNIT Each GIS layer As imported Its own fields Lengths, stations and elevations are in the project's units (US survey feet or metres). Pipe and structure diameters are in inches for feet projects and millimetres for metre projects; DIA_UNIT says which. The formats Shapefile: one .zip with a shapefile per kind (.shp, .shx, .dbf), a .cpg saying the text is UTF-8, and a .prj naming the coordinate system. Shapes with elevations are written as the shapefile's Z types. Field names longer than 10 characters are shortened, as the format requires, and the status line lists them. GeoPackage: one .gpkg file with a table per kind, the coordinate system stored with its EPSG code. One file instead of many, long field names kept. KML or KMZ (KML zipped): one file of latitude and longitude for Google Earth and web maps, a folder per kind, each feature a placemark named from its NAME field with every field as extended data; heights in meters (altitude mode absolute). Like GeoJSON, it needs the project's coordinate system. GeoJSON: one file of latitude and longitude (the GeoJSON standard allows nothing else) with every feature carrying a kind property (pipes, structures, …) so the receiver can split them. Heights are converted to metres, as the standard asks; they stay on your project's vertical datum (CivilCraft has no geoid model). A project without a coordinate system cannot export GeoJSON, and the dialog says why. Grid, not ground GIS departments keep their data in grid coordinates of a coordinate system. If your project uses ground or local coordinates (set in Coordinates), every coordinate is converted back to grid on the way out: the numbers in the files differ from the plan's by your scale factor (and rotation, for a local system). The point fields N and E hold the grid values too. The dialog reminds you. Send a short note with the files: the coordinate system and its EPSG code, the vertical datum of your elevations, the units, and what each file is. What was checked Every file written was read back by GDAL, the library most GIS programs use to read these files: each shape, elevation and attribute, the feature counts, and the coordinate system as its EPSG code (.prj and GeoPackage). Our own readers read them back the same. GeoJSON files follow RFC 7946: longitude before latitude, no crs member, outer rings counter-clockwise and holes clockwise; positions matched PROJ to 2 mm. A ground-coordinate project was exported and every point checked against its grid coordinates. Nothing here has been opened in other GIS programs yet. Check the first delivery with the department before relying on it."},{"slug":"work-live","scope":"civilcraft","title":"Design one site together, live","summary":"What changes in CivilCraft while you are in a CadCraft Studio live session: everyone's points, alignments, pads and parcels in one project, the surface built in every window, your own undo, and joining from a browser on the same network.","section":"tasks","order":145,"keywords":["live","studio live","together","collaborate","session","share live","join","points","alignment","surface","tin","undo","host"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"everyone-works-at-once","text":"Everyone works at once","level":2},{"id":"undo-undoes-your-own-work","text":"Undo undoes your own work","level":2},{"id":"join-from-a-browser","text":"Join from a browser","level":2},{"id":"when-the-session-ends","text":"When the session ends","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat happens to a project while several people work on it at the same time. CadCraft Studio starts and joins the session (Share live, Join); this page is about the project itself.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"everyone-works-at-once\"\u003EEveryone works at once\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe host&#039;s points and design come to everyone who joins. Edit points, draw alignments, pads, feature lines and parcels as usual. Your own edits show at once; other people&#039;s appear a moment after they make them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPoints\u003C/strong\u003E merge one by one: two people moving two points both keep their changes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAlignments, pads, feature lines and parcels\u003C/strong\u003E merge per object and setting; the same setting goes to the later change.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe surface\u003C/strong\u003E is built in every window from the same points and breaklines, so contours and volumes agree.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENot shared in a session: a surface loaded from a grid file, GIS layers&#039; features, map pictures and grid shift files. They stay in the window that opened them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"undo-undoes-your-own-work\"\u003EUndo undoes your own work\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes your own last edit and leaves everyone else&#039;s alone. When someone changed the same thing since, nothing is undone and the status bar says who.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"join-from-a-browser\"\u003EJoin from a browser\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA teammate without Studio can scan the picture under the host&#039;s code with a phone, tablet or computer on the same network. CivilCraft opens in the browser and fills with the host&#039;s project once the host lets you in. Invite links, for joining from another network, are not open yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-the-session-ends\"\u003EWhen the session ends\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOnly the host saves the project. Everyone else keeps an unsaved copy: save it if you want to keep it.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn What happens to a project while several people work on it at the same time. CadCraft Studio starts and joins the session (Share live, Join); this page is about the project itself. Everyone works at once The host's points and design come to everyone who joins. Edit points, draw alignments, pads, feature lines and parcels as usual. Your own edits show at once; other people's appear a moment after they make them. Points merge one by one: two people moving two points both keep their changes. Alignments, pads, feature lines and parcels merge per object and setting; the same setting goes to the later change. The surface is built in every window from the same points and breaklines, so contours and volumes agree. Not shared in a session: a surface loaded from a grid file, GIS layers' features, map pictures and grid shift files. They stay in the window that opened them. Undo undoes your own work Ctrl+Z undoes your own last edit and leaves everyone else's alone. When someone changed the same thing since, nothing is undone and the status bar says who. Join from a browser A teammate without Studio can scan the picture under the host's code with a phone, tablet or computer on the same network. CivilCraft opens in the browser and fills with the host's project once the host lets you in. Invite links, for joining from another network, are not open yet. When the session ends Only the host saves the project. Everyone else keeps an unsaved copy: save it if you want to keep it."},{"slug":"coming-from","scope":"civilcraft","title":"Coming from other civil software","summary":"How familiar civil design workflows map to CivilCraft, and the differences that trip up experienced users in the first hour.","section":"switching","order":10,"keywords":["switching","migrate","familiar","differences","workflow","commands","mapping","cogo","corridor","grading","pipe network","styles","command line"],"headings":[{"id":"workflow-map","text":"Workflow map","level":2},{"id":"differences-that-trip-people-up","text":"Differences that trip people up","level":2},{"id":"what-is-missing","text":"What is missing","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EIf you have used Civil 3D, you already know the ideas in CivilCraft: points and point groups, TIN surfaces with breaklines and boundaries, alignments by PI, profiles by PVI, assemblies swept into corridors, grading to daylight, gravity pipe networks, LandXML. What is different is almost everything around them: there is no drawing file, no command line (unless you pick the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrafting classic\u003C/span\u003E menu style), no styles, no Prospector and no settings dialogs. CivilCraft is a small, focused tool in a browser, and in this build it does a fraction of what a full civil package does. This page maps what you know to what is here.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf you like the ribbon of Civil 3D, pick the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECivil ribbon\u003C/span\u003E menu style in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECivilCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/span\u003E: Home, Surfaces, Modify, Geolocation, View and Output tabs, the middle button pans, and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E with the middle button orbits the 3D view. If you type commands, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrafting classic\u003C/span\u003E adds a command line (ALIGNMENT, PAD, PROFILE, ZOOM …). See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/menus-keys-and-mouse\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/menus-keys-and-mouse\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"workflow-map\"\u003EWorkflow map\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat you want to do\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFamiliar way\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIn CivilCraft\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBring in survey points\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EImport points with a point file format\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E, then check the column order in \u003Cstrong\u003EImport points\u003C/strong\u003E (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/import-points\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/import-points\"\u003EImport survey points\u003C/a\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESort points\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPoint groups, description keys\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAutomatic groups by the first word of the description; no description keys\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit a point\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPoint editor, grips\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot available yet: fix the file and import again\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBuild existing ground\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECreate a surface, add point groups as data\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAutomatic: the surface rebuilds whenever its inputs change\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdd breaklines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdd breaklines from polylines or feature lines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESet a point group to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBreakline\u003C/span\u003E, or click points with the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBreakline\u003C/span\u003E tool (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"B\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETrim the surface\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOuter boundary, hide boundary, maximum triangle length\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBoundary\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"O\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, outer only) and \u003Cstrong\u003ELongest outside edge\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShow contours\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESurface style\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDisplay\u003C/strong\u003E in the left panel: interval, major every, labels, bands, slope arrows\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELay out a centerline\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAlignment creation tools, curve and spiral settings\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAlignment\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"A\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E): click PIs, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E; radii in the PI table; no spirals\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECreate a profile view\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EProfile view wizard, bands\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProfile\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E): one profile view under the plan, always fitted\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDesign the grade\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EProfile layout tools\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStart from the ground\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd PVIs\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"I\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), drag PVIs, type \u003Cstrong\u003ECurve L\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBuild a road\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAssembly, subassemblies, corridor, targets, regions\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETick \u003Cstrong\u003ESweep an assembly along the design profile\u003C/strong\u003E under the alignment; one symmetric lane, shoulder and side slope\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGrade a pad\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFeature lines, grading groups, grading criteria\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPad\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"G\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E): a flat pad with cut and fill slopes to daylight\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECompute earthwork\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EVolume surfaces, volumes dashboard\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEarthwork\u003C/strong\u003E in the right panel, grid method, updates live\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELay out storm or sanitary\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPipe network with a parts list, rules\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPipes\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E): click structures downstream; three rules you set; automatic inverts\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExchange with others\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELandXML import and export\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E a LandXML file; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELandXML\u003C/span\u003E to export (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/landxml\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/landxml\"\u003EExchange LandXML\u003C/a\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESend linework to CAD\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExport to DWG\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDWG\u003C/span\u003E (2000 to 2018 format) or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDXF\u003C/span\u003E (R12)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlot\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELayouts, viewports, sheet sets\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPDF\u003C/span\u003E: one landscape sheet of the plan at a chosen scale\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShare work between files\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EData shortcuts, references\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot available: one project per file\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUndo\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUndo\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, 200 steps; display settings are not undo steps\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"differences-that-trip-people-up\"\u003EDifferences that trip people up\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThere is no command line.\u003C/strong\u003E Tools are toolbar buttons and single letters: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"A\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E alignment, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"G\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E pad, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E pipes, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"B\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E breakline, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"O\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E boundary, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E profile, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"I\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E add PVIs, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"V\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E select, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E fit. Typing a command name does nothing. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/keyboard\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/keyboard\"\u003EKeys and mouse\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEnter finishes; double-click is risky.\u003C/strong\u003E Click the last point once, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. In this build a double-click finishes too, but drops the point you double-clicked. Right-click does not finish or repeat; it only pans when you drag with it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEverything is dynamic, all the time.\u003C/strong\u003E Change a point group, a radius, a PVI or a pad slope, and the surface, contours, corridor, daylight lines and volumes update immediately. There is nothing to rebuild and nothing goes out of date.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENothing is a drawing object.\u003C/strong\u003E The plan is a view of the design, not a drawing you can edit with CAD commands. You cannot add your own lines or text, change a layer&#039;s color, or explode anything. Export to DXF and finish the drawing in a CAD program.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENo styles.\u003C/strong\u003E Colors, line weights, label sizes and layer names are fixed (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/plan-layers\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/plan-layers\"\u003EPlan layers\u003C/a\u003E). Paper and Model in the app bar switch between a light and a dark screen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOne of each.\u003C/strong\u003E One existing ground surface, one graded result, one pipe network per project. Many alignments and pads are fine.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUnits are labels.\u003C/strong\u003E Choosing \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUS feet\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMetres\u003C/span\u003E does not convert anything. There is no drawing scale. A coordinate system is optional: set one under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECoordinates\u003C/span\u003E and nothing moves, it only says what the numbers mean (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/coordinate-systems\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/coordinate-systems\"\u003ESet the project&#039;s coordinate system\u003C/a\u003E). Feet are treated as US survey feet when written to LandXML.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBreakline groups join in point-number order.\u003C/strong\u003E A group set to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBreakline\u003C/span\u003E becomes one line through all its points, in number order. Shared codes (two flowlines both coded &quot;FL&quot;) make one zig-zag line; draw those by hand.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EStations look the same, metres differ.\u003C/strong\u003E Feet use 100 ft stations (12+34.56); metres use 1000 m stations (1+234.567).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDelete on a Mac.\u003C/strong\u003E The Mac key labeled delete is Backspace to the browser. Use Fn and delete, or the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E button, to delete a selection.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFiles are downloads.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E downloads a \u003Ccode\u003E.civilcraft.json\u003C/code\u003E file each time; there is no &quot;save over the open file&quot; and no autosave. Opening a point file replaces the points without asking. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/files\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/files\"\u003EFiles you can open, save and export\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERules are yours.\u003C/strong\u003E Pipe network rules and the corridor assembly start with placeholder values that are not a standard. Set your own before trusting any check.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EVolumes are grid-based.\u003C/strong\u003E Cut and fill come from a fine grid between the two surfaces, inside the daylight lines, with no swell or shrink. They are close to, but not the same as, a TIN-to-TIN volume.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-missing\"\u003EWhat is missing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESpirals, curbs and sidewalks, sloped pads, inlet capacity and detention, parts catalogs, pipes in profile, parcel layouts and lot sizing, least-squares adjustment and survey databases, opening DWG, and more. The full list, with what to do instead today, is in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"If you have used Civil 3D, you already know the ideas in CivilCraft: points and point groups, TIN surfaces with breaklines and boundaries, alignments by PI, profiles by PVI, assemblies swept into corridors, grading to daylight, gravity pipe networks, LandXML. What is different is almost everything around them: there is no drawing file, no command line (unless you pick the Drafting classic menu style), no styles, no Prospector and no settings dialogs. CivilCraft is a small, focused tool in a browser, and in this build it does a fraction of what a full civil package does. This page maps what you know to what is here. If you like the ribbon of Civil 3D, pick the Civil ribbon menu style in Settings › CivilCraft › Menus, keys and mouse: Home, Surfaces, Modify, Geolocation, View and Output tabs, the middle button pans, and Shift with the middle button orbits the 3D view. If you type commands, Drafting classic adds a command line (ALIGNMENT, PAD, PROFILE, ZOOM …). See Menus, keys and mouse. Workflow map What you want to do Familiar way In CivilCraft Bring in survey points Import points with a point file format Open, then check the column order in Import points (Import survey points) Sort points Point groups, description keys Automatic groups by the first word of the description; no description keys Edit a point Point editor, grips Not available yet: fix the file and import again Build existing ground Create a surface, add point groups as data Automatic: the surface rebuilds whenever its inputs change Add breaklines Add breaklines from polylines or feature lines Set a point group to Breakline, or click points with the Breakline tool (B) Trim the surface Outer boundary, hide boundary, maximum triangle length Boundary (O, outer only) and Longest outside edge Show contours Surface style Display in the left panel: interval, major every, labels, bands, slope arrows Lay out a centerline Alignment creation tools, curve and spiral settings Alignment (A): click PIs, Enter; radii in the PI table; no spirals Create a profile view Profile view wizard, bands Profile (R): one profile view under the plan, always fitted Design the grade Profile layout tools Start from the ground, Add PVIs (I), drag PVIs, type Curve L Build a road Assembly, subassemblies, corridor, targets, regions Tick Sweep an assembly along the design profile under the alignment; one symmetric lane, shoulder and side slope Grade a pad Feature lines, grading groups, grading criteria Pad (G): a flat pad with cut and fill slopes to daylight Compute earthwork Volume surfaces, volumes dashboard Earthwork in the right panel, grid method, updates live Lay out storm or sanitary Pipe network with a parts list, rules Pipes (P): click structures downstream; three rules you set; automatic inverts Exchange with others LandXML import and export Open a LandXML file; LandXML to export (Exchange LandXML) Send linework to CAD Export to DWG DWG (2000 to 2018 format) or DXF (R12) Plot Layouts, viewports, sheet sets PDF: one landscape sheet of the plan at a chosen scale Share work between files Data shortcuts, references Not available: one project per file Undo Undo Ctrl+Z and Ctrl+Y, 200 steps; display settings are not undo steps Differences that trip people up There is no command line. Tools are toolbar buttons and single letters: A alignment, G pad, P pipes, B breakline, O boundary, R profile, I add PVIs, V select, F fit. Typing a command name does nothing. See Keys and mouse. Enter finishes; double-click is risky. Click the last point once, then press Enter. In this build a double-click finishes too, but drops the point you double-clicked. Right-click does not finish or repeat; it only pans when you drag with it. Everything is dynamic, all the time. Change a point group, a radius, a PVI or a pad slope, and the surface, contours, corridor, daylight lines and volumes update immediately. There is nothing to rebuild and nothing goes out of date. Nothing is a drawing object. The plan is a view of the design, not a drawing you can edit with CAD commands. You cannot add your own lines or text, change a layer's color, or explode anything. Export to DXF and finish the drawing in a CAD program. No styles. Colors, line weights, label sizes and layer names are fixed (see Plan layers). Paper and Model in the app bar switch between a light and a dark screen. One of each. One existing ground surface, one graded result, one pipe network per project. Many alignments and pads are fine. Units are labels. Choosing US feet or Metres does not convert anything. There is no drawing scale. A coordinate system is optional: set one under Coordinates and nothing moves, it only says what the numbers mean (see Set the project's coordinate system). Feet are treated as US survey feet when written to LandXML. Breakline groups join in point-number order. A group set to Breakline becomes one line through all its points, in number order. Shared codes (two flowlines both coded \"FL\") make one zig-zag line; draw those by hand. Stations look the same, metres differ. Feet use 100 ft stations (12+34.56); metres use 1000 m stations (1+234.567). Delete on a Mac. The Mac key labeled delete is Backspace to the browser. Use Fn and delete, or the Delete button, to delete a selection. Files are downloads. Save downloads a .civilcraft.json file each time; there is no \"save over the open file\" and no autosave. Opening a point file replaces the points without asking. See Files you can open, save and export. Rules are yours. Pipe network rules and the corridor assembly start with placeholder values that are not a standard. Set your own before trusting any check. Volumes are grid-based. Cut and fill come from a fine grid between the two surfaces, inside the daylight lines, with no swell or shrink. They are close to, but not the same as, a TIN-to-TIN volume. What is missing Spirals, curbs and sidewalks, sloped pads, inlet capacity and detention, parts catalogs, pipes in profile, parcel layouts and lot sizing, least-squares adjustment and survey databases, opening DWG, and more. The full list, with what to do instead today, is in Not built yet."},{"slug":"keyboard","scope":"civilcraft","title":"Keys and mouse","summary":"Every keyboard shortcut and mouse, trackpad and touch action in CivilCraft, grouped, with the browser and platform differences.","section":"reference","order":10,"keywords":["keyboard","shortcuts","hotkeys","keys","mouse","trackpad","touch","pan","zoom","pinch","wheel","right-click","mac","command key"],"headings":[{"id":"files-and-editing","text":"Files and editing","level":2},{"id":"tools","text":"Tools","level":2},{"id":"while-drawing","text":"While drawing","level":2},{"id":"plan-view","text":"Plan view","level":2},{"id":"profile-view","text":"Profile view","level":2},{"id":"screen-and-help","text":"Screen and help","level":2},{"id":"mouse-trackpad-and-touch","text":"Mouse, trackpad and touch","level":2},{"id":"browser-keys-that-win","text":"Browser keys that win","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ESingle-letter keys work when you are not typing in a box. Shortcuts with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E use Command on a Mac and Ctrl elsewhere. See also \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\"\u003EKeys in the browser\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"files-and-editing\"\u003EFiles and editing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EAction\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen a point file, LandXML or project\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave the project (downloads a \u003Ccode\u003E.civilcraft.json\u003C/code\u003E file)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command P\"\u003E⌘P\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+P\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlot the plan to PDF (opens the plot dialog, not the browser&#039;s print)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUndo\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERedo\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDelete the selected survey points, alignment, pad, structure, pipe or PVI\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECopy the selected survey points (also as point rows for a spreadsheet)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command X\"\u003E⌘X\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+X\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECut the selected survey points\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPaste points: they follow the pointer, click where they go\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command V\"\u003E⇧⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPaste in place: at the points&#039; own coordinates\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDuplicate the selected points: a copy follows the pointer\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect every survey point\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect none (and cancel a paste)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command P\"\u003E⌘P\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+P\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E work even while you are typing in a box. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E in a text box undo your typing instead, as usual, and so do \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command X\"\u003E⌘X\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+X\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: in a box they copy, cut, paste and select its text. With text selected on the page (in a panel), \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E copies that text. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command Z\"\u003E⇧⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E does nothing in CivilCraft: use \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E button.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe Mac key labeled \u003Cstrong\u003Edelete\u003C/strong\u003E is \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to the browser. To delete a selection, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (Fn and delete), or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"tools\"\u003ETools\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETool\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"V\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"A\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAlignment\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"G\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPad\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPipes\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"B\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBreakline\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"O\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBoundary\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShow or hide the profile view\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"I\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd PVIs\u003C/span\u003E on or off (opens the profile view if it is closed)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E: show everything\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"D\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E: show the site in 3D, or go back to the plan\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"E\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShow or hide grades and elevations along every feature line (on the screen only)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPressing the key of the active tool again goes back to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E. Letters with Shift do nothing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"while-drawing\"\u003EWhile drawing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EAction\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFinish the alignment, pad, pipe run, breakline or boundary\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERemove the last point clicked\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"C\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFeature line: turn arcs on or off (after a vertex, click a point on the curve, then its end)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECancel the drawing (or the paste); pressed again (or with nothing drawn), go back to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E and clear the selection\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"plan-view\"\u003EPlan view\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey or action\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EResult\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWheel\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom in or out at the pointer\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag on empty space\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan (in any tool; a short click still clicks; with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E held it draws a selection box instead)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with the middle button\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with the right button\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan (no menu opens)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and drag\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect (with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E), or place a point (with a drawing tool)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and click\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdd a survey point to the selection, or take it out\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and drag\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraw a box: the survey points inside it are added to the selection. Drag round them for a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/lasso-selection\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/lasso-selection\"\u003Elasso\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while drawing a lasso\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWindow, then crossing, then fence\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick while pasting\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPut the pasted points there (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E puts them at their own coordinates)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag a grip\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMove a PI, pad corner or structure (one undo step)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↑\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↓\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"←\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"→\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan, when the plan has the focus (click it first)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"+\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"−\"\u003E−\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E−\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom in and out at the middle, when the plan has the focus\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe = key zooms in like \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"+\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, so you do not need Shift.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"profile-view\"\u003EProfile view\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EAction\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EResult\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd PVIs\u003C/span\u003E on\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdd a PVI there\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick a PVI\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag a PVI\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMove it (one undo step)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERemove the selected PVI (Backspace only when the profile view has the focus)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe profile view does not zoom or pan.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"screen-and-help\"\u003EScreen and help\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EAction\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen or close this help (the side panel); \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHelp\u003C/span\u003E in the app bar does the same\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClose the help panel when it is open\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECycle the screen modes: full screen, full screen with the bars hidden, normal\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELeave full screen (the browser&#039;s own key)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E reaches the help before CivilCraft sees it, so it works in any tool. On most Mac keyboards, press fn with F1, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHelp\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E uses the Control key on every platform, including a Mac. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/help\" data-topic=\"basics/help\"\u003EUsing help\u003C/a\u003E for how to search this help.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mouse-trackpad-and-touch\"\u003EMouse, trackpad and touch\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:mouse\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMouse\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWheel zooms at the pointer. A mouse with a free-spinning wheel zooms quickly: give it short turns.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPan with any drag on empty space, the middle button, the right button, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and drag.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a Mac trackpad:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETwo-finger scrolling \u003Cstrong\u003Ezooms\u003C/strong\u003E (it does not pan), and pinching zooms too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo pan, press and drag on empty space with one finger, or hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and drag.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA two-finger click (right-click) opens no menu and does nothing on its own.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a touch screen:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe first touch makes buttons and menu rows finger-sized.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETap to select or to place a point; one finger dragged on empty space pans. A tap may wobble a little: it only pans after the finger moves about 10 pixels.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETwo fingers pinch to zoom and move together to pan.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag a grip or a PVI with one finger. Grips take a finger&#039;s width round them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhile you draw an alignment, pad, breakline, boundary, pipe run, feature line or parcel, a bar over the plan offers \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFinish\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo point\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECancel\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E). A long press (half a second without moving) also finishes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E, a long press on a survey point adds it to the selection, or takes it out, as \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click does.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the 3D view, one finger orbits, two pinch and pan, and a tap picks.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThere is no hover on touch, so the status bar readouts update only while a finger is down.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"browser-keys-that-win\"\u003EBrowser keys that win\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome keys belong to the browser and never reach CivilCraft, for example \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command W\"\u003E⌘W\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+W\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (close tab), \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command T\"\u003E⌘T\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+T\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (new tab), \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command N\"\u003E⌘N\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+N\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (new window) and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F11\"\u003EF11\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF11\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (full screen in Windows and Linux browsers). CivilCraft does not use them. If a shortcut opens something in the browser instead, click the plan once so the page has the focus, and try again.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Single-letter keys work when you are not typing in a box. Shortcuts with Ctrl use Command on a Mac and Ctrl elsewhere. See also Keys in the browser. Files and editing Key Action Ctrl+O Open a point file, LandXML or project Ctrl+S Save the project (downloads a .civilcraft.json file) Ctrl+P Plot the plan to PDF (opens the plot dialog, not the browser's print) Ctrl+Z Undo Ctrl+Y Redo Delete Delete the selected survey points, alignment, pad, structure, pipe or PVI Ctrl+C Copy the selected survey points (also as point rows for a spreadsheet) Ctrl+X Cut the selected survey points Ctrl+V Paste points: they follow the pointer, click where they go Ctrl+Shift+V Paste in place: at the points' own coordinates Ctrl+D Duplicate the selected points: a copy follows the pointer Ctrl+A Select every survey point Esc Select none (and cancel a paste) Ctrl+S, Ctrl+O and Ctrl+P work even while you are typing in a box. Ctrl+Z and Ctrl+Y in a text box undo your typing instead, as usual, and so do Ctrl+C, Ctrl+X, Ctrl+V and Ctrl+A: in a box they copy, cut, paste and select its text. With text selected on the page (in a panel), Ctrl+C copies that text. Ctrl+Shift+Z does nothing in CivilCraft: use Ctrl+Y or the Redo button. The Mac key labeled delete is Backspace to the browser. To delete a selection, press Delete (Fn and delete), or choose Delete in the toolbar's More menu. Tools Key Tool V Select A Alignment G Pad P Pipes B Breakline O Boundary R Show or hide the profile view I Turn Add PVIs on or off (opens the profile view if it is closed) F Fit: show everything D 3D: show the site in 3D, or go back to the plan E Show or hide grades and elevations along every feature line (on the screen only) Pressing the key of the active tool again goes back to Select. Letters with Shift do nothing. While drawing Key Action Enter Finish the alignment, pad, pipe run, breakline or boundary Backspace Remove the last point clicked C Feature line: turn arcs on or off (after a vertex, click a point on the curve, then its end) Esc Cancel the drawing (or the paste); pressed again (or with nothing drawn), go back to Select and clear the selection Plan view Key or action Result Wheel Zoom in or out at the pointer Drag on empty space Pan (in any tool; a short click still clicks; with Shift held it draws a selection box instead) Drag with the middle button Pan Drag with the right button Pan (no menu opens) Space and drag Pan Click Select (with Select), or place a point (with a drawing tool) Shift and click Add a survey point to the selection, or take it out Shift and drag Draw a box: the survey points inside it are added to the selection. Drag round them for a lasso Space while drawing a lasso Window, then crossing, then fence Click while pasting Put the pasted points there (Enter puts them at their own coordinates) Drag a grip Move a PI, pad corner or structure (one undo step) ↑ ↓ ← → Pan, when the plan has the focus (click it first) + and − Zoom in and out at the middle, when the plan has the focus The = key zooms in like +, so you do not need Shift. Profile view Action Result Click with Add PVIs on Add a PVI there Click a PVI Select it Drag a PVI Move it (one undo step) Delete or Backspace Remove the selected PVI (Backspace only when the profile view has the focus) The profile view does not zoom or pan. Screen and help Key Action F1 Open or close this help (the side panel); Help in the app bar does the same Esc Close the help panel when it is open Ctrl+Shift+F Cycle the screen modes: full screen, full screen with the bars hidden, normal Esc Leave full screen (the browser's own key) F1 reaches the help before CivilCraft sees it, so it works in any tool. On most Mac keyboards, press fn with F1, or click Help. Ctrl+Shift+F uses the Control key on every platform, including a Mac. See Using help for how to search this help. Mouse, trackpad and touch Wheel zooms at the pointer. A mouse with a free-spinning wheel zooms quickly: give it short turns. Pan with any drag on empty space, the middle button, the right button, or Space and drag. On a Mac trackpad: Two-finger scrolling zooms (it does not pan), and pinching zooms too. To pan, press and drag on empty space with one finger, or hold Space and drag. A two-finger click (right-click) opens no menu and does nothing on its own. On a touch screen: The first touch makes buttons and menu rows finger-sized. Tap to select or to place a point; one finger dragged on empty space pans. A tap may wobble a little: it only pans after the finger moves about 10 pixels. Two fingers pinch to zoom and move together to pan. Drag a grip or a PVI with one finger. Grips take a finger's width round them. While you draw an alignment, pad, breakline, boundary, pipe run, feature line or parcel, a bar over the plan offers Finish (Enter), Undo point (Backspace) and Cancel (Esc). A long press (half a second without moving) also finishes. In Select, a long press on a survey point adds it to the selection, or takes it out, as Shift+click does. In the 3D view, one finger orbits, two pinch and pan, and a tap picks. There is no hover on touch, so the status bar readouts update only while a finger is down. Browser keys that win Some keys belong to the browser and never reach CivilCraft, for example Ctrl+W (close tab), Ctrl+T (new tab), Ctrl+N (new window) and F11 (full screen in Windows and Linux browsers). CivilCraft does not use them. If a shortcut opens something in the browser instead, click the plan once so the page has the focus, and try again."},{"slug":"menus-keys-and-mouse","scope":"civilcraft","title":"Menus, keys and mouse","summary":"Pick a menu style that matches the way you already work, give any command your own key, move commands between ribbon tabs, and set what the wheel and the mouse buttons do in the plan and in 3D.","section":"reference","order":12,"keywords":["menu style","menus","ribbon","civil ribbon","toolbar","command line","drafting classic","keyboard shortcuts","hotkeys","keys","customize","remap","rebind","mouse","wheel","invert","zoom direction","middle button","right button","pan","orbit","export","import","switching","familiar"],"headings":[{"id":"the-three-styles","text":"The three styles","level":2},{"id":"make-it-your-own","text":"Make it your own","level":2},{"id":"mouse","text":"Mouse","level":2},{"id":"take-it-with-you","text":"Take it with you","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ECivilCraft&#039;s commands are the same whichever way you reach them. A \u003Cstrong\u003Emenu style\u003C/strong\u003E only decides where they sit on screen, what they are called there and which keys run them, so you can keep the habits you have. Choose one in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECivilCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/span\u003E; the change is immediate, and this browser remembers it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-three-styles\"\u003EThe three styles\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EStyle\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat you get\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECivilCraft\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECivilCraft&#039;s own toolbar (File, Export, Surface, Design, Edit, GIS, View) and single keys: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"A\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E alignment, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"G\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E pad, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E pipes, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"B\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E breakline, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"O\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E boundary, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"L\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E feature line, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E profile, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"X\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E sections, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"E\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E grades, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"D\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E 3D, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E fit, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"V\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E select. The start.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrafting classic\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA small ribbon (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHome\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInsert\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOutput\u003C/span\u003E) and a command line, for people who type commands: ALIGNMENT, PAD, PIPE, FEATURELINE, BREAKLINE, PROFILE, SECTIONS, U (undo), ZOOM, 3D, PLOT, LANDXML, XATTACH, MAPIMPORT, HELP. Start typing anywhere and the letters go to the command line; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E runs, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on nothing repeats the last command, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E clears it. The middle button pans.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECivil ribbon\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETabs of large buttons the way civil design programs group their tools: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHome\u003C/span\u003E (ground data, create design, profile and section views), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESurfaces\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModify\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeolocation\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOutput\u003C/span\u003E, with a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E menu and quick buttons on the right. The single keys stay. The middle button pans the plan; in 3D the middle button pans and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E with the middle button orbits.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery style keeps the \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E keys: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command P\"\u003E⌘P\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+P\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command X\"\u003E⌘X\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+X\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA key that belongs to CivilCraft&#039;s own layout but that your style gave another command does nothing, and the status line says where its command went (&quot;In My keys, Pad is Ctrl+Alt+G&quot;). \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and the arrows always keep their meaning, and while you draw a line its points keep their keys (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-it-your-own\"\u003EMake it your own\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick the style closest to what you want, then click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECustomize…\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeys and menus\u003C/span\u003E, find a command (the search box helps). \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a key\u003C/span\u003E and press the key, or two keys in a row for a two-letter key. Click a key to take it off.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith a ribbon, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhere it sits\u003C/span\u003E moves the command to another tab and panel, to the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E menu, or to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeys only\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMouse\u003C/span\u003E, set the wheel, the buttons that pan the plan, and what each button does in the 3D view.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGive it a name and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESaving a built-in style makes your own copy (&quot;My Civil ribbon&quot;); the built-in one never changes. A key given to two commands, or one that would run before a two-letter key could be typed, is shown in red and has to be fixed before you can save.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mouse\"\u003EMouse\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMouse wheel\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScroll up zooms in\u003C/span\u003E (the start) or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScroll up zooms out\u003C/span\u003E, and how fast, in the plan and in 3D. A trackpad pinch always zooms the way your fingers move.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPan the plan with\u003C/strong\u003E: the middle button, the right button, or both. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and drag pans too. The left button always picks and draws.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDrag in the 3D view\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEach view&#039;s own\u003C/span\u003E keeps CivilCraft&#039;s buttons (left orbits, right pans). Or set each button to orbit, pan, zoom or nothing; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E with the button swaps orbit and pan. A click without dragging still picks.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"take-it-with-you\"\u003ETake it with you\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E saves the style in use as a small \u003Ccode\u003E.ccinput.json\u003C/code\u003E file; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport…\u003C/span\u003E adds one, from another computer or a colleague. An imported file can only point at CivilCraft&#039;s own commands: anything else in it is left out and counted in the status line. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete this style\u003C/span\u003E removes one of your own.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThese settings stay in this browser. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUse this style in every app\u003C/span\u003E under the style cards makes every app open in its matching style (its own toolbar, a ribbon or Drafting classic), with the changes you made in each app. CivilCraft has no menu-bar style: when another app&#039;s menu bar is chosen for every app, CivilCraft keeps its own toolbar.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERelated: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/keyboard\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/keyboard\"\u003EKeyboard and mouse\u003C/a\u003E · \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/settings\"\u003ECivilCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E · \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/coming-from\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/coming-from\"\u003EComing from other civil software\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/p\u003E","text":"CivilCraft's commands are the same whichever way you reach them. A menu style only decides where they sit on screen, what they are called there and which keys run them, so you can keep the habits you have. Choose one in Settings (Ctrl+,) › CivilCraft › Menus, keys and mouse; the change is immediate, and this browser remembers it. The three styles Style What you get CivilCraft CivilCraft's own toolbar (File, Export, Surface, Design, Edit, GIS, View) and single keys: A alignment, G pad, P pipes, B breakline, O boundary, L feature line, R profile, X sections, E grades, D 3D, F fit, V select. The start. Drafting classic A small ribbon (Home, Insert, Output) and a command line, for people who type commands: ALIGNMENT, PAD, PIPE, FEATURELINE, BREAKLINE, PROFILE, SECTIONS, U (undo), ZOOM, 3D, PLOT, LANDXML, XATTACH, MAPIMPORT, HELP. Start typing anywhere and the letters go to the command line; Enter runs, Enter on nothing repeats the last command, Esc clears it. The middle button pans. Civil ribbon Tabs of large buttons the way civil design programs group their tools: Home (ground data, create design, profile and section views), Surfaces, Modify, Geolocation, View and Output, with a File menu and quick buttons on the right. The single keys stay. The middle button pans the plan; in 3D the middle button pans and Shift with the middle button orbits. Every style keeps the Ctrl keys: Ctrl+Z, Ctrl+Y, Ctrl+S, Ctrl+O, Ctrl+P, Ctrl+C, Ctrl+X, Ctrl+V, Ctrl+D, Ctrl+A. A key that belongs to CivilCraft's own layout but that your style gave another command does nothing, and the status line says where its command went (\"In My keys, Pad is Ctrl+Alt+G\"). Delete, Esc, Enter, Tab, Space and the arrows always keep their meaning, and while you draw a line its points keep their keys (Backspace, Enter). Make it your own Pick the style closest to what you want, then click Customize…. On Keys and menus, find a command (the search box helps). Add a key and press the key, or two keys in a row for a two-letter key. Click a key to take it off. With a ribbon, Where it sits moves the command to another tab and panel, to the File menu, or to Keys only. On Mouse, set the wheel, the buttons that pan the plan, and what each button does in the 3D view. Give it a name and click Save. Saving a built-in style makes your own copy (\"My Civil ribbon\"); the built-in one never changes. A key given to two commands, or one that would run before a two-letter key could be typed, is shown in red and has to be fixed before you can save. Mouse Mouse wheel: Scroll up zooms in (the start) or Scroll up zooms out, and how fast, in the plan and in 3D. A trackpad pinch always zooms the way your fingers move. Pan the plan with: the middle button, the right button, or both. Space and drag pans too. The left button always picks and draws. Drag in the 3D view: Each view's own keeps CivilCraft's buttons (left orbits, right pans). Or set each button to orbit, pan, zoom or nothing; Shift with the button swaps orbit and pan. A click without dragging still picks. Take it with you Export saves the style in use as a small .ccinput.json file; Import… adds one, from another computer or a colleague. An imported file can only point at CivilCraft's own commands: anything else in it is left out and counted in the status line. Delete this style removes one of your own. These settings stay in this browser. Use this style in every app under the style cards makes every app open in its matching style (its own toolbar, a ribbon or Drafting classic), with the changes you made in each app. CivilCraft has no menu-bar style: when another app's menu bar is chosen for every app, CivilCraft keeps its own toolbar. Related: Keyboard and mouse · CivilCraft settings · Coming from other civil software"},{"slug":"toolbar","scope":"civilcraft","title":"Toolbar reference","summary":"Every toolbar button in CivilCraft, what it does, its key, and when it is grayed out or refuses.","section":"reference","order":20,"keywords":["toolbar","buttons","commands","tools","file","export","surface","design","edit","view","menu"],"headings":[{"id":"file","text":"File","level":2},{"id":"export","text":"Export","level":2},{"id":"surface","text":"Surface","level":2},{"id":"design","text":"Design","level":2},{"id":"edit","text":"Edit","level":2},{"id":"gis","text":"GIS","level":2},{"id":"view","text":"View","level":2},{"id":"buttons-in-the-panels","text":"Buttons in the panels","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe toolbar runs across the top of the app in seven groups. Every button shows its tooltip and key when you hover over it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"file\"\u003EFile\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EButton\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Enone\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStarts an empty project. Asks first if there are unsaved changes.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpens a point file (CSV, TXT), LandXML or a CivilCraft project.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDownloads the project as \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt;.civilcraft.json\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"export\"\u003EExport\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EButton\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELandXML\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Enone\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDownloads \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt;.xml\u003C/code\u003E: points, surfaces, alignments, profiles and the pipe network as LandXML 1.2. &quot;Nothing to export yet.&quot; when the project is empty.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDXF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Enone\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDownloads \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt;.dxf\u003C/code\u003E: the plan linework as R12 DXF.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPDF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command P\"\u003E⌘P\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+P\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpens \u003Cstrong\u003EPlot the plan to PDF\u003C/strong\u003E. &quot;Nothing to plot yet.&quot; when the project is empty.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"surface\"\u003ESurface\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EButton\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBreakline\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"B\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick survey points in order, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to finish. The breakline takes the points&#039; elevations.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBoundary\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"O\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick the corners of the outer boundary, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to finish. Replaces any boundary.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBoth say &quot;There is no surface yet: open survey points first.&quot; when there is no surface.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"design\"\u003EDesign\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EButton\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAlignment\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"A\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick the PIs in order, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to finish.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPad\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"G\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick the pad&#039;s corners, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to finish. Like the surface tools, it warns when there is no surface; a pad grades only on one.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPipes\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick structures from upstream down (click an existing one to join it), \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to finish.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProfile\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShows or hides the profile view under the plan.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"edit\"\u003EEdit\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EButton\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUndoes the last change (up to 200). Grayed out when there is nothing to undo.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERedoes what was undone. Grayed out when there is nothing to redo.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECopies the selected survey points, and puts them on the clipboard as point rows a spreadsheet can take. Grayed out until points are selected.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPastes points: they follow the pointer, and a click puts them down with numbers no other point has. Point rows copied in a spreadsheet paste too.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Enone\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpens the menu below.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EItem\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command X\"\u003E⌘X\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+X\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECopies the selected points and deletes them (one undo step).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAs the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E button.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAs the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E button.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste in place\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command V\"\u003E⇧⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPastes the points at their own coordinates, with numbers no other point has.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuplicate\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA copy of the selected points follows the pointer; click where it goes. The clipboard is left alone.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDeletes the selected survey points, or the selected alignment, pad, structure (with its pipes), pipe or PVI.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect all points\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelects every survey point.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect none\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClears the selection.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn item that cannot act right now is grayed out, and its tooltip says why. Details: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/copy-paste-points\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/copy-paste-points\"\u003ESelect, copy and paste survey points\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUndo covers edits to the design, the surface settings and the survey points (paste, cut, duplicate, delete). It does not cover display settings, and opening or importing any file clears it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"gis\"\u003EGIS\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EButton\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECoordinates\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Enone\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpens the \u003Cstrong\u003ECoordinate system\u003C/strong\u003E dialog: say which system the project is in, or use ground or local coordinates. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/coordinate-systems\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/coordinate-systems\"\u003ESet the project&#039;s coordinate system\u003C/a\u003E and \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/ground-and-local-coordinates\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/ground-and-local-coordinates\"\u003EGround and local coordinates\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Enone\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EImports GIS data: shapefiles, GeoJSON, GeoPackage, elevation grids and LiDAR. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/gis-import\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/gis-import\"\u003EImport GIS data\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Enone\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExports as-built data for a GIS department. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/gis-export\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/gis-export\"\u003EExport as-built data for GIS\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMap\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Enone\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpens the \u003Cstrong\u003EMap layers\u003C/strong\u003E dialog: a georeferenced picture, or map servers you add, drawn under the plan. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/map-layers\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/map-layers\"\u003EShow map layers under the plan\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe GIS tools load the first time you use one of these buttons, open a GIS file, or open a project that has a coordinate system or GIS layers.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"view\"\u003EView\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EButton\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"V\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick to select alignments, pads, structures, pipes and points (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and a click or a drag adds points); drag grips to move PIs, pad corners and structures.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZooms the plan to show the surface, points, alignments and pads (in the 3D view: shows everything).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"D\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShows the site in 3D over the plan, or goes back to the plan. The bar over the 3D view sets the view, exaggeration, colouring and what shows, and exports GLB or OBJ. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/site-3d\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/site-3d\"\u003EView the site in 3D\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"buttons-in-the-panels\"\u003EButtons in the panels\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EButton\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhere\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen the sample site\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe box on an empty plan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELoads the sample survey (needs a connection, unless CivilCraft is saved for offline use)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove the boundary\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELeft panel, Surface\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETakes the outer boundary away\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove drawn breaklines\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELeft panel, Surface\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERemoves every breakline drawn with the tool (not the breakline groups)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERebuild the surface from the points\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELeft panel, Surface (LandXML surface only)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrops the LandXML TIN and builds from the points; cannot be undone\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStart from the ground\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERight panel, Alignment\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStarts a design profile with a PVI on the ground at each end\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProfile view\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERight panel, Alignment\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpens the profile view for this alignment\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd PVIs\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EProfile bar\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhile pressed, a click in the profile adds a PVI\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESet every invert from the rules\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERight panel, Pipe network\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESets every pipe&#039;s inverts from the rules\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESet its inverts from the rules\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERight panel, Pipe\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESets this pipe&#039;s inverts from the rules\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E","text":"The toolbar runs across the top of the app in seven groups. Every button shows its tooltip and key when you hover over it. File Button Key What it does New none Starts an empty project. Asks first if there are unsaved changes. Open Ctrl+O Opens a point file (CSV, TXT), LandXML or a CivilCraft project. Save Ctrl+S Downloads the project as \u003Cname\u003E.civilcraft.json. Export Button Key What it does LandXML none Downloads \u003Cname\u003E.xml: points, surfaces, alignments, profiles and the pipe network as LandXML 1.2. \"Nothing to export yet.\" when the project is empty. DXF none Downloads \u003Cname\u003E.dxf: the plan linework as R12 DXF. PDF Ctrl+P Opens Plot the plan to PDF. \"Nothing to plot yet.\" when the project is empty. Surface Button Key What it does Breakline B Click survey points in order, Enter to finish. The breakline takes the points' elevations. Boundary O Click the corners of the outer boundary, Enter to finish. Replaces any boundary. Both say \"There is no surface yet: open survey points first.\" when there is no surface. Design Button Key What it does Alignment A Click the PIs in order, Enter to finish. Pad G Click the pad's corners, Enter to finish. Like the surface tools, it warns when there is no surface; a pad grades only on one. Pipes P Click structures from upstream down (click an existing one to join it), Enter to finish. Profile R Shows or hides the profile view under the plan. Edit Button Key What it does Undo Ctrl+Z Undoes the last change (up to 200). Grayed out when there is nothing to undo. Redo Ctrl+Y Redoes what was undone. Grayed out when there is nothing to redo. Copy Ctrl+C Copies the selected survey points, and puts them on the clipboard as point rows a spreadsheet can take. Grayed out until points are selected. Paste Ctrl+V Pastes points: they follow the pointer, and a click puts them down with numbers no other point has. Point rows copied in a spreadsheet paste too. More none Opens the menu below. The More menu: Item Key What it does Cut Ctrl+X Copies the selected points and deletes them (one undo step). Copy Ctrl+C As the Copy button. Paste Ctrl+V As the Paste button. Paste in place Ctrl+Shift+V Pastes the points at their own coordinates, with numbers no other point has. Duplicate Ctrl+D A copy of the selected points follows the pointer; click where it goes. The clipboard is left alone. Delete Delete Deletes the selected survey points, or the selected alignment, pad, structure (with its pipes), pipe or PVI. Select all points Ctrl+A Selects every survey point. Select none Esc Clears the selection. An item that cannot act right now is grayed out, and its tooltip says why. Details: Select, copy and paste survey points. Undo covers edits to the design, the surface settings and the survey points (paste, cut, duplicate, delete). It does not cover display settings, and opening or importing any file clears it. GIS Button Key What it does Coordinates none Opens the Coordinate system dialog: say which system the project is in, or use ground or local coordinates. See Set the project's coordinate system and Ground and local coordinates. Import none Imports GIS data: shapefiles, GeoJSON, GeoPackage, elevation grids and LiDAR. See Import GIS data. Export none Exports as-built data for a GIS department. See Export as-built data for GIS. Map none Opens the Map layers dialog: a georeferenced picture, or map servers you add, drawn under the plan. See Show map layers under the plan. The GIS tools load the first time you use one of these buttons, open a GIS file, or open a project that has a coordinate system or GIS layers. View Button Key What it does Select V or Esc Click to select alignments, pads, structures, pipes and points (Shift and a click or a drag adds points); drag grips to move PIs, pad corners and structures. Fit F Zooms the plan to show the surface, points, alignments and pads (in the 3D view: shows everything). 3D D Shows the site in 3D over the plan, or goes back to the plan. The bar over the 3D view sets the view, exaggeration, colouring and what shows, and exports GLB or OBJ. See View the site in 3D. Buttons in the panels Button Where What it does Open the sample site The box on an empty plan Loads the sample survey (needs a connection, unless CivilCraft is saved for offline use) Remove the boundary Left panel, Surface Takes the outer boundary away Remove drawn breaklines Left panel, Surface Removes every breakline drawn with the tool (not the breakline groups) Rebuild the surface from the points Left panel, Surface (LandXML surface only) Drops the LandXML TIN and builds from the points; cannot be undone Start from the ground Right panel, Alignment Starts a design profile with a PVI on the ground at each end Profile view Right panel, Alignment Opens the profile view for this alignment Add PVIs Profile bar While pressed, a click in the profile adds a PVI Set every invert from the rules Right panel, Pipe network Sets every pipe's inverts from the rules Set its inverts from the rules Right panel, Pipe Sets this pipe's inverts from the rules"},{"slug":"settings","scope":"civilcraft","title":"CivilCraft settings","summary":"Choose the column order for copied and pasted point rows and for guessing a file's columns, how pasted points are numbered, what point labels show, the corner indicator, the 3D vertical exaggeration, how latitude and longitude show, and whether map layers come from the internet.","section":"reference","order":25,"keywords":["settings","preferences","pnezd","penzd","column order","point rows","renumber","point numbers","start number","point labels","labels","description","elevation","view cube","axis icon","corner indicator","compass","vertical exaggeration","3d","latitude","longitude","dms","decimal degrees","map layers","internet","privacy"],"headings":[{"id":"point-rows","text":"Point rows","level":2},{"id":"numbers-for-pasted-points","text":"Numbers for pasted points","level":2},{"id":"point-labels","text":"Point labels","level":2},{"id":"corner-indicator","text":"Corner indicator","level":2},{"id":"3d-vertical-exaggeration","text":"3D vertical exaggeration","level":2},{"id":"latitude-and-longitude","text":"Latitude and longitude","level":2},{"id":"map-layers-from-the-internet","text":"Map layers from the internet","level":2},{"id":"assistants","text":"Assistants","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E with the gear in the app bar or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The first tab, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECivilCraft\u003C/span\u003E, holds the settings that belong to this app; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E holds the ones every CadCraft app shares (theme, toolbar, panels). CivilCraft&#039;s settings exist only in the app: you change them here, and this browser remembers them. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset CivilCraft settings\u003C/span\u003E puts every setting back to its start.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"point-rows\"\u003EPoint rows\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EP, N, E, Z, D\u003C/strong\u003E (the start) or \u003Cstrong\u003EP, E, N, Z, D\u003C/strong\u003E: point, northing, easting, elevation, description, or easting before northing. It is used in three places:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECopy\u003C/strong\u003E: the rows CivilCraft puts on the system clipboard for a spreadsheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPaste\u003C/strong\u003E: rows pasted from a spreadsheet or a text file that have no header row.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EImport\u003C/strong\u003E: the guess in the \u003Cstrong\u003EImport points\u003C/strong\u003E dialog when a file has no header row (a file with four columns reads as PENZ, three as ENZ). See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/point-file-formats\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/point-file-formats\"\u003EPoint file formats\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA header row that names its columns always wins, and so does a choice in the import dialog&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003EColumns\u003C/strong\u003E list.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"numbers-for-pasted-points\"\u003ENumbers for pasted points\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EKeep free numbers\u003C/strong\u003E (the start): a pasted point keeps its number when no point has it; only clashing points get new numbers.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAlways new numbers\u003C/strong\u003E: every pasted point gets a new number, even if its own is free.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENew numbers start at\u003C/strong\u003E: leave it empty to count on from one more than the highest number in use. Type a number, for example 5000, to give new points numbers from 5000 up; numbers already in use are skipped. Two points never share a number either way. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/copy-paste-points#point-numbers-never-clash\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/copy-paste-points\" data-hash=\"point-numbers-never-clash\"\u003ESelect, copy and paste survey points\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"point-labels\"\u003EPoint labels\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat a point label shows when \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPoint labels\u003C/span\u003E is on under \u003Cstrong\u003EDisplay\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cstrong\u003ENumber\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ENumber and elevation\u003C/strong\u003E, or \u003Cstrong\u003ENumber, elevation and description\u003C/strong\u003E (the start). The DXF, DWG and PDF exports show the same labels as the screen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"corner-indicator\"\u003ECorner indicator\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat sits in the corner of the plan and the 3D view: \u003Cstrong\u003EView cube\u003C/strong\u003E (the start), \u003Cstrong\u003EAxis icon\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003ENone\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EView cube\u003C/strong\u003E (top right). On the plan it shows the top, with the compass ring: N is grid north (up the plan). Click any other side, edge or corner of the cube, or drag it, and the \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/site-3d\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/site-3d\"\u003E3D view\u003C/a\u003E opens looking from there. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHome\u003C/span\u003E on the plan cube fits the plan.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the 3D view the cube turns with the view: click a side, edge or corner to look from there, drag the cube to orbit, drag the compass ring to turn about the vertical, click a letter to look from that side, and use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHome\u003C/span\u003E for the iso view of the whole site. Looking straight at a side, small arrows step to the next side and curved arrows turn the view a quarter turn.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAxis icon\u003C/strong\u003E (bottom left): a small X (east) and Y (north) marker on the plan, X, Y and Z (up) in 3D.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"3d-vertical-exaggeration\"\u003E3D vertical exaggeration\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow much the 3D view stretches elevations: \u003Cstrong\u003E1×\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003E2×\u003C/strong\u003E (the start), \u003Cstrong\u003E3×\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003E5×\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003E10×\u003C/strong\u003E. The list on the 3D view&#039;s bar is the same setting, so changing either changes both. Volumes, profiles and 3D exports stay at true scale.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"latitude-and-longitude\"\u003ELatitude and longitude\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow the status bar shows the pointer&#039;s latitude and longitude when the project has a coordinate system: \u003Cstrong\u003Edegrees, minutes and seconds\u003C/strong\u003E (the start, for example \u003Ccode\u003E30°18′00.00″ N\u003C/code\u003E) or \u003Cstrong\u003Edecimal degrees\u003C/strong\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003E30.300000° N\u003C/code\u003E). See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/coordinate-systems#latitude-and-longitude-in-the-status-bar\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/coordinate-systems\" data-hash=\"latitude-and-longitude-in-the-status-bar\"\u003ESet the project&#039;s coordinate system\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"map-layers-from-the-internet\"\u003EMap layers from the internet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOff\u003C/strong\u003E (the start) or \u003Cstrong\u003EOn\u003C/strong\u003E. When on, CivilCraft asks the map servers you add for pictures of the area on screen. Your drawing stays on this computer; the server sees the area and your internet address. While it is off, no map server is asked for anything, even when a project you open names one; a georeferenced picture from a file works either way. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/map-layers\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/map-layers\"\u003EShow map layers under the plan\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"assistants\"\u003EAssistants\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELet AI assistants and scripts use CivilCraft\u003C/strong\u003E (off at the start) lets an assistant on your computer reach the open site through CadCraft Studio: \u003Cstrong\u003ERead\u003C/strong\u003E describes the site and lists points, surface elevations and stations; the second switch lets it select survey points. \u003Cstrong\u003EActivity\u003C/strong\u003E lists its calls. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/assistants\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/assistants\"\u003ELet an AI assistant work with your site\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Open Settings with the gear in the app bar or Ctrl+,. The first tab, CivilCraft, holds the settings that belong to this app; General holds the ones every CadCraft app shares (theme, toolbar, panels). CivilCraft's settings exist only in the app: you change them here, and this browser remembers them. Reset CivilCraft settings puts every setting back to its start. Point rows P, N, E, Z, D (the start) or P, E, N, Z, D: point, northing, easting, elevation, description, or easting before northing. It is used in three places: Copy: the rows CivilCraft puts on the system clipboard for a spreadsheet. Paste: rows pasted from a spreadsheet or a text file that have no header row. Import: the guess in the Import points dialog when a file has no header row (a file with four columns reads as PENZ, three as ENZ). See Point file formats. A header row that names its columns always wins, and so does a choice in the import dialog's Columns list. Numbers for pasted points Keep free numbers (the start): a pasted point keeps its number when no point has it; only clashing points get new numbers. Always new numbers: every pasted point gets a new number, even if its own is free. New numbers start at: leave it empty to count on from one more than the highest number in use. Type a number, for example 5000, to give new points numbers from 5000 up; numbers already in use are skipped. Two points never share a number either way. See Select, copy and paste survey points. Point labels What a point label shows when Point labels is on under Display: Number, Number and elevation, or Number, elevation and description (the start). The DXF, DWG and PDF exports show the same labels as the screen. Corner indicator What sits in the corner of the plan and the 3D view: View cube (the start), Axis icon or None. View cube (top right). On the plan it shows the top, with the compass ring: N is grid north (up the plan). Click any other side, edge or corner of the cube, or drag it, and the 3D view opens looking from there. Home on the plan cube fits the plan. In the 3D view the cube turns with the view: click a side, edge or corner to look from there, drag the cube to orbit, drag the compass ring to turn about the vertical, click a letter to look from that side, and use Home for the iso view of the whole site. Looking straight at a side, small arrows step to the next side and curved arrows turn the view a quarter turn. Axis icon (bottom left): a small X (east) and Y (north) marker on the plan, X, Y and Z (up) in 3D. 3D vertical exaggeration How much the 3D view stretches elevations: 1×, 2× (the start), 3×, 5× or 10×. The list on the 3D view's bar is the same setting, so changing either changes both. Volumes, profiles and 3D exports stay at true scale. Latitude and longitude How the status bar shows the pointer's latitude and longitude when the project has a coordinate system: degrees, minutes and seconds (the start, for example 30°18′00.00″ N) or decimal degrees (30.300000° N). See Set the project's coordinate system. Map layers from the internet Off (the start) or On. When on, CivilCraft asks the map servers you add for pictures of the area on screen. Your drawing stays on this computer; the server sees the area and your internet address. While it is off, no map server is asked for anything, even when a project you open names one; a georeferenced picture from a file works either way. See Show map layers under the plan. Assistants Let AI assistants and scripts use CivilCraft (off at the start) lets an assistant on your computer reach the open site through CadCraft Studio: Read describes the site and lists points, surface elevations and stations; the second switch lets it select survey points. Activity lists its calls. See Let an AI assistant work with your site."},{"slug":"point-file-formats","scope":"civilcraft","title":"Point file formats","summary":"The column orders CivilCraft reads, how it finds the separator and the header row, which header names it recognizes, and how rows are checked.","section":"reference","order":30,"keywords":["pnezd","penzd","pnez","penz","nezd","enzd","nez","enz","csv","txt","pnt","xyz","point file format","columns","header","delimiter"],"headings":[{"id":"column-orders","text":"Column orders","level":2},{"id":"separators","text":"Separators","level":2},{"id":"header-row","text":"Header row","level":2},{"id":"guessing-from-the-column-count","text":"Guessing from the column count","level":2},{"id":"how-rows-are-checked","text":"How rows are checked","level":2},{"id":"point-groups","text":"Point groups","level":2},{"id":"writing-points","text":"Writing points","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA point file is plain text, one point per line. CivilCraft reads it in the browser; nothing is uploaded.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"column-orders\"\u003EColumn orders\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe letters name the columns from left to right: \u003Cstrong\u003EP\u003C/strong\u003E point number or name, \u003Cstrong\u003EN\u003C/strong\u003E northing, \u003Cstrong\u003EE\u003C/strong\u003E easting, \u003Cstrong\u003EZ\u003C/strong\u003E elevation, \u003Cstrong\u003ED\u003C/strong\u003E description.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EOrder\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EColumns\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EExample line\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPNEZD\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Epoint, northing, easting, elevation, description\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E101,2080000.00,6150000.00,250.00,GRD\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPENZD\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Epoint, easting, northing, elevation, description\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E101,6150000.00,2080000.00,250.00,GRD\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPNEZ\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Epoint, northing, easting, elevation\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E101,2080000.00,6150000.00,250.00\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPENZ\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Epoint, easting, northing, elevation\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E101,6150000.00,2080000.00,250.00\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENEZD\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Enorthing, easting, elevation, description\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E2080000.00,6150000.00,250.00,GRD\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EENZD\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eeasting, northing, elevation, description\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E6150000.00,2080000.00,250.00,GRD\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENEZ\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Enorthing, easting, elevation\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E2080000.00 6150000.00 250.00\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EENZ\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eeasting, northing, elevation\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E6150000.00 2080000.00 250.00\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOrders without P number the points 1, 2, 3 … in file order. X is easting and Y is northing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"separators\"\u003ESeparators\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECivilCraft looks at the first 20 lines and picks the first of these that appears on at least 60% of them: comma, tab, semicolon. Otherwise it splits on runs of spaces.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn comma, tab and semicolon files, a field in double quotes can hold the separator (\u003Ccode\u003E&quot;FL, NORTH&quot;\u003C/code\u003E), and \u003Ccode\u003E&quot;&quot;\u003C/code\u003E inside quotes is one quote mark.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn space-separated files, a description at the end of the line may contain spaces: everything after the last coordinate column is the description.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELines starting with \u003Ccode\u003E#\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E;\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E//\u003C/code\u003E, and blank lines, are skipped.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA byte-order mark at the start of the file is ignored.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"header-row\"\u003EHeader row\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe first line is treated as a header when fewer than two of its fields are numbers. If every field of the header is a name CivilCraft recognizes, the column order comes from it (&quot;from the header row&quot;). Names are matched without regard to case, spaces, dots, underscores, hyphens and brackets:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EColumn\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ERecognized names\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EP\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ept, pnt, point, point no, point number, number, no, id, name, pt#, point#, #\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EN\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003En, north, northing, y, y coord, lat, latitude\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EE\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ee, east, easting, x, x coord, lon, long, longitude\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZ\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ez, elev, elevation, el, height, h, z coord, rl\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ED\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ed, desc, description, code, raw description, full description, note, notes, feature\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf any header name is not recognized, or the names do not make one of the eight orders, the header is skipped and the order is guessed from the column count.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA header that names both a latitude column (\u003Ccode\u003Elat\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Elatitude\u003C/code\u003E) and a longitude column (\u003Ccode\u003Elon\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Elong\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Elongitude\u003C/code\u003E) is read as degrees, not as northing and easting. On \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport\u003C/span\u003E the points are converted through the project&#039;s coordinate system, and a project without one asks you to choose it first. Rows outside ±90° latitude or ±180° longitude are refused. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/gis-import#point-files-with-latitude-and-longitude\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/gis-import\" data-hash=\"point-files-with-latitude-and-longitude\"\u003EImport GIS data\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"guessing-from-the-column-count\"\u003EGuessing from the column count\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith no usable header and \u003Cstrong\u003EColumns\u003C/strong\u003E left on \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDetect\u003C/span\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E5 or more columns: PNEZD.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E4 columns: PNEZ when the first column is a whole number on every sampled line and the fourth is a number; NEZD otherwise.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E3 columns: NEZ.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPNEZD and PENZD cannot be told apart from the numbers. The guess follows \u003Cstrong\u003EPoint rows\u003C/strong\u003E in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/settings\"\u003ECivilCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E: P, N, E, Z, D at the start, or P, E, N, Z, D (then four columns read as PENZ and three as ENZ). The dialog says &quot;guessed from the column count — check that northing and easting are not swapped&quot;. Pick the right order in \u003Cstrong\u003EColumns\u003C/strong\u003E if the preview looks wrong.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-rows-are-checked\"\u003EHow rows are checked\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA row may leave out the description.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENorthing and easting must be numbers. Elevation may be empty (the point is kept but not used in the surface).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe point number may be any text (letters too, such as \u003Ccode\u003ECP1\u003C/code\u003E), but not empty.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf a point number repeats, the first row is kept and the others are listed as not read.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDescriptions are kept up to 200 characters; control characters become spaces.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUp to 200 unread rows are kept for the dialog (25 shown), and all of them are counted.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"point-groups\"\u003EPoint groups\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA point&#039;s group is the first word of its description in capitals. &quot;GRD&quot;, &quot;grd shot&quot; and &quot;Grd&quot; are all group GRD. A point without a description is in &quot;(no description)&quot;. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/import-points\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/import-points\"\u003EImport survey points\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"writing-points\"\u003EWriting points\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECivilCraft does not export a point text file yet. Points go out in LandXML (as CgPoints), in the project file, and through GIS \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E as a shapefile, GeoPackage or GeoJSON with P, N, E, Z and D fields (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/gis-export\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/gis-export\"\u003EExport as-built data for GIS\u003C/a\u003E). See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"A point file is plain text, one point per line. CivilCraft reads it in the browser; nothing is uploaded. Column orders The letters name the columns from left to right: P point number or name, N northing, E easting, Z elevation, D description. Order Columns Example line PNEZD point, northing, easting, elevation, description 101,2080000.00,6150000.00,250.00,GRD PENZD point, easting, northing, elevation, description 101,6150000.00,2080000.00,250.00,GRD PNEZ point, northing, easting, elevation 101,2080000.00,6150000.00,250.00 PENZ point, easting, northing, elevation 101,6150000.00,2080000.00,250.00 NEZD northing, easting, elevation, description 2080000.00,6150000.00,250.00,GRD ENZD easting, northing, elevation, description 6150000.00,2080000.00,250.00,GRD NEZ northing, easting, elevation 2080000.00 6150000.00 250.00 ENZ easting, northing, elevation 6150000.00 2080000.00 250.00 Orders without P number the points 1, 2, 3 … in file order. X is easting and Y is northing. Separators CivilCraft looks at the first 20 lines and picks the first of these that appears on at least 60% of them: comma, tab, semicolon. Otherwise it splits on runs of spaces. In comma, tab and semicolon files, a field in double quotes can hold the separator (\"FL, NORTH\"), and \"\" inside quotes is one quote mark. In space-separated files, a description at the end of the line may contain spaces: everything after the last coordinate column is the description. Lines starting with #, ; or //, and blank lines, are skipped. A byte-order mark at the start of the file is ignored. Header row The first line is treated as a header when fewer than two of its fields are numbers. If every field of the header is a name CivilCraft recognizes, the column order comes from it (\"from the header row\"). Names are matched without regard to case, spaces, dots, underscores, hyphens and brackets: Column Recognized names P pt, pnt, point, point no, point number, number, no, id, name, pt#, point#, # N n, north, northing, y, y coord, lat, latitude E e, east, easting, x, x coord, lon, long, longitude Z z, elev, elevation, el, height, h, z coord, rl D d, desc, description, code, raw description, full description, note, notes, feature If any header name is not recognized, or the names do not make one of the eight orders, the header is skipped and the order is guessed from the column count. A header that names both a latitude column (lat, latitude) and a longitude column (lon, long, longitude) is read as degrees, not as northing and easting. On Import the points are converted through the project's coordinate system, and a project without one asks you to choose it first. Rows outside ±90° latitude or ±180° longitude are refused. See Import GIS data. Guessing from the column count With no usable header and Columns left on Detect: 5 or more columns: PNEZD. 4 columns: PNEZ when the first column is a whole number on every sampled line and the fourth is a number; NEZD otherwise. 3 columns: NEZ. PNEZD and PENZD cannot be told apart from the numbers. The guess follows Point rows in CivilCraft settings: P, N, E, Z, D at the start, or P, E, N, Z, D (then four columns read as PENZ and three as ENZ). The dialog says \"guessed from the column count — check that northing and easting are not swapped\". Pick the right order in Columns if the preview looks wrong. How rows are checked A row may leave out the description. Northing and easting must be numbers. Elevation may be empty (the point is kept but not used in the surface). The point number may be any text (letters too, such as CP1), but not empty. If a point number repeats, the first row is kept and the others are listed as not read. Descriptions are kept up to 200 characters; control characters become spaces. Up to 200 unread rows are kept for the dialog (25 shown), and all of them are counted. Point groups A point's group is the first word of its description in capitals. \"GRD\", \"grd shot\" and \"Grd\" are all group GRD. A point without a description is in \"(no description)\". See Import survey points. Writing points CivilCraft does not export a point text file yet. Points go out in LandXML (as CgPoints), in the project file, and through GIS Export as a shapefile, GeoPackage or GeoJSON with P, N, E, Z and D fields (Export as-built data for GIS). See Not built yet."},{"slug":"units-and-stations","scope":"civilcraft","title":"Units, stations and number formats","summary":"Feet and metre projects, how stations, grades, side slopes, bearings, areas, volumes and pipe sizes are written and typed, and what changing units does.","section":"reference","order":40,"keywords":["type a length","arithmetic","calculator","sum","fraction","feet and inches","4+7/16","units","feet","us survey feet","metres","meters","metric","imperial","station format","stationing","grade","percent","side slope","h:v","bearing","cubic yards","diameter"],"headings":[{"id":"project-units","text":"Project units","level":2},{"id":"typing-lengths-and-sums","text":"Typing lengths and sums","level":2},{"id":"stations","text":"Stations","level":2},{"id":"grades-and-slopes","text":"Grades and slopes","level":2},{"id":"bearings-and-angles","text":"Bearings and angles","level":2},{"id":"offsets","text":"Offsets","level":2},{"id":"number-display","text":"Number display","level":2},{"id":"coordinates","text":"Coordinates","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"project-units\"\u003EProject units\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA project is in \u003Cstrong\u003EUS feet\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003Emetres\u003C/strong\u003E (\u003Cstrong\u003EUnits\u003C/strong\u003E in the left panel). Every coordinate, elevation, length and station in the project is in that unit.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChanging \u003Cstrong\u003EUnits\u003C/strong\u003E changes the labels, the station format, the defaults and how files are written. It does \u003Cstrong\u003Enot\u003C/strong\u003E convert any number. A site surveyed in feet and switched to metres keeps the same numbers, now read as metres. The status bar says &quot;Units are now metres. Coordinates were not converted.&quot; Set the units to match your data before you design.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat depends on the units:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003E\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EUS feet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMetres\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStation format\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E12+34.56\u003C/code\u003E (100 ft stations)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E1+234.567\u003C/code\u003E (1000 m stations)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EContour intervals offered\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0.25 to 20 ft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0.1 to 10 m\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArea\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Esq ft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Esq m\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EVolume\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ecu yd\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ecu m\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPipe and structure diameter\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Einches\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Emillimetres\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDefault curve radius\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E100 ft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E30 m\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStationing ticks on the plan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eevery 50 ft, labels every 100 ft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eevery 20 m, labels every 100 m\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlot scales\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E1&quot; = 5&#039; to 1&quot; = 1000&#039;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E1:50 to 1:10000\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELandXML linear unit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUSSurveyFoot\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Emeter\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen you open LandXML, the project takes the file&#039;s units. A file in international feet opens as a feet project; the numbers are not changed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"typing-lengths-and-sums\"\u003ETyping lengths and sums\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAny box that takes a length or an elevation reads more than a plain number: press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and the box shows the result in the project&#039;s units.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYou type\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIn a feet project\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIn a metre project\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E12.5\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E12.5 ft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E12.5 m\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039; 6 1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E3 ft 6 in\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E12.5 ft, 12.54 ft, 0.5 ft, 3.5 ft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethe same lengths in metres\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E4 7/16\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E4-7/16\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E4.4375 ft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E4.4375 m\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E450 mm\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1.2 m\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E90 cm\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ein feet\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0.45 m, 1.2 m, 0.9 m\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E4+7/16\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;6&quot; + 3/4&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E(24&#039;-0&quot;)/3\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E2*450mm\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethe sum, in feet\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethe sum, in metres\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUse \u003Ccode\u003E+ - * /\u003C/code\u003E and brackets, and \u003Ccode\u003Eabs\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eround\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eceil\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003Efloor\u003C/code\u003E. A dash written into a length is part of it (\u003Ccode\u003E24&#039;-6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E6-1/2\u003C/code\u003E); with spaces round it, it subtracts (\u003Ccode\u003E24&#039; - 6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E = 23.5 ft). A plain number multiplies or divides a length, and is a length in the project&#039;s units when it is added to one. If CivilCraft can&#039;t read what you typed, the box is marked and the status bar says where.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EStations are not sums: \u003Ccode\u003E10+00\u003C/code\u003E in a station box is station 10+00. Grades in percent, point numbers and descriptions are read as they are.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"stations\"\u003EStations\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA station is a distance along an alignment, written with a plus sign:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFeet: the part before the plus is hundreds of feet. \u003Ccode\u003E12+34.56\u003C/code\u003E is 1,234.56 ft.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMetres: the part before the plus is thousands of metres. \u003Ccode\u003E1+234.567\u003C/code\u003E is 1,234.567 m.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENegative stations keep the sign in front: \u003Ccode\u003E-0+50.00\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWherever you type a station (\u003Cstrong\u003EStart station\u003C/strong\u003E, a PVI&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003EStation\u003C/strong\u003E), you can type it with the plus (\u003Ccode\u003E12+34.56\u003C/code\u003E) or as a plain number (\u003Ccode\u003E1234.56\u003C/code\u003E). Spaces are ignored.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"grades-and-slopes\"\u003EGrades and slopes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EGrades\u003C/strong\u003E (profiles, pipes, lane and shoulder slopes) are in percent: \u003Ccode\u003E+1.25%\u003C/code\u003E rises up-station, \u003Ccode\u003E-0.50%\u003C/code\u003E falls.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESide slopes\u003C/strong\u003E (pad cut and fill, corridor cut and fill) are horizontal to vertical: \u003Ccode\u003E3\u003C/code\u003E means 3 : 1, three across for one up or down.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EK\u003C/strong\u003E for a vertical curve is its length divided by the change in grade in percent.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"bearings-and-angles\"\u003EBearings and angles\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBearings are quadrant bearings in degrees, minutes and seconds: \u003Ccode\u003EN 63°12&#039;05&quot; E\u003C/code\u003E. Curve deflections (Δ) use the same degrees, minutes and seconds, with &quot;left&quot; or &quot;right&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"offsets\"\u003EOffsets\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOffsets from an alignment are distances square to it, marked \u003Cstrong\u003EL\u003C/strong\u003E (left) or \u003Cstrong\u003ER\u003C/strong\u003E (right) looking up-station.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"number-display\"\u003ENumber display\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECoordinates and elevations show 2 decimals in panels and the status bar (3 for a selected survey point).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAreas and volumes are grouped by thousands: \u003Ccode\u003E128,250 sq ft\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E454.9 cu yd\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECoordinates are never grouped, so they can be copied as they are.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"coordinates\"\u003ECoordinates\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECivilCraft works in a flat plane: easting to the right, northing up. Design work always happens in projected coordinates (state plane, UTM or a local grid). You can tell CivilCraft which coordinate system they are in, and whether they are grid, ground or local coordinates; it then shows latitude and longitude and places GIS files and map layers, but it never changes units or moves your coordinates by itself. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/coordinate-systems\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/coordinate-systems\"\u003ESet the project&#039;s coordinate system\u003C/a\u003E and \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/ground-and-local-coordinates\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/ground-and-local-coordinates\"\u003EGround and local coordinates\u003C/a\u003E. Large state plane values (millions of feet) are fine; CivilCraft works relative to the site internally to keep precision.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Project units A project is in US feet or metres (Units in the left panel). Every coordinate, elevation, length and station in the project is in that unit. Changing Units changes the labels, the station format, the defaults and how files are written. It does not convert any number. A site surveyed in feet and switched to metres keeps the same numbers, now read as metres. The status bar says \"Units are now metres. Coordinates were not converted.\" Set the units to match your data before you design. What depends on the units: US feet Metres Station format 12+34.56 (100 ft stations) 1+234.567 (1000 m stations) Contour intervals offered 0.25 to 20 ft 0.1 to 10 m Area sq ft sq m Volume cu yd cu m Pipe and structure diameter inches millimetres Default curve radius 100 ft 30 m Stationing ticks on the plan every 50 ft, labels every 100 ft every 20 m, labels every 100 m Plot scales 1\" = 5' to 1\" = 1000' 1:50 to 1:10000 LandXML linear unit USSurveyFoot meter When you open LandXML, the project takes the file's units. A file in international feet opens as a feet project; the numbers are not changed. Typing lengths and sums Any box that takes a length or an elevation reads more than a plain number: press Enter and the box shows the result in the project's units. You type In a feet project In a metre project 12.5 12.5 ft 12.5 m 12'6\", 12' 6 1/2\", 6\", 3 ft 6 in 12.5 ft, 12.54 ft, 0.5 ft, 3.5 ft the same lengths in metres 4 7/16, 4-7/16 4.4375 ft 4.4375 m 450 mm, 1.2 m, 90 cm in feet 0.45 m, 1.2 m, 0.9 m 4+7/16, 12'6\" + 3/4\", (24'-0\")/3, 2*450mm the sum, in feet the sum, in metres Use + - * / and brackets, and abs, round, ceil and floor. A dash written into a length is part of it (24'-6\", 6-1/2); with spaces round it, it subtracts (24' - 6\" = 23.5 ft). A plain number multiplies or divides a length, and is a length in the project's units when it is added to one. If CivilCraft can't read what you typed, the box is marked and the status bar says where. Stations are not sums: 10+00 in a station box is station 10+00. Grades in percent, point numbers and descriptions are read as they are. Stations A station is a distance along an alignment, written with a plus sign: Feet: the part before the plus is hundreds of feet. 12+34.56 is 1,234.56 ft. Metres: the part before the plus is thousands of metres. 1+234.567 is 1,234.567 m. Negative stations keep the sign in front: -0+50.00. Wherever you type a station (Start station, a PVI's Station), you can type it with the plus (12+34.56) or as a plain number (1234.56). Spaces are ignored. Grades and slopes Grades (profiles, pipes, lane and shoulder slopes) are in percent: +1.25% rises up-station, -0.50% falls. Side slopes (pad cut and fill, corridor cut and fill) are horizontal to vertical: 3 means 3 : 1, three across for one up or down. K for a vertical curve is its length divided by the change in grade in percent. Bearings and angles Bearings are quadrant bearings in degrees, minutes and seconds: N 63°12'05\" E. Curve deflections (Δ) use the same degrees, minutes and seconds, with \"left\" or \"right\". Offsets Offsets from an alignment are distances square to it, marked L (left) or R (right) looking up-station. Number display Coordinates and elevations show 2 decimals in panels and the status bar (3 for a selected survey point). Areas and volumes are grouped by thousands: 128,250 sq ft, 454.9 cu yd. Coordinates are never grouped, so they can be copied as they are. Coordinates CivilCraft works in a flat plane: easting to the right, northing up. Design work always happens in projected coordinates (state plane, UTM or a local grid). You can tell CivilCraft which coordinate system they are in, and whether they are grid, ground or local coordinates; it then shows latitude and longitude and places GIS files and map layers, but it never changes units or moves your coordinates by itself. See Set the project's coordinate system and Ground and local coordinates. Large state plane values (millions of feet) are fine; CivilCraft works relative to the site internally to keep precision."},{"slug":"landxml-reference","scope":"civilcraft","title":"LandXML: what is read and written","summary":"The LandXML 1.2 elements and attributes CivilCraft reads and writes, element by element, and everything it skips.","section":"reference","order":50,"keywords":["landxml","xml","cgpoints","surface","tin","breaklines","boundary","alignment","coordgeom","profile","profalign","pvi","paracurve","pipenetworks","struct","pipe","planfeatures","planfeature","feature line","schema"],"headings":[{"id":"written","text":"Written","level":2},{"id":"read","text":"Read","level":2},{"id":"skipped-and-named-in-the-notes","text":"Skipped, and named in the notes","level":2},{"id":"messages-when-a-file-will-not-open","text":"Messages when a file will not open","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ECivilCraft writes LandXML 1.2 with the official namespace and schema location. It reads any file whose root element is \u003Ccode\u003ELandXML\u003C/code\u003E and takes the elements below by name (namespace prefixes are ignored; the version is not checked). Its XML reader accepts only the five standard entities and numeric character references; it never loads a DTD or external file. For the steps, see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/landxml\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/landxml\"\u003EExchange LandXML\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELandXML writes coordinates northing first: a point is &quot;N E&quot; or &quot;N E Z&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"written\"\u003EWritten\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EElement\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat CivilCraft writes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EUnits\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EImperial\u003C/code\u003E (linearUnit USSurveyFoot, areas in square feet, volumes in cubic yards, diameters in inches) or \u003Ccode\u003EMetric\u003C/code\u003E (meter, square meter, cubic meter, millimeter)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EProject\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ename\u003C/code\u003E = the project name\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EApplication\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ename CivilCraft, manufacturer CadCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ECgPoints\u003C/code\u003E / \u003Ccode\u003ECgPoint\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ename\u003C/code\u003E = point number, \u003Ccode\u003Edesc\u003C/code\u003E = description, \u003Ccode\u003Ecode\u003C/code\u003E = group; text &quot;N E Z&quot; (or &quot;N E&quot; without elevation)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EAlignments\u003C/code\u003E / \u003Ccode\u003EAlignment\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ename\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Elength\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EstaStart\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ECoordGeom\u003C/code\u003E / \u003Ccode\u003ELine\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EstaStart\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Elength\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EStart\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EEnd\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ECoordGeom\u003C/code\u003E / \u003Ccode\u003ECurve\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EcrvType\u003C/code\u003E arc, \u003Ccode\u003Erot\u003C/code\u003E cw or ccw, \u003Ccode\u003Eradius\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Elength\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EstaStart\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Echord\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Edelta\u003C/code\u003E (degrees), \u003Ccode\u003Etangent\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eexternal\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EmidOrd\u003C/code\u003E; \u003Ccode\u003EStart\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ECenter\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EEnd\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EPI\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EProfile\u003C/code\u003E / \u003Ccode\u003EProfSurf\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethe existing ground along the alignment, as station and elevation pairs (\u003Ccode\u003EPntList2D\u003C/code\u003E), split where it leaves the surface\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EProfile\u003C/code\u003E / \u003Ccode\u003EProfAlign\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethe design profile (named &quot;&lt;alignment&gt; design&quot;): \u003Ccode\u003EPVI\u003C/code\u003E for ends and angle points, \u003Ccode\u003EParaCurve\u003C/code\u003E with \u003Ccode\u003Elength\u003C/code\u003E for vertical curves\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESurfaces\u003C/code\u003E / \u003Ccode\u003ESurface\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ename\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003Edesc\u003C/code\u003E; first the existing ground, then &quot;Proposed&quot; when pads or corridors grade\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESourceData\u003C/code\u003E / \u003Ccode\u003EBreaklines\u003C/code\u003E / \u003Ccode\u003EBreakline\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EbrkType\u003C/code\u003E standard, \u003Ccode\u003Ename\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EPntList3D\u003C/code\u003E (existing ground only)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESourceData\u003C/code\u003E / \u003Ccode\u003EBoundaries\u003C/code\u003E / \u003Ccode\u003EBoundary\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EbndType\u003C/code\u003E outer, \u003Ccode\u003EedgeTrim\u003C/code\u003E true, \u003Ccode\u003EPntList2D\u003C/code\u003E (existing ground only, when there is one)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDefinition\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EsurfType\u003C/code\u003E TIN, \u003Ccode\u003Earea2DSurf\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Earea3DSurf\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EelevMax\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EelevMin\u003C/code\u003E; \u003Ccode\u003EPnts\u003C/code\u003E / \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E with \u003Ccode\u003Eid\u003C/code\u003E; \u003Ccode\u003EFaces\u003C/code\u003E / \u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPipeNetworks\u003C/code\u003E / \u003Ccode\u003EPipeNetwork\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ename\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EpipeNetType\u003C/code\u003E storm or sanitary\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EStruct\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ename\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EelevRim\u003C/code\u003E (the rim used, set or from the surface), \u003Ccode\u003EelevSump\u003C/code\u003E; \u003Ccode\u003ECenter\u003C/code\u003E; \u003Ccode\u003ECircStruct\u003C/code\u003E with \u003Ccode\u003Ediameter\u003C/code\u003E; \u003Ccode\u003EInvert\u003C/code\u003E with \u003Ccode\u003Eelev\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EflowDir\u003C/code\u003E in or out, \u003Ccode\u003ErefPipe\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPipe\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ename\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ErefStart\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ErefEnd\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Elength\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eslope\u003C/code\u003E; \u003Ccode\u003ECircPipe\u003C/code\u003E with \u003Ccode\u003Ediameter\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPlanFeatures\u003C/code\u003E / \u003Ccode\u003EPlanFeature\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eone per feature line: \u003Ccode\u003Ename\u003C/code\u003E; \u003Ccode\u003ECoordGeom\u003C/code\u003E with \u003Ccode\u003ELine\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003Elength\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EStart\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EEnd\u003C/code\u003E) and \u003Ccode\u003ECurve\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003Erot\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EcrvType\u003C/code\u003E arc, \u003Ccode\u003Eradius\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Elength\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Echord\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Edelta\u003C/code\u003E; \u003Ccode\u003EStart\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EEnd\u003C/code\u003E as &quot;N E Z&quot;, \u003Ccode\u003ECenter\u003C/code\u003E as &quot;N E&quot;); \u003Ccode\u003EFeature\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003Ecode=&quot;CivilCraft&quot;\u003C/code\u003E with \u003Ccode\u003EProperty\u003C/code\u003E use and closed\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENot written: pads and corridors as objects (their result is the &quot;Proposed&quot; surface), alignments with errors, pipes without both inverts, network rules.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"read\"\u003ERead\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EElement\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat CivilCraft takes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EUnits\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EImperial\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003EMetric\u003C/code\u003E and its \u003Ccode\u003ElinearUnit\u003C/code\u003E decide feet or metres. An unknown unit is noted.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EProject\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ename\u003C/code\u003E becomes the project name.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ECgPoints\u003C/code\u003E / \u003Ccode\u003ECgPoint\u003C/code\u003E (nested too)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ename (or \u003Ccode\u003EoID\u003C/code\u003E), &quot;N E&quot; or &quot;N E Z&quot;, \u003Ccode\u003Edesc\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003Ecode\u003C/code\u003E) as the description\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESurface\u003C/code\u003E with \u003Ccode\u003EDefinition surfType=&quot;TIN&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPnts\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EFaces\u003C/code\u003E as the existing ground (first surface only); invisible faces (\u003Ccode\u003Ei=&quot;1&quot;\u003C/code\u003E) are left out\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESourceData\u003C/code\u003E / \u003Ccode\u003EBreaklines\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E3D breaklines, kept with the surface\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESourceData\u003C/code\u003E / \u003Ccode\u003EBoundaries\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethe outer boundary\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EAlignment\u003C/code\u003E / \u003Ccode\u003ECoordGeom\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ELine\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003ECurve\u003C/code\u003E elements; PIs rebuilt from each curve&#039;s \u003Ccode\u003EPI\u003C/code\u003E, or from its \u003Ccode\u003ECenter\u003C/code\u003E and tangents; \u003Ccode\u003EstaStart\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EProfile\u003C/code\u003E / \u003Ccode\u003EProfAlign\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPVI\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EParaCurve\u003C/code\u003E (with \u003Ccode\u003Elength\u003C/code\u003E); the first design profile with at least two PVIs\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPlanFeatures\u003C/code\u003E / \u003Ccode\u003EPlanFeature\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eeach one as a feature line: \u003Ccode\u003ELine\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ECurve\u003C/code\u003E (its \u003Ccode\u003EStart\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ECenter\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EEnd\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003Erot\u003C/code\u003E) and \u003Ccode\u003EIrregularLine\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EPntList3D\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003EPntList2D\u003C/code\u003E) joined end to end (a gap is joined with a straight segment and noted); closed when it ends where it began; use and closed from CivilCraft&#039;s own \u003Ccode\u003EFeature\u003C/code\u003E, else \u003Cstrong\u003EOnly drawn\u003C/strong\u003E. Points without an elevation get 0 (noted).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPipeNetwork\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethe first network that has pipes: \u003Ccode\u003EStruct\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003ECenter\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EelevRim\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EelevSump\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ECircStruct\u003C/code\u003E diameter, \u003Ccode\u003EInvert\u003C/code\u003E per pipe) and \u003Ccode\u003EPipe\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003ErefStart\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ErefEnd\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ECircPipe\u003C/code\u003E diameter)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"skipped-and-named-in-the-notes\"\u003ESkipped, and named in the notes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESurfaces after the first, grid surfaces and other non-TIN surfaces\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAlignments that contain \u003Ccode\u003ESpiral\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EIrregularLine\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003EChain\u003C/code\u003E elements, or that have no geometry\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDesign profiles after the first; \u003Ccode\u003ECircCurve\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EUnsymParaCurve\u003C/code\u003E vertical curves (their PVIs are kept without a curve)\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPoints that only refer to other points (\u003Ccode\u003EpntRef\u003C/code\u003E), and points without readable coordinates\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERepeated point names (the first is kept)\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHide, show and data-clip boundaries; breaklines without 3D points\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPipe networks after the first; network types other than storm and sanitary (read as storm)\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERectangular, inlet and outlet structures (read as round); egg, elliptical and box pipes and channels (read as round pipes of no size)\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpiral\u003C/code\u003E elements in plan features\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EParcels\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ERoadways\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EGradeModel\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003ESurvey\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EGround profiles (\u003Ccode\u003EProfSurf\u003C/code\u003E) in a file are not used: CivilCraft samples the ground from the surface itself.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"messages-when-a-file-will-not-open\"\u003EMessages when a file will not open\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMessage\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeaning\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;This is XML but not LandXML (its root element is &lt;…&gt;).&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe file is another kind of XML.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;… has no points, TIN surfaces or alignments CivilCraft can read.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEverything in the file is in the skipped list. The notes after it say what was there.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Mismatched end tag …&quot;, &quot;Unclosed tag …&quot;, &quot;The file ends inside …&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe file is damaged or cut short.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E","text":"CivilCraft writes LandXML 1.2 with the official namespace and schema location. It reads any file whose root element is LandXML and takes the elements below by name (namespace prefixes are ignored; the version is not checked). Its XML reader accepts only the five standard entities and numeric character references; it never loads a DTD or external file. For the steps, see Exchange LandXML. LandXML writes coordinates northing first: a point is \"N E\" or \"N E Z\". Written Element What CivilCraft writes Units Imperial (linearUnit USSurveyFoot, areas in square feet, volumes in cubic yards, diameters in inches) or Metric (meter, square meter, cubic meter, millimeter) Project name = the project name Application name CivilCraft, manufacturer CadCraft CgPoints / CgPoint name = point number, desc = description, code = group; text \"N E Z\" (or \"N E\" without elevation) Alignments / Alignment name, length, staStart CoordGeom / Line staStart, length, Start, End CoordGeom / Curve crvType arc, rot cw or ccw, radius, length, staStart, chord, delta (degrees), tangent, external, midOrd; Start, Center, End, PI Profile / ProfSurf the existing ground along the alignment, as station and elevation pairs (PntList2D), split where it leaves the surface Profile / ProfAlign the design profile (named \"\u003Calignment\u003E design\"): PVI for ends and angle points, ParaCurve with length for vertical curves Surfaces / Surface name and desc; first the existing ground, then \"Proposed\" when pads or corridors grade SourceData / Breaklines / Breakline brkType standard, name, PntList3D (existing ground only) SourceData / Boundaries / Boundary bndType outer, edgeTrim true, PntList2D (existing ground only, when there is one) Definition surfType TIN, area2DSurf, area3DSurf, elevMax, elevMin; Pnts / P with id; Faces / F PipeNetworks / PipeNetwork name, pipeNetType storm or sanitary Struct name, elevRim (the rim used, set or from the surface), elevSump; Center; CircStruct with diameter; Invert with elev, flowDir in or out, refPipe Pipe name, refStart, refEnd, length, slope; CircPipe with diameter PlanFeatures / PlanFeature one per feature line: name; CoordGeom with Line (length, Start, End) and Curve (rot, crvType arc, radius, length, chord, delta; Start and End as \"N E Z\", Center as \"N E\"); Feature code=\"CivilCraft\" with Property use and closed Not written: pads and corridors as objects (their result is the \"Proposed\" surface), alignments with errors, pipes without both inverts, network rules. Read Element What CivilCraft takes Units Imperial or Metric and its linearUnit decide feet or metres. An unknown unit is noted. Project name becomes the project name. CgPoints / CgPoint (nested too) name (or oID), \"N E\" or \"N E Z\", desc (or code) as the description Surface with Definition surfType=\"TIN\" Pnts and Faces as the existing ground (first surface only); invisible faces (i=\"1\") are left out SourceData / Breaklines 3D breaklines, kept with the surface SourceData / Boundaries the outer boundary Alignment / CoordGeom Line and Curve elements; PIs rebuilt from each curve's PI, or from its Center and tangents; staStart Profile / ProfAlign PVI and ParaCurve (with length); the first design profile with at least two PVIs PlanFeatures / PlanFeature each one as a feature line: Line, Curve (its Start, Center, End and rot) and IrregularLine (PntList3D or PntList2D) joined end to end (a gap is joined with a straight segment and noted); closed when it ends where it began; use and closed from CivilCraft's own Feature, else Only drawn. Points without an elevation get 0 (noted). PipeNetwork the first network that has pipes: Struct (Center, elevRim, elevSump, CircStruct diameter, Invert per pipe) and Pipe (refStart, refEnd, CircPipe diameter) Skipped, and named in the notes Surfaces after the first, grid surfaces and other non-TIN surfaces Alignments that contain Spiral, IrregularLine or Chain elements, or that have no geometry Design profiles after the first; CircCurve and UnsymParaCurve vertical curves (their PVIs are kept without a curve) Points that only refer to other points (pntRef), and points without readable coordinates Repeated point names (the first is kept) Hide, show and data-clip boundaries; breaklines without 3D points Pipe networks after the first; network types other than storm and sanitary (read as storm) Rectangular, inlet and outlet structures (read as round); egg, elliptical and box pipes and channels (read as round pipes of no size) Spiral elements in plan features Parcels, Roadways, GradeModel and Survey Ground profiles (ProfSurf) in a file are not used: CivilCraft samples the ground from the surface itself. Messages when a file will not open Message Meaning \"This is XML but not LandXML (its root element is \u003C…\u003E).\" The file is another kind of XML. \"… has no points, TIN surfaces or alignments CivilCraft can read.\" Everything in the file is in the skipped list. The notes after it say what was there. \"Mismatched end tag …\", \"Unclosed tag …\", \"The file ends inside …\" The file is damaged or cut short."},{"slug":"plan-layers","scope":"civilcraft","title":"Plan layers","summary":"The fixed layer names, colors and line weights CivilCraft uses on screen, in DXF and in PDF, and which display setting controls each.","section":"reference","order":60,"keywords":["layers","layer names","ncs","colors","aci","line weights","dxf layers","pdf","display settings","linework"],"headings":[{"id":"line-weights-in-pdf","text":"Line weights in PDF","level":2},{"id":"label-sizes","text":"Label sizes","level":2},{"id":"not-shown-on-any-layer","text":"Not shown on any layer","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EEverything on the plan belongs to a layer. The names follow the common civil layer pattern (discipline, major group, minor group). Layers are fixed in this build: you cannot rename them, change their colors or add your own. In DXF, each layer has the color number shown; in PDF with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayer colors\u003C/span\u003E, the same colors are used, and with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBlack\u003C/span\u003E everything prints black.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ELayer\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EHolds\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EColor (DXF number)\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EShown when\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EC-TOPO-MINR\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMinor contours\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGray (8)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EContours\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EC-TOPO-MAJR\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMajor contours\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhite or black (7)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EContours\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EC-TOPO-TEXT\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EContour labels\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhite or black (7)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EContours\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EContour labels\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EC-TOPO-TINN\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETriangle edges\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGray (8)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETriangles\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EC-TOPO-BRKL\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBreaklines and the boundary\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrange (30)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBreaklines\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EC-TOPO-BNDY\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESurface edge\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhite or black (7)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESurface edge\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EC-TOPO-SLOP\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESlope arrows\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECyan (4)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESlope arrows\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EV-NODE\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESurvey points\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGreen (3)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPoints\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EV-NODE-TEXT\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPoint labels: number, elevation, description\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGreen (3)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPoints\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPoint labels\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EV-NODE-LINE\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELines from line codes (groups that are not breaklines)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGreen (3)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPoints\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EC-ROAD-CNTR\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAlignment centerlines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERed (1)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAlways\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EC-ROAD-STAN\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStation ticks and labels, PC and PT\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERed (1)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAlways\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EC-ROAD-CORR\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECorridor edges\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYellow (2)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAlways\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EC-ROAD-SCTN\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESample lines (where sections are cut)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGray (8)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhen the alignment has sample lines\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EC-GRAD-PADS\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPad outlines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBlue (5)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAlways\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EC-GRAD-DAYL\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDaylight lines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMagenta (6)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAlways\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EC-GRAD-SLOP\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGrading slope lines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMagenta (6)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAlways\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EC-GRAD-FEAT\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFeature lines (3D)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGreen (3)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAlways\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EC-ROAD-SWPT\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESwept paths: outline, outermost wheel paths, name\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrange (30)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAlways\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EC-STRM-PIPE\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPipes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBlue (5)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPipes\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EC-STRM-STRC\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStructures\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBlue (5)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPipes\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EC-STRM-TEXT\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPipe and structure labels\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBlue (5)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPipes\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EC-STRM-PROB\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPipes and structures with problems\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERed (1)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPipes\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBreaklines are drawn only while \u003Cstrong\u003ESurface\u003C/strong\u003E under \u003Cstrong\u003EDisplay\u003C/strong\u003E is \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExisting ground\u003C/span\u003E; the boundary is drawn with either surface.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"line-weights-in-pdf\"\u003ELine weights in PDF\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWeight\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ELayers\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0.50 mm\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAlignment centerlines, pads, pipes, problem pipes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0.35 mm\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMajor contours, daylight lines, structures\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0.25 mm\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBreaklines\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0.13 mm\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMinor contours\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0.09 mm\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETriangle edges, grading slope lines\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0.18 mm\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEverything else\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDXF files carry no line weights; set them by layer in your CAD program.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"label-sizes\"\u003ELabel sizes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELabels are sized on paper: contour labels 2.0 mm, station labels 1.8 mm, PC and PT labels and pipe and structure labels 1.6 mm, point labels 1.5 mm. Pipe labels give the name, diameter and slope; structure labels give the name and rim. On screen they stay the same size as you zoom. In DXF, they are scaled for the scale named in the export message; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/dxf-export\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/dxf-export\"\u003EExport the plan to DXF\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-shown-on-any-layer\"\u003ENot shown on any layer\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EElevation bands are drawn under the linework on screen only. The profile view is not part of the plan.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Everything on the plan belongs to a layer. The names follow the common civil layer pattern (discipline, major group, minor group). Layers are fixed in this build: you cannot rename them, change their colors or add your own. In DXF, each layer has the color number shown; in PDF with Layer colors, the same colors are used, and with Black everything prints black. Layer Holds Color (DXF number) Shown when C-TOPO-MINR Minor contours Gray (8) Contours C-TOPO-MAJR Major contours White or black (7) Contours C-TOPO-TEXT Contour labels White or black (7) Contours and Contour labels C-TOPO-TINN Triangle edges Gray (8) Triangles C-TOPO-BRKL Breaklines and the boundary Orange (30) Breaklines C-TOPO-BNDY Surface edge White or black (7) Surface edge C-TOPO-SLOP Slope arrows Cyan (4) Slope arrows V-NODE Survey points Green (3) Points V-NODE-TEXT Point labels: number, elevation, description Green (3) Points and Point labels V-NODE-LINE Lines from line codes (groups that are not breaklines) Green (3) Points C-ROAD-CNTR Alignment centerlines Red (1) Always C-ROAD-STAN Station ticks and labels, PC and PT Red (1) Always C-ROAD-CORR Corridor edges Yellow (2) Always C-ROAD-SCTN Sample lines (where sections are cut) Gray (8) When the alignment has sample lines C-GRAD-PADS Pad outlines Blue (5) Always C-GRAD-DAYL Daylight lines Magenta (6) Always C-GRAD-SLOP Grading slope lines Magenta (6) Always C-GRAD-FEAT Feature lines (3D) Green (3) Always C-ROAD-SWPT Swept paths: outline, outermost wheel paths, name Orange (30) Always C-STRM-PIPE Pipes Blue (5) Pipes C-STRM-STRC Structures Blue (5) Pipes C-STRM-TEXT Pipe and structure labels Blue (5) Pipes C-STRM-PROB Pipes and structures with problems Red (1) Pipes Breaklines are drawn only while Surface under Display is Existing ground; the boundary is drawn with either surface. Line weights in PDF Weight Layers 0.50 mm Alignment centerlines, pads, pipes, problem pipes 0.35 mm Major contours, daylight lines, structures 0.25 mm Breaklines 0.13 mm Minor contours 0.09 mm Triangle edges, grading slope lines 0.18 mm Everything else DXF files carry no line weights; set them by layer in your CAD program. Label sizes Labels are sized on paper: contour labels 2.0 mm, station labels 1.8 mm, PC and PT labels and pipe and structure labels 1.6 mm, point labels 1.5 mm. Pipe labels give the name, diameter and slope; structure labels give the name and rim. On screen they stay the same size as you zoom. In DXF, they are scaled for the scale named in the export message; see Export the plan to DXF. Not shown on any layer Elevation bands are drawn under the linework on screen only. The profile view is not part of the plan."},{"slug":"defaults","scope":"civilcraft","title":"Starting values and placeholders","summary":"Every value CivilCraft starts with, for new projects, display, alignments, pads, corridors, pipe networks, volumes and plots, and which ones are placeholders to replace.","section":"reference","order":70,"keywords":["defaults","settings","starting values","placeholders","design criteria","standards","rules","assumptions"],"headings":[{"id":"new-project","text":"New project","level":2},{"id":"display","text":"Display","level":2},{"id":"alignments-and-profiles","text":"Alignments and profiles","level":2},{"id":"pads","text":"Pads","level":2},{"id":"corridor-assembly-placeholders","text":"Corridor assembly (placeholders)","level":2},{"id":"pipe-network","text":"Pipe network","level":2},{"id":"volumes","text":"Volumes","level":2},{"id":"plot","text":"Plot","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ECivilCraft has no settings dialog or template. These are the values things start with. Values marked \u003Cstrong\u003Eplaceholder\u003C/strong\u003E are not taken from any standard: replace them with your project&#039;s criteria before you rely on a check or a quantity.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"new-project\"\u003ENew project\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESetting\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EValue\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EName\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUntitled site (the first point file&#039;s name replaces it)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUnits\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUS feet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESurface name\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExisting ground\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPoint groups\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEvery group starts as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPoints\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELongest outside edge\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0 (keep every triangle)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUndo steps kept\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E200\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"display\"\u003EDisplay\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESetting\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EValue\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESurface\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExisting ground\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EContours every\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E1 (ft or m)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMajor every\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E5th contour\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBands\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E8\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOn\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EContours, contour labels, points, breaklines, surface edge, pipes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOff\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETriangles, elevation bands, slope arrows, point labels\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"alignments-and-profiles\"\u003EAlignments and profiles\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESetting\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EValue\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EName\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAlignment 1, Alignment 2 …\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStart station\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0+00\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECurve radius at each interior PI\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E100 ft or 30 m, smaller if that does not fit in half of each tangent\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDesign profile\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENone until you start one; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStart from the ground\u003C/span\u003E puts a PVI on the ground at each end\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EVertical curve length\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0 (no curve) until you type one\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pads\"\u003EPads\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESetting\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EValue\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EName\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPad 1, Pad 2 …\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EElevation\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe ground at the pad&#039;s middle, rounded to 0.1 (0 if the middle is off the surface)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECut slope\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2 : 1 (placeholder)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFill slope\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E3 : 1 (placeholder)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"corridor-assembly-placeholders\"\u003ECorridor assembly (placeholders)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESetting\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EUS feet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMetres\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELane width\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E12 ft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E3.6 m\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELane slope\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2%\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2%\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShoulder width\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E4 ft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E1.2 m\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShoulder slope\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E4%\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E4%\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECut slope\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2 : 1\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2 : 1\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFill slope\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E3 : 1\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E3 : 1\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESection every\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E10 ft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E5 m\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pipe-network\"\u003EPipe network\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESetting\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EUS feet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMetres\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EName and type\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStorm, storm\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStorm, storm\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELeast slope (placeholder)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0.50%\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0.50%\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELeast cover (placeholder)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2 ft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0.6 m\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrop in structures (placeholder)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0.10 ft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0.03 m\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENew structure diameter\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E48 in\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E1200 mm\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENew pipe diameter\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E12 in\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E300 mm\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESump depth\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERim\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFrom the surface\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFrom the surface\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA pipe network opened from LandXML uses these rules too, because LandXML does not carry them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"volumes\"\u003EVolumes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESetting\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EValue\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMethod\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGrid, sampled at cell centers inside the daylight lines\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECell size\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChosen so the graded area&#039;s bounding rectangle holds about 250,000 cells (1, 2 or 5 times a power of ten)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESwell and shrink\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENone\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"plot\"\u003EPlot\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESetting\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EValue\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArea\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEverything\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPaper\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETabloid 11 × 17 in (feet projects), ISO A3 (metre projects); always landscape\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EScale\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe largest listed scale that fits\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EInk\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayer colors\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDXF labels are sized for the scale at which everything fits on Tabloid (feet) or A3 (metres).\u003C/p\u003E","text":"CivilCraft has no settings dialog or template. These are the values things start with. Values marked placeholder are not taken from any standard: replace them with your project's criteria before you rely on a check or a quantity. New project Setting Value Name Untitled site (the first point file's name replaces it) Units US feet Surface name Existing ground Point groups Every group starts as Points Longest outside edge 0 (keep every triangle) Undo steps kept 200 Display Setting Value Surface Existing ground Contours every 1 (ft or m) Major every 5th contour Bands 8 On Contours, contour labels, points, breaklines, surface edge, pipes Off Triangles, elevation bands, slope arrows, point labels Alignments and profiles Setting Value Name Alignment 1, Alignment 2 … Start station 0+00 Curve radius at each interior PI 100 ft or 30 m, smaller if that does not fit in half of each tangent Design profile None until you start one; Start from the ground puts a PVI on the ground at each end Vertical curve length 0 (no curve) until you type one Pads Setting Value Name Pad 1, Pad 2 … Elevation The ground at the pad's middle, rounded to 0.1 (0 if the middle is off the surface) Cut slope 2 : 1 (placeholder) Fill slope 3 : 1 (placeholder) Corridor assembly (placeholders) Setting US feet Metres Lane width 12 ft 3.6 m Lane slope 2% 2% Shoulder width 4 ft 1.2 m Shoulder slope 4% 4% Cut slope 2 : 1 2 : 1 Fill slope 3 : 1 3 : 1 Section every 10 ft 5 m Pipe network Setting US feet Metres Name and type Storm, storm Storm, storm Least slope (placeholder) 0.50% 0.50% Least cover (placeholder) 2 ft 0.6 m Drop in structures (placeholder) 0.10 ft 0.03 m New structure diameter 48 in 1200 mm New pipe diameter 12 in 300 mm Sump depth 0 0 Rim From the surface From the surface A pipe network opened from LandXML uses these rules too, because LandXML does not carry them. Volumes Setting Value Method Grid, sampled at cell centers inside the daylight lines Cell size Chosen so the graded area's bounding rectangle holds about 250,000 cells (1, 2 or 5 times a power of ten) Swell and shrink None Plot Setting Value Area Everything Paper Tabloid 11 × 17 in (feet projects), ISO A3 (metre projects); always landscape Scale The largest listed scale that fits Ink Layer colors DXF labels are sized for the scale at which everything fits on Tabloid (feet) or A3 (metres)."},{"slug":"troubleshooting","scope":"civilcraft","title":"Troubleshooting","summary":"Symptoms, causes and fixes for the problems people meet in CivilCraft, with the exact messages the app shows.","section":"trouble","order":10,"keywords":["problems","errors","not working","fix","help","messages","troubleshoot","refused","missing","wrong","swapped","contours missing"],"headings":[{"id":"opening-files","text":"Opening files","level":2},{"id":"importing-points","text":"Importing points","level":2},{"id":"the-surface","text":"The surface","level":2},{"id":"drawing","text":"Drawing","level":2},{"id":"alignments-and-profiles","text":"Alignments and profiles","level":2},{"id":"corridors-and-pads","text":"Corridors and pads","level":2},{"id":"pipes","text":"Pipes","level":2},{"id":"saving-and-exporting","text":"Saving and exporting","level":2},{"id":"the-app-itself","text":"The app itself","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EMessages appear in the status bar at the bottom right, in boxes in the left and right panels, and in the dialogs. They are quoted here as the app shows them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"opening-files\"\u003EOpening files\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;That file is over 200 MB; CivilCraft does not open files that large yet.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E Split the point file, or thin the data before opening it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Could not open site.json: This is not a CivilCraft project file.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E The file ends in \u003Ccode\u003E.json\u003C/code\u003E or starts with \u003Ccode\u003E{\u003C/code\u003E but is not a CivilCraft project. Point files must not start with \u003Ccode\u003E{\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Could not open … This project file is from a newer CivilCraft (version 2).&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E The project was saved by a newer build. Open it there, or export LandXML from it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Could not open … This is XML but not LandXML (its root element is &lt;…&gt;).&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E The file is another kind of XML. Export LandXML from the other program.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;… has no points, TIN surfaces or alignments CivilCraft can read.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E Everything in the file is something CivilCraft skips (for example only grid surfaces, or only spiral alignments). The rest of the message says what was there. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/landxml-reference\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/landxml-reference\"\u003ELandXML: what is read and written\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;The sample could not be loaded: …&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen the sample site\u003C/span\u003E reads a file from the CadCraft site. With no connection it works only if CivilCraft was saved for offline use (the app bar button shows \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E). Go online, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E, and it works offline from then on. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003EOffline\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E shows \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECould not save\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/strong\u003E Some files did not download. Check the connection and click the button again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E shows \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline not available\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/strong\u003E This browser window cannot keep an offline copy, usually because it is a private window or the computer is managed. Open CivilCraft in a normal window.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOffline, the app does not open at all.\u003C/strong\u003E It was not saved for offline use in this browser, or the browser cleared its site data (Safari does this for sites not visited for several weeks). Go online, open CivilCraft and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe offline copy is an older build.\u003C/strong\u003E Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave again (get the newest version)\u003C/span\u003E while online.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA file is grayed out in the open box.\u003C/strong\u003E The box lists only point files, LandXML and project files by their endings. Drag the file onto the plan instead, or rename it to \u003Ccode\u003E.txt\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"importing-points\"\u003EImporting points\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;No points could be read from this file.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E Pick the column order in \u003Cstrong\u003EColumns\u003C/strong\u003E by hand. If nothing works, open the file in a text editor and check that it is one point per line with numbers in it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe site looks mirrored, rotated or stretched into a line.\u003C/strong\u003E Northing and easting are swapped. The summary said &quot;guessed from the column count&quot;. Choose PENZD instead of PNEZD (or the other way round) in \u003Cstrong\u003EColumns\u003C/strong\u003E, or import again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPoints far away from the rest, and the plan zoomed out to a dot.\u003C/strong\u003E A row has a typo in a coordinate, or the file mixes coordinate systems. Find the point (click it to see its number) and fix the file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERows listed under &quot;Rows not read&quot;.\u003C/strong\u003E See the table in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/import-points#rows-that-are-not-read\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/import-points\" data-hash=\"rows-that-are-not-read\"\u003EImport survey points\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMy old points are gone.\u003C/strong\u003E Importing a point file replaces all points, and clears undo. Open your saved project to get them back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-surface\"\u003EThe surface\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Surface: Not built&quot; with a red box.\u003C/strong\u003E The box says why: &quot;A surface needs at least three points with elevations.&quot;, &quot;The points are all on one line: no triangles.&quot;, &quot;No points with elevations.&quot;, &quot;The boundary does not overlap the points.&quot; or &quot;No triangles are left inside the boundary.&quot; Check the column order, the point groups (are they \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELeft out\u003C/span\u003E?), and the boundary.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EContours cut straight across a ditch or ridge.\u003C/strong\u003E That line is not a breakline. Set its group to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBreakline\u003C/span\u003E, or draw it with the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBreakline\u003C/span\u003E tool. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/build-surface\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/build-surface\"\u003EBuild and refine the surface\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA breakline group draws a zig-zag.\u003C/strong\u003E The group holds more than one line, or its points are not numbered along the line. Set the group back to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPoints\u003C/span\u003E and draw each line with the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBreakline\u003C/span\u003E tool.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELong thin triangles along the outside.\u003C/strong\u003E Draw a boundary (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"O\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) or set \u003Cstrong\u003ELongest outside edge\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENo contours at all, although the surface is built.\u003C/strong\u003E Either \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EContours\u003C/span\u003E is off under \u003Cstrong\u003EDisplay\u003C/strong\u003E, or the interval is so small that there would be more than 2,000 contour levels. Choose a larger interval.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBreaklines or a boundary change nothing.\u003C/strong\u003E The surface came from LandXML (&quot;Surface (from LandXML)&quot;). It is used as it is. Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERebuild the surface from the points\u003C/span\u003E to build from the points instead.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;There is no surface yet: open survey points first.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E Breakline, boundary and pad need an existing ground surface. Import points first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Click a survey point with an elevation: a breakline takes the points’ elevations.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBreakline\u003C/span\u003E tool only picks survey points. Zoom in and click on a point (an orange square shows the one it will take).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"drawing\"\u003EDrawing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMy last point disappeared when I double-clicked to finish.\u003C/strong\u003E In this build, double-clicking finishes the alignment, pad, breakline or boundary but drops the point you double-clicked. Click the last point once and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Use \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E if it already happened, then draw again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EI cannot finish a drawing on a tablet.\u003C/strong\u003E Finishing needs \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E; there is no on-screen button yet. Attach a keyboard. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/keyboard#mouse-trackpad-and-touch\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/keyboard\" data-hash=\"mouse-trackpad-and-touch\"\u003EMouse, trackpad and touch\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDragging to place a point pans instead.\u003C/strong\u003E A drag of more than a few pixels pans the plan in every tool. Click without moving.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;An alignment needs at least two points.&quot; / &quot;A pad needs at least three corners.&quot; / &quot;A pipe run needs at least two structures.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E You pressed \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E too early. The drawing is discarded; start again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EKeys like A or G do nothing.\u003C/strong\u003E A text box or a list in a panel still has the focus, so the letters go to it. Click the plan once, then press the key. On a Mac, the delete key is Backspace to the browser: use Fn and delete to delete a selection.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"alignments-and-profiles\"\u003EAlignments and profiles\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;The curves at PI 2 and PI 3 overlap: … Use smaller radii.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E Two curves need more tangent than there is between their PIs. Lower one or both radii in the PI table, or move the PIs apart.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;PI 3 turns straight back; it cannot take a curve.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E The alignment doubles back on itself at that PI. Move or remove the PI.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;This alignment has no ground under it to start from.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E The alignment is off the surface. Move it onto the surface, or add PVIs by clicking with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd PVIs\u003C/span\u003E on.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;The vertical curve at PVI 3 (L = …) starts before the curve or PVI before it ends.&quot; / &quot;… runs past the next PVI.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E Shorten the curve length or move the PVIs apart.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;There is already a PVI at …&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E A PVI exists at that station. Click elsewhere, or drag the existing one.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe profile view is empty: &quot;Draw an alignment to see its profile.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E There is no alignment, or the selected one has errors.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"corridors-and-pads\"\u003ECorridors and pads\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;The corridor needs a design profile without errors.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E Start a design profile (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStart from the ground\u003C/span\u003E) and fix any curve overlaps.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;… side slopes run off the surface before meeting the ground.&quot; / &quot;… slope lines run off the surface before meeting it …&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E The grading reaches the edge of the surface before meeting the ground. Move the design in from the edge, or bring it closer to the ground. A new pad drawn partly off the surface may start at elevation 0: type a real elevation.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;The corridor’s daylight lines cross …&quot; / &quot;The daylight line crosses itself …&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E Slopes from different sides run into each other: tight curves, narrow pads, deep cuts or high fills. Use a larger radius, a narrower assembly, a wider pad or steeper slopes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;No volumes yet: a pad needs to grade without problems.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E Fix the problems listed in the red box above it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProposed (graded)\u003C/span\u003E is grayed out.\u003C/strong\u003E No pad or corridor grades yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pipes\"\u003EPipes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;P2 has no inverts yet.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESet its inverts from the rules\u003C/span\u003E or type the inverts.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;P1: no ground at S1 to start from.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E The upstream structure is off the surface and has no rim. Type a rim for it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEvery pipe is flagged after I moved a structure.\u003C/strong\u003E Moving a structure does not change inverts. Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESet every invert from the rules\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAll network messages are explained in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/pipe-network#read-and-fix-problems\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/pipe-network\" data-hash=\"read-and-fix-problems\"\u003ELay out a gravity pipe network\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"saving-and-exporting\"\u003ESaving and exporting\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Nothing to export yet.&quot; / &quot;Nothing to plot yet.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E The project is empty. Import points first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EI cannot find the saved file.\u003C/strong\u003E It is in the browser&#039;s downloads (usually the Downloads folder). See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/files#where-the-files-go\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/files\" data-hash=\"where-the-files-go\"\u003EFiles you can open, save and export\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe PDF scale is wrong on paper.\u003C/strong\u003E Print at 100% or actual size, not &quot;fit to page&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EQuestion marks in the PDF text.\u003C/strong\u003E Names or descriptions use characters outside the Western European set. The status message counts them. Rename with plain characters.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe plot is cut off at the edges.\u003C/strong\u003E The scale is too large for the sheet. Choose the scale marked &quot;(fits)&quot;, a larger sheet, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhat is on screen\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command P\"\u003E⌘P\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+P\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E opened the browser&#039;s print dialog.\u003C/strong\u003E The page did not have the focus. Close the dialog, click the plan, and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command P\"\u003E⌘P\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+P\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPDF\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-app-itself\"\u003EThe app itself\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe screen is blank or the toolbar does nothing.\u003C/strong\u003E Use a current version of Chrome, Edge, Firefox or Safari, and reload. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/browsers\" data-topic=\"basics/browsers\"\u003EBrowsers\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe app is slow with a big point file.\u003C/strong\u003E Turn off \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETriangles\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPoint labels\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElevation bands\u003C/span\u003E, and use a larger contour interval. Very large surfaces take a moment to rebuild after each change.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe browser asks &quot;Leave site?&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E The project has unsaved changes. Stay, save with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then leave.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Messages appear in the status bar at the bottom right, in boxes in the left and right panels, and in the dialogs. They are quoted here as the app shows them. Opening files \"That file is over 200 MB; CivilCraft does not open files that large yet.\" Split the point file, or thin the data before opening it. \"Could not open site.json: This is not a CivilCraft project file.\" The file ends in .json or starts with { but is not a CivilCraft project. Point files must not start with {. \"Could not open … This project file is from a newer CivilCraft (version 2).\" The project was saved by a newer build. Open it there, or export LandXML from it. \"Could not open … This is XML but not LandXML (its root element is \u003C…\u003E).\" The file is another kind of XML. Export LandXML from the other program. \"… has no points, TIN surfaces or alignments CivilCraft can read.\" Everything in the file is something CivilCraft skips (for example only grid surfaces, or only spiral alignments). The rest of the message says what was there. See LandXML: what is read and written. \"The sample could not be loaded: …\" Open the sample site reads a file from the CadCraft site. With no connection it works only if CivilCraft was saved for offline use (the app bar button shows Offline ready). Go online, click Save for offline, and it works offline from then on. See Offline. Save for offline shows Could not save. Some files did not download. Check the connection and click the button again. Save for offline shows Offline not available. This browser window cannot keep an offline copy, usually because it is a private window or the computer is managed. Open CivilCraft in a normal window. Offline, the app does not open at all. It was not saved for offline use in this browser, or the browser cleared its site data (Safari does this for sites not visited for several weeks). Go online, open CivilCraft and click Save for offline. The offline copy is an older build. Click Offline ready, then Save again (get the newest version) while online. A file is grayed out in the open box. The box lists only point files, LandXML and project files by their endings. Drag the file onto the plan instead, or rename it to .txt. Importing points \"No points could be read from this file.\" Pick the column order in Columns by hand. If nothing works, open the file in a text editor and check that it is one point per line with numbers in it. The site looks mirrored, rotated or stretched into a line. Northing and easting are swapped. The summary said \"guessed from the column count\". Choose PENZD instead of PNEZD (or the other way round) in Columns, or import again. Points far away from the rest, and the plan zoomed out to a dot. A row has a typo in a coordinate, or the file mixes coordinate systems. Find the point (click it to see its number) and fix the file. Rows listed under \"Rows not read\". See the table in Import survey points. My old points are gone. Importing a point file replaces all points, and clears undo. Open your saved project to get them back. The surface \"Surface: Not built\" with a red box. The box says why: \"A surface needs at least three points with elevations.\", \"The points are all on one line: no triangles.\", \"No points with elevations.\", \"The boundary does not overlap the points.\" or \"No triangles are left inside the boundary.\" Check the column order, the point groups (are they Left out?), and the boundary. Contours cut straight across a ditch or ridge. That line is not a breakline. Set its group to Breakline, or draw it with the Breakline tool. See Build and refine the surface. A breakline group draws a zig-zag. The group holds more than one line, or its points are not numbered along the line. Set the group back to Points and draw each line with the Breakline tool. Long thin triangles along the outside. Draw a boundary (O) or set Longest outside edge. No contours at all, although the surface is built. Either Contours is off under Display, or the interval is so small that there would be more than 2,000 contour levels. Choose a larger interval. Breaklines or a boundary change nothing. The surface came from LandXML (\"Surface (from LandXML)\"). It is used as it is. Click Rebuild the surface from the points to build from the points instead. \"There is no surface yet: open survey points first.\" Breakline, boundary and pad need an existing ground surface. Import points first. \"Click a survey point with an elevation: a breakline takes the points’ elevations.\" The Breakline tool only picks survey points. Zoom in and click on a point (an orange square shows the one it will take). Drawing My last point disappeared when I double-clicked to finish. In this build, double-clicking finishes the alignment, pad, breakline or boundary but drops the point you double-clicked. Click the last point once and press Enter. Use Ctrl+Z if it already happened, then draw again. I cannot finish a drawing on a tablet. Finishing needs Enter; there is no on-screen button yet. Attach a keyboard. See Mouse, trackpad and touch. Dragging to place a point pans instead. A drag of more than a few pixels pans the plan in every tool. Click without moving. \"An alignment needs at least two points.\" / \"A pad needs at least three corners.\" / \"A pipe run needs at least two structures.\" You pressed Enter too early. The drawing is discarded; start again. Keys like A or G do nothing. A text box or a list in a panel still has the focus, so the letters go to it. Click the plan once, then press the key. On a Mac, the delete key is Backspace to the browser: use Fn and delete to delete a selection. Alignments and profiles \"The curves at PI 2 and PI 3 overlap: … Use smaller radii.\" Two curves need more tangent than there is between their PIs. Lower one or both radii in the PI table, or move the PIs apart. \"PI 3 turns straight back; it cannot take a curve.\" The alignment doubles back on itself at that PI. Move or remove the PI. \"This alignment has no ground under it to start from.\" The alignment is off the surface. Move it onto the surface, or add PVIs by clicking with Add PVIs on. \"The vertical curve at PVI 3 (L = …) starts before the curve or PVI before it ends.\" / \"… runs past the next PVI.\" Shorten the curve length or move the PVIs apart. \"There is already a PVI at …\" A PVI exists at that station. Click elsewhere, or drag the existing one. The profile view is empty: \"Draw an alignment to see its profile.\" There is no alignment, or the selected one has errors. Corridors and pads \"The corridor needs a design profile without errors.\" Start a design profile (Start from the ground) and fix any curve overlaps. \"… side slopes run off the surface before meeting the ground.\" / \"… slope lines run off the surface before meeting it …\" The grading reaches the edge of the surface before meeting the ground. Move the design in from the edge, or bring it closer to the ground. A new pad drawn partly off the surface may start at elevation 0: type a real elevation. \"The corridor’s daylight lines cross …\" / \"The daylight line crosses itself …\" Slopes from different sides run into each other: tight curves, narrow pads, deep cuts or high fills. Use a larger radius, a narrower assembly, a wider pad or steeper slopes. \"No volumes yet: a pad needs to grade without problems.\" Fix the problems listed in the red box above it. Proposed (graded) is grayed out. No pad or corridor grades yet. Pipes \"P2 has no inverts yet.\" Click Set its inverts from the rules or type the inverts. \"P1: no ground at S1 to start from.\" The upstream structure is off the surface and has no rim. Type a rim for it. Every pipe is flagged after I moved a structure. Moving a structure does not change inverts. Click Set every invert from the rules. All network messages are explained in Lay out a gravity pipe network. Saving and exporting \"Nothing to export yet.\" / \"Nothing to plot yet.\" The project is empty. Import points first. I cannot find the saved file. It is in the browser's downloads (usually the Downloads folder). See Files you can open, save and export. The PDF scale is wrong on paper. Print at 100% or actual size, not \"fit to page\". Question marks in the PDF text. Names or descriptions use characters outside the Western European set. The status message counts them. Rename with plain characters. The plot is cut off at the edges. The scale is too large for the sheet. Choose the scale marked \"(fits)\", a larger sheet, or What is on screen. Ctrl+P opened the browser's print dialog. The page did not have the focus. Close the dialog, click the plan, and press Ctrl+P again, or click PDF. The app itself The screen is blank or the toolbar does nothing. Use a current version of Chrome, Edge, Firefox or Safari, and reload. See Browsers. The app is slow with a big point file. Turn off Triangles, Point labels and Elevation bands, and use a larger contour interval. Very large surfaces take a moment to rebuild after each change. The browser asks \"Leave site?\" The project has unsaved changes. Stay, save with Ctrl+S, then leave."},{"slug":"not-built-yet","scope":"civilcraft","title":"Not built yet","summary":"What this build of CivilCraft does not do yet, and what to do instead today.","section":"trouble","order":20,"keywords":["missing","limitations","roadmap","not available","spirals","widening","dwg","dxf","inlets","detention","cross sections","coming later","limits"],"headings":[{"id":"points","text":"Points","level":2},{"id":"surfaces","text":"Surfaces","level":2},{"id":"alignments-and-profiles","text":"Alignments and profiles","level":2},{"id":"corridors","text":"Corridors","level":2},{"id":"grading-and-volumes","text":"Grading and volumes","level":2},{"id":"pipe-networks","text":"Pipe networks","level":2},{"id":"parcels","text":"Parcels","level":2},{"id":"traverses","text":"Traverses","level":2},{"id":"staking","text":"Staking","level":2},{"id":"files-and-output","text":"Files and output","level":2},{"id":"gis-and-coordinate-systems","text":"GIS and coordinate systems","level":2},{"id":"input","text":"Input","level":2},{"id":"3d-view","text":"3D view","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ECivilCraft is early. This page lists what the current build does not do, so you do not look for it. Nothing here is promised for a date.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"points\"\u003EPoints\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdding a second point file to the first\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EJoin the files in a text editor or spreadsheet first\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExporting a point text file\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExport LandXML (points go out as CgPoints), use GIS \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E (points with P, N, E, Z and D fields), or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E the points into a spreadsheet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPoint label styles, symbols per description key, curves (arcs) from line codes, a description key file to share between projects\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELabels show the number, elevation and description, or less (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/settings\"\u003ECivilCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E); keys are kept in the project file\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUnit conversion\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EConvert the file before importing; \u003Cstrong\u003EUnits\u003C/strong\u003E only relabels\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"surfaces\"\u003ESurfaces\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMore than one surface built from points\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne project per surface\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHide (inner) boundaries\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse \u003Cstrong\u003ELongest outside edge\u003C/strong\u003E or an outer boundary\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelecting or editing one drawn breakline\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove drawn breaklines\u003C/span\u003E and redraw\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEditing a surface read from LandXML (breaklines, boundary)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERebuild the surface from the points\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEditing a surface made from an elevation grid\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EImport the grid again with other choices\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EConverting a surface read from LandXML when the coordinate system changes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERebuild the surface from the points\u003C/span\u003E first\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELabeled spot elevations and slope values\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead them in the status bar\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"alignments-and-profiles\"\u003EAlignments and profiles\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESpirals (transition curves)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECircular curves only; LandXML alignments with spirals are skipped\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EInserting a PI between two others\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERedraw the alignment, or retype PI coordinates\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAlignments from objects, offsets, widenings\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraw by PIs\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETyping K for a vertical curve\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType L (K is shown)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECircular and unsymmetrical vertical curves\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESymmetric parabolic curves only\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMore than one design profile per alignment\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne alignment per variant\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZooming the profile view, profile bands under the profile\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe profile view fits the whole alignment; plot it on \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/plan-profile-sheets\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/plan-profile-sheets\"\u003Eplan and profile sheets\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESections in DWG or LandXML\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESection sheets as PDF; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECSV\u003C/span\u003E of the areas and volumes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESample lines at an angle, or of different widths along the road\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne width each side for the whole alignment\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"corridors\"\u003ECorridors\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECurb and gutter, sidewalks, ditches, other subassemblies\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne lane, one shoulder and a side slope each side\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDifferent left and right sides, or changes along the road\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESplit the work into separate alignments\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESpiral transitions for superelevation, rotation about an edge, superelevation in LandXML\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESuperelevation rotates about the centerline on circular curves; widen a lane with a width target to a feature line\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERegions, targets for the side slopes (daylight to a line or a surface), corridor surfaces of their own\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETargets move the edges of pavement and shoulders (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/corridor\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/corridor\"\u003ECorridors\u003C/a\u003E); the corridor is graded into the proposed surface\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECorridors exported as LandXML roadways\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe proposed surface is exported\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESwept paths with speed, tyre slip, steering time, steering trailers or body clearance; a library of standard design vehicles; a swept path drawn by dragging the vehicle\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELow-speed swept paths along a feature line or an alignment, with your own vehicle sizes (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/swept-paths\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/swept-paths\"\u003EVehicle swept paths\u003C/a\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"grading-and-volumes\"\u003EGrading and volumes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBenches, open feature lines graded into the design, grading to a feature line from elsewhere\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClose the feature line (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/feature-lines\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/feature-lines\"\u003EFeature lines\u003C/a\u003E), or use it as a breakline of the existing ground\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EInserting or removing a feature line vertex\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraw the line again, or make a stepped offset of it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArcs in drawn breaklines (through survey points), spirals in feature lines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraw a feature line with arcs and use it as a breakline of the existing ground\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGrading to a distance, elevation or another surface\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDaylight to existing ground only\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdding or removing a pad corner\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDelete the pad and draw it again\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETIN-to-TIN (prism) volumes, choosing the grid cell size\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGrid method, cell size chosen for you\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESwell and shrink, topsoil, pavement depths\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EApply factors to the reported numbers yourself\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFree-haul and overhaul costs, haul across different alignments, borrow pits and waste sites placed on the plan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe mass-haul diagram with balance points, volumes moved and average haul distances\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pipe-networks\"\u003EPipe networks\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMore than one network per project\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESeparate projects for storm and sanitary\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWater mains (pressure networks: demands, pressures, fire flow, tanks, pumps, valves)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECheck them in water distribution software; sanitary design flows are in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/sanitary-design\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/sanitary-design\"\u003ESanitary design flows\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESewer flows over time (hydrographs, dynamic routing, storage, pumps, overflows)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/sanitary-design\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/sanitary-design\"\u003ESanitary design flows\u003C/a\u003E gives the steady peak flow a design sheet uses\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EInlet capacity, gutter spread, detention routing, hydrographs, energy losses other than one junction coefficient\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECheck them in other software; flows, sizes and the grade lines are in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/storm-drainage\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/storm-drainage\"\u003EStorm drainage\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECatchments in the PDF plot, DWG, DXF or LandXML; catchments from the surface (watersheds)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraw catchments by hand; they are saved in the project file\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPipe catalogs, non-round pipes, structures that are not upright boxes or cylinders (cones, steps, benching), a drainage category in the library\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStructure types from parameters (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/pipe-network\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/pipe-network\"\u003EPipe network\u003C/a\u003E); pipes with a diameter\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPipes in the profile view, curved pipes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead inverts and cover in the right panel\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"parcels\"\u003EParcels\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELaying out lots by area or frontage, parcels from the lines around them, right-of-way\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraw each parcel by its corners, or enter its calls\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECurve tables and line tables on the plan; labels moved by hand\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEvery course is labeled along itself\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EParcels in LandXML\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EParcels are saved in the project file and go to the PDF, DWG and DXF\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"traverses\"\u003ETraverses\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELeast-squares adjustment, the transit rule, weighting angles against distances\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe compass rule\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDeflection angles, zenith angles and slope distances, elevations by trigonometric leveling\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReduce to horizontal distances and angles right, or bearings\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESide shots, raw data files from data collectors\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EImport the points as a point file\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"staking\"\u003EStaking\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOffset stakes (reference points set back from the stake), slope stakes at the catch point\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStake at the offsets you need, including past the shoulder\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESending stakes straight to a data collector&#039;s own format\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe CSV opens in the office software of most data collectors\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStaking pipes, pads and parcels\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse survey points near an alignment, or the parcel&#039;s courses\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"files-and-output\"\u003EFiles and output\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EText, hatches and dimensions from DWG or DXF; paper space; linked drawings (xrefs); binary DXF\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinework from model space opens (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/open-dwg-dxf\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/open-dwg-dxf\"\u003EOpen a DWG or DXF as linework\u003C/a\u003E); open linked drawings on their own\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMoving several points at once by dragging\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECut and paste them, or edit them one at a time\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPortrait sheets, title blocks of your own, viewports you arrange, plan and profile sheets in DWG\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne landscape plan sheet per PDF, or plan and profile sheets along an alignment (PDF)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlotting elevation bands and profiles\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBands are screen only; take a screenshot if you need them\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChanging layer names, colors or label styles\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChange them in a CAD program after DXF export\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECloud saving and version history\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave \u003Ccode\u003E.civilcraft.json\u003C/code\u003E files and keep them backed up; to work on one project together, use a live session in CadCraft Studio (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/work-live\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/work-live\"\u003EDesign one site together, live\u003C/a\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECadCraft Studio for iPad and iPhone (planned for 0.6), and from Google Play\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse CivilCraft in the browser on the device; on Android, CadCraft Studio installs from its APK on getcadcraft.com/download\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpening several point or project files at once\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne file per open (GIS files that belong together, such as a shapefile&#039;s parts, open together)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"gis-and-coordinate-systems\"\u003EGIS and coordinate systems\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFile Geodatabase; KML styles, icons, ground overlays and network links\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EConvert to GeoPackage, shapefile or GeoJSON first; KML placemarks are read without their styles\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEditing GIS features or their attributes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit them in a GIS program and import again\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELabel styles (size, placement) and labels that avoid each other\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELabels are one size and may overlap where features are dense\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EVertical datums and geoid models\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EElevations are used as the file gives them; check the vertical datum yourself\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGrid shifts other than to NAD83 from NAD27 suggested by area (OSTN15, NTv2 for other datums work when you add the file), several grids at once, vertical grids\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdd the grid file for your datum yourself; one grid a project\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMap layers in the DXF or in 3D exports\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMap layers go into the PDF plot, and beside the DWG as a picture\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMap servers that need a sign-in, key or token\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse public services, or a georeferenced image file\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWriting LAS or LAZ files\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExport surfaces as a GeoTIFF elevation grid\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELiDAR intensity, color and returns; elevation grids with several bands; grid or LiDAR files over 200 MB\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELiDAR is filtered by class only, grids use their first band; cut large files into tiles first\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"input\"\u003EInput\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBox selection by touch (it needs \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELong-press each survey point to add it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETyped coordinates or distances while drawing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraw roughly, then type exact PI coordinates in the alignment&#039;s PI table\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETyping coordinates for pad corners and structures\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag their grips; zoom in for precision and watch E and N in the status bar\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESnapping to objects (other than survey points for breaklines)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom in and watch the status bar\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"3d-view\"\u003E3D view\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrawing or editing in the 3D view\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraw and edit in the plan; the 3D view follows every edit\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelecting corridor feature lines or GIS features by clicking in 3D\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick them in the plan\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExporting the exaggerated view or in feet\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExports are true scale in meters; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/export-3d\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/export-3d\"\u003EExport the site in 3D\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELabels in GLB or OBJ exports, label sizes and styles in 3D\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELabels show in the 3D view only, one size; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/civilcraft/site-3d\" data-topic=\"civilcraft/site-3d\"\u003EView the site in 3D\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETextures from photos, recording a drive as video, shadows from beyond the site, sun studies over a day\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMap layers draped on the surfaces; drive and the sun&#039;s position at one time\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E","text":"CivilCraft is early. This page lists what the current build does not do, so you do not look for it. Nothing here is promised for a date. Points Not built yet Instead, today Adding a second point file to the first Join the files in a text editor or spreadsheet first Exporting a point text file Export LandXML (points go out as CgPoints), use GIS Export (points with P, N, E, Z and D fields), or Copy the points into a spreadsheet Point label styles, symbols per description key, curves (arcs) from line codes, a description key file to share between projects Labels show the number, elevation and description, or less (CivilCraft settings); keys are kept in the project file Unit conversion Convert the file before importing; Units only relabels Surfaces Not built yet Instead, today More than one surface built from points One project per surface Hide (inner) boundaries Use Longest outside edge or an outer boundary Selecting or editing one drawn breakline Ctrl+Z, or Remove drawn breaklines and redraw Editing a surface read from LandXML (breaklines, boundary) Rebuild the surface from the points Editing a surface made from an elevation grid Import the grid again with other choices Converting a surface read from LandXML when the coordinate system changes Rebuild the surface from the points first Labeled spot elevations and slope values Read them in the status bar Alignments and profiles Not built yet Instead, today Spirals (transition curves) Circular curves only; LandXML alignments with spirals are skipped Inserting a PI between two others Redraw the alignment, or retype PI coordinates Alignments from objects, offsets, widenings Draw by PIs Typing K for a vertical curve Type L (K is shown) Circular and unsymmetrical vertical curves Symmetric parabolic curves only More than one design profile per alignment One alignment per variant Zooming the profile view, profile bands under the profile The profile view fits the whole alignment; plot it on plan and profile sheets Sections in DWG or LandXML Section sheets as PDF; CSV of the areas and volumes Sample lines at an angle, or of different widths along the road One width each side for the whole alignment Corridors Not built yet Instead, today Curb and gutter, sidewalks, ditches, other subassemblies One lane, one shoulder and a side slope each side Different left and right sides, or changes along the road Split the work into separate alignments Spiral transitions for superelevation, rotation about an edge, superelevation in LandXML Superelevation rotates about the centerline on circular curves; widen a lane with a width target to a feature line Regions, targets for the side slopes (daylight to a line or a surface), corridor surfaces of their own Targets move the edges of pavement and shoulders (Corridors); the corridor is graded into the proposed surface Corridors exported as LandXML roadways The proposed surface is exported Swept paths with speed, tyre slip, steering time, steering trailers or body clearance; a library of standard design vehicles; a swept path drawn by dragging the vehicle Low-speed swept paths along a feature line or an alignment, with your own vehicle sizes (Vehicle swept paths) Grading and volumes Not built yet Instead, today Benches, open feature lines graded into the design, grading to a feature line from elsewhere Close the feature line (Feature lines), or use it as a breakline of the existing ground Inserting or removing a feature line vertex Draw the line again, or make a stepped offset of it Arcs in drawn breaklines (through survey points), spirals in feature lines Draw a feature line with arcs and use it as a breakline of the existing ground Grading to a distance, elevation or another surface Daylight to existing ground only Adding or removing a pad corner Delete the pad and draw it again TIN-to-TIN (prism) volumes, choosing the grid cell size Grid method, cell size chosen for you Swell and shrink, topsoil, pavement depths Apply factors to the reported numbers yourself Free-haul and overhaul costs, haul across different alignments, borrow pits and waste sites placed on the plan The mass-haul diagram with balance points, volumes moved and average haul distances Pipe networks Not built yet Instead, today More than one network per project Separate projects for storm and sanitary Water mains (pressure networks: demands, pressures, fire flow, tanks, pumps, valves) Check them in water distribution software; sanitary design flows are in Sanitary design flows Sewer flows over time (hydrographs, dynamic routing, storage, pumps, overflows) Sanitary design flows gives the steady peak flow a design sheet uses Inlet capacity, gutter spread, detention routing, hydrographs, energy losses other than one junction coefficient Check them in other software; flows, sizes and the grade lines are in Storm drainage Catchments in the PDF plot, DWG, DXF or LandXML; catchments from the surface (watersheds) Draw catchments by hand; they are saved in the project file Pipe catalogs, non-round pipes, structures that are not upright boxes or cylinders (cones, steps, benching), a drainage category in the library Structure types from parameters (Pipe network); pipes with a diameter Pipes in the profile view, curved pipes Read inverts and cover in the right panel Parcels Not built yet Instead, today Laying out lots by area or frontage, parcels from the lines around them, right-of-way Draw each parcel by its corners, or enter its calls Curve tables and line tables on the plan; labels moved by hand Every course is labeled along itself Parcels in LandXML Parcels are saved in the project file and go to the PDF, DWG and DXF Traverses Not built yet Instead, today Least-squares adjustment, the transit rule, weighting angles against distances The compass rule Deflection angles, zenith angles and slope distances, elevations by trigonometric leveling Reduce to horizontal distances and angles right, or bearings Side shots, raw data files from data collectors Import the points as a point file Staking Not built yet Instead, today Offset stakes (reference points set back from the stake), slope stakes at the catch point Stake at the offsets you need, including past the shoulder Sending stakes straight to a data collector's own format The CSV opens in the office software of most data collectors Staking pipes, pads and parcels Use survey points near an alignment, or the parcel's courses Files and output Not built yet Instead, today Text, hatches and dimensions from DWG or DXF; paper space; linked drawings (xrefs); binary DXF Linework from model space opens (Open a DWG or DXF as linework); open linked drawings on their own Moving several points at once by dragging Cut and paste them, or edit them one at a time Portrait sheets, title blocks of your own, viewports you arrange, plan and profile sheets in DWG One landscape plan sheet per PDF, or plan and profile sheets along an alignment (PDF) Plotting elevation bands and profiles Bands are screen only; take a screenshot if you need them Changing layer names, colors or label styles Change them in a CAD program after DXF export Cloud saving and version history Save .civilcraft.json files and keep them backed up; to work on one project together, use a live session in CadCraft Studio (Design one site together, live) CadCraft Studio for iPad and iPhone (planned for 0.6), and from Google Play Use CivilCraft in the browser on the device; on Android, CadCraft Studio installs from its APK on getcadcraft.com/download Opening several point or project files at once One file per open (GIS files that belong together, such as a shapefile's parts, open together) GIS and coordinate systems Not built yet Instead, today File Geodatabase; KML styles, icons, ground overlays and network links Convert to GeoPackage, shapefile or GeoJSON first; KML placemarks are read without their styles Editing GIS features or their attributes Edit them in a GIS program and import again Label styles (size, placement) and labels that avoid each other Labels are one size and may overlap where features are dense Vertical datums and geoid models Elevations are used as the file gives them; check the vertical datum yourself Grid shifts other than to NAD83 from NAD27 suggested by area (OSTN15, NTv2 for other datums work when you add the file), several grids at once, vertical grids Add the grid file for your datum yourself; one grid a project Map layers in the DXF or in 3D exports Map layers go into the PDF plot, and beside the DWG as a picture Map servers that need a sign-in, key or token Use public services, or a georeferenced image file Writing LAS or LAZ files Export surfaces as a GeoTIFF elevation grid LiDAR intensity, color and returns; elevation grids with several bands; grid or LiDAR files over 200 MB LiDAR is filtered by class only, grids use their first band; cut large files into tiles first Input Not built yet Instead, today Box selection by touch (it needs Shift) Long-press each survey point to add it Typed coordinates or distances while drawing Draw roughly, then type exact PI coordinates in the alignment's PI table Typing coordinates for pad corners and structures Drag their grips; zoom in for precision and watch E and N in the status bar Snapping to objects (other than survey points for breaklines) Zoom in and watch the status bar 3D view Not built yet Instead, today Drawing or editing in the 3D view Draw and edit in the plan; the 3D view follows every edit Selecting corridor feature lines or GIS features by clicking in 3D Click them in the plan Exporting the exaggerated view or in feet Exports are true scale in meters; see Export the site in 3D Labels in GLB or OBJ exports, label sizes and styles in 3D Labels show in the 3D view only, one size; see View the site in 3D Textures from photos, recording a drive as video, shadows from beyond the site, sun studies over a day Map layers draped on the surfaces; drive and the sun's position at one time"},{"slug":"help","scope":"basics","title":"Using this help","summary":"Open the help, search it, and see the steps for your computer.","section":"start","order":10,"keywords":["help","f1","search","tutorials","manual"],"headings":[{"id":"open-the-help","text":"Open the help","level":2},{"id":"find-a-topic","text":"Find a topic","level":2},{"id":"steps-for-your-computer","text":"Steps for your computer","level":2},{"id":"the-same-help-on-the-website","text":"The same help on the website","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EEvery app carries its own help. It is part of the app, so it opens with no connection and always describes the version you are running.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-the-help\"\u003EOpen the help\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHelp\u003C/span\u003E in the bar at the top of the app.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClose\u003C/span\u003E to put it away. Your drawing stays as it was.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn most Mac keyboards the top row controls brightness and sound. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E together with the \u003Cstrong\u003Efn\u003C/strong\u003E key (or the globe key), or use the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHelp\u003C/span\u003E button. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys#function-keys-on-a-mac\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\" data-hash=\"function-keys-on-a-mac\"\u003EKeys on a Mac\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"find-a-topic\"\u003EFind a topic\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType in the search box at the top of the help. Results update as you type and include other programs&#039; command names, so typing what you would have typed elsewhere usually finds the right page.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETopics are grouped the same way in every app: getting started, walkthroughs, coming from other software, reference, and troubleshooting.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELinks inside a topic open the next topic in the same panel. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack\u003C/span\u003E returns to where you were.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps-for-your-computer\"\u003ESteps for your computer\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome steps differ between Mac, Windows and Linux, between browsers, and between being online and offline. The help works out which applies to you and shows those steps. The line at the top of a topic says what it chose, for example &quot;Showing steps for Mac, Chrome or Edge, online&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChange\u003C/span\u003E on that line to pick another computer or browser, for example to help a colleague.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow all\u003C/span\u003E to see every version of every step, each labeled.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EKeyboard shortcuts are written the way your keyboard labels them: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E reads as ⌘S on a Mac and Ctrl+S on Windows and Linux.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-same-help-on-the-website\"\u003EThe same help on the website\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe website shows the same topics at getcadcraft.com/tutorials, with every platform&#039;s steps. In the app, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen on the website\u003C/span\u003E at the bottom of a topic opens that page when you are online.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Every app carries its own help. It is part of the app, so it opens with no connection and always describes the version you are running. Open the help Press F1, or choose Help in the bar at the top of the app. Press Esc or choose Close to put it away. Your drawing stays as it was. On most Mac keyboards the top row controls brightness and sound. Press F1 together with the fn key (or the globe key), or use the Help button. See Keys on a Mac. Find a topic Type in the search box at the top of the help. Results update as you type and include other programs' command names, so typing what you would have typed elsewhere usually finds the right page. Topics are grouped the same way in every app: getting started, walkthroughs, coming from other software, reference, and troubleshooting. Links inside a topic open the next topic in the same panel. Back returns to where you were. Steps for your computer Some steps differ between Mac, Windows and Linux, between browsers, and between being online and offline. The help works out which applies to you and shows those steps. The line at the top of a topic says what it chose, for example \"Showing steps for Mac, Chrome or Edge, online\". Choose Change on that line to pick another computer or browser, for example to help a colleague. Choose Show all to see every version of every step, each labeled. Keyboard shortcuts are written the way your keyboard labels them: Ctrl+S reads as ⌘S on a Mac and Ctrl+S on Windows and Linux. The same help on the website The website shows the same topics at getcadcraft.com/tutorials, with every platform's steps. In the app, Open on the website at the bottom of a topic opens that page when you are online."},{"slug":"browsers","scope":"basics","title":"Browsers and computers","summary":"Which browsers the apps run in, what differs between them, and what your computer needs.","section":"start","order":20,"keywords":["chrome","edge","firefox","safari","brave","browser","mac","windows","linux","ipad","tablet","webgl","requirements","memory"],"headings":[{"id":"which-browser","text":"Which browser","level":2},{"id":"what-differs-between-browsers","text":"What differs between browsers","level":2},{"id":"mac-windows-and-linux","text":"Mac, Windows and Linux","level":2},{"id":"tablets-and-touch-screens","text":"Tablets and touch screens","level":2},{"id":"what-your-computer-needs","text":"What your computer needs","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe apps run inside a web browser. They do the work on your own computer: your files are opened there and never sent to us (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/your-files\" data-topic=\"basics/your-files\"\u003EWhere your files go\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"which-browser\"\u003EWhich browser\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUse an up-to-date version of one of these:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EBrowser\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EEngine\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENotes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChrome, Edge, Brave, Opera, Vivaldi\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChromium\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe most complete: folder picking, saving for offline use, file handling.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFirefox\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGecko\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWorks. Opening a whole folder uses a simpler picker. Offline saving may not work in private windows.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESafari\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWebKit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWorks on Mac and iPad. Opening a whole folder uses a simpler picker. Safari may delete an offline copy you have not used for a few weeks.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAll three engines are fine for everyday work. Where an app does something differently in one of them, its help shows the steps for your browser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-differs-between-browsers\"\u003EWhat differs between browsers\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOpening a folder.\u003C/strong\u003E Chromium browsers show a real folder picker and can read the folder directly. Firefox and Safari ask you to choose a folder and then read every file in it, which takes longer for big project folders; dragging the folder onto the app works in all three.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESaving.\u003C/strong\u003E The apps save by downloading a file. Your browser decides where downloads go and whether it asks first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:chromium\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EChrome or Edge\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn Chrome or Edge, turn on &quot;Ask where to save each file before downloading&quot; (in Settings, under Downloads) to choose a folder every time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:firefox\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EFirefox\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn Firefox, open Settings, then General, then Downloads, and choose &quot;Always ask you where to save files&quot; to pick a folder every time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ESafari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn Safari, open Settings, then General, and set &quot;File download location&quot; to &quot;Ask for each download&quot; to pick a folder every time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mac-windows-and-linux\"\u003EMac, Windows and Linux\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe apps are the same on every computer. What changes is the keyboard: shortcuts are written for your computer automatically. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\"\u003EKeys, mice and trackpads\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"tablets-and-touch-screens\"\u003ETablets and touch screens\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome apps accept touch (one finger draws or selects, two fingers pinch to zoom and pan). Each app&#039;s help says whether it does. On an iPad, use Safari or Chrome. A keyboard helps with apps that use typed commands.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-your-computer-needs\"\u003EWhat your computer needs\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThese are planned minimums for the apps, not measured requirements:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E8 GB of memory (16 GB is recommended for large federated models).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA graphics chip with WebGL 2 for the 3D apps. If a 3D view stays blank, check that hardware acceleration is on in your browser&#039;s settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA screen at least 1280 pixels wide for the full toolbars; smaller screens wrap them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EVery large files (big plan sets, big models) use a lot of memory in a browser tab. Close other heavy tabs if an app slows down.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The apps run inside a web browser. They do the work on your own computer: your files are opened there and never sent to us (see Where your files go). Which browser Use an up-to-date version of one of these: Browser Engine Notes Chrome, Edge, Brave, Opera, Vivaldi Chromium The most complete: folder picking, saving for offline use, file handling. Firefox Gecko Works. Opening a whole folder uses a simpler picker. Offline saving may not work in private windows. Safari WebKit Works on Mac and iPad. Opening a whole folder uses a simpler picker. Safari may delete an offline copy you have not used for a few weeks. All three engines are fine for everyday work. Where an app does something differently in one of them, its help shows the steps for your browser. What differs between browsers Opening a folder. Chromium browsers show a real folder picker and can read the folder directly. Firefox and Safari ask you to choose a folder and then read every file in it, which takes longer for big project folders; dragging the folder onto the app works in all three. Saving. The apps save by downloading a file. Your browser decides where downloads go and whether it asks first. In Chrome or Edge, turn on \"Ask where to save each file before downloading\" (in Settings, under Downloads) to choose a folder every time. In Firefox, open Settings, then General, then Downloads, and choose \"Always ask you where to save files\" to pick a folder every time. In Safari, open Settings, then General, and set \"File download location\" to \"Ask for each download\" to pick a folder every time. Mac, Windows and Linux The apps are the same on every computer. What changes is the keyboard: shortcuts are written for your computer automatically. See Keys, mice and trackpads. Tablets and touch screens Some apps accept touch (one finger draws or selects, two fingers pinch to zoom and pan). Each app's help says whether it does. On an iPad, use Safari or Chrome. A keyboard helps with apps that use typed commands. What your computer needs These are planned minimums for the apps, not measured requirements: 8 GB of memory (16 GB is recommended for large federated models). A graphics chip with WebGL 2 for the 3D apps. If a 3D view stays blank, check that hardware acceleration is on in your browser's settings. A screen at least 1280 pixels wide for the full toolbars; smaller screens wrap them. Very large files (big plan sets, big models) use a lot of memory in a browser tab. Close other heavy tabs if an app slows down."},{"slug":"keys","scope":"basics","title":"Keys, mice and trackpads","summary":"How shortcuts are written, function keys on a Mac, right-clicking and panning without a mouse.","section":"start","order":30,"keywords":["keyboard","shortcut","ctrl","command","cmd","option","fn","function keys","trackpad","right click","middle button","magic mouse"],"headings":[{"id":"how-shortcuts-are-written","text":"How shortcuts are written","level":2},{"id":"function-keys-on-a-mac","text":"Function keys on a Mac","level":2},{"id":"shortcuts-the-browser-keeps","text":"Shortcuts the browser keeps","level":2},{"id":"mouse-buttons-on-a-trackpad","text":"Mouse buttons on a trackpad","level":2},{"id":"typing-in-a-text-box","text":"Typing in a text box","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"how-shortcuts-are-written\"\u003EHow shortcuts are written\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe help writes each shortcut the way your keyboard labels it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWritten\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EOn a Mac\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EOn Windows and Linux\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECommand (⌘) and S\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECtrl and S\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EControl (⌃), Shift (⇧) and F\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECtrl, Shift and F\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option ←\"\u003E⌥←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOption (⌥) and ←\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAlt and ←\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethe Delete key\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBackspace\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Efn and Delete (forward delete)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDelete\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReturn\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEnter\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA plus sign means hold the keys together. &quot;Then&quot; means press one after the other.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMost app shortcuts accept either Command or Control on a Mac, so \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E works both ways. A few, such as full screen (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), use the Control key on every computer; the help says so where that applies.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"function-keys-on-a-mac\"\u003EFunction keys on a Mac\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn most Mac keyboards the top row changes brightness, volume and media. To send F1 to F12:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ehold \u003Cstrong\u003Efn\u003C/strong\u003E (or the globe key) while you press the key, or\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eopen \u003Cstrong\u003ESystem Settings\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003EKeyboard\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003EKeyboard Shortcuts\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003EFunction Keys\u003C/strong\u003E, and turn on &quot;Use F1, F2, etc. keys as standard function keys&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome F-keys are also claimed by macOS itself (for example Mission Control). If a key does nothing in the app, try it with fn, or use the toolbar button instead.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"shortcuts-the-browser-keeps\"\u003EShortcuts the browser keeps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA web page cannot take some shortcuts from the browser, whatever it does. In every app:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command N\"\u003E⌘N\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+N\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command T\"\u003E⌘T\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+T\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command W\"\u003E⌘W\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+W\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E always open or close browser windows and tabs. The apps use buttons for New and Close instead.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Q\"\u003E⌘Q\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Q\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on a Mac and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option F4\"\u003E⌥ F4\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+F4\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on Windows quit the browser. If you have unsaved work, the browser asks first (&quot;Leave site?&quot;); choose to stay, then save.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe browser&#039;s own full screen (\u003Cspan class=\"hp\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:mac\" title=\"Mac\"\u003EControl-Command-F, or the green button on the window\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:windows,linux\" title=\"Windows or Linux\"\u003EF11\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/span\u003E) still works. The apps also have their own screen modes on \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, which can hide the app&#039;s bars too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mouse-buttons-on-a-trackpad\"\u003EMouse buttons on a trackpad\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe apps use the right button and the middle button. Without a three-button mouse:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERight-click:\u003C/strong\u003E click with two fingers. Turn on &quot;Secondary click&quot; in \u003Cstrong\u003ESystem Settings\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003ETrackpad\u003C/strong\u003E, if two-finger click does nothing. Holding \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while you click opens the browser&#039;s menu, but inside a drawing some apps read it as an ordinary click (with Control held), so prefer the two-finger click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMiddle-button drag (pan):\u003C/strong\u003E each app offers another way to pan, such as holding \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while you drag or dragging with the right button. The app&#039;s keyboard page says which.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWheel zoom:\u003C/strong\u003E two-finger scroll on the trackpad zooms in most apps. Pinch also zooms where the app supports it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:windows,linux\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EWindows or Linux\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERight-click:\u003C/strong\u003E click with two fingers, or click the lower-right corner of the touchpad. This depends on your touchpad settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMiddle-button drag (pan):\u003C/strong\u003E each app offers another way to pan, such as holding \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while you drag or dragging with the right button. The app&#039;s keyboard page says which.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWheel zoom:\u003C/strong\u003E two-finger scroll on the touchpad zooms in most apps.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"typing-in-a-text-box\"\u003ETyping in a text box\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile you type in a box (a name, a note, a search), the app&#039;s one-letter shortcuts are off, so typing &quot;L&quot; types an L. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or click on the drawing to give the keys back to the app.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"How shortcuts are written The help writes each shortcut the way your keyboard labels it. Written On a Mac On Windows and Linux Ctrl+S Command (⌘) and S Ctrl and S Ctrl+Shift+F Control (⌃), Shift (⇧) and F Ctrl, Shift and F Alt+← Option (⌥) and ← Alt and ← Backspace the Delete key Backspace Delete fn and Delete (forward delete) Delete Enter Return Enter A plus sign means hold the keys together. \"Then\" means press one after the other. Most app shortcuts accept either Command or Control on a Mac, so Ctrl+Z works both ways. A few, such as full screen (Ctrl+Shift+F), use the Control key on every computer; the help says so where that applies. Function keys on a Mac On most Mac keyboards the top row changes brightness, volume and media. To send F1 to F12: hold fn (or the globe key) while you press the key, or open System Settings, then Keyboard, then Keyboard Shortcuts, then Function Keys, and turn on \"Use F1, F2, etc. keys as standard function keys\". Some F-keys are also claimed by macOS itself (for example Mission Control). If a key does nothing in the app, try it with fn, or use the toolbar button instead. Shortcuts the browser keeps A web page cannot take some shortcuts from the browser, whatever it does. In every app: Ctrl+N, Ctrl+T and Ctrl+W always open or close browser windows and tabs. The apps use buttons for New and Close instead. Ctrl+Q on a Mac and Alt+F4 on Windows quit the browser. If you have unsaved work, the browser asks first (\"Leave site?\"); choose to stay, then save. The browser's own full screen (Control-Command-F, or the green button on the window / F11) still works. The apps also have their own screen modes on Ctrl+Shift+F, which can hide the app's bars too. Mouse buttons on a trackpad The apps use the right button and the middle button. Without a three-button mouse: Right-click: click with two fingers. Turn on \"Secondary click\" in System Settings, then Trackpad, if two-finger click does nothing. Holding Ctrl while you click opens the browser's menu, but inside a drawing some apps read it as an ordinary click (with Control held), so prefer the two-finger click. Middle-button drag (pan): each app offers another way to pan, such as holding Space while you drag or dragging with the right button. The app's keyboard page says which. Wheel zoom: two-finger scroll on the trackpad zooms in most apps. Pinch also zooms where the app supports it. Right-click: click with two fingers, or click the lower-right corner of the touchpad. This depends on your touchpad settings. Middle-button drag (pan): each app offers another way to pan, such as holding Space while you drag or dragging with the right button. The app's keyboard page says which. Wheel zoom: two-finger scroll on the touchpad zooms in most apps. Typing in a text box While you type in a box (a name, a note, a search), the app's one-letter shortcuts are off, so typing \"L\" types an L. Press Esc or click on the drawing to give the keys back to the app."},{"slug":"find-tools","scope":"basics","title":"Find a tool or setting","summary":"Press Ctrl+K and type a few letters to run any tool, change a setting or open a help topic.","section":"start","order":32,"keywords":["find","search","command palette","quick open","run command","ctrl k","cmd k","tool","setting","shortcut"],"headings":[{"id":"find-and-run","text":"Find and run","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EEvery app has a search box for its tools, its settings and this help. You don&#039;t need to know where a tool sits in the toolbar.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"find-and-run\"\u003EFind and run\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command K\"\u003E⌘K\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+K\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFind\u003C/span\u003E in the bar at the top.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType a few letters of what you want: &quot;fillet&quot;, &quot;panel text&quot;, &quot;units&quot;. Every word you type has to match, so add a second word to narrow the list.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to run the first match, or move with the arrow keys first. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E closes the box.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA tool runs as if you had clicked it in the toolbar. A setting opens \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E at that setting. A help topic opens here, in the help panel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhat you used last comes first the next time, so a tool you use often is one key press away.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETools that can&#039;t run right now, for example before a file is open, are listed but greyed out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd-ons&#039; tools are in the list too, once the add-on is switched on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Every app has a search box for its tools, its settings and this help. You don't need to know where a tool sits in the toolbar. Find and run Press Ctrl+K, or choose Find in the bar at the top. Type a few letters of what you want: \"fillet\", \"panel text\", \"units\". Every word you type has to match, so add a second word to narrow the list. Press Enter to run the first match, or move with the arrow keys first. Esc closes the box. A tool runs as if you had clicked it in the toolbar. A setting opens Settings at that setting. A help topic opens here, in the help panel. Things to know What you used last comes first the next time, so a tool you use often is one key press away. Tools that can't run right now, for example before a file is open, are listed but greyed out. Add-ons' tools are in the list too, once the add-on is switched on."},{"slug":"arrange-panels","scope":"basics","title":"Arrange the panels and use a second monitor","summary":"Dock the side panels left, right or at the bottom, float them over the drawing, or move them to their own window on another screen.","section":"start","order":35,"keywords":["panels","dock","float","move panel","drag","layout","second monitor","two screens","dual monitor","multiple monitors","window","pop-up","reset layout"],"headings":[{"id":"move-a-panel-in-the-window","text":"Move a panel in the window","level":2},{"id":"use-the-keyboard","text":"Use the keyboard","level":2},{"id":"put-the-panels-on-a-second-monitor","text":"Put the panels on a second monitor","level":2},{"id":"more-than-one-panels-window","text":"More than one panels window","level":2},{"id":"put-the-panels-back","text":"Put the panels back","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe panels beside the drawing can move. Put them where they suit you, or give them a window of their own on a second monitor so the drawing gets the whole of the first one.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis works in the apps whose panels have a grip: a slim bar with the panel&#039;s name and a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E button at the top of each panel. The other apps are getting it next.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"move-a-panel-in-the-window\"\u003EMove a panel in the window\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag the panel by its name at the top.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhile you drag, outlined areas appear on the left, the right and the bottom of the drawing. Drop the panel on one to dock it there. The area you are over darkens.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrop it anywhere else over the drawing to make it float.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBoth panels can share one side: they stack, and the line between them drags to share the space. Drag the inner edge of a docked side to make it wider or narrower; double-click the edge to put it back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA floating panel stays over the drawing. Move it by its name, and resize it by dragging its lower-right corner (or tab to the corner and use the arrow keys). Double-click its name to dock it again where it was.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDrag with one finger on the panel&#039;s name. Drag the lower-right corner of a floating panel with one finger to resize it, and the edges between docked panels the same way. To float or dock with no dragging, use the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E menu.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"use-the-keyboard\"\u003EUse the keyboard\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery panel&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E button opens a menu: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDock on the left\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDock on the right\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDock at the bottom\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFloat over the drawing\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove to a new window\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPut it back\u003C/span\u003E. Tab to the button, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, move with the arrow keys and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E closes the menu.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"put-the-panels-on-a-second-monitor\"\u003EPut the panels on a second monitor\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E on a panel, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove all panels to a new window\u003C/span\u003E. You can also choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove panels to a new window\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA small window opens with the panels in it. Drag it to your other monitor and make it as big as you like.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EKeep working: the panels act on the drawing as before, and the drawing fills the main window.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EShortcuts keep working while the panels window is in front: keys you press there, other than typing in a box, go to the drawing. Questions and dialogs the app asks still open in the main window, so look there when one appears. The theme and the panel text size follow the main window.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:chromium\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EChrome or Edge\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChrome and Edge can place the window on the other screen for you. With a second screen connected, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPut it on my other screen\u003C/span\u003E in the panels window (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPut the panels on my other screen\u003C/span\u003E in a panel&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E menu). The browser asks once whether this site may see your screens; after you allow it, the panels window opens on that screen every time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:firefox,safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EFirefox or Safari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDrag the panels window to the other monitor yourself. It opens in the same place and at the same size next time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"more-than-one-panels-window\"\u003EMore than one panels window\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach panel can have a window of its own: up to four panels windows, for example one per monitor.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn a panel&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E menu, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove to a new window\u003C/span\u003E opens another window for that panel, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove to panels window 2\u003C/span\u003E (and so on) moves it into one that is open. The second window is called &quot;panels 2&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag a panel by its name from one window to another: let go over the main window to dock it there (the outlined areas show while you are over it) or to float it, and over another panels window to move it in. Let go anywhere else and it stays where it was.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA panels window that has no panels left closes by itself.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAfter a reload the app offers your panels windows again. Browsers open one window per click, so choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen the next one\u003C/span\u003E once for each window.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"put-the-panels-back\"\u003EPut the panels back\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPut the panels back\u003C/span\u003E in the panels window, or simply close it. The panels go back where they were.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClosing or reloading the main window closes the panels window too. When the app opens again it says your panels were in their own window; choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen it\u003C/span\u003E to bring the window back, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeep them here\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset layout\u003C/span\u003E puts every panel back where it started.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf nothing opens when you move panels to a new window, the browser blocked the pop-up. The app says so and keeps the panels where they were. Allow pop-ups for getcadcraft.com, then try again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe layout is kept for each app in this browser, along with your other settings.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The panels beside the drawing can move. Put them where they suit you, or give them a window of their own on a second monitor so the drawing gets the whole of the first one. This works in the apps whose panels have a grip: a slim bar with the panel's name and a ⋯ button at the top of each panel. The other apps are getting it next. Move a panel in the window Drag the panel by its name at the top. While you drag, outlined areas appear on the left, the right and the bottom of the drawing. Drop the panel on one to dock it there. The area you are over darkens. Drop it anywhere else over the drawing to make it float. Both panels can share one side: they stack, and the line between them drags to share the space. Drag the inner edge of a docked side to make it wider or narrower; double-click the edge to put it back. A floating panel stays over the drawing. Move it by its name, and resize it by dragging its lower-right corner (or tab to the corner and use the arrow keys). Double-click its name to dock it again where it was. Drag with one finger on the panel's name. Drag the lower-right corner of a floating panel with one finger to resize it, and the edges between docked panels the same way. To float or dock with no dragging, use the ⋯ menu. Use the keyboard Every panel's ⋯ button opens a menu: Dock on the left, Dock on the right, Dock at the bottom, Float over the drawing, Move to a new window and Put it back. Tab to the button, press Enter, move with the arrow keys and press Enter again. Esc closes the menu. Put the panels on a second monitor Choose ⋯ on a panel, then Move all panels to a new window. You can also choose Move panels to a new window in Settings. A small window opens with the panels in it. Drag it to your other monitor and make it as big as you like. Keep working: the panels act on the drawing as before, and the drawing fills the main window. Shortcuts keep working while the panels window is in front: keys you press there, other than typing in a box, go to the drawing. Questions and dialogs the app asks still open in the main window, so look there when one appears. The theme and the panel text size follow the main window. Chrome and Edge can place the window on the other screen for you. With a second screen connected, choose Put it on my other screen in the panels window (or Put the panels on my other screen in a panel's ⋯ menu). The browser asks once whether this site may see your screens; after you allow it, the panels window opens on that screen every time. Drag the panels window to the other monitor yourself. It opens in the same place and at the same size next time. More than one panels window Each panel can have a window of its own: up to four panels windows, for example one per monitor. In a panel's ⋯ menu, Move to a new window opens another window for that panel, and Move to panels window 2 (and so on) moves it into one that is open. The second window is called \"panels 2\". Drag a panel by its name from one window to another: let go over the main window to dock it there (the outlined areas show while you are over it) or to float it, and over another panels window to move it in. Let go anywhere else and it stays where it was. A panels window that has no panels left closes by itself. After a reload the app offers your panels windows again. Browsers open one window per click, so choose Open the next one once for each window. Put the panels back Choose Put the panels back in the panels window, or simply close it. The panels go back where they were. Closing or reloading the main window closes the panels window too. When the app opens again it says your panels were in their own window; choose Open it to bring the window back, or Keep them here. Settings › General › Reset layout puts every panel back where it started. If nothing opens when you move panels to a new window, the browser blocked the pop-up. The app says so and keeps the panels where they were. Allow pop-ups for getcadcraft.com, then try again. The layout is kept for each app in this browser, along with your other settings."},{"slug":"offline","scope":"basics","title":"Working offline","summary":"Save an app for offline use, what works with no connection, and what needs one.","section":"start","order":40,"keywords":["offline","no internet","airplane","site visit","cache","service worker","install","download","connection"],"headings":[{"id":"save-an-app-for-offline-use","text":"Save an app for offline use","level":2},{"id":"what-works-offline","text":"What works offline","level":2},{"id":"what-needs-a-connection","text":"What needs a connection","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe apps do their work on your computer, so most of it works with no connection. The browser only needs to have the app&#039;s files saved first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save-an-app-for-offline-use\"\u003ESave an app for offline use\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the app while you are online.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E in the bar at the top.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWait for &quot;Offline ready&quot;. The app&#039;s files, its file readers and this help are now saved in your browser. Apps that read DWG or PDF files save their readers too, so this can take a minute the first time.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDo this once per app, in each browser you use. To go back to the app later with no connection, open the same address you used before, or a bookmark of it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"net:offline\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EOffline\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou are offline now. If this app was saved for offline use, everything below works. If it was not, you can still use what has already loaded in this tab, but don&#039;t reload it until you are online again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-works-offline\"\u003EWhat works offline\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpening files from your computer, drawing, editing, measuring and checking.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESaving and exporting: every save is a download to your computer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThis help, including search.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-needs-a-connection\"\u003EWhat needs a connection\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpening the app for the first time, and saving it for offline use.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGetting a newer version of the app. When you are online, the app picks up the newest version the next time it opens, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E refreshes the saved copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELinks to the website, such as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen on the website\u003C/span\u003E and links to other pages.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECloud saving and sync between computers, which are not available yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA saved copy belongs to one browser on one computer. Clearing your browser&#039;s site data removes it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPrivate windows in some browsers, and some locked-down work computers, do not allow offline saving. The button tells you when it cannot save.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ESafari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESafari removes saved site data from sites you have not visited for several weeks. Open the app now and then while online, or save it again before a trip.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECadCraft Studio, the desktop app with all eight apps in one install, is on getcadcraft.com/download.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"The apps do their work on your computer, so most of it works with no connection. The browser only needs to have the app's files saved first. Save an app for offline use Open the app while you are online. Choose Save for offline in the bar at the top. Wait for \"Offline ready\". The app's files, its file readers and this help are now saved in your browser. Apps that read DWG or PDF files save their readers too, so this can take a minute the first time. Do this once per app, in each browser you use. To go back to the app later with no connection, open the same address you used before, or a bookmark of it. You are offline now. If this app was saved for offline use, everything below works. If it was not, you can still use what has already loaded in this tab, but don't reload it until you are online again. What works offline Opening files from your computer, drawing, editing, measuring and checking. Saving and exporting: every save is a download to your computer. This help, including search. What needs a connection Opening the app for the first time, and saving it for offline use. Getting a newer version of the app. When you are online, the app picks up the newest version the next time it opens, and Save for offline refreshes the saved copy. Links to the website, such as Open on the website and links to other pages. Cloud saving and sync between computers, which are not available yet. Things to know A saved copy belongs to one browser on one computer. Clearing your browser's site data removes it. Private windows in some browsers, and some locked-down work computers, do not allow offline saving. The button tells you when it cannot save. Safari removes saved site data from sites you have not visited for several weeks. Open the app now and then while online, or save it again before a trip. CadCraft Studio, the desktop app with all eight apps in one install, is on getcadcraft.com/download."},{"slug":"your-files","scope":"basics","title":"Where your files go","summary":"Files stay on your computer, how saving works, and what the browser remembers.","section":"start","order":50,"keywords":["privacy","upload","cloud","save","download","storage","local","security","confidential"],"headings":[{"id":"your-files-stay-on-your-computer","text":"Your files stay on your computer","level":2},{"id":"how-saving-works","text":"How saving works","level":2},{"id":"what-the-browser-remembers","text":"What the browser remembers","level":2},{"id":"cloud-saving","text":"Cloud saving","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"your-files-stay-on-your-computer\"\u003EYour files stay on your computer\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen you open a drawing, model or PDF, the app reads it inside your browser. \u003Cstrong\u003EThe file is not uploaded.\u003C/strong\u003E We never see it, and it works the same with no connection.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-saving-works\"\u003EHow saving works\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA browser page cannot overwrite files on your computer by itself, so the apps save by \u003Cstrong\u003Edownloading\u003C/strong\u003E a new file:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe download goes where your browser puts downloads, usually your Downloads folder, unless you have set the browser to ask each time (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/browsers#what-differs-between-browsers\" data-topic=\"basics/browsers\" data-hash=\"what-differs-between-browsers\"\u003EBrowsers and computers\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EApps that change a file save a copy with a new name, such as &quot;(edited)&quot; or &quot;(marked up)&quot;, so your original stays untouched.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf you close the tab with unsaved work, the browser asks first (&quot;Leave site?&quot;).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-the-browser-remembers\"\u003EWhat the browser remembers\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome apps remember small settings in your browser, such as your author name or tool styles. They stay in that browser on that computer, and clearing site data removes them. Each app&#039;s help says what it keeps.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"cloud-saving\"\u003ECloud saving\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECloud saving and sync between computers are part of the commercial license. They are not available yet. Until then, keep your files where you keep your other project files and back them up the usual way.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Your files stay on your computer When you open a drawing, model or PDF, the app reads it inside your browser. The file is not uploaded. We never see it, and it works the same with no connection. How saving works A browser page cannot overwrite files on your computer by itself, so the apps save by downloading a new file: The download goes where your browser puts downloads, usually your Downloads folder, unless you have set the browser to ask each time (see Browsers and computers). Apps that change a file save a copy with a new name, such as \"(edited)\" or \"(marked up)\", so your original stays untouched. If you close the tab with unsaved work, the browser asks first (\"Leave site?\"). What the browser remembers Some apps remember small settings in your browser, such as your author name or tool styles. They stay in that browser on that computer, and clearing site data removes them. Each app's help says what it keeps. Cloud saving Cloud saving and sync between computers are part of the commercial license. They are not available yet. Until then, keep your files where you keep your other project files and back them up the usual way."},{"slug":"work-together-live","scope":"basics","title":"Work together live","summary":"Share the file open in an app so teammates edit it with you on your own network with a short code, and join someone else's session. Invite links over the internet are not open yet.","section":"tasks","order":90,"keywords":["live","studio live","work together","collaborate","share live","join","code","qr","invite link","link","host","approve","guest","network","relay","license","many people","limit","allow anyway","slow","recent edits","edit log","who changed","history"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"share-the-file-you-have-open","text":"Share the file you have open","level":2},{"id":"let-people-in","text":"Let people in","level":2},{"id":"when-many-people-join","text":"When many people join","level":2},{"id":"codes-on-your-own-network","text":"Codes on your own network","level":2},{"id":"invite-links-over-the-internet-not-open-yet","text":"Invite links over the internet: not open yet","level":2},{"id":"join-someone-else-s-session","text":"Join someone else's session","level":2},{"id":"who-changed-what-recent-edits","text":"Who changed what: Recent edits","level":2},{"id":"assistants-in-a-session","text":"Assistants in a session","level":2},{"id":"when-the-session-ends","text":"When the session ends","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to share a file live from CadCraft Studio, let people in, and join a session someone else shares. What you see in the drawing itself while you work together is on the app&#039;s own page about live sessions.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"share-the-file-you-have-open\"\u003EShare the file you have open\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the file in its app inside CadCraft Studio on a computer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELive\u003C/span\u003E in the app&#039;s bar, type the name others should see, then choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShare live\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStudio shows a code like \u003Ccode\u003EK7-Q4M2X\u003C/code\u003E with a picture to scan and the computer&#039;s address. Give the code to the people you want to invite.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYour file stays on your computer. Only you save it, and everyone sees your saves.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"let-people-in\"\u003ELet people in\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENobody gets in without you. When someone knocks, a card shows their name and device and whether they came on this network or through the internet:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E lets them change the file with you.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView only\u003C/span\u003E lets them watch; anything they try to change is taken back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDeny\u003C/span\u003E turns them away.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe list under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIn the session\u003C/span\u003E shows everyone. Change a person&#039;s role there, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove\u003C/span\u003E them: they keep a copy of what they had, and they can&#039;t come back without a new invitation.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-many-people-join\"\u003EWhen many people join\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA session&#039;s usual size is up to 20 people besides you. It is not a hard limit: how many people can work well together depends on their computers, the file and the network, so Studio asks you before a session grows past it instead of deciding for you.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen someone knocks and would make more than 20, their card says so, and Studio explains what to expect: every change goes to everyone, so with many people each change takes longer to reach each person, and slower laptops and connections fall behind first. Then you choose:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAllow anyway\u003C/span\u003E lets more people in for this session. Each person still waits for your \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView only\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDeny\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeep it at 20\u003C/span\u003E turns away the people waiting over the limit; they are told you are keeping the session small.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo allow more before anyone knocks, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAllow more people…\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIn the session\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack to 20\u003C/span\u003E puts the usual size back. Your choice lasts for this session only; the next one starts at 20 again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf things slow down, fewer people, a quieter file (fewer people changing it at the same moment) or a better connection (a cable instead of Wi-Fi) helps.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"codes-on-your-own-network\"\u003ECodes on your own network\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA code works for 10 minutes. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew code\u003C/span\u003E makes another; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStop inviting\u003C/span\u003E closes it. People already in stay.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOne-time code\u003C/span\u003E lets one person in per code.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAfter 3 wrong codes in a row, Studio closes the code and makes a new one, and tells you.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESessions on your own network are free, for everyone.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"invite-links-over-the-internet-not-open-yet\"\u003EInvite links over the internet: not open yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EInvite links aren&#039;t open yet. Sessions on your own network are free. Today the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOver the internet\u003C/span\u003E part of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELive\u003C/span\u003E panel says so in a plain sentence instead of offering a link.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen invite links open, this is how they will work:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOver the internet\u003C/span\u003E, choose how long the link works (1 hour, 8 hours, or until you end the session), then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInvite by link\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECopy the link and send it like a meeting link.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe link&#039;s secret part (after \u003Ccode\u003E#\u003C/code\u003E) never reaches getcadcraft.com: the site only passes on encrypted messages it can&#039;t read, and you still approve each person. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew link\u003C/span\u003E makes the old one stop working at once; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStop the link\u003C/span\u003E ends it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMaking an invite link will need you signed in, with a commercial license in force for the app. Guests will need nothing: no account, no license.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"join-someone-else-s-session\"\u003EJoin someone else&#039;s session\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn CadCraft Studio&#039;s home screen, find \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoin a live session\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the code the host gave you, or type the address shown under their code followed by the code. (Once invite links open, you will paste the host&#039;s link here too.)\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType your name and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoin\u003C/span\u003E. Studio opens the right app; it fills with the host&#039;s file once the host lets you in.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWithout Studio, scan the picture under the host&#039;s code with a device on the same network: the app opens in the browser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn CadCraft, if you already have the same file open in a Studio window, you can join from that window instead: choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELive\u003C/span\u003E, then under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoin with the drawing open here\u003C/span\u003E type the code or the address and code, and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoin here\u003C/span\u003E. Only the changes made since the file was opened travel. If the host&#039;s drawing is a different one, it replaces the one in that window; your file on disk stays as it is.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EGuest, hotel and school Wi-Fi often keep devices apart, so a code can&#039;t find the host there. Use a network that lets devices see each other, such as the office network. Invite links, for when you are not on the same network, are not open yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"who-changed-what-recent-edits\"\u003EWho changed what: Recent edits\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe Live panel lists \u003Cstrong\u003ERecent edits\u003C/strong\u003E: who made each change and what it was (the step&#039;s name, such as Line or Move), newest first, with how long ago. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow more\u003C/span\u003E lists more of them. With something selected in CadCraft or MarkupCraft, the panel also says who changed it last, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOnly what is selected\u003C/span\u003E keeps just the edits that touched it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe list is made in your own window from the session&#039;s edits while the session runs. It is gone when the session ends: it is never saved in the file or sent anywhere. Everyone in the session can see who made each change. If you joined after people had started, or were away for a while, the edits from before are not in your list, and the panel says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"assistants-in-a-session\"\u003EAssistants in a session\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf someone turned on assistants in their app&#039;s settings, their AI assistant can ask to work in the session too. The host&#039;s own assistant joins at once; anyone else&#039;s waits for the host, with a card saying whose assistant it is: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAllow\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDeny\u003C/span\u003E. An assistant edits like its person, with its own undo, and leaves the session when its person leaves.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-the-session-ends\"\u003EWhen the session ends\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe host chooses \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEnd the session\u003C/span\u003E, or closes the file. Everyone else keeps an unsaved copy of the file as it was: save it if you want to keep it. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELeave\u003C/span\u003E does the same for you alone.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to share a file live from CadCraft Studio, let people in, and join a session someone else shares. What you see in the drawing itself while you work together is on the app's own page about live sessions. Share the file you have open Open the file in its app inside CadCraft Studio on a computer. Choose Live in the app's bar, type the name others should see, then choose Share live. Studio shows a code like K7-Q4M2X with a picture to scan and the computer's address. Give the code to the people you want to invite. Your file stays on your computer. Only you save it, and everyone sees your saves. Let people in Nobody gets in without you. When someone knocks, a card shows their name and device and whether they came on this network or through the internet: Edit lets them change the file with you. View only lets them watch; anything they try to change is taken back. Deny turns them away. The list under In the session shows everyone. Change a person's role there, or Remove them: they keep a copy of what they had, and they can't come back without a new invitation. When many people join A session's usual size is up to 20 people besides you. It is not a hard limit: how many people can work well together depends on their computers, the file and the network, so Studio asks you before a session grows past it instead of deciding for you. When someone knocks and would make more than 20, their card says so, and Studio explains what to expect: every change goes to everyone, so with many people each change takes longer to reach each person, and slower laptops and connections fall behind first. Then you choose: Allow anyway lets more people in for this session. Each person still waits for your Edit, View only or Deny. Keep it at 20 turns away the people waiting over the limit; they are told you are keeping the session small. To allow more before anyone knocks, choose Allow more people… under In the session. Back to 20 puts the usual size back. Your choice lasts for this session only; the next one starts at 20 again. If things slow down, fewer people, a quieter file (fewer people changing it at the same moment) or a better connection (a cable instead of Wi-Fi) helps. Codes on your own network A code works for 10 minutes. New code makes another; Stop inviting closes it. People already in stay. One-time code lets one person in per code. After 3 wrong codes in a row, Studio closes the code and makes a new one, and tells you. Sessions on your own network are free, for everyone. Invite links over the internet: not open yet Invite links aren't open yet. Sessions on your own network are free. Today the Over the internet part of the Live panel says so in a plain sentence instead of offering a link. When invite links open, this is how they will work: Under Over the internet, choose how long the link works (1 hour, 8 hours, or until you end the session), then Invite by link. Copy the link and send it like a meeting link. The link's secret part (after #) never reaches getcadcraft.com: the site only passes on encrypted messages it can't read, and you still approve each person. New link makes the old one stop working at once; Stop the link ends it. Making an invite link will need you signed in, with a commercial license in force for the app. Guests will need nothing: no account, no license. Join someone else's session In CadCraft Studio's home screen, find Join a live session. Type the code the host gave you, or type the address shown under their code followed by the code. (Once invite links open, you will paste the host's link here too.) Type your name and choose Join. Studio opens the right app; it fills with the host's file once the host lets you in. Without Studio, scan the picture under the host's code with a device on the same network: the app opens in the browser. In CadCraft, if you already have the same file open in a Studio window, you can join from that window instead: choose Live, then under Join with the drawing open here type the code or the address and code, and choose Join here. Only the changes made since the file was opened travel. If the host's drawing is a different one, it replaces the one in that window; your file on disk stays as it is. Guest, hotel and school Wi-Fi often keep devices apart, so a code can't find the host there. Use a network that lets devices see each other, such as the office network. Invite links, for when you are not on the same network, are not open yet. Who changed what: Recent edits The Live panel lists Recent edits: who made each change and what it was (the step's name, such as Line or Move), newest first, with how long ago. Show more lists more of them. With something selected in CadCraft or MarkupCraft, the panel also says who changed it last, and Only what is selected keeps just the edits that touched it. The list is made in your own window from the session's edits while the session runs. It is gone when the session ends: it is never saved in the file or sent anywhere. Everyone in the session can see who made each change. If you joined after people had started, or were away for a while, the edits from before are not in your list, and the panel says so. Assistants in a session If someone turned on assistants in their app's settings, their AI assistant can ask to work in the session too. The host's own assistant joins at once; anyone else's waits for the host, with a card saying whose assistant it is: Allow or Deny. An assistant edits like its person, with its own undo, and leaves the session when its person leaves. When the session ends The host chooses End the session, or closes the file. Everyone else keeps an unsaved copy of the file as it was: save it if you want to keep it. Leave does the same for you alone."},{"slug":"pen-and-touch","scope":"basics","title":"Draw with a pen on a tablet","summary":"What the app does when you draw with a pen on a touch screen: it ignores your palm, can leave the drawing to the pen while your fingers move the view, and lets the pen's buttons and a two-finger tap undo.","section":"tasks","order":94,"keywords":["pen","stylus","tablet","touch","palm","palm rejection","finger","fingers","two fingers","tap","undo","redo","side button","barrel button","eraser","pressure","hover","phone"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"a-pen-is-noticed-the-first-time","text":"A pen is noticed the first time","level":2},{"id":"your-palm-is-ignored","text":"Your palm is ignored","level":2},{"id":"let-the-pen-draw-and-your-fingers-move-the-view","text":"Let the pen draw and your fingers move the view","level":2},{"id":"undo-with-a-tap","text":"Undo with a tap","level":2},{"id":"the-pen-s-buttons","text":"The pen's buttons","level":2},{"id":"these-are-the-device-s-settings","text":"These are the device's settings","level":2},{"id":"not-built-yet","text":"Not built yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow the app works with a pen on a tablet or a touch-screen laptop, and the settings that change it. They are in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPen and touch\u003C/span\u003E, which shows on a touch screen, or once you have used a pen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-pen-is-noticed-the-first-time\"\u003EA pen is noticed the first time\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENothing changes until you touch the drawing with a pen. From then on the app remembers, on this device, that you draw with one, and the choices below start to apply. Before that, your fingers draw and select as usual.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-palm-is-ignored\"\u003EYour palm is ignored\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIgnore my palm while I use a pen\u003C/span\u003E on (it starts on), a touch on the drawing is ignored while the pen touches the screen, for a moment after you lift it or while it hovers just above the screen, and whenever the contact is as big as a palm. Rest your hand on the screen and draw.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf a quick finger right after the pen does nothing, wait a moment: that moment is the palm rule at work.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"let-the-pen-draw-and-your-fingers-move-the-view\"\u003ELet the pen draw and your fingers move the view\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFingers on the drawing, once you use a pen here\u003C/span\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw and select\u003C/span\u003E (the start): a finger does what the pen does.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOnly move the view\u003C/span\u003E: one finger on the drawing does nothing, so a stray touch never draws or picks. Two fingers still move and zoom the view as usual.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe toolbars, panels and dialogs work with your fingers either way.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"undo-with-a-tap\"\u003EUndo with a tap\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETap with two fingers to undo, three to redo\u003C/span\u003E on (it starts on), tap the drawing with two fingers together to undo your last step, and with three to redo it. It works with or without a pen. Moving the fingers is a pan or a zoom, never a tap.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-pen-s-buttons\"\u003EThe pen&#039;s buttons\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPen side button\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhat the app does (a right click)\u003C/span\u003E at the start, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPen eraser end\u003C/span\u003E (on pens that have one): \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhat the app does\u003C/span\u003E at the start, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E. In ModelCraft, what the app does is to erase while the eraser end touches.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"these-are-the-device-s-settings\"\u003EThese are the device&#039;s settings\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe pen and touch choices are kept for this device and apply in every app on it, so you set them once. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the layout\u003C/span\u003E and resetting an app&#039;s settings leave them as they are.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPen pressure and tilt don&#039;t change line weights yet, and no app has a handwriting or sketch-to-shape tool. The app reads the pen&#039;s pressure, tilt and hover, so these can come later per app.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn How the app works with a pen on a tablet or a touch-screen laptop, and the settings that change it. They are in Settings › General › Pen and touch, which shows on a touch screen, or once you have used a pen. A pen is noticed the first time Nothing changes until you touch the drawing with a pen. From then on the app remembers, on this device, that you draw with one, and the choices below start to apply. Before that, your fingers draw and select as usual. Your palm is ignored With Ignore my palm while I use a pen on (it starts on), a touch on the drawing is ignored while the pen touches the screen, for a moment after you lift it or while it hovers just above the screen, and whenever the contact is as big as a palm. Rest your hand on the screen and draw. If a quick finger right after the pen does nothing, wait a moment: that moment is the palm rule at work. Let the pen draw and your fingers move the view Under Fingers on the drawing, once you use a pen here: Draw and select (the start): a finger does what the pen does. Only move the view: one finger on the drawing does nothing, so a stray touch never draws or picks. Two fingers still move and zoom the view as usual. The toolbars, panels and dialogs work with your fingers either way. Undo with a tap With Tap with two fingers to undo, three to redo on (it starts on), tap the drawing with two fingers together to undo your last step, and with three to redo it. It works with or without a pen. Moving the fingers is a pan or a zoom, never a tap. The pen's buttons Pen side button: What the app does (a right click) at the start, or Undo or Redo. Pen eraser end (on pens that have one): What the app does at the start, or Undo or Redo. In ModelCraft, what the app does is to erase while the eraser end touches. These are the device's settings The pen and touch choices are kept for this device and apply in every app on it, so you set them once. Reset the layout and resetting an app's settings leave them as they are. Not built yet Pen pressure and tilt don't change line weights yet, and no app has a handwriting or sketch-to-shape tool. The app reads the pen's pressure, tilt and hover, so these can come later per app."},{"slug":"lost-toolbar","scope":"basics","title":"Where did my toolbar go?","summary":"The bars, menus or panels went missing. Here is what hides them on purpose, the keys that bring them back, and one button that puts the whole layout back.","section":"trouble","order":5,"keywords":["toolbar gone","toolbar missing","lost toolbar","lost menu","menu disappeared","ribbon disappeared","bars hidden","show bars","full screen","panels gone","reset layout","layout","ribbon","collapsed","locked toolbar"],"headings":[{"id":"the-quickest-fix-reset-the-layout","text":"The quickest fix: reset the layout","level":2},{"id":"the-bars-are-hidden-full-screen","text":"The bars are hidden (full screen)","level":2},{"id":"the-toolbar-looks-different","text":"The toolbar looks different","level":2},{"id":"the-toolbar-s-own-menu","text":"The toolbar's own menu","level":2},{"id":"everything-is-too-big-or-too-small","text":"Everything is too big or too small","level":2},{"id":"the-panels-are-gone","text":"The panels are gone","level":2},{"id":"on-a-phone","text":"On a phone","level":2},{"id":"still-missing","text":"Still missing?","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ENothing is deleted when a bar or a panel goes away: the app only hides or moves it. Each of these ways is on purpose, and each has a way back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-quickest-fix-reset-the-layout\"\u003EThe quickest fix: reset the layout\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E with the gear button in the app bar, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayout\u003C/span\u003E, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the layout\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe toolbar goes back to its first order and buttons, the panels go back to their sides, the screen mode goes back to the normal view and the menu style goes back to the one the app starts with. Your theme, language, sizes and the app&#039;s own settings stay as they are, and so do any menu styles you made yourself (choose them again under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/span\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn CadCraft Studio, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E in Studio&#039;s own window also has \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the layout in every app\u003C/span\u003E. Each app&#039;s windows follow at once, and an app you open later starts from its first layout.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-bars-are-hidden-full-screen\"\u003EThe bars are hidden (full screen)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe screen-mode button in the app bar, and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, go from the normal view to full screen, then to full screen with the bars hidden, then back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile the bars are hidden, a small \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow bars\u003C/span\u003E tab stays at the top middle of the window. Choose it, or point at the top edge of the window, and the bars slide down; they hide again a moment after you move away. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to leave this mode, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to leave full screen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf the app opens with its bars hidden every time, look at \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScreen\u003C/span\u003E: the app does what is chosen there when it opens.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETap the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow bars\u003C/span\u003E tab, or tap the top edge of the screen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-toolbar-looks-different\"\u003EThe toolbar looks different\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETools in another order, or tools you can&#039;t find:\u003C/strong\u003E someone arranged the toolbar. Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOriginal order\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EToolbar\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOnly icons, or only words:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EToolbar buttons\u003C/span\u003E in the same place picks icons and labels, icons only or labels only. Hold the pointer over an icon to see its name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETools won&#039;t move:\u003C/strong\u003E the toolbar is locked. Right-click the toolbar (or press and hold it) and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnlock the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E, or clear \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELock the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EToolbar\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA ribbon that shows only its tab names:\u003C/strong\u003E it is collapsed. Choose a tab name to open its tools for one command, or double-click a tab name (or choose the pin at the end of the tab row) to keep the ribbon open.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMenus or a ribbon instead of the toolbar:\u003C/strong\u003E a menu style is on. Pick the app&#039;s own style under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › the app&#039;s tab › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/span\u003E. Every command is in every style; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command K\"\u003E⌘K\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+K\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E finds any of them by name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-toolbar-s-own-menu\"\u003EThe toolbar&#039;s own menu\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERight-click the toolbar, or press and hold it on a touch screen, for a short menu:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArrange the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E: drag tools within their group and groups by their name, or move the focused one with the arrow keys. Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDone arranging\u003C/span\u003E in the same menu, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, when it looks right.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELock the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E / \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnlock the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E: a locked toolbar can&#039;t be arranged or reset by accident.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E: tools back in their first order.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith a ribbon menu style: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECollapse the ribbon\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPin the ribbon open\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a keyboard, the menu key (or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift F10\"\u003E⇧ F10\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+F10\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) opens it for the tool that has the focus.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"everything-is-too-big-or-too-small\"\u003EEverything is too big or too small\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInterface size\u003C/span\u003E makes the bars, toolbars, panels and dialogs of this app bigger or smaller, from 80 % to 140 %. The drawing keeps its own zoom, so a smaller interface leaves more room for it, for example with two apps side by side on one monitor. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPanel text size\u003C/span\u003E beside it changes only the text in the side panels, on top of the interface size. Each app keeps its own size; CadCraft Studio&#039;s own window has one in its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-panels-are-gone\"\u003EThe panels are gone\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPanels can float over the drawing, dock on another side, or move to a window of their own (often on a second monitor, or behind this window).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELook for a second window called after the app with &quot;panels&quot;, and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPut the panels back\u003C/span\u003E in it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOr choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset layout\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPanels\u003C/span\u003E, which brings every panel home.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"on-a-phone\"\u003EOn a phone\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a phone the panels are sheets: open one from the bar at the bottom of the screen. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETools\u003C/span\u003E button in that bar hides and shows the toolbar, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E puts it at the top or the bottom of the screen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"still-missing\"\u003EStill missing?\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFind any tool by name with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command K\"\u003E⌘K\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+K\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, even while its bar is hidden. If something is still not where it should be after resetting the layout, tell us on the support page with the app&#039;s name and what you were doing.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Nothing is deleted when a bar or a panel goes away: the app only hides or moves it. Each of these ways is on purpose, and each has a way back. The quickest fix: reset the layout Open Settings with the gear button in the app bar, or press Ctrl+,. Choose the General tab. Under Layout, choose Reset the layout. The toolbar goes back to its first order and buttons, the panels go back to their sides, the screen mode goes back to the normal view and the menu style goes back to the one the app starts with. Your theme, language, sizes and the app's own settings stay as they are, and so do any menu styles you made yourself (choose them again under Menus, keys and mouse). In CadCraft Studio, Settings in Studio's own window also has Reset the layout in every app. Each app's windows follow at once, and an app you open later starts from its first layout. The bars are hidden (full screen) The screen-mode button in the app bar, and Ctrl+Shift+F, go from the normal view to full screen, then to full screen with the bars hidden, then back. While the bars are hidden, a small Show bars tab stays at the top middle of the window. Choose it, or point at the top edge of the window, and the bars slide down; they hide again a moment after you move away. Press Ctrl+Shift+F to leave this mode, or Esc to leave full screen. If the app opens with its bars hidden every time, look at Settings › General › Screen: the app does what is chosen there when it opens. Tap the Show bars tab, or tap the top edge of the screen. The toolbar looks different Tools in another order, or tools you can't find: someone arranged the toolbar. Choose Original order under Settings › General › Toolbar. Only icons, or only words: Toolbar buttons in the same place picks icons and labels, icons only or labels only. Hold the pointer over an icon to see its name. Tools won't move: the toolbar is locked. Right-click the toolbar (or press and hold it) and choose Unlock the toolbar, or clear Lock the toolbar under Settings › General › Toolbar. A ribbon that shows only its tab names: it is collapsed. Choose a tab name to open its tools for one command, or double-click a tab name (or choose the pin at the end of the tab row) to keep the ribbon open. Menus or a ribbon instead of the toolbar: a menu style is on. Pick the app's own style under Settings › the app's tab › Menus, keys and mouse. Every command is in every style; Ctrl+K finds any of them by name. The toolbar's own menu Right-click the toolbar, or press and hold it on a touch screen, for a short menu: Arrange the toolbar: drag tools within their group and groups by their name, or move the focused one with the arrow keys. Choose Done arranging in the same menu, or press Esc, when it looks right. Lock the toolbar / Unlock the toolbar: a locked toolbar can't be arranged or reset by accident. Reset the toolbar: tools back in their first order. With a ribbon menu style: Collapse the ribbon or Pin the ribbon open. On a keyboard, the menu key (or Shift+F10) opens it for the tool that has the focus. Everything is too big or too small Settings › General › Interface size makes the bars, toolbars, panels and dialogs of this app bigger or smaller, from 80 % to 140 %. The drawing keeps its own zoom, so a smaller interface leaves more room for it, for example with two apps side by side on one monitor. Panel text size beside it changes only the text in the side panels, on top of the interface size. Each app keeps its own size; CadCraft Studio's own window has one in its Settings too. The panels are gone Panels can float over the drawing, dock on another side, or move to a window of their own (often on a second monitor, or behind this window). Look for a second window called after the app with \"panels\", and choose Put the panels back in it. Or choose Reset layout under Settings › General › Panels, which brings every panel home. On a phone On a phone the panels are sheets: open one from the bar at the bottom of the screen. The Tools button in that bar hides and shows the toolbar, and Move puts it at the top or the bottom of the screen. Still missing? Find any tool by name with Ctrl+K, even while its bar is hidden. If something is still not where it should be after resetting the layout, tell us on the support page with the app's name and what you were doing."},{"slug":"recover","scope":"basics","title":"Recover unsaved work","summary":"How the apps keep a recovery copy while you work, and how to get your work back after a crash or a closed tab.","section":"trouble","order":55,"keywords":["autosave","recover","recovery","crash","lost work","closed tab","unsaved","restore","backup"],"headings":[{"id":"when-the-copy-is-kept","text":"When the copy is kept","level":2},{"id":"get-your-work-back","text":"Get your work back","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EWhile you work, each app keeps a recovery copy of what you have not saved yet. It is not a save: your file on your computer only changes when you save it. The copy is there so a crash, a closed tab or a computer that turns off does not cost you your work.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-the-copy-is-kept\"\u003EWhen the copy is kept\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA few seconds after you stop editing, and at least every 30 seconds while you keep going.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhen you switch to another tab or window.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe copy stays in this browser, on this computer. Nothing is uploaded.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"get-your-work-back\"\u003EGet your work back\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the same app again, in the same browser.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf it closed before your work was saved, it asks \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERecover your work?\u003C/span\u003E and lists what it kept, with the time of the last copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERecover\u003C/span\u003E to open it, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDiscard\u003C/span\u003E to throw it away.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESave it to a file as soon as it opens.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENot now\u003C/span\u003E keeps the copies for 30 days, and the app asks again the next time it opens. A reload counts too: reload a tab with unsaved work and the app offers it back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhen you save, or close a file on purpose, its recovery copy is removed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAnother tab of the same app that is still open never offers your copies. It only offers work from a tab that has gone.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPrivate windows in some browsers, and a browser with no space left, cannot keep a copy. The app tells you when that happens, so save to a file more often.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClearing your browser&#039;s site data removes the copies.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENot every app keeps a recovery copy yet. Each app&#039;s own help says whether it does.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"While you work, each app keeps a recovery copy of what you have not saved yet. It is not a save: your file on your computer only changes when you save it. The copy is there so a crash, a closed tab or a computer that turns off does not cost you your work. When the copy is kept A few seconds after you stop editing, and at least every 30 seconds while you keep going. When you switch to another tab or window. The copy stays in this browser, on this computer. Nothing is uploaded. Get your work back Open the same app again, in the same browser. If it closed before your work was saved, it asks Recover your work? and lists what it kept, with the time of the last copy. Choose Recover to open it, or Discard to throw it away. Save it to a file as soon as it opens. Not now keeps the copies for 30 days, and the app asks again the next time it opens. A reload counts too: reload a tab with unsaved work and the app offers it back. Things to know When you save, or close a file on purpose, its recovery copy is removed. Another tab of the same app that is still open never offers your copies. It only offers work from a tab that has gone. Private windows in some browsers, and a browser with no space left, cannot keep a copy. The app tells you when that happens, so save to a file more often. Clearing your browser's site data removes the copies. Not every app keeps a recovery copy yet. Each app's own help says whether it does."}]};
